2 CONT William Ball Jr. born in England, June 2, 1641, died in Lancaster County, VA Sept 30, 1694.  Will dated Sept 28, 1694; proven Nov4, 1694.m#1, it is said, Miss Williamson, probably Margert or Mary, daughter of James Williamson of R
2 CONC appenhannock County, VA. m#2 Miss Harris of "Bay View" Northumberland Co. Va. m#3 about 1675 Margaret Downman, daughter of Rawleigh Downan.
2 CONT Children of last marriage:  A. William Ball born about 1676, died March 1774-5. m Hannah Beal. B.  Richard Ball, born about 1677, died 1727. m Sarah Young.  C.  James Ball, born 1678, died Oct 13, 1754, aged 76 years. m#1 June 15,
2 CONC  1699 Elizabeth Howson, m#2 April 16, 1707 Mrs. Mary Conway Dangerfield, m#3 April 25, 1742 Mrs.Mary Ann (Bertrand) Ballandine  D.  Joseph Ball, born about 1680, died Sept 1721 m. Mary Spencer  E.  George Ball, born about 1683, d 
2 CONC 1746. m#1 Miss Haynis, m#2 Grace Waddy.  F.  Samuel Ball, born Sept 26, 1686, d between Aug 18 and Nov 22, 1751. m Ann Catherine Tayloe in Lancaster Co, Va on Nov 25, 1717  G.  David Ball, twin with Samuel Ball, b Sept 26, 1686, d
2 CONC ied Dec 14, 1732. m#1 Mary ----- m#2 on May 29, 1727 Ellen Beal of Lancaster Co., Va  H. Stretchly Ball, died young  I.  Margaret Ball, born ----- d 1758. m Rawleight Downman, her cousin.  (See Virginia Genealogies by the Rev. Hor
2 CONC ace Edwin Hayden for the preceding.)
2 CONT
2 CONT From Colonial Families of the Southern States by Stella Pickett Hardy
2 CONT BALL FAMILY
2 CONT The Balls of Virginia have been closely associated with the history of the country since the Colonial days and have held many positions of honor and trust.  The emigrant forefather, Col. William, Ball, came to the Colony in 1657; 
2 CONC he was a descendant of William Ball, Lord of the Manor of Barkham, who died in 1480; and whose son and heir,
2 CONT 2-1. Robert Ball, d 1543; and his son and heir;
2 CONT 3-1. Wililam Ball, II, d. 1550 at Workingham; and his son and heir,
2 CONT 4-1. John Ball, d. 1599;  m. (First) Alice Hayes, of Finchamster; (Second) Agnes Holloway, dau. of Richard Hollaway, of Barhkam; his son by his second marriage,
2 CONT 5-5.  John Ball, II, their son and fifth child; d. 1628; m. Elizabeth Webb, dau. of Thomas Webb, of Roscombe; and their son and heir,
2 CONT 6-1.  William Ball, III, of Lincoln Inn; Attorney in the Office of Please in the Exchequer; was living in 1634; his son and heir,
2 CONT 7-1.  William Ball, IV, of England and I. of Virginia (Proginator of Ball family of Virginia)
2 CONT 8-2.  Capt. William Ball
2 CONT CAPT. WILLIAM BALL, Jr.., of St. Mary's Parish, Lancaster Co. Va. b. June 2, 1641 in England; d. Sept 30, 1694 in Virginia; made will Sept 28, 1694; probated Nov 4, 1694; m. (first) Mary or Margaret Williamson, dau. of James Willi
2 CONC amson , of Rappahannock Co. Va; (second) Miss Harris, of Bay View, Northumberland Co. Va; (third) Margaret Downman, dau of Rawleigh Downman. (It is not positively known that Capt. Ball married thrice, but it is positively known th
2 CONC at he married Margaret Doenman, and the other marriages have the authority of long accepted tradition.) Issue by third marriage:
2 CONT 9-1 William, b. 1676
2 CONT 9-2 Richard, b 1677
2 CONT 9-3 James, B. 1678
2 CONT 9-4 Joseph, b. 1680
2 CONT 9-5 George,
2 CONT 9-6 Samuel, b Sept 26, 1686
2 CONT 9-7 David, b Sept 26, 1686
2 CONT 9-8 Stretchley - died young
2 CONT 9-9 Margaret
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: 30 SEP 1694 in Millenbeck, Lancaster Co., VA 3 5 Burial: Bay View, Millenbeck, Lancaster Co., VA Note: will probate on 4 Nov. 1694 in Lancaster Co., VA - per A. (Nettie) Towle r GEnie message Feb. 2, 1994. Note: GAV-8   Fat
2 CONC her: William BALL b: ABT 1615 in Wiltshire (London?), England  Mother: Hannah ATHEROLD  b: 1616 in Burgh, Suffolk, England  Marriage 1 Margaret\\Mary WILLIAMSON  b: ABT 1644 in Isle of Wight Co., VA
2 CONT Married: 26 MAR 1673 in Rappahannock Co., VA
2 CONT Note: "Married by 20 Feb. 1673 per "Ball Families of Virginia's Northern Neck : An Outline", compiled by Margaret Lester Hill for Mary Ball Washingto n Museum & Library, Inc., 1990, p. 1. 6 7 1
2 CONT
2 CONT Will: 28 SEP 1694 1 Probate: 4 NOV 1694 Note: 
2 CONT There is a land grant in Maryland to William and Richard Ball in 1659. Mentioned in his father's will. His marriage with Margaret Downman is beyond doubt. The other marriages have the authority of long accepted tradition.   It is 
2 CONC not certainly known that Capt. Ball was thrice married; but it is impossible to ignore traditions that have any shadow of support. His marriage with Margaret Downman is beyond doubt. The other marriages have the authority of long 
2 CONC accepted tradition. The letter of Col. James Ball and a number of the Ball charts give the name of the first wife as "Williamson." This Col. James was the great-grandson of Capt. William, and is entitled to some credit in the matt
2 CONC er. However, I find no proof of the marriage of Capt. Ball with Miss Williamson.  (Geanealogy of the Glassell Family, page 53)
2 CONT
2 CONT Name: Margaret DOWNMAN Sex: F Note: dau. of Rawleigh Downman
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: William Ball married by 1672 to Margaret Williamson, an heiress to sizeable tracts now found in Richmond and Westmoreland Counties. Margaret was William's only wife, several confused traditions notwithstanding. One confusion
2 CONC  arose when Margaret's son George Ball married the granddaughter of John Harris and the Harris estate came to the Ball family through their maternal ancestry. This confusion reigned among the Ball heirs of the Harrises down at lea
2 CONC st to the 1890. All of the Harris-Ball documents are recorded. William Ball never had a Harris wife nor was there a Downman wife. The ancestry of William Ball shown in this genealogy is the one accepted by the Mary Ball Library.
2 CONT
2 CONT CAPTAIN WILLIAM BALL WILL   
2 CONT Graciously donated by Diane Cukro <mailto:cukro@optonline.net%20%20> 
2 CONT Will of Captain William Ball
2 CONT Dated 28 September 1694
2 CONT In the name of God. Amen.
2 CONT I Capt. William Ball of the parish of St. Mary's White Chapel in the County of Lancaster in Virginia being sick & weak in body but sound & Perfect memory Praised be ye Almighty God for the same & considering the frailty of this li
2 CONC fe being but a blast I doe make this my Last Will and testament in manner and form Following hereby revoking and making Void all other Will or Wills formerly by me made sealed or published. First I give my soule to God that gave i
2 CONC t me & commit my body to the Earth to be burried in decent order at the Discretion of my Executors hereafter named in hope and true confidence that I shall have a joyful Resurrection at the Last day thru the merits of my most dear
2 CONC  & Precious redeemer the Lord Jesus Christ. I give unto my Dr. wife all and every part of this 270 acres of Land whereon I now Live with all the housing & appurtances thereunto belonging and also my mill hereon standing during her
2 CONC  Natural life and also full Power to plant, make and manure for her own use a Plantation if she pleae on every other Parcel of Land hereafter by me hereby given & my will is that she bring up my dear Children until they be 16 yrs.
2 CONC  of age in Writing, Reading & c. and that they be under the tuition of my sons William and Richard, and that whenever the said mill be out of repair my will is that my said sons William Ball & Richard Ball give their assistance to
2 CONC  mend repaire or new build her and that they be paid for their paines as the mill earns it. I give unto my said sons William Ball & Richard Ball all my Tract lyeing in this Neck next to Richard Cundiff's being about 950 acres to b
2 CONC e equally divided between them provided they acquit all their right which they or iether of them may possibly have of any and to that Divident of Land in Richmond County next above Perpetua Creek being about 1,000 acres on which m
2 CONC y Quarter Plantation now is unto their two brothers Joseph Ball & Geirge Ball then
2 CONT I do give unto my said sons William Ball and Richard Ball to them and their heirs forever all and every part of the above said 950 avres of Land Lying in this Neck next to Richard Cundiffs as aforesaid but if my said two sond Will
2 CONC iam Ball & Richard Ball molest their two brothers Joseph Ball & George Ball in their Quiet Possession at any time hereafter either them or their heirs in the said 1,000 acres of Land at Perpetua Creek where my Quarter now is that 
2 CONC I then do hereby give said two sond Joseph Ball & George Ball and their heirs all and every part & Parcel of that 950 acres before hereby given unto my two sons William Ball & Richard Ball as aforesaid.
2 CONT I give unto my two sons Joseph Ball & George Ball to them and their heirs all and every part & parcel of that Divident of Land in Richmond County whereon my Quarter now is near the head of Perpetua Creek to be Equally divided betw
2 CONC een them.
2 CONT I give unto my der Daughter Margaret Ball and to her and her heirs forever all that and every part & Parcel of tht Divident whereon my mother Lives at the mouth of Corratomon being about 350 acres more to bee laid out of my ajoyni
2 CONC ng Divident next to the Plantation that James Wood lives on only reserving to my dear (wife or mother?) one third part of all the sider made thereon clear of all charge making & cask. I give to my said son William Ball a man serv'
2 CONC t named Luke Dickson and a nergo man named Robin, and a negro woman named Dyner and my Silver Tumbler and a Punch cup at mother's when she is dead.
2 CONT I give to my son Richard Ball a man serv't called Thomas Alderton and a negro woman called Dadoe and a Silver Mustard Pott. I give to my dear wife 3 negroes called Sambo, Tom & Mary and 3 Englis or white servants at my Quarter in 
2 CONC Richmond County called Robin_____James Canady & Sarah and a Silver salt siller, Tankard & Drinking Cup of Silver and which of my Cups she shall make Choice of. I give unto my son William all the residue of my cups after my dear wi
2 CONC fe hath her choice of one.
2 CONT I give to my son James Ball two slaves called Dominy & Kate and a boy and a girle.
2 CONT I give to my son David Ball a girle slave called Poll.
2 CONT I give to my daughter Margaret Ball two salve girls called Hanah & Betty thats at mother's.
2 CONT I give unto my son Stretchley Ball a Girl Slave called Saran.
2 CONT I give unto my son Samuel Ball a girl slave called Doll. The rest of my white servants and slaves not before hereby given, being Cox, Scotch, Tom, Betty & her mulatto child, 3 negro women Called Doll & Betty & Bess at mother's. I 
2 CONC give unto my three eldest sond, viz., William Ball, Richard Ball and James Ball in cinsideration that they be at the charge of bringing uo and maintaining their three youngest brothers. i give and Bequeath unto my said sons Willia
2 CONC m & Richard Ball 2/6s part of all the rest of my estate not hereby before given Desiring them to take all the care they can of their dear mother. And the residue of all my estate whereon had, found or being not before given I give
2 CONC  to be equally Devided between my dear children here named, James, Joseph, David, Geore, Margaret, Stretchley & Samuel after their mother's decease but my will is that my dear wife have the use of it all During her Natural Life.
2 CONT I doe make & Ordaine my son William Ball and my son Richard Ball the Executors of this my last Will and testament and my loving Brother Captain David Fox and my loving friends Mr. George Heale & Mr. Edwin Conway the overseers of t
2 CONC his my last will and testament.
2 CONT In Witness Whereof I have hereonto set my hand & seale this 28th day of Sept. A 1694.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: 
2 CONT In his fathers will, William inherited the home plantation known as Millenbeck. William married three times: to Mary or Margaret Williamson, daughter of James & Ann Underwood Williamson of Rappahannock Co., VA; to Margaret Downman
2 CONC  in 1675 (at "Marattico," their homestead in Lancaster Co., VA.), daughter of Rawleigh Downman and to a Miss Harris who died prior to 1695. (Ref: Colonial Families of the United States of America, Vol. 2 , by MacKinsey, p. 36. ) I
2 CONC n 1670, William is referenced as a member of the House of Burgess and by 1680, it is reported that he was serving as a Justice. In 1687 he was appointed to lay off the boundry's between, Lancaster & Northumberland Co's.; William i
2 CONC nherited "Millenbeck" from his father.
