2 CONT ==Ball Family Papers, 1680-1785. Accession 23554. Personal Papers Collection, The Library of Virginia, Richmond, Virginia. 
2 CONT Acquisition Information  
2 CONT
2 CONT Gift, 29 June 1950, of Frank Taylor of Fredericksburg, Virginia, through George H. S. King of Fredericksburg.  Descriptive Summary  
2 CONT
2 CONT Repository: The Library of Virginia  Accession Number: 23554  Title: Ball Family Papers, 1680-1785  Physical Description: 12 leaves and 47 pages  Language: English  Creator: Ball Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT Scope and Content  
2 CONT
2 CONT Papers, 1680-1785, of the Ball family of Lancaster County, Virginia, consisting of wills, 1680-1785, of William Ball (1615-1680), Richard Ball (ca. 1675-1726), Margaret Ball (ca. 1702-1783), and William Ball (d. 1785), all of Lanc
2 CONC aster County; inventories, 1727-1773, of the estates of Richard Ball, William Ball, Jr. (1700-1741), John Ball (d. 1772), and an unidentified estate; deeds, 1733-1758, for property in Lancaster County and slaves; letters, 1764 and
2 CONC  1780, discussing the property of Margaret Ball; depositions, 1775-1782, concerning the purchase of slaves from the estate of William Ball, and concerning crops made on the estate of John Ball; legal opinions, 1779 and no date, co
2 CONC ncerning the estate of Mary Ball, and concerning a deed between Margaret Ball and William Ball; a list, no date, of slaves from an unidentified estate; and Ball family genealogical notes.  Biographical Information  
2 CONT
2 CONT * Born about 1615, [https://www.geni.com/people/Col-William-Ball-of-Millenbeck/6000000014280951747?through=6000000000591496498 William Ball] emigrated to Virginia and became a prosperous landowner in Lancaster County, Virginia. He
2 CONC  served as a justice, a colonel of the militia, and as a delegate to the House of Burgesses. He fathered four children, including 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/people/Capt-William-Ball-of-Millenbeck/6000000000591496498 William Ball] (1641-1694).. [who] had nine children, including 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/people/Col-William-Ball-of-Millenbeck/6000000002713586427?through=6000000000591496498 William Ball] (ca. 1674-1744). He married [https://www.geni.com/people/Hannah-Beale-Ball/6000000002713586455?through=600
2 CONC 0000002713586427 Hannah Heale] (d. ca. 1744) and they had 6 children, including 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/people/William-Ball-IV/6000000004555806518?through=6000000002713586455  William Ball] (ca. 1700-1741). He married his cousin [https://www.geni.com/people/Mary-Ball/6000000008066903605?through=60000000045558
2 CONC 06518 Margaret Ball]  (ca. 1702-1783), the daughter of his uncle '''[https://www.geni.com/people/Richard-Ball-of-Lancaster-VA/6000000000591521279?through=6000000008066903605 Richard Ball]''' (ca. 1675-1726) 17 February 1723/4. The
2 CONC y had 6 children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents List  
2 CONT * Will, dated 5 October 1680 and recorded 10 November 1680, of William Ball (1615-1680) of Lancaster County.  
2 CONT * Will, recorded 8 February 1726/7, of Richard Ball (ca. 1675-1726) of Lancaster County.  
2 CONT * Inventory, recorded 9 August 1727, of the estate of Richard Ball.  
2 CONT * Deed, dated 12 May 1733, for 1/2 acre in Lancaster County from Hopkins Wilder to Lawrence Blade, both of Lancaster County, subsequent assignments dated 29 June 1742 and 22 October 1745.  
2 CONT * Inventory, dated 4 October 1742, of the estate of William Ball Jr. (ca. 1700-1741).  
2 CONT * Deed, dated 8 May 1747, for land and slaves from Margaret Ball (ca. 1702-1783) to William Ball (d. 1785), both of Lancaster County.  
2 CONT * Ball family genealogical chart, 24 August 1748.  
2 CONT * Deed, recorded in general court 27 October 1758, for 50 acres in Lancaster County from Margaret Ball to William Ball.  
2 CONT * Letter, 30 October 1764, from David Boyd to Margaret Ball concerning property inherited from her husband.  
2 CONT * Deposition, 1 August 1775, of James Newby (1723-1791) concerning the purchase of slaves by John Lee of Essex County, Virginia, from the estate of William Ball.  
2 CONT * Deposition, 1 August 1775, of Richard Ball (b. 1732) concerning the purchase of slaves by John Lee of Essex County, Virginia, from the estate of William Ball.  
2 CONT * Inventory, recorded 19 July 1773 in Lancaster County and 27 July 1773 in Fauquier County, Virginia, of the estate of John Ball (d. 1772).  
2 CONT * Legal opinion, 15 April 1779, of David Boyd concerning the estate of Mary Ball.  
2 CONT * Legal opinion, 22 December 1779, of Edmund Randolph concerning the estate of Mary Ball.  
2 CONT * Legal opinion, 22 December 1779, of Edmund Randolph concerning the estate of Mary Ball.  
2 CONT * Letter, 21 April 1780, from Margaret Ball to William Ball concerning her property.  
2 CONT * Deposition, 22 September 1782, by Norman Utterback, overseer, concerning crops made on the estate of John Ball.  
2 CONT * Will, dated 21 June 1777 and recorded 16 October 1783, of Margaret Ball.  
2 CONT * Will, dated 17 June 1785 and recorded 22 July 1785, of William Ball.  
2 CONT * Inventory, no date, of an unidentified estate.  
2 CONT * List, no date, of slaves of an unidentified estate.  
2 CONT * Legal opinion, no date, concerning a deed between Margaret Ball and William Ball.  
2 CONT * Genealogical notes, no date, on the Ball family.  
2 CONT * Will, no date, of Margaret Ball. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Ball b. abt 1675 m. Sarah Young
2 CONT
2 CONT Will Book 10, p. 510:
2 CONT
2 CONT Will dated Aug. 6, 1742; probated Sept. 10, 1742. Issue. In the name of god amen I Richard Ball of the Psh of St Marys white Chappel in the County of Lancaster being sick and weak of body but in perfect mind and memory thanks be t
2 CONC o god for the same, & calling to mind the urcertainty of this life I do therefore make & ordain this my last will & testament in manner and form following Imprimis I give & bequeath my soul to god hat gave it me in full hope of pa
2 CONC rdon & remission for all my sins in & through the merrits of my blessed Saviour Jesus Christ & my body to be decently buried at the disposition of my Exec'rs hereafter named Item I give to my daughter Sarah Selden my plantation wh
2 CONC ereon I now live and my water mill & all the land that lays below that road from colo. Willm Balls mill to Cundiffs to her and the heirs forever lawfully begotten and for want of such heirs then to my daughter Margaret Ball & the 
2 CONC second heirs male of her and my Son in law Wm Ball Bun'rs body lawfully begotten forever and for want of such then to the heirs male of their two bodies lawfully begotten & to the second son of that heir male & his heirs male fore
2 CONC ver Lawfully begotten Item I give to my daughter Margaret Ball my plantation & Land at the mouth of Corotomon River & to the heirs male of my son in law William Ball jun'r and of my sd daughter Margaret forever Lawfully begotten a
2 CONC nd for want of heirs male of their two bodys Lawfully begotten then I give the said land & plantation at the mouth of Corotomon to my sd daughter Margaret Ball & her heirs forever I likewise give to the second son of my son in Law
2 CONC  Willm Ball jun'r and my sd daughter Margaret to be begotten all the land on the upper side of the branch that runs along the North side of my old plantation so far as the road that leads to Cundiff's But upon condition that my so
2 CONC n in Law Wm Ball gives his Quarter plantation & land to the same son that then the aforesaid land to go to the sd second son & his heirs forever but not to be made use of till he come of age to enjoy it himself But if it should so
2 CONC  happen that there should be no such son or that my son in Law should not give him his Quarter plantation & land in like manner that then the said land shall go to my daughter Hannah in like manner as the land heretofore given her
2 CONC  Item I give to my daughter Hannah Ball my old plantation & all the land above the road that leads from Colo Wm Ball's mill to Cundiff's & all the land on the East side of the sd road from the corner hiccory that stands between th
2 CONC e two mill paths and from thence running a straight course to Dennis Conarse's to her and her heirs Lawfully begotten for ever and for want of such issue then I give he said plantation & Land to my daughter Easther Ball & her heir
2 CONC s lawfully begotten for ever. Item I give to my daughter Easter Ball one hundred and fifty pounds ster'g to be laid out in land & the land to go to her & her heirs Lawfully begotten forever. But in case my sd daughter should dye w
2 CONC 'hout issue and before the said money be laid out in Land that then the sd money be divided between the rest of my daughters. Will Book 12, p. 17: At a Court held in Lancaster County on the 3d day of ffeb'ry 1726 Mrs Sarah Ball wi
2 CONC dow of Richard Ball gent dec'd making oath in open Court that she found this writing among the papers of the sd dec'd and that she verily believed it to be his last will (there being no other to be found) and that the sd (R. B.) w
2 CONC rote & acquainted her with it May last And it being the Courts opinion that it is his own handwriting on the (oath) of the said Sarah the same was admitted to record and is recorded. Test T Edwards Cl Cur: Copia Test. S Edwards Cl
2 CONC  Cur. Sarah B. appears as adm'x Aug. 5, 1727; was admitted Exec'x of Capt R. B., North'd Co., 1731.* Reference: [http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=adboyd&id=I5328 RootsWeb's WorldConnect] - [http://www.geni
2 CONC .com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Oct 31 2016, 2:21:28 UTC''
2 CONT
