1 OCCU George Washington's grandmother
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Mary''' was the maternal grandmother of George Washington.  '''Her parentage is unknown'''.   She was not a knowndaughter of https://www.geni.com/people/John-Bennett/6000000010593043424 and his wife https://www.geni.com/people/
2 CONC Mary-Bennett/6000000000946092466?through=6000000010593043424, or of https://www.geni.com/people/Peter-Mountague-of-Nansemond/6000000001353952362 and his wife Maria Minor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary married three times.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prior to marrying Col. Joseph Ball, she was married to (1) a ___ Johnson, and had children Elizabeth Bonham(Johnson) and John Johnson (mentioned in her will).  She then married (2) Col. Joseph Ball, and had one daughter, Mary Wash
2 CONC ington (Ball).  She then married (3) Richard Hewes (Hughes).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary ___ Johnson Ball Hewes died in 1721 at Cherry Point Farm in Northumberland Co., VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of his first wife, Joseph Ball married Mary Johnson (died 1721), a widow who had two children from her previous marriage. Together they had one child, a daughter named Mary Ball (1708-1789). Joseph passed away, how
2 CONC ever, soon after when Mary Ball was only three years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Johnson Ball was remarried the following year to a man named Richard Hewes, who left her a widow for the third time in 1713. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her daughter, Mary Ball, was completely orphaned by the age of 12 or 13 and was subsequently raised by a guardian named http://www.geni.com/people/Col-George-Eskridge/6000000038878001141, a local lawyer, land speculator, and Burge
2 CONC ss, whose plantation was called Sandy Point. In 1731, at the age of 23, Mary Ball  married Augustine Washington (1694-1743), a middle-aged widower with three children, who ranged in age from 13 to 9. Their first child, a little bo
2 CONC y they named George Washington (1732-1799), was born early the following year.
2 CONT
2 CONT The will of Mary Johnson Ball, which was unearthed by G. W. Beale of more recent years, is dated December 17, 1721. It was made at St. Stephens parish and mentions Mary Ball, John Johnson, and her daughter, Elizabeth Bonim (Mrs. S
2 CONC amuel Bonim). John Johnson and George Eskridge are named as executors.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==will of Joseph Ball==
2 CONT
2 CONT ↑ Will Abstract of Joseph Ball, in Lee, Ida J. Abstracts Lancaster County, Virginia, Wills, 1653-1800. (Richmond: Dietz Press, 1959).
2 CONT
2 CONT p 6 - 
2 CONT BALL, Joseph,. Psh. St. Mary's White Chapel. Will. 25 June 1711. Rec. 11 July 1711. Wife Mary; son Joseph; daus. Hannah Travers; Anne Conway; Esther Chinn; Elizabeth Cornegie; Mary Ball; Eliza Johnson (dau. of his wife), dau. Mary
2 CONC , 400 acres of land in Richmond county; grandson James Cornegie (not 21) acknowledges gift to son Joseph Ball, and daus. Hannah Travers, Anne Conway and Esther Chinn made 11 Feb. 1707; Overseer John Hagan; negroes formerly belongi
2 CONC ng to Jon. Carnegie, decd. Extr. Joseph Ball. Wits. Geo. Finch, Elizabeth Finch, Margaret Miller, Joseph Taylor. W.B. 10, p. 88.
2 CONT
2 CONT [identifies Eliza(beth) Johnson as a daughter of Joseph Ball's wife, Mary]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT === Disputed Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Unknown-226833
2 CONT
2 CONT Lots of books have been written about theories of the ancestry of Mary, until we find "original sources" or well supported evidence explained, her parents will remain as "unknown". Here are several theories about the origins of Ma
2 CONC ry:
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1. That she was daughter of Peter & Mary (Doodes/Minor) Montague.[citation needed] There appears to be no proven facts to back this claim, but several "suggestions":
2 CONT * Handwritten notation of "Montague" in old Ball papers.[citation needed]
2 CONT * George Washington used as his common seal the Montague crest; he wrote in 1792 "I have also seen, and have used, as you may perceive by the seal on this packet, a flying griffin for the crest." (The crest of the Montague family 
2 CONC includes a griffin) Washington never claimed to be a Montague, he believe that the crest he was using was the Washington crest. [13]
2 CONT * 2. The tombstones in White Chapel church proves the intermarriages of the Montagues and the Balls. [citation needed]
2 CONT * 3. Mary Bennett who had married first William Johnson.[14] [15]
2 CONT * 4. That Mary was the housekeeper of Joseph Ball, a claim which has been used to "prove" that President George Washington was of "plebeian" origins.[16]
2 CONT * 5. Mary may have been born Mary Montague Bennett or Mary Montague (Unknown). With Montague being her mother's Maternal line supported by previous and future genealogy naming habits or one that was related by marriage. Transcript
2 CONC s of Marriage Records show she was Mary Bennett when she married William Johnson who passed making her widow Johnson with young child. Later Widow Johnson was either living with, keeping house for, or next door to Joseph Ball who 
2 CONC she went on to quickly marry. Before Ball passed he and Mary had Mary Ball. Mary went on to marry again to Hewes. Given the number of times that Mary has been known to marry and become a widow, more research is still needed to con
2 CONC firm her maiden name. Bennett could be the name of a husband prior to William Johnson, it could be a name given to her if adopted into another family, or it could be her actual last name.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.colonial-settlers-md-va.us/getperson.php?personID=I24753&tree=Tree1
2 CONT
2 CONT Conclusion
2 CONT
2 CONT Based on the body of evidence compiled here, the only factual statements are: (1) Mary Montague, b. abt 1664, is the dau. of Peter Montague and Mary Minor; and (2) she m. 24 Oct 1682, Thomas Paine (or Payne) inMiddlesex County, Vi
2 CONC rginia. What follows is my interpretation of what the indirect evidence suggests:
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Montague probably d. in 1686/7 as Mary Payne--probably while giving birth to her first child, Thomas Paine Jr. This supposition is enhanced further by the fact that Mary (Montague) does not appear in the record after 1687. It
2 CONC  is not insignificant, for example, that Mary is not mentioned with her siblings in the settling of her father's estate in 1695. Furthermore, her husband Thomas appears to have remarried, as the few extant records containing Thoma
2 CONC s Paine show he had a father-in-law named Ellett or Elliott. Thomas Paine apparently did not leave a will when his estate was being settled in 1694/5, but there is no record of a widow or other court orders against his estate as o
2 CONC ne might expect were there any surviving family. On the other hand, the evidence supporting Mary Bennett as grandmother of George Washington is far more convincing, if not actually proven.
2 CONT
2 CONT > Mary Bennet daughter  of John Bennett b 1624 (and Mary Crawley). 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=hughestree&id=I1525 (dead link) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Little is known of Mary Johnson Ball, the mother of Mary Ball, other than the fact of her English birth and the supposition that she was descended from the Montagues. She was a widow with two children, Elizabeth and John Johnson, 
2 CONC when she became housekeeper for Joseph Ball. Many are the romantic fancies that have been woven about her name without the least fact to support them. That she was good and true and worthy to be the mother of such a woman as Mary 
2 CONC Ball Washington and the grandmother of such a hero as General Washington is sufficient for us. Her name will live when many of royal blood are forgotten.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Mr. Ball, Mary Johnson Ball became the wife of Captain Richard Hewes, a vestrynam of St Stephen's parish, Northumberland, and removed to this parish with her three children, John and Elizabeth Johnson, and Mary 
2 CONC Ball, who had not reached her fifth birthday. Captain Hewes died in 1713, and she followed him to the other world when Mary Ball was but thirteen. Her will, which was unearthed by G. W. Beale of more recent years, is dated Decembe
2 CONC r 17, 1721. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It was made at St. Stephens parish and mentions Mary Ball, John Johnson, and her daughter, Elizabeth Bonim (Mrs. Samuel Bonim). John Johnson and George Eskridge are named as executors. John Johnson did not long survive his mother,
2 CONC  for his will was probated on February 22, 1726, in Westmoreland. He left all that his stepfather had given him in Stafford County to Mary Ball, and a young dappled-gray riding horse besides. Mary was then just eighteen.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://genforum.genealogy.com/montague/messages/1485.html
2 CONT
2 CONT George Washington's maternal grandmother is likely Mary Bennett, rather than Mary Montague.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT II. GEORGE WASHINGTON
2 CONT
2 CONT A tradition has existed for fifty years or more, that George Washington was of Montague descent, through his mother Mary Ball. It probably originated from the fact that William Montague married, 1727, a dau. of Capt. Richard Ball,
2 CONC  who was Mary Ball's cousin [their fathers were brothers]. This subject has been thoroughly investigated by Rev. Horace E. Hayden in his Va. Genealogies, published Wilkes-Barre, Pa., 1891. The compiler also has made a thorough sea
2 CONC rch, and left no means untried to obtain the truth.
2 CONT
2 CONT The result is, that the only place where such descent could be possible, was through Mary Ball's mother who was, before Col. Ball married her, a Mrs. Mary Johnson, a widow, of Lancaster Co., Va. A tradition exists in the Ball fami
2 CONC ly that Mrs. Mary Johnson was born in England. This tradition has been traced to Mrs. Ann Shearman, whose mother was Esther Ball, the half sister of Mary Ball. If it is true, that she was born in England, then - any descent from P
2 CONC eter Montague was impossible. No record has been found to show the maiden name of Mrs. Mary Johnson, or who she was before her marriage to Johnson. If she was a Miss Montague, she would have to be a daughter of one of the sons of 
2 CONC the emigrant Peter Montague. One of his sons did have a daughter whose name was Mary Montague, but church records prove that she married, Oct. 24, 1682, Thomas Payne, and no record exists to show that she ever afterward married an
2 CONC y one else. Records of that time and locality are lost, and the maiden name of Mrs. Mary Johnson [Washington's grandmother] will probably never be known. 
2 CONT
2 CONT MARRIAGES: Many references indicate that Mary Montague had been married several times. No one knows for sure just how many times nor which surname she used when marrying Capt. Joseph Ball. This has become a confusing factor in doi
2 CONC ng family research and led to many claiming Capt. Ball's wife as an ancestor. My most convincing source of information is my Grandfather, Fielding Montague. Many is the time that he told us about his Great Grandfather Andrew Monta
2 CONC gue being a Cousin and friend of George Washington. 
2 CONT
2 CONT George Washington's Grandmother, Mary Montague, and Andrew Montague's Great Grandfather, Peter Montague, were brother and sister.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary's maiden name may have been Bennett.  A Mary Bennet married a William Johnson 10 Feb 1688/89 at Christ Church Parish.  This was the same church she later attended. However, no record of this Mr. Johnson's death exists.  Her m
2 CONC aiden name has also been speculated to be Montague, as a Mary Montague of roughly the same age attended the same church, and had a cousin marry in to the Ball family.  But this Mary Montague is known to have married a Thomas Payne
2 CONC  prior to Mary Ball's marriage to Joseph Ball. Further, a burial record for Mary Payne is found int he Christ Church parish register.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Various records, primarily from the Christ Church Parish Records.  See [http://genforum.genealogy.com/montague/messages/1485.html here] for a summary timeline of both Mary Ball and Mary Montague and citations to all record
2 CONC s.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT # Pierce, Elizabeth Combs. "Mary Johnson, second wife of Col. Joseph Ball," William and Mary College Quarterly Historical Magazine 15, no. 2: 176-7.
2 CONT # "MOUNT VERNON, ITS CHILDREN, ITS ROMANCES, ITS ALLIED FAMILIES AND MANSIONS" by Minnie Kendall Lowther (contributed by Marcia Hovenden)
2 CONT # Roberts, Gary Boyd. Ancestors of American Presidents. (Boston, Massachusetts: New England Historic Genealogical Society, 2009), p. 1.
