1 NOTE Name: Dr. James WILLIAMSON Sex: M Birth: in Rappahannock Co., Va Death: 1656 Fact 1: Burgess Death	1656	
2 CONT Occupation	Physician	
2 CONT Education	Medical Doctor	
2 CONT Notes Dr. James Williamson (d. 1656) was Justice of the Peace, Commmissioner, Land Owner and Physician.
2 CONT  Father: Richard D. WILLIAMSON  b: 1593 in London, England  Mother: Margaret SHERWOOD  b: 1593 in London, England  Marriage 1 Ann UNDERWOOD b: in Virgina 
2 CONT Married: BEF. 1652
2 CONT Children 
2 CONT Margaret WILLIAMSON  b: ABT. 1650 in Northumberland Co., Va 
2 CONT Elizabeth WILLIAMSON 
2 CONT Mary WILLIAMSON   (married John Rosier)
2 CONT William WILLIAMSON  (born before 1656 and died young)
2 CONT Arabella WILLIAMSON  b: 1676 in Stafford Co,VA
2 CONT Note: One source suggests that Anne and James may have had an additional son named James. That James made his will in Essex County in 1716. He owned land on Hoskins and Lucas creeks. Additional research is necessary.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT Douglas' and Purcell's of Tidewater VA
2 CONT  Updated: Wed Sep 3 15:48:12 2003 Contact: William Roberts 
2 CONT billjo2@comcast.net
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: 
2 CONT Virginia Biography, Volume I-II  "Williamson, Dr. James, married Ann Underwood, daughter William Underwood of Isle of Wight county, John Hammond dedicated to him his tract "Leah and Rachel" He afterwards moved to Lancaster County,
2 CONC  where he was one of the justices in 1652, but died before 1656, leavening two daughters Margaret, who married William Ball, JR and Mary , who married John Rosier, JR.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: 
2 CONT James is referenced as a Justice in the Isle of Wright County Court for the year 1646. On 9 June 1649, James was a witness to a transaction between John Upton and William Dawson. On 20 September 1649, a power of attorney was made 
2 CONC by William Lea of Middlesex (England) to William Sheard of London to collect a debt of 3200 lbs. of tobacco from James Williamson, Gentlemen of Red Poynt, Isle of Wright Co. Virginia. In approximately 1650, James moved from Isle o
2 CONC f Wright County farther north in Virginia to the area that would become Lancaster County in 1651 and later Rappahannock County in 1656.  On 22 May 1650, Sir William Berkeley, Governor, granted to James Williamson 1800 acres of lan
2 CONC d lying in Rappahannock River near the mouth of Millock Creek at the line of William Underwood, on the mayne river side above the land of Anthony Jackman, for the transportation of thirty six persons into the colony. (Ref: Land Pa
2 CONC tents II, pg. 212). On the same day, James Williamson took possession of 1150 acres on the north side of the Rappahannock River for the transport of 23 individuals into the colony. (150 of which were marsh) (Ref: Land Patents II, 
2 CONC pg. 211). This transaction is also referenced in 'Cavaliers and Pioneers: Abstracts of Virginia Land Patents and Grants 1623-1666': Bekk Naruib Bygebt: Genealogical Publishing Company, Baltimore, Md., 1979, p. 190.  On 1 Janaury 1
2 CONC 652 John is referenced as a Chief Justice of the Lancaster County Court. At one time he served as a member of the House of Burgesses as a representative of Lancaster County. He is reported to have been a friend of John Hammond of 
2 CONC Isle of Wright who later removed to the colony of Maryland and was author of "Leah and Rachel" which he had dedicated to the instant James. On 20 November 1655, John mortgaged an estate he had in England, in fee, to Thomas Cox.  J
2 CONC ames is believed to not have left a will, but the inventory of his estate was returned on 16 December 1656. His plantation is believed to have been known as 'Cobham Park'. The estate of James was executed by his brother-in-law Wil
2 CONC liam Underwood (Ref: Records 1654 - 1702, pg. 308). The appraisal of James estate included 33 cattle and 54,100 lbs. of tobacco. According to 'Hoskins of Virginia and Related Families', James had a plantation that included 1800 ac
2 CONC res on the North side of the Rappahannock River above Toutuskey Creek, and additional lands that totaled 3670 acres.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Dr. James was a justice of Isle of Wight County in 1646. On June 9, 1649, james and Thomas Wombwell witnessed a deed of John Upton. . .On May 22, 1650, Mr. James Wiliamson was granted 1150 acres of land bordering the Rappahannock
2 CONC  River on the North, with some of the area marshland. On the same dated he was granted 1800 acres on the Rappahannock, and on the Totaskey Creek. James died prior to 1656 when his estate was inventoried. (He received the land for 
2 CONC the importation of 23 colonists)." (Benjamin Williamson, Williamson Genforum database, #5116, 20 Jan 2005)  "james Williamson married Ann Underwood and removed from Isle of Wight, with the Underwoods, to Rappahannock. John Hammond
2 CONC  dedicated to him his tract 'Leah and Rachel'." (William and Mary College Quarterly, Vol 7, #7, Isle of Wight County Records, p 221, On-Line Genealogy Library databse)  "Underwood, Mr. Wm. . .Ref to as Lancaster Co. Sells to Meads
2 CONC  700 acres, half of a patent of 1400 acres, above tha land of Mr. Jas. Williamson, etc. Wit: Ric Loes, Jno Phillips. 12 Sept 1653 p. 111." (Virginia Colonial Abstracts, Vol I, Lancaster Co., p 228, On-Line Genealogy Library databa
2 CONC se)  "Captain John Upton whose will on January 16, 1651/2, gave his stepchildren equal shares in 1500 acres in Isle of Wight County adjoining Ambrose Bennett, excepting 300 acres sold to Mr. Robert Brasswell, and gave Ann Williams
2 CONC on an equal share with them in the lands in Rappahannock County." (The Williamson Family of Isle of Wight County, VA, Hugh Buckner Johnston, Historical Southern Families, Vol III, p 159, On-Line Genealogy Library database)  Invent
2 CONC ory of estate, and administration of his orphans: 8 Dec 1656, Lancaster Co., vA
