1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT https://ancestors.familysearch.org/en/L69D-GBT/john-chilton-iii-1666-1726
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/103485423/john-chilton
2 CONT
2 CONT John Chilton, II, was born about 1666, probably in Northampton County, Virginia. He was referred to as the "II" probably because his father, also John Chilton (1624-1708) was the first Chilton immigrant to arrive in the colonies. 
2 CONC However, John was actually the 4th John Chilton identified. John's mother's name was either Jane or Joan. His father, John Chilton settled in Westmoreland County, Virginia and in 1660 he purchased from John Gerard a tract of land 
2 CONC on Curryomen Bay and called his estate "Curriorman, the village of Chiltons". In future generations, the Chilton also appeared as Shelton.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the bio below, John first married an unidentified person. His second wife was Lettice Mary Ball. Lettice was born about 1670 in Westmoreland County, Virginia, the daughter of William Ball and Margaret Downman. Lettice
2 CONC  was a first cousin to Mary (Ball) Washington, mother to the first president of the United States, General George Washington.
2 CONT
2 CONT John and Mary had the following known children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1. John Chilton, III, (ca. 1699-1744) 
2 CONT * 2. Thomas Chilton (ca. 1699-1765)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Lettice died about 1700 in Westmoreland County, Virginia. John then married Mary (nee unknown) 1) Bowcock, 2) Watts, 3) Summerville. John died July 11, 1726, in Westmoreland County, Virginia. John's will was made July 7, 1726, and
2 CONC  named his two sons, John and Thomas. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Chilton-64
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 John (c.1666-1726) as early as 1697 bought land in Westmoreland where he made his home on Curriomen Creek. He acquired large holdings in Westmoreland and Stafford.
2 CONT
2 CONT "On 1 June 1699 John Hartley charged in court that John Chilton combined and confederated with others and unlawfully burned and destroyed a periwig and hat belonging to Hartley."
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1711 he was sworn in as justice of the peace.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1724 Capt. John Chilton was in the Westmoreland militia.
2 CONT
2 CONT John had two wives. The first (name unknown) (most genealogies assume first wife is Letitia Ball who is listed on this profile until proven otherwise) bore him sons John and Thomas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before April 1718 he remarried to Mary --- Bowcock Watts Summerville who had children only by the first two of her three previous husbands.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/103485423/john-chilton
2 CONT
2 CONT The below bio was posted by an unnamed person but have kept it as it was when the memorial was transferred to me (unsolicited), but as John was my 8th great-grandfather, I am happy to manage it.
2 CONT
2 CONT For convenience, this is John Chilton II; there was a John Chilton III. He was probably conceived during the voyage bringing his parents, John and Joan, from England to claim the estate of his brother, George, who died 1666 withou
2 CONC t issue. Immigrant John Chilton died in 1707.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1690s JC2 settled in Westmoreland County where Currioman Creek empties into the Potomac River. This arrival preceded that of the Lee family, who built their seat, Stratford Hall, nearby. His identity as a "Merchant" meant t
2 CONC hat he captained a vessel for episodic trade with England. The name of John's first wife is not known. At age 51 in March 1718, he found reason to become the 4th husband of Mary Bowcock Watts Somerville. That reason ran out in 172
2 CONC 6. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The vertical split in the marker (see photo) was caused by the blade of a plowshare as it was surprisingly disinterred by farmer Mr. Sanders. A local historian contacted a descendant, serving in the US Army. Major McDonnell hired 
2 CONC a mason to frame the shards and elevate them well above ground. By his retirement, this man was General McDonnell.
2 CONT
2 CONT Visiting this grave marker has been a pilgrimage for Many Chilton's over the last 85 years. When my visit occurred in summer 1984 the Potomac bank property was still in the Sanders family. Eventually, when it came up for sale, a s
2 CONC mall group of Chilton's could not compete for price with land developers. 
2 CONT
2 CONT US history books are salted with John Chilton's prominent descendants. So is Wikipedia and, of course, Find A Grave.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Gravesite Details
2 CONT
2 CONT This is a lone grave marker. It is being pushed up by the roots of a large tree nearby.
2 CONT
2 CONT Inscription
2 CONT
2 CONT Here Lyeth in hopes of a Joyful Resurrection the Body of Mr. John Chilton Merchant who Departed this life the 11th day of July Anno Domini 1726 Aged about 60 years
2 CONT
2 CONT Restored July 12th 1927
2 CONT By Major John Chilton McDonnell
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000194846347959&size=large}}
2 CONT ----
