1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Rawleigh Travers===
2 CONT *Birth: abt 1680 Lancaster County, VA
2 CONT * Death: before 9 May 1733 Overwharton Parish, Stafford County, VA
2 CONT * Parents:  Rawleigh Travers, 2nd and (unknown) Cale
2 CONT * Married: Hannah Ball
2 CONT
2 CONT Children 
2 CONT # Elizabeth Travers Abt 1707 -
2 CONT # Sarah Travers 1708-1714 - 1789
2 CONT # Rawleigh Travers, IV  1711 - 1749
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://www.colonial-settlers-md-va.us/getperson.php?personID=I024234&tree=Tree1
2 CONT
2 CONT -------
2 CONT
2 CONT From "The Hunter Family of Virginia and Connections" from Genealogy.com:
2 CONT
2 CONT Justice of Lancaster County 1682, 1687-8. "Mr. Rawleigh Travers, a member of this Court, appeared in behalf of his mother, Mrs. Elizabeth Wilkes (the Chart notes, Wilkes by a subsequent marriage) Oct. 11, 1693, Mr. Rawleigh Traver
2 CONC s vs. Mrs. Elizabeth Wilkes, admnx. of Rawleigh Travers, late of this County, deceased." In 1687, Rawleigh Travers paid taxes on four tithables. He was living in 1694. Issue, according to Hayden, p. 300, 24 E, Rawleigh Travers, of
2 CONC  Stafford, died single, Oct. 13, 1749; 25 E, Elizabeth (Cooke); 26 E, Sarah, died 1789, married, second, Peter Daniel. Rawleigh Travers, the father of those just mentioned, married Hannah Ball, who was mentioned in her father's Wi
2 CONC ll, June 26, 1711 (Hayden, p. 58). Joseph Ball was of Epping Forest, Lancaster County, born, 1649, died, 1711, married 1675 (?), Elizabeth Romney. By Exec. Jour. Coun. Col. Va., Vol. 1, p. 444, Joseph Ball was appointed Lt. Col. o
2 CONC f Militia of Lancaster County, June 3, 1699, and in Vol. 2, p. 438, he is on a list of persons appointed to take and certify affidavits in Lancaster County, Jan. 5, 1704-5. In Jour. H of B, 1695-1702, on page iv, Joseph Ball is li
2 CONC sted as a Burgess for Lancaster County for 1698, 1700, 1702.
2 CONT
2 CONT The statement of the children of Rawleigh Travers is somewhat more complete in Hayden on p. 300 than was given in the preceding paragraph: Rawleigh Travers married before 1707 (because on p. 57, Hayden, a deed of Joseph Ball, Feb.
2 CONC  7, 1707, names Mrs. Hannah Travers and others) to Hannah, daughter of Joseph Ball and Elizabeth (Romney) Ball; they had 24 E, Rawleigh, died single, Oct. 15, 1749, Stafford County, Will dated Oct. 10, 1749, proved, Dec. 12, 1749;
2 CONC  25 E, Elizabeth; 26 E, Sarah, died, 1789, who married, first, Capt. Christopher Pierson, second, Peter Daniel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Miss Fanny Blow Hunter, W and M, Vol. 10, p. 64, says Rawleigh Travers and Hannah Ball were married in 1696, which seems questionable if she were born in 1683. The writer would rather consider the date as 1700 to 1702.
2 CONT
2 CONT The father and mother of Rawleigh Travers were married prior to 1644 and the father was dead in 1674, a period of 30 years, during which Rawleigh Travers was born, a rather wide range; but this is somewhat reduced, for, we find he
2 CONC  was a Justice for Lancaster County in 1682 which would not have been before he was at least 21 years of age, and scarcely that young. We will say he was given this responsible position when he was 25 years of age. This would put 
2 CONC his birth in 1657; if so, he was 26 years of age when Hannah Ball was born; and, if he married her in 1702, when she was 19 years of age, he was at that time, 45 years old and in 1733, he would have been 76 years of age while she 
2 CONC was 50 years old. This is a conservative view of the situation, possibly too conservative, for 25 years would be a low age for a Justice of the Peace. If we say he was born in 1645, then his age in 1702 would have been 57, 38 year
2 CONC s older than Hannah Ball; and, in 1733, he would have been 88 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT The purpose of these figures as to Rawleigh Travers' probable age at different periods is merely to speculate upon the likelihood of the union of an old gentleman and a young bride coupled with the possibility of his death long be
2 CONC fore 1733, the year in which his Inventory was filed; and upon the likelihood of her having married 9 D, Simon Pearson, many years before Simon's death in 1733, and of her having been the mother of Simon's children, instead of the
2 CONC ir stepmother.
2 CONT
2 CONT To further the examination of this subject, it may be well, at this time, to make some rough guesses as to the ages in 1733 of the known children of Rawleigh Travers and Hannah (Ball) Travers in order to observe if their ages were
2 CONC  appreciably greater than the ages of the Pearson children.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Letter from Peter Vivian DanielLetter from Peter Vivian Daniel  http://search.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/sse.dll?rank=1&amp;new=1&amp;MSAV=1&amp;msT=1&amp;gss=angs-d&amp;gsfn=peter+vivian+&amp;gsln=daniel&amp;gskw=biographies+of+notable
2 CONC +americans&amp;ne=2&amp;db=vabio&amp;ct=11759- states he was with the Virginia house of Burgesses.  In 1663, Gerard Fowke conveyed 3,350 acres of Crow&apos;s Nest to Rawleigh Travers. By 1723, the land was known as &apos; Travers 
2 CONC Neck&apos;.Letter from Peter Vivian DanielLetter from Peter Vivian Daniel.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
