1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Augustine Washington''' (November 12, 1694 – April 12, 1743) is best known today as the father of George Washington, the first president of the United States.  
2 CONT
2 CONT “''Father, I Can Not Tell a Lie: I Cut the Tree''," Posthumous 1867 lithograph by John C. McRae after a painting by G. G. White. < [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augustine_Washington#/media/File:Augustine_Washington.jpg link] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000192697034821&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Augustine Washington
2 CONT * Born: 12 November 1694 - 	Mattox Creek, Washington Parish, Westmoreland, Colony of Virginia, British Colonial America 
2 CONT * Died: 12 April 1743 (aged 48) - Ferry Farm, King George (now Stafford) County, Colony of Virginia, British Colonial America 
2 CONT * Burial: George Washington Birthplace National Monument, Latanes, Westmoreland County, Virginia, USA.  Plot: Popes Creek Plantation
2 CONT * Parents: Lawrence Washington and Mildred Warner
2 CONT * Spouse: Jane Butler (1715-1730; her death) - married 20 Apr 1715, Washington Parish, Westmoreland County, Virginia
2 CONT * Spouse: Mary Ball (1731-1743; his death) - married 6 Mar 1731, Washington Parish, Westmoreland County, Virginia
2 CONT * Ethnicity:	English
2 CONT * Occupation: Planter
2 CONT * Religion: Anglican Church
2 CONT
2 CONT He was part-owner of two iron-furnaces at < [https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=2261 Accokeek] > in Virginia and < [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Principio_Furnace Principio] > in Maryland and was financially able to visit England on tw
2 CONC o occasions to deal directly with his partners. He owned land and buildings in 3 Virginia counties and was master of at least 49 slaves. Though not as wealthy as certain other planters in the < [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North
2 CONC ern_Neck Northern Neck] >  (the tidewater counties lying between the Potomac and Rappahannock Rivers), he was a man of good social standing.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From  < [https://presidentgeorgewashington.wordpress.com/2010/03/26/george-washingtons-father-augustine-washington/ “George Washington’s father”] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ''George Washington with his father at his father’s iron furnace (one of the first in America) near Fredericksburg, Virginia.  As such, George Washington’s father Augustine Washington was one of the first individuals to initiate i
2 CONC ndustrialization in America''.
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000192694742821&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT George Washington's father, Augustine Washington (Lawrence, John, Lawrence), was born at Mattox Creek, Westmoreland County, Virginia on November 12, 1694. He remembered little of his father, as Lawrence Washington died when August
2 CONC ine was only 4 years old. Two years later his mother, Mildred Warner, married https://www.geni.com/people/Colonel-George-Gale/6000000003108911326?through=6000000003110467510 [or Gayle], and during the autumn of 1700, the family mo
2 CONC ved to England. Their family life abroad was short-lived, however, as Augustine's mother died a year later, when he was only seven. His stepfather, who seems to have been a kindly man, sent Augustine and his brother, John, to < [h
2 CONC ttps://www.jstor.org/stable/1919932 Appelby School] >  [in lake district]. Their schooling, too, was cut short, for a year or two later the boys returned to Virginia to live with their elder cousin, John Washington of "Chotank," w
2 CONC hose < [https://colonialvirginiaportraits.org/location/chotank-creek/ plantation] > was located on the Potomac, about 20 miles up the river from Bridges Creek in Stafford now King George Co. While little is known of Augustine's te
2 CONC en-age activities one can surmise that he enjoyed plantation life to the utmost while living with various relatives whose farms were located on the wooded south shore of the Potomac River.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 20 Apr 1715 at Washington Parish, Westmoreland County,  when he was 21, Augustine first married to Jane Butler. She was  born 24 Oct 1699 at Popes Creek in Washington Parish, Westmoreland County, and died in Washington Parish o
2 CONC n 24 Nov 1729.  Jane was the daughter of Caleb Butler Esq. of Westmoreland County and his wife Mary Foxall.  Also an orphan, she brought 1,300 acres of land to their marriage, which she had inherited from her father.  
2 CONT
2 CONT At this time in his life, Augustine was richer than his father had been.  His home was called “Lisson Estate.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Jane Butler and Augustine Washington had four children, but only Lawrence and Augustine survived into adulthood.  The children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Butler Washington was born in 1716 in Bridge Creek, Westmoreland Co., VA. He died before 1729.
2 CONT # Lawrence Washington   (1718-1752).  Married Anne Fairfax.
2 CONT # Augustine Washington , Jr. - (1720-1762). Married Anne Aylett.
2 CONT # Jane Washington was born about 1722 in Bridge's Creek, VA. She died on 17 Jan 1735.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Jane’s death in 1728, Augustine Washington married second to Mary Ball, at married at Sandy Point, Westmoreland County, on 17 March 1731 (N.S.), the Rev. Walter Jones of Cople Parish officiating.  She was born in 1708 in Lan
2 CONC caster County and died on 25 Aug 1789 in Fredericksburg, Virgina.  Mary was the daughter of Joseph Ball and Mary Bennett.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Ball and Augustine Washington had six children.  Their youngest, Mildred, died as an infant.  The children were: 
2 CONT # President George Washington was born on 11 Feb 1732 in Pope's Creek, Westmoreland Co., VA. He died on 14 Dec 1799 in Mount Vernon, Fairfax Co., VA. George married Martha Dandridge. Martha was born on 21 Jun 1731 in New Kent Co.,
2 CONC  VA. She died on 22 May 1802 in Mount Vernon, Fairfax Co., VA.
2 CONT # Elizabeth Washington was born on 20 Jun 1733 in Pope's Creek, Wakefield, Westmoreland Co., VA. She died on 31 Mar 1797 in Western View, Culpeper Co., VA. Elizabeth married Colonel Fielding Lewis on 7 May 1750. Fielding was born 
2 CONC in 1725. He died in 1781.
2 CONT # Samuel Washington    (1734-1781).  Married 1) Jane Champe 2) Mildred Thornton 3) Lucy Chapman 4) Anne Steptoe 5) Susannah Perrin 
2 CONT # John Augustine Washington  (1736-1787).  Married Hannah Bushrod.
2 CONT # Charles Washington (1738-1799).  Married Mildred Thornton.
2 CONT # Mildred Washington was born in 1739 in Pope's Creek, Wakefield, Westmoreland Co., VA. She died on 23 Oct 1740 in Pope's Creek, Wakefield, Westmoreland Co., VA.  	
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT From “Augustine Washington” at < [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augustine_Washington ''Wikipedia''] > retrieved 12 March 2023
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Augustine Washington Sr'''. (November 12, 1694 – April 12, 1743) was the father of the first U.S. president, George Washington. He belonged to the Colony of Virginia's landed gentry. Like his father and sons, Washington owned p
2 CONC lantations which he operated by the use of enslaved labor, as well as speculated in less developed land and even operated an iron mine. Although Washington did not serve as a legislator (unlike his father and son), he held various
2 CONC  offices in the counties in which he held land.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Early and family life
2 CONT
2 CONT Augustine Washington was born in Westmoreland County, Virginia, on November 12, 1694, to Mildred Warner and her husband, Capt. Lawrence Washington, a militia captain and a member of the Virginia House of Burgesses. His paternal gr
2 CONC andparents were Lt. Col. John Washington (c. 1631–1677) and his first wife, Anne Pope. His maternal grandparents owned Warner Hall and associated plantations in Gloucester County.
2 CONT
2 CONT Augustine was four years old when his father died. His mother remarried and moved her family to England, where she died when all were still children; although their mother's will named their stepfather George Gale as their guardia
2 CONC n, their cousin John Washington fought to have himself named the children's guardian and brought them back to Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Washington came of age (and into his inheritance) in 1715, he married Jane Butler, another orphan, who had inherited about 640 acres (2.6 km2) from her father, Caleb Butler. The young couple settled on the Bridges Creek prope
2 CONC rty and had four children, only two of whom (Lawrence and Augustine Jr.) lived to adulthood. After Jane's death in November 1728[1] or 1729,[2] Washington married Mary Ball in 1731, and the couple had five children who survived to
2 CONC  adulthood — George, Betty, Samuel, Charles, and John Augustine — and a daughter, Mildred.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Career
2 CONT
2 CONT When he reached legal age in 1715, Augustine Washington inherited about 1,000 acres (4.0 km2) on Bridges Creek in Westmoreland County; his sister Mildred inherited what was called the < [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_Huntin
2 CONC g_Creek Little Hunting Creek] > property;[4] they both inherited slaves. In 1718, Washington purchased land on Pope's Creek, adjoining his property on Bridges Creek, and set about establishing himself. Between 1723 and 1735 he hir
2 CONC ed a local contractor to build a house, which was probably completed about 1726 despite the contractors death (later called Wakefield).[5] In the same year, Washington purchased the Little Hunting Creek property from his sister Mi
2 CONC ldred.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1725, Augustine Washington entered into an agreement with the Principio Company of England to start an iron works on Accokeek Creek in Stafford County, and he also owned a stake in their Maryland ironworks.[7] In 1735, the fami
2 CONC ly moved to the Little Hunting Creek property, which was closer to the Accokeek Furnace.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1738, Augustine Washington purchased the 150-acre Strother property across the Rappahannock River (now known as Ferry Farm) and moved the family there at the end of that same year.[4][8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Augustine Washington was active in the Anglican Church, the local militia and politics.[9] He took the oath as justice of the peace for the Westmoreland county court in July 1716,[10] and served as county sheriff.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT After Augustine Washington died in 1743 at the age of 48, his 11-year old son George inherited the former Strother property and its slaves.[11] Because he had not reached legal age, his mother Mary managed this property for him un
2 CONC til he came of age. She lived on the property until 1772 when she was 64, when George moved her to a house in Fredericksburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lawrence Washington inherited the Little Hunting Creek property and renamed it "Mount Vernon," to honor Admiral Edward Vernon, with whom he had served in the Royal Navy in 1741 during the Battle of Cartagena de Indias during the W
2 CONC ar of Jenkins' Ear.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Augustine Sr's will, if his son Lawrence died without children, the Little Hunting Creek property would go to Augustine Jr. and Augustine Jr, in turn, would have to give up the Popes Creek property to his brother Geor
2 CONC ge. If Augustine Jr. did not want the Little Hunting Creek property, it would then be inherited by George. Upon Lawrence's death Augustine Jr. chose Popes Creek and its slaves rather than the former Little Hunting Creek property. 
2 CONC Lawrence's only surviving child Sarah only lived until 1754; therefore, George Washington ultimately inherited the Little Hunting Creek property which by that time was known as Mount Vernon. At his death, Augustine Washington Sr. 
2 CONC held a total of 64 slaves who were assigned among the various plantations.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Lawrence Washington's widow Ann had a life interest in the Little Hunting Creek plantation. Because she remarried and was not living at Mount Vernon, she leased the property to George beginning in 1754. Upon her death in 1761, Geo
2 CONC rge Washington inherited the plantation outright.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===About that cherry tree 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ''George Washington admits to his father, Augustine, that he chopped his cherry tree''. 
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000192694725842&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From “George Washington and the Cherry Tree.” National Mall and Memorial Parks.  < [https://www.nps.gov/articles/george-washington-and-the-cherry-tree.htm nps.gov] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT The story goes that when Washington was six years old, he received a hatchet as a gift, after which he promptly went and cut down his father’s favorite cherry tree. When his father found out about it, he was understandably angry a
2 CONC nd confronted his son, asking if he had done it, to which little George replied that yes, indeed, he had done it. And with those brave words, father’s anger melted away and he embraced his son, exclaiming that his honesty was wort
2 CONC h more than a thousand trees.
2 CONT
2 CONT That sounds like a nice, uplifting story, but did it happen? Nobody knows for sure.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Washington died in 1799, there was a great demand around the country to learn more about our first national hero. Minister and itinerant bookseller Mason Locke Weems, more popularly known as Parson Weems, was more than willin
2 CONC g to fulfill that need and in 1800 quickly wrote and published The Life of Washington, an immediate bestseller that cemented Washington’s legendary status. It wasn’t until the book’s fifth edition in 1806 that the cherry tree stor
2 CONC y first made an appearance. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Though profit may have been high on Weems’ priority list in writing this biography, as a minister he wanted to teach morality, and the best way to do that was to show by example using the one individual everyone looked up to. Wash
2 CONC ington’s admission of guilt was proof that his public greatness was due to his private virtues, to which anyone could aspire. Bringing in the father also served to add more depth to who George Washington was as a person. Though mu
2 CONC ch was known and written about Washington’s military feats, very little was known about his relationship with his father, who had died when George was 11. The cherry tree example of a warm and generous relationship between father 
2 CONC and son added strong emphasis to the character of the quickly-being-mythologized George Washington.
2 CONT
2 CONT So is it true? Weems  said he got the story from an elderly woman who had been friends with the family. Since she chose to remain anonymous it is an unreliable source. The official answer from both Ferry Farm, Washington’s childho
2 CONC od home where it would have occurred, and Mount Vernon, is no, it’s only a story. But, really, does it matter?
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1861, on his way to his inauguration in Washington, D.C., newly-elected president Abraham Lincoln stopped to speak at the New Jersey Senate. He told them he had read a number of books growing up that taught him basic life lesso
2 CONC ns, Pilgrim’s Progress, Aesop’s Fables, and Lessons in Elocution, to name just a few. But it was ''The Life of George Washington'', he continued, that really sparked his imagination and showed “there must have been something more 
2 CONC than common” in Washington and what he had stood for. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For Weems’ book and story to have a good message (i.e. don’t lie) and for it to have made such an impression on someone as equally influential as Lincoln, who are we to protest?
2 CONT
2 CONT Chop away, Mr. Washington!
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Will and Estate 
2 CONT
2 CONT SOME WILLS FROM THE BURNED COUNTIES OF VIRGINIA AND NOTHER WILLS NOT LISTED IN VIRGINIA WILLS AND ADMINISTRATIONS 1632-1800 by William Lindsay Hopkins, p. 71 
2 CONT
2 CONT *AUGUSTINE WASHINGTON King Geo. Co 11 Apr 1743/6 May 1743 
2 CONT * Son Lawrence Washington. 
2 CONT * Son Augustine Washington. 
2 CONT * Son George Washington land bought from the executors of Mr. William Strother, decd. 
2 CONT * Son Samuel Washington.
2 CONT *  Son John Washington. 
2 CONT * Son Charles Wash ington land whereon Thomas Lewis not lives and the land purchased of Gabriel Adams in Prince William Co. Land purchased of Mr. James Hooe. 
2 CONT * Daughter Betty Washington (under 18). 
2 CONT * Present wife (not named). 
2 CONT * Exors: Son Lawrence Washington, 
2 CONT * Daniel McCarty and Nathaniel Chapman. 
2 CONT * Wit: Robert Jackson, Anthony Strother, James Thompson. 
2 CONT * Also land in Westmoreland Co. where Benjamin Wicks and Thomas Fi nch now live, lots in fredericksburg purchased of the Executors of Col. Henry Willis and a piece of land where Matthew Tiffy late lived.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT From “Augustine Washington”  at < [https://www.colonial-settlers-md-va.us/getperson.php?personID=I113473&tree=Tree1 “Early Colonial Settlers of Southern Maryland and Virginia’s Northern Neck Counties] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT Augustine Washington's Will: "I Augustine Washington of the County of King George--Gentleman, being sick and weak but of perfect disposing sence (sic) andmemory. Do make my last will and testament in manner herebyrevoking all form
2 CONC er will or wills whatsoever by me heretoforemade.
2 CONT
2 CONT Imprimis: I give unto my Son Lawrence Washington and his heirs forever all that plantation and tract of Land at Hunting Creek in the County of Prince William containing by estimate, two hundred and five hundred acres with the Wate
2 CONC r Mill adjoining thereto or lying near the same and all the Slaves, Cattle and Stocks of all Kinds whatsoever and all the Household Furnature(sic) whatsoever now in and upon or which have been commonly possessed by my said son, to
2 CONC gether with said plantation track of Land and Mill.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item: I give unto my son Augustine Washington and his heirs forever all my lands in the County of Westmoreland except suchonly as are hereinafter otherwise disposed of together with twenty five head of neat Cattle forty hogs and t
2 CONC wenty sheep and a negro man named Frank besides those negros formerly given him by his mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item: I give to my son George Washington and his heirs the land I now live on which I purchased of the Executors of Mr. Wm Strther deceased. And one, one moiety of my land lying on Deeps Run and ten negro Slaves.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item: I give unto my son Samuel Washington and his heirs my land at Chotank in the County of Stafford containing about six hundred acres and also the other moity (sic) of my land lying on Deeps Run.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item: I give unto my son John Washington and his heirs my land at the head of Mattox in the County of Westmoreland containing about seven hundred acres.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item: I give unto my son Charles Washington and his heirs the land I purchased of my son Lawrence Washington whereon Thomas Lewis now lives, adjoining to my said son Lawrence's land above devised. I also give unto my said son Char
2 CONC les and his heirs the land I purchased of Gabriel Adams in the County of Prince William containg about seven hundred acres.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item: It is my will and desire that all the rest of my negroes not herein particularly devised may be equally divided between my wife and my three sons Samuel, John and Charles, and that Ned, Jack, Bob, Sue, and Lucy may be includ
2 CONC ed in my wife's part, which part of my said wife's, after her decease I desire may be equally divided between my sons George, Samuel, John and Charles, and part of my said negroes so devised to my wife I mean and intend to be in f
2 CONC ull satisfaction and in lieu of her dower in my negroes. But if she should insist notwithstanding on her right of Dower in my negroes I will and desire that so many as may be wanting to make up her share may be taken out of the ne
2 CONC groes given hereby to my sons George, Samuel, John and Charles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item: I give and bequeath unto my said wife and four sons George, Samuel, John and Charles, all the rest of my Personal Estate to be equally divided between them which is not particularly bequeathed by this will to my wife and it 
2 CONC is my will and desire that my said four sons Estates may be kept in my wife's hands until they respectively attain the age of twenty one years, in case my said wife continues so long unmarried but in case she Should happen to marr
2 CONC y before that time I desire it may be in the power of my Executors to oblige her husband from time to time as they shall think proper to give security for the performance of this last will in paying and delivering my said four son
2 CONC s their Estates respectively as they come of age, or on failure to give such security to take my said sons and their estates out of the custody an tuition of my said wife and her husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item: I give and bequeath to my said wife the crops made at Bridge Creek, Chotank, and Rappahanock (sic) quarters at the time of my decase (sic) for the support of herself and her children and I desire my wife may have the liberty
2 CONC  of working my land at Bridge Creek Quarters for the time of Five years after my decease, during which time she may fix a quarters on Deep Run.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item: I give my son Lawrence Washington and the heirs of his body lawfully begotten forever that tract of Land I purchased of Mr. James Hooe adjoining to the Said Lawrence Washinton's land on Maddox in the County of Westmoreland w
2 CONC hich I gave him in lieu of the land my said son bought for me in Prince William County of Spencer and Harrison and for want of such heirs then I give and devise the same to my son Augustine and his heirs forever.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item: I give to my son Lawrence all the right title and interest I have to in or out of the Iron Works in which I am concerned in Virginia and Maryland provided that he do and shall, out of the profits raised thereby purchaes for 
2 CONC my son Augustine three young working slaves as I have herein before directed and also pay my daughter Betty when she arrives at the age Eighteen years the sum of four hundred pounds which right title and interest on the condition 
2 CONC aforesaid I give to my said son Lawrence and his heirsforever.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item: I give to my said daughter Betty a negro child named Mary daughter of Sue and an other named Betty daughter of Judy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item: It is my will and desire that my sons Lawrence and Augustine do pay out of their respective Estates devised to themo ne half or moity (sic) of the debts I justly owe and for that purpose I give and bequeath unto my said two 
2 CONC sons one half of the debts owing to me.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item: For as much as my several children in this will metioned being of several venters cannot inherit from one another in order to make a proper provision against their dying without issue. It is my will and desire that in case m
2 CONC y son Lawrence should die without heirs of his body lawfully begotten that then the land and Mill given him by this my will lying in the county of Prince William shall go and remain to my son George and his heirs but in case my so
2 CONC n Augustine should choose to have the said lands rather than the lands he holds in Maddox either by this will or any Settlement. Then I give and devise said lands in Prince William to my said son Augustine and his heirs on conveyi
2 CONC ng the said lands in maddox to my son George and his heirs. And in case my said son Augustine shall happen to die without issue of his body lawfully begotten, then I give and bequeath all the lands by him held in Maddox to my son 
2 CONC George and his heirs and if both sons Lawrence and Augustine should happen to die without issue of their several bodies begotten then my will and desire is that my son George and his heirs may have his and their choice either to h
2 CONC ave the lands of my son Lawrence or the lands of my son Augustine to hold to him and his heirs and the land of such of my said sons Lawrence and Augustine as shall not be so chosen by my son George or his heirs shall go to and be 
2 CONC equally divided by my sons Samuel, John and Charles and their heirs share and share alike and in case my son George by the death of both or either of my sons Lawrence and Augustine should according to this my intention come to be 
2 CONC possessed of either their lands then my will and desire is that said lands hereby devised to my said son George and his heirs should go over and be equally divided between my sons Samuel, John and Charles and their heirs share and
2 CONC  share alike and in case all my children by my present wife should happen to die without issue of their bodies. Then my will and desire is that all the lands by this my will devised to any of my said children should go to my sons 
2 CONC Augustine and Lawrence if living and to their heirs or if one of them should be dead without issue then to the survivor and his heirs. But my true Intent and meaning is that each of my children by my present wife may have their la
2 CONC nds in fee simple upon the contingency of their arriving at full age or leaving heirs of their bodies lawfully begotten or on their dying under age and without lawful issue their several parts to descend from one to another accord
2 CONC ing to their course of descent and the remainder of their or any of their land in this clause mentioned to my sons Lawrence and Augustine or the survivors of them in only upon the contingency of all my said children by my present 
2 CONC wife dying under age and without issue living, my sons Lawrence and Augustine or either of them. Lastly; I constitute and appoint my son Lawrence Washington and my good friends Daniel McLarity and Nathaniel Chapman --Gentlemen Exe
2 CONC cutors of this my last will and Testament. In witness whereof I have hereunto set my hand and Seal theEleventh day of April 1743.
2 CONT
2 CONT AUGUSTINE WASHINGTON (L.S.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed sealed and published in the presence of us.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Jackson
2 CONT
2 CONT Anthony Strother
2 CONT
2 CONT Jas Thompson
2 CONT
2 CONT Provided further that if my lands at Chotank devised to my son Samuel should by course of law be taken away then I give to the said Samuel in lieu thereof a tract of Land in Westmoreland County where Benjamin Wicks and Thomas Finc
2 CONC h now live by estimation seven hundred acres.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item: I bequeath to my son George one lot of land in the town of Fredericksburg which I purchased of Col. John Walton also two other lots in the said town which I purchased of the Executors of Colo. Henry Willis with all the house
2 CONC s and appurtenances thereunto belonging. And whereas some proposals have been made by Mr. Anthony Strother for purchasing a piece of land where Matthew Tiffy lately lived now if my Executors shall think it for the benefit of my sa
2 CONC id son George then I hereby empower them to make conveyance of the said land and premices (sic) to the said Strother. In witness whereof I have hereunto set my hand and Seal theEleventh day of April 1743.
2 CONT
2 CONT AUGUSTINE WASHINGTON (L.S.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed sealed and published in the presence of us.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Jackson
2 CONT
2 CONT Anthony Strother
2 CONT
2 CONT Jas Thompson
2 CONT
2 CONT At a court held for King George County the 6th day of May 1743 The last will and testament of Augustine Washington Gent'n deceased was presented into Court by Lawrence Washington Gent'n one of the Executors who made oath thereunto
2 CONC  and the same was provided by the oath of Anthony Strother and James Thompson admitted to Record.
2 CONT
