1 TITL Colonel
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *Charles served as a Colonel during the American Revolutionary War for Virginia - DAR Ancestor #  A121948 and SAR Patriot # P-314185 - https://services.dar.org/public/dar_research/search_adb/default.cfm / https://s
2 CONC arpatriots.sar.org/patriot/display/314185
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonel Charles Washington was an American planter, public official, and military officer best known as the youngest full brother of President George Washington and the founder of Charles Town, Virginia (Present day West Virginia)
2 CONC . While overshadowed by his eldest brother, primary sources document him as a significant civic leader in colonial and revolutionary Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vital Timeline & Early Life
2 CONT Birth: Born May 2, 1738, at Little Hunting Creek (later renamed Mount Vernon by his half-brother, Lawrence). He was the youngest son of Augustine Washington and his second wife, Mary Ball Washington.
2 CONT
2 CONT Childhood: Following his father’s death in 1743 when Charles was only five, he spent his youth at Ferry Farm near Fredericksburg, Virginia. He inherited roughly 750 acres of land and several enslaved people from his father's estat
2 CONC e.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: In October 1757, he married his cousin, Mildred Thornton. They raised four children who survived to adulthood:
2 CONT The legally documented and factually recorded children of Colonel Charles Washington (the youngest full brother of President George Washington) and his wife, Mildred Thornton, include four children who survived to adulthood.
2 CONT According to authoritative records maintained by organizations like George Washington's Mount Vernon and the historical papers of the Washington estate, their children in chronological order are:
2 CONT 1. George Augustine Washington
2 CONT Born: Circa 1759 (Stafford County, Virginia)
2 CONT Died: February 5, 1793 (Mount Vernon, Virginia)
2 CONT Documented Facts: He served as an officer during the Revolutionary War and later became the trusted manager of George Washington's Mount Vernon estate. He married Martha Washington's niece, Eleanor "Nelly" Custis.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Frances Ann Washington
2 CONT Born: June 4, 1763 (Virginia)
2 CONT Died: February 1815
2 CONT Documented Facts: She married twice, first to Colonel Burgess Ball (a cousin of George Washington through the Ball line) and later to Francis Peyton. Her descendants are extensively tracked in Virginia court and property records.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Captain Samuel Thornton Washington
2 CONT Born: Circa 1770–1774 (Virginia)
2 CONT Died: 1831 (Kanawha County, Virginia / now West Virginia)
2 CONT Documented Facts: He spent much of his adult life in what is now West Virginia and is highly documented in local land registries. He inherited part of his father's estate and served as an officer in the local militia.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Mildred Gregory Washington
2 CONT Born: Circa 1772–1777 (Virginia)
2 CONT Died: 1804
2 CONT Documented Facts: She married Colonel Thomas Hammond around 1797 in Charles Town, Virginia (Present day West Virginia) - the city founded by her father, Charles.
2 CONT ⚠️ Addressing the "Elizabeth 'Lizzie' Ransome" Daughter Myth
2 CONT If you look at public, user-generated family trees like  for Charles Washington, you will sometimes see a fifth child appended to this list named "Elizabeth 'Lizzie' Ransome (born Washington)".
2 CONT However, this is a documented genealogical error. Professional historians and the official Washington family estate records explicitly confirm that Charles and Mildred only had the four children listed above who reached adulthood.
2 CONC  "Lizzie Ransome" does not appear in Charles Washington's final will, his land distribution deeds, or any primary colonial Virginia records. It is a modern insertion created by online family trees attempting to force a legal conne
2 CONC ction to the Ransome family oral legend.
2 CONT https://www.geni.com/people/Elizabeth-Lizzie-Ransome/6000000044159812185?through=6000000007420371968
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: Died September 16, 1799 (aged 61) at his estate in what is now West Virginia—predeceasing George Washington by only three months. He is buried in the family plot at his estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Public Career & Revolutionary Patriotism
2 CONT The Rising Sun Tavern: In 1760, Charles constructed a prominent home in Fredericksburg, Virginia. He lived there until 1780, after which the building was sold and ultimately became the historic Rising Sun Tavern, a famous meeting 
2 CONC place for early revolutionaries like Patrick Henry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Civic Leader: He served actively as a magistrate for Stafford County, a justice of the Spotsylvania County Court, and a warden and vestryman at St. George's Episcopal Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anti-Stamp Act Rebellion: In February 1766, Charles joined his brothers Samuel and John Augustine in signing the Leedstown Resolutions (sometimes referred to as the Westmoreland Resolves). This was one of the earliest formal acts 
2 CONC of colonial resistance against British taxation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Military Service: He was a member of the Spotsylvania Committee of Safety in 1774 and later served as a Lieutenant Colonel in the Virginia Militia during the Revolutionary War.
2 CONT
2 CONT Founding Charles Town
2 CONT Land Inheritance: In 1752, Charles inherited a tract of land in the Shenandoah Valley (then Berkeley County, Virginia) from his deceased half-brother, Lawrence Washington.
2 CONT
2 CONT Happy Retreat: In 1780, Charles relocated his family to this land and commenced building his historic estate, Happy Retreat, where George Washington was a frequent visitor.
2 CONT Town Layout (1786): Charles petitioned the Virginia General Assembly to establish a town on 80 acres of his property. He plotted the streets himself, naming the main thoroughfare Washington Street and mapping the intersecting cros
2 CONC s-streets after his wife, Mildred, and his brothers.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Four Corners: He gifted the four corner lots at the center intersection of George and Washington Streets to the public. Today, those exact lots in Charles Town, West Virginia, still house the Jefferson County Courthouse, City 
2 CONC Hall, and Charles Washington Hall.
2 CONT Updated: 06/2026
2 CONT —-
2 CONT Charles WASHINGTON was born on 2 May 1738 in Little Hunting Creek, Stafford (now Fairfax) County, Virginia, son of Augustine WASHINGTON and Mary BALL.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles married Mildred THORNTON, daughter of Col. Francis THORNTON III and Frances GREGORY, in October 1757 in Virginia.
2 CONT In 1757, nineteen-year-old Charles had proposed to Mildred, his cousin. This caused some turmoil in both families. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mildred's father Francis was deceased, and her mother requested some written assurance of her daughter's future well being from the head of the Washington family. This was 25-year-old George Washington, then residing in Mount Vern
2 CONC on, who viewed her request for a written contract as an aspersion on his "principles of conduct."  
2 CONT
2 CONT Fielding Lewis, now his brother-in-law, worked out an accommodation with John Thornton which satisfied both families. Thornton, Mildred's uncle became his niece's legal guardian. The wedding was then allowed to proceed.  
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1760 Charles built a home in Fredericksburg, Virginia that was later to become The Rising Sun Tavern. The tavern at 1306 Caroline Street was the only one in Fredericksburg at the time and served as a meeting place for such Revo
2 CONC lutionary War figures as Patrick Henry and the Lees. Charles had purchased the property from Fielding's older brother, Warner Lewis, late of Gloucester County. The house on Caroline Street, where Charles lived until moving away in
2 CONC  1780, was the scene of visits from many friends and relatives, including his mother and George and Martha Washington.  
2 CONT
2 CONT While not as well-known as his famous brother, Charles led an active life and was involved in civic affairs. He was a magistrate in Stafford County, Virginia before the Revolutionary War, and he joined brothers Samuel and John Aug
2 CONC ustine in being some of the first signers of the "Leetown Resolve" in 1766. This was a response by residents of Westmoreland County, Virginia in protest to the British Stamp Act. By signing this Resolve, these men established them
2 CONC selves as serious participants in the resistance to the British Crown, which developed ten years later into the Revolutionary War.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles saw military service on 21 September 1780 when he served as Lt. Colonel and colonel of the Spotsylvania County, Virginia militia during the Revolutionary War. He was comissary of the Caroline County Militia 1775-76.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Town, West Virginia was founded in 1786 by an act of the Virginia Legislature on land provided by Charles Washington. Charles had already made a name for himself as a signer of the "Westmoreland Resolution" in 1766 and as 
2 CONC colonel in the Virginia militia. His brother, George Washington, had surveyed the land there in 1748 and liked it so much that he urged Lawrence Washington, his half-brother and the owner of Mount Vernon, to purchase land in the a
2 CONC rea. In 1750, an 18-year-old George Washington invested his earnings as a surveyor in 500 acres of farmland on Bullskin Run, two miles southwest of Charles Town, later enlarging his holding to some 2,300 acres. When Lawrence Washi
2 CONC ngton died in 1752 he willed Mount Vernon to George and the land upon which Charles Town would be built to his 14-year-old half-brother, Charles. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the fall of 1780 a 42-year-old Charles moved his family from Fredericksburg to what would become Charles Town and built a home he named "Happy Retreat". George Washington was a frequent guest. In 1786, on 80 acres of his adjoin
2 CONC ing land, Charles laid out the town-- naming the streets himself, some after close relatives-- and expressed the wish to donate the four corner lots at the center of town for the use of the community. Today, his mansion at the end
2 CONC  of Blakely Street is in private hands. Recently, the graves of Charles and Mildred Washington were found on the grounds. One of the buildings on Charles Town's public square is the famous Jefferson County Courthouse, site of two 
2 CONC notorious treason trials, John Brown in 1859 and William Blizzard of Blair Mountain fame in 1921. 
2 CONT
2 CONT George Washington mentions in his diary staying at Happy Retreat at least two times. Also Gen. Daniel  Morgan, hero of the Battle of Cowpens in the Revolution, met here with Gen. Washington during one of his visits.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles WASHINGTON was named an heir in the will of George WASHINGTON dated on 9 July 1799 in Mt. Vernon. One of the provisions of the will was, "To my brother, Charles Washington, I give and bequeath the gold headed cane left to 
2 CONC me by Doctr [Ben] Franklin in his will. I add nothing to it because of the ample provision I made for his issue.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles WASHINGTON left a will dated 25 July 1799 in Charlestown, West Virginia. To wife Mildred certain slaves, and after her death to Samuel Washington. The will was proven 23 Sep 1799.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles WASHINGTON died on 16 September 1799 in Charles Town, Jefferson County, Virginia (now West Virginia), at age 61.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Charles WASHINGTON and Mildred THORNTON:
2 CONT * George Augustine WASHINGTON b. 1758, d. c 1793
2 CONT *  Frances Ann WASHINGTON+ b. 4 Jun 1763, d. Feb 1815
2 CONT * Samuel WASHINGTON b. c 1765
2 CONT * Mildred WASHINGTON b. c 1777
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT * [S158] Augustine Washington family Bible.
2 CONT * [S504] United Daughters of the American Revolution, DAR Lineage Book, Miss Mary Randolph Ball, No. 44, page 36-38.
2 CONT * [S633] Charles Town Walking Tour.
2 CONT * [S700] "The Papers of George Washington", George Washington's Will.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT DAR Ancestor #: A121948 (see attached document) 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''Charles Washington''' (May 2, 1738 – September 16, 1799) was the youngest brother of United States President George Washington. He was a son of Augustine Washington and his second wife, Mary Ball Washington.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography''' <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles was born at Hunting Creek in Stafford County, Virginia (now Fairfax County). He married Mildred Thornton, daughter of Colonel Francis Thornton and Frances Gregory in 1757.[1] They had four children and resided in Frederick
2 CONC sburg, Virginia. He arrived in present Jefferson County, West Virginia, between April and October 1780 and founded Charles Town. There he erected a house, Happy Retreat, the same year. In 1786, on 80 acres (323,750 m²) of his adjo
2 CONC ining land, Charles laid out the streets of Charles Town, naming many of them after his brothers and one after his wife, Mildred. He donated the four corner lots at the intersection of George and Washington Streets for public buil
2 CONC dings of the town and county, provided the town become the seat of the county upon its separation from Berkeley County. Jefferson County was formed in 1801 as Charles anticipated. The county court house stands on one of these lots
2 CONC .[2] Charles died sometime between July and September, 1799, only a short while before the death of his brother, George. The grave sites of Charles and Mildred are near Evitts Run and have recently been located and surrounded by a
2 CONC  stone wall.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Washington Wikipedia]
2 CONT
