2 FORM png
2 TITL Captain, Augustine Washington Sr. 
1 NOTE !BIRTH - THE WASHINGTONS AND THEIR HOMES by John W. Wayland p. 317
2 CONT Charles was born May 2, 1738, son of Augustine I and Mary Ball Washington in Westmoreland Co.,VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT VIRGINIA CO. RECORDS, VOL I, SPOTSYLVANIA CO. 1721-1800 Edited by William Armstrong Crozier, p. 73
2 CONT Guardians Bonds - Will Book B
2 CONT L2,000 Fielding Lewis, guard. to Chas. Washington, orph. of Augustine Washington, with John Thornton, security Oct. 4, 1757.
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR - Ibid
2 CONT He married Mildred Thornton, daughter of Col. Francis Thornton, of Spotsylvania Co. VA., prob. about 1759.
2 CONT
2 CONT An Act to establish a town on the lands of Charles Washington in the county of Berkeley
2 CONT Be it enacted by the General Assembly that 80 acres of land, the property of Charles Washington lying in the county of Berkeley, be laid out in such manner as he may judge best, into lots of half an acre each with convenient stree
2 CONC ts, which shall be, and is hereby established a town by the name of Charlestown.  That John Augustine Washington, Robert Rutherford, William Darke, James Crane, Cato Moore, Benjamin Rankin, Magnus Tage, Thornton Washington, Willia
2 CONC m Little, Alexander White and Richard Ransone, gentlemen, are hereby appointed trustees of the said town. - Ibid, p. 162
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Town is a worthy and growing monument to Charles Washington and his near relatives.  Around it they owned thousands of acres of fertile land, on which they built a dozen or more splendid homes; and within the borders of Ch
2 CONC arles Town, especially in the Episcopal churchyard, more Washingtons, it is believed, are buried than in any other town or city of the United States. - Ibid, p. 164
2 CONT
2 CONT !DEATH - Ibid, p. 167
2 CONT By 1799 he evidently saw the end approaching.  On July 25, 1799, he made his will and it was proved before the court of Berkeley Co. on Sept. 23, the same year.
2 CONT
2 CONT The home of Charles Washington and his family was located a short distance due south of the town and was known as Happy Retreat.  By subsequent owners it was named Mordington.  The handsome brick house now standing there is perhap
2 CONC s a hundred years old but is not the one in which the Washingtons lived.  The graves of Col. Washington and his wife are believed to be on the farm, near the dwelling place, but are not marked and cannot at this time be certainly 
2 CONC located. - Ibid p. 168
2 CONT
2 CONT VIRGINIA CO. RECORDS, VOL I, SPOTSYLVANIA CO. 1721-1800 Edited by William Armstrong Crozier, p. 353
2 CONT April 20, 1780 - Charles Washington of Spts. Co., Gent., and Mildred, his wife, to Thomas Strachan of sd. County, Gent.  L8000 currency.  759 acres in Spts. Co., purchased of John Smith and Mary, his wife, as by deed, Octr. 29, 17
2 CONC 59.  Fras. Thornton, Jno. Ches, Rob., Bevy. Chew, Joseph Brock, Jno. Chew, jr. June 15, 1780.
