2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna (Porter) Gaylord immigrated to New England as a child during the Puritan Great Migration (1621-1640).
2 CONT Flag of Essex (historic flag)
2 CONT Anna (Porter) Gaylord was born in Essex, England.
2 CONT Ann/Anna Porter, baptized 22 September 1621 in Felsted, Essex,[1] was the oldest of twelve children of John Porter and Ann White.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann arrived in New England with her parents and eight siblings about 1638,[2] possibly aboard the ship "Susan and Ellen", on 17 July 1638. The family settled at Windsor, Connecticut by 1639/40, when her father was appointed consta
2 CONC ble.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann married 24 February 1641[/2?], at Windsor, Hartford, Connecticut, William Gaylord.[2][4][5] They had the following children born at Windsor, Connecticut:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann, born 24 April 1645,[6] died 27 September 1690, married Isaac Phelps[2]
2 CONT Hannah, born 30 January 1646,[7] died 3 August 1678, married John Crandall[2]
2 CONT John, born 27 January 1648,[8] bap. 4 February 1648/9, died 27 April 1699, married Mary Clarke[2]
2 CONT William, born 25 February 1650[/1],[9] died 1680, married Ruth Crowe[2]
2 CONT Hezekiah, born 11 February 1652,[10] bap. 14 February 1652, died 12 September 1677 unmarried[2]
2 CONT Ann died in Windsor, Connecticut on 21 July 1653.[2][11]
2 CONT
2 CONT Research Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT The Anna Porter (under age 20 and unmarried) named in the 20 April 1648 will of their father, John Porter, is NOT the Ann of this profile, but another daughter of that name (probably sister Hannah). John Porter's will does not nam
2 CONC e his eldest two daughters (the Ann of this profile and Sarah). In 1650, a statement acknowledging this omission was added by the executors of John's will, giving John's two eldest daughters 30 pounds each.[2]
2 CONT
