1 NOTE {geni:about_me} “In the late summer of 1705 five men outlawed in Maryland on charges of high treason seized the West River sloop Little Hannah and were ‘suspected to be going on a Pyratical design’ The villains included one''' Tho
2 CONC mas Sparrow'''; a Philadelphia mariner named John Stapes; John Taylor, described as a tall, thin brown man; a flaxen-haired youth of middle stature and clean complexion called Sterritt. The leader of the band was a notorious despe
2 CONC rado named Richard Clark from the South River region of Anne Arundel County.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT Maryland archival records to follow indicate that after his escape from Maryland in 1705, Richard Clarke was said to have made his way to the Neuse River area in North Carolina.[1] Donald G. Shomatte in Pirates on the Chesapeake, 
2 CONC states that “In the late summer of 1705 five men outlawed in Maryland on charges of high treason seized the West River sloop ‘Little Hannah’ and were ‘suspected to be going on a Pyratical design’ The villains included one Thomas S
2 CONC parrow; a Philadelphia mariner named John Stapes; John Taylor, described as a tall, thin brown man; a flaxen-haired youth of middle stature and clean complexion called Sterritt. The leader of the band was a notorious desperado nam
2 CONC ed Richard Clark from the South River region of Anne Arundel County.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT
