2 CONT
2 CONT ===Col. William Underwood 
2 CONT * Birth:	Jan 2 1599/00 / abt 1606 - St Bride Fleet St, London, England
2 CONT * Death:	Jan 1643/44 / 1637 St Olave, Bermondsey, Surrey, England
2 CONT * Parents:
2 CONT *Wife:	Margaret 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Underwood, of Isle of Wight and Lancaster counties, was a member of the House of Burgesses in 1632.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Underwood-172
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Edmond Underwood and Alice (Papworth) Underwood (is this proven?)
2 CONT
2 CONT William married c. 1625 at London to Margaret Mason (1610 in St. Giles, Cripplegate, London, MIddlesex, England).[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT # Anne/Ann Underwood b c.1620 in Kent, England
2 CONT # William Underwood b c.1623 in England
2 CONT # Mary Underwood b c.1626
2 CONT # Elizabeth Underwood b 1632 in Old Rappahannock Co., VA
2 CONT # Sarah Underwood b 1632 in VA
2 CONT # Margaret Underwood b c. 1633
2 CONT
2 CONT Children not included in source:
2 CONT
2 CONT *Thomas Underwood
2 CONT *George Underwood
2 CONT
2 CONT Property
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1658, William patented 883 acres in Westmoreland County; later inherited by his granddaughter, Margaret Williamson who married William Ball (Westmoreland County Virginia, edited by Walter Brisco Norris, pg 227).[1] "Ingleside" 
2 CONC was built on this land in 1834.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT William died before 1645 at England.[1] Alternate death information is 1637 at London, county Middlesex, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 18 Nov 1630, Captain John Upton and Margaret his wife conveyed unto William Underwood of Isle of Wight 400 acres of land. The will of John Upton, proved 16 Dec 1652, bequeathed land at Rappahannock to William, Elizabeth, Sarah,
2 CONC  and Margaret Underwood.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Research notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/175712442/william-underwood Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Oct 15 2020, 16:24:02 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT William UNDERWOOD ( I ) seems to be the same William UNDERWOOD who was the 2nd surviving son of Edmund UNDERWOOD, Grocer of London, and ROSE (FAIRCLOUGH), his wife.  This William may have been born as early as 1606, and probably i
2 CONC n County Kent, or somewhere outside of London, due to yet another plague infestation.  If he is the same child, then he was not christened until he was 6 years old at St. Stephen Walbrook in London in 1612, or possibly earlier in 
2 CONC a different church that researchers have yet to confirm.
2 CONT
2 CONT This William UNDERWOOD seems to have died between the years 1637 and 1644, and a tentative approximate date is given as circa 1642.  His birth and death are both current mysteries of sorts.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Biography a work in progress.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Underwood-172#Marriage_.26_Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Unconfirmed dates and citations. No verification that the christening date and marriage to Margaret Mason apply to this William Underwood.
2 CONT
2 CONT No verification that Edmund Underwood and Alice Papworth are his actual parents. There are several William Underwoods born about 1606+/- in various parts of England who could be our William. More research is needed to determine hi
2 CONC s correct parents.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was born in 1606 at Kent, England.[7] He was christened on 19 April 1612 at St. Stephen, county Middlesex, England.[7] He was a son of Edmund Underwood[7][8] and Alice Papworth.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage to Margaret Mason about 1625.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT A HISTORY OF TWO VIRGINIA FAMILIES TRANSPLANTED FROM COUNTY KENT, ENGLAND, by Dr. and Mrs. William Carter Stubbs:  Capt. William Underwood, Sr., mar. Margaret ___, name unknown. "Untangling the Underwood - A Combs &c. RootsWeb Res
2 CONC earch Report", researched by Sherrill Underwood Williams:  Another possiblity, of course, is that the William Underwood of St. giles had been married earlier and Col. William Underwood (and also his sister, Ann?) were issue of the
2 CONC  earlier marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT === Reference: ===
2 CONT HYPERLINK: http://www.combs-families.org/combs/assoc/u-1.htm 2 Nov 1659. Old Rappahannock. "I, Will UNDERWOOD of Rappahannock County with the consent of Mary my wife," for the sum of 120 pounds Sterling money paid according to the
2 CONC  tenor of a contract dated 30th May 1659 conveyed unto Capt. Richard LOES and Rice JONES a plantation lying on the north side of Rappahannock River.....uppermost half of the lowermost half whereof I formerly sold unto ........eads
2 CONC  (mutilated) dec'd this land adjoining the land of Coll FAUNTLEROY known by the name of Mangorick and on the eastermost side of Bushwood Creek and runneth for length into the woods NE and by E and by the River NW and by W, to hold
2 CONC  the said plantation thereto with all houses, outhouses Edifices thereon erected (the Courthouse excepted), containing 650 acres or thereabouts, and all gardens, orchards and fences -unto the said Capt. Richard LOES and Rice JONES
2 CONC . Teste: John CATLETT and Tho. GOODRICH (Vol. 1656-64, p. 70, Rappahannock Records).
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT === Life Sketch ===
2 CONT Find a Grave States: 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Underwood
2 CONT
2 CONT BIRTH	1610
2 CONT
2 CONT Kent, England, United Kingdom
2 CONT
2 CONT DEATH 1640 (aged 29–30)
2 CONT
2 CONT Lancaster County, Virginia, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT BURIAL Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT MEMORIAL ID 125182295· 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Underwood was born around 1600, probably in England. He married a woman named Margaret about 1630, in England. Her family name might have been Masan. They came to Virginia, where William died in Lancaster County about 1640
2 CONC . Margaret then married Capt. John Upton of Isle of Wright. Upton died in 1651, and she later married Capt. Thomas Lucas. Margaret died sometime after 1656.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family Members
2 CONT
2 CONT Children Elizabeth Underwood Butler 1632–1673
2 CONT
2 CONT From England, Sir William Bekkeley granted William Underwood gentleman, 1400 acres near Rapahanook River in 1650
2 CONT
2 CONT Capt. William Underwood of Isle of Wight and Lancastershire. Member of the House of Burgess in 1632. This generation and his children is conjecture on my part from traditions passed down in this family combined with patterns of ea
2 CONC rly Underwoods living in America. It's entirely possible that the family that includes William as a child is simply a different branch of Underwoods that does not include our Thomas directly. However, as they have his first wife m
2 CONC arrying him in 1621 and they do not have any listed children before 1629, I'm suggesting that Thomas and George also belong in this family. They came over on the same ship at the same time... and both disappeared from Old Rappahon
2 CONC nak Co, VA records after their arrival. There is a tradition passed down in the family that Thomas was one of 3 brothers that migrated together, with all 3 dispersing in different directions after they arrived. Margaret Mason, his
2 CONC  wife, married John Upton after William's death. John Upton's will only names their children William, Elizabeth, Sarah and Margaret Underwood which accounts for all the children still living in the area except for Mary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Possible christening date and place: 18 Apr 1606, 19 Apr 1612, St. Stephens, Walbrook, London, London, England (IGI (International Genealogical Index))  Both list the father as Edmund or Edmond Underwood.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alternate death date: 17 May 1673 (IGI (International Genealogical Index))
2 CONT
2 CONT St. Giles Cripplegate London Wall, EC2 The first church here dates to 1090. John Milton is buried at St. Giles.
2 CONT
2 CONT (website:/www.britainexpress.com/London/london-churches.htm)
2 CONT
