2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT # http://dgmweb.net/FGS/S/SlaughterFrancis-ElizabethUnderwood.html Cites 
2 CONT # 1.  Robert K. Headley, Jr..  c2003.  Married Well and Often: Marriages of the Northern Neck of Virginia, 1649-1800: Marriages and Marriage references for the Counties of Lancaster, Northumberland, Old Rappahannock, Richmond, and
2 CONC  Westmoreland.  Genealogical Publ. Co., Baltimore, MD (online at Ancestry.com):
2 CONT # 2.  George Norbury MacKenzie.  1907-12.  Colonial Families of the United States of America.  7 vols.  Vol. I by Grafton Press, New York; Vols. II-VII by Seaforth Press, Baltimore, MD (republ. 1966/95 by Genealogical Publ. Co., B
2 CONC altimore, MD; Broderbund CD-189; online at Ancestry.com).
2 CONT #3.  Anon.  1902.  "Underwood."  William and Mary College Quarterly Historical Magazine.  1st series, vol. X, pp. 275-276 (reprinted in 1982 as pp. 247-248 in Vol. 5 of Genealogies of Virginia Families by Genealogical Publ. Co., B
2 CONC altimore, MD; online at Ancestry.com): v. 5  p. 247	UNDERWOOD.—Capt. John Upton was commander-in-chief of Isle of Wight county, was a member of he House of Burgesses, and muster-master-general.  His will, dated 16 Jan., 1651-'52, 
2 CONC was proved in Isle of Wight county December 16, 1652.  (Vol. VII., p. 220.)  His son, John Upton, died without issue.  His widow, Margaret Upton, had issue by a former husband (1) William, (2), Elizabeth, (3) Sarah, (4) Margaret a
2 CONC nd (5) Ann Underwood.  She removed to Lancaster county, and married, thirdly, in 1657, Thomas Lucas, Sr.  The will of Thomas Lucas was dated Dec. 14, 1669, and was proved March 14, 1673.  (Virginia Magazine, III., p. 61.)  Of the 
2 CONC daughters, Elizabeth married, first, Capt. Francis Slaughter, of Rappahannock county, who was a justice, and died about 1657.  Issue, Francis Slaughter, Jr.  She married, secondly, Col. John Catlett, of Rappahannock county.  She m
2 CONC arried, thirdly, in 1672, Rev. Amory Butler.  Her will is on record in Tappahannock, dated May 17, 1673, proved June 16, 1673.  (Virginia Magazine, III., 64.)...
2 CONT # 4.  Mary Denham Ackerly & Lula Eastman Jeter Parker.  c1930.  Our Kin:  the Genealogies of Some of the Early Families Who Made Histor in the Founding and Development of Bedford County, Virginia.  J.P. Bell, Lynchburg, VA (online
2 CONC  at Ancestry.com):  this is the most fact-filled genealogy -- need to extract.
