2 TITL Margaret, wife of John Druett, widow of Richard Williamson 
1 NOTE Name: Richard D. WILLIAMSON Sex: M Birth: 1593 in London, England Death: 1659 in Isle of Wight, VA Note: Residence: Cobham Hall on the Pagen River 2 
2 CONT  Father: Thomas WILLIAMSON  b: 1563 in London, England  Mother: Ann (Unknown) WILLIAMSON  b: in London, England  Marriage 1 Margaret SHERWOOD b: 1593 in London, England 
2 CONT Children 
2 CONT Dr. James WILLIAMSON  b: in Rappahannock Co., Va 
2 CONT Richard WILLIAMSON 
2 CONT James WILLIAMSON  
2 CONT Thomas WILLIAMSON 
2 CONT John WILLIAMSON  
2 CONT Henry WILLIAMSON
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: 
2 CONT "There was a Richard Williamson who can be placed in Virginia as early as 18 June 1627. I do not know if he is the same as ours. . .In the book: 'Genealogies of Virginia Families', Vol IV, Genealogical Publishing Co., Baltimore, c
2 CONC r 1981, pp 95-96, 'Will of Robert Hoxton, Middlesex, 18, June 1627; proved 24 Feb. 1631/2. . .Witnesses: Thomas Abbot; Robert Williamson. . .Records indicate he was transported to the colony from the British Isles by Ambrose Benne
2 CONC tt, who used his name for a headright, (he got 50 acres of land for each person he transported.)." (Benjamin Williamson, Williamson Genforum database, #5116, 20 Jan 2005)  "Richard Williamson Sr. is said to be the ancestor of all 
2 CONC Virginia Williamsons. The home was Cobham Hall. This Williamson was in Virginia as early as 1640 in Isle of Wight County, Virginia and witnessed a deed as Richard Williamson Sr. He died by 1659 and left a widow, margaret Sherwood 
2 CONC Williamson, who married John Jarrett." (Richard Williamson, Williamson Genforum database, #507, 19 Jan 1999)  "Richard Williamson on June 23, 1640, witnessed a deed of Captain John Upton to Christopher and Ann Benn of some land on
2 CONC  Pagan Creek in Isle of Wight County, Virginia." (The Williamson Family of Isle of Wight County, VA, Hugh Buckner Johnston, Vol III, p 158, On-Line Genealogy Library database)  "June 23, 1640 witnessed a deed of Cpt. John Upton to
2 CONC  Christopher and Anne Benn, Isle of Wight, Va. . .April 18, 1646, Richard Williamson and John Newman witnessed a deed made to John Snellock by Dr. Farrah Flinton. . .March 14, 1646, Richard Williamson witnessed a deed of William Y
2 CONC arrett to Thomas Brandwood. . .October 12, 1653, Gregory Perrott patented land in Isle of Wight County for Williamson as a headright and for a servant's headright assigned him by said Williamson. (May have been Richard, Jr.). . .I
2 CONC n October 1653 Gregory Pettot received 150 acres of land for transporting 3 persons, Richard Williamson twice, and William Thomas. Since a person could only be transported once, it is most likely these men were Richard, Jr., and R
2 CONC ichard III." (Benjamin Williamson, Williamson Genforum database, #5116, 20 Jan 2005)  "Richard Williamson born about 1593 in London, in Isle of Wight Co., VA by 1640, married Margaret Brown (daughter of George and Elizabeth Brown)
2 CONC , died by Aug. 9, 1659. His widow married 2nd John Druett (John Jarrett?). . .His home in Surry County was called Cobham Hall. He was probably the uncle of Sir Joseph Williamson of Kent Co., England whose home was also called Cobh
2 CONC am Hall." (Warren Williamson, Williamson Genforum databse, #1833, 3 May 2000)  "Several soruces indicate that Richard died in 1659, however his widow remarried to John Druett in 1654. Deed records indicate that on 25, September 16
2 CONC 76, Arthur Davis of Surry Co., VA was granted 461 acres in Surry and Isle of Wight Counties. This was land that was originally granted to Thomas Taberra in 1652. On 1, May 1655 they surrendered the grant to Richard Williamson Jr. 
2 CONC orphan son of Richard Williamson dec'd. Richard Jr. later surrendered it to Arthur Davis. . .(Cavaliers and Pioneers, Vol 1, pp 78, 202, 254, 108, Nell Nugent)." (Benjamin Williamson, Williamson Genforum database, #5116, 20 Jan 20
2 CONC 05)
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: 
2 CONT Richard is said to be the ancestor of all the Virginia Williamsons.  He first came to Virginia in 1638 but returned to England shortly thereafter. In 1641, Richard returned to Virginia for good. His passage was paid for by Ambrose
2 CONC  Bennet.  Settling in Isle of Wight County, Richard soon began acquiring property. He named the family home "Cobham Hall." That land was thought to have been purchased in 1641.  There is confusion over Richard Williamson's wife. H
2 CONC e was married twice --- to Margaret Brown and Margaret Sherwood.   Sources: Debra Persson, Ron Meyers, Linda Mott, Dave Williamson.
1 NOTE Residence: Cobham Hall on the Pagen River
