1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Elizabeth (Betsy) LEE (Twin), daughter of Colonel Richard LEE Sr. and Anne CONSTABLE, was born in 1653 in Paradise Plantataion, Gloucester County, Virginia and died about 1709 in Wicomico Parish, Northumberland Cou
2 CONC nty, Virginia about age 56. 
2 CONT
2 CONT General Notes: Through the lawsuit of Edmund Lister (Anne Lees' second husband), the Stratford Estate in Essex England was sold and the profits divided among daughters Elizabeth (Betsey) and Ann. 
2 CONT
2 CONT An indenture file in Essex County England (Records Office) has a photo copy in the Archives of the Virginia Historical Society. This indenture, made in England, transfers property from Richard Lee of the county of Northumberland, 
2 CONC Virginia, son of Richard Lee late of London, Esq, deceased, to Leonard Howson and Elizabeth Lee, Thomas Youell and Ann Lee, both daughters of Richard Lee of Virginia, and Francis Lee of England. Their signatures (Rich'd Lee, Leo' 
2 CONC Howson, Elizabeth Howson, Tho Youelle and Ann Youelle, Francis Lee) are still legible according to Floyd Wickenkamp. Leonard Howson and Elizabeth, Tho's Youell & Ann identified as from Northumberland in Virginia. Dated 16 Sep 1678
2 CONC . 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth married Leonard HOWSON Sr. about 1670 in Dividing Creeks, Northumberland County, Virginia. Leonard was born about 1641 in York, England and died on Apr 17, 1689 in Wicomico Parish, Northumberland County, Virginia about a
2 CONC ge 48. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth next married John TURBERVILLE. John was born about 1650 and died about 1728 about age 78. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward Arlene D added this on 11 Jun 2009 MargaretFernMastororiginally submitted this to Vogel/Dunigan Family Tree on 25 Apr 2008
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth (Betsy; 1654–1714), who married first Leonard Howson (b. 1648) of Northumberland County and after his death John Turberville (1650–1728) of Lancaster County. Betsy Lee and Leonard Howson married in 1670 and had at least 
2 CONC five children, including: Elizabeth; William (1672–1702); Leonard, Jr. (b. 1673); Hannah (1677–1713), who married Francis Kenner in 1706; and John (1679–1714). One of Hannah Kenner’s sons married the daughter of George Eskridge, w
2 CONC ho was Mary Ball’s guardian and whom George Washington was named after. Betsy Lee and her second husband had one child, George Turberville (1694–1742), who lived at Hickory Hill in Westmoreland County. 
2 CONT
2 CONT BIRTH OF Elizabeth Lee 1653 - 1700 , Virginia 
2 CONT
2 CONT Five IGI's (Family Search.org Records) state that Elizabeth Lee was born at Paradise Plantation in Gloucester County, Virginia.  Her father, Colonel Richard Lee, had patented land at Tindal's Point across the York River from Yorkt
2 CONC own.  Then, on the north side of the York River, he patented a thousand acres of land at the head of Poropotank Creek about twenty miles north of the York River on August 10, 1642. This was then York County, but Gloucester County 
2 CONC was formed out of this area in 1651 (various histories).  Richard Lee acquired 500 acres on May 24, 1651 on land adjacent to "War Captain's Neck." That same year he acquired an additional 500 acres on Poropotank Creek.  He sold so
2 CONC me land, but kept 850 acres to which he gave the name "Paradise" when he resettled there in 1653 (Lee, Edmund 50-60; The Lees of Virginia--Digital Archives).   
2 CONT
2 CONT His land grants were given in payment for the large number of immigrants he imported from England.  Colonel Richard Lee, while Secretary of State for Virginia, received eight hundred acres of Land in Northumberland County on May, 
2 CONC 1651.  This was on the south side of the Dividing Creek stream.  This grant was for the transportation of sixteen immigrants (Lee, Edmunds 55-56).
2 CONT
2 CONT About 1656 (Wikipedia article), Colonel Richard Lee moved his family to Virginia's Northern Neck on the Chickacoan peninsula between the Potomac and Rappahannock Rivers.  Northumberland County was formed in 1645 from this Chickaco
2 CONC an peninsula.  Today, it contains four counties.  The family settled on a tract of land purchased from the Wicomico Indians.  This was called the Dividing Creek planation, near the present day town of Kilmarnock.  This was known i
2 CONC n later generations as Cobb's Hall, just south of Wicomico Church but north and east of the Lancaster County line on the "Chickacoan" peninsula.  Cobb's Hall is in present day Northumberland County.  St. Stephen's Church is at Hea
2 CONC thsville, the government center for Northumberland County. Colonel Richard Lee, this American Lee patriarch, aquired 13,000 acres in Northumberland County before his 1664 death at the Dividing Creek Planatation.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Without checking the IGI information, sources agree that Elizabeth Lee was born on the Paradise Plantation area in Gloucester County.  
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCES
2 CONT
2 CONT Lee, Edmund Jennings, M.D.  The Descendants of Colonel Richard Lee.  Clearfield Company, Inc. by Genealogical Publishing Co., Inc., Baltimore Maryland, 1999.  Ancestry.com book on-line. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "The Lees of America:  An American Legacy."  Lee Family Digital Archive.  Washington and Lee University, 8/1/2010.  Http:// leearchive.wlu.edu/?pae+legacy/index.html.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Lee I - Wikipedia.  http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Lee_I
