1 TITL Capt.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.ewellfamily.org/genealogy/getperson.php?personID=I2743&tree=ewellcombo
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain Charles Ewell, Jr., and his wife Sarah, were among the first to settle in Prince William County, in the period between 1739 and 1747. On 1,000 acres, shortly after the Revolution, they built their two and half story brink 
2 CONC Colonial Williamsburg style home 'Bel Air', on the highest land in the area, several miles north of the town of Dumfries. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The brick portion was added above an older stone foundation built in the seventeen century, by parties unknown. The upper portion of the original home had been destroyed by fire. This home was similiar in style to the orginal Moun
2 CONC t Vernon. It is possible that these bricks came James Ewell's brick plant. It is said George Washington stopped at 'Bel Air' for a night on his wedding trip. The 'Bel Air' home measures 38 by 51 feet and is 2 1/2 stories high. 
2 CONT
2 CONT There are 14 rooms, and the walls vary in thickness from one to three feet. Its 20 foot wide chimney is believed to be the largest in Colonial America. The main floor consists of a large hall with walls on one side made of removab
2 CONC le panels which lead to a large drawing room with a fireplace. A small sitting room is next to the "Washingto Guest Chamber" where the future President and his bride spent a night of their honeymoon. On this floor there is also a 
2 CONC library, and an office. The basement consists of a large family room, a large eat-in kitchen, and a pantry which is suspected of being a dungeon at one time.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT https://archive.org/stream/EwellTheEwellsInAmericaAndSomeAlliedFamilies16351990/Ewell+-+The+Ewells+in+America+and+Some+Allied+Families%2C+1635-1990_djvu.txt
2 CONT
2 CONT To make the tie-in between the families even closer, CharleB and Solomon's mother, Mary Ann Bertrand Ewell, married for her third marriage, the father of her daughters-in-law, Major James Ball. This made her the step-mother to her
2 CONC  own sons. Major Ball became stepfather to his own daughters and the stepfather to his sons-in law. Both the Ball and Ewell families were big supporters of St. Mary’s White Chapel. Major Ball and Mary Ann were buried at White Chap
2 CONC el, Lancaster Co. , Virginia.
