1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/Charles-Ewell/6000000226303849005
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT - - -
2 CONT
2 CONT '''FROM: Colonial Families of the USA, 1607-1775
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Charles Ewell and  Mrs-Charles Ewell. He married 1415. Mary Ann Bertrand 1705 in Lancaster, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT See Documents''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://ewellfamily.org/genealogy/familygroup.php?familyID=F906&tree=ewellcombo
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.genealogy.com/ftm/s/h/a/John-L-Sharp/GENE5-0112.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Ewell, born 1660 in London, Middlesex, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Died April 1722 in Lancaster, Virginia, Colonial America
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.ewellfamily.org/genealogy/getperson.php?personID=I2743&tree=ewellcombo
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.avocadoridge.com/carney/getperson.php?personID=I13884&tree=tree1
2 CONT
2 CONT https://freepages.rootsweb.com/~bell/genealogy/a10.htm#i432226
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT https://archive.org/stream/EwellTheEwellsInAmericaAndSomeAlliedFamilies16351990/Ewell+-+The+Ewells+in+America+and+Some+Allied+Families%2C+1635-1990_djvu.txt
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT CHARLES EWELL (3,15,31,42,146), s/o Charles Ewell (£1), b.ca. 1660-65 in  England, d. 1722. Charles Ewell is referred in the referenced book as as a  bricklayer. He m.ca. 1705/10 Harv Ann Bertrand , the di/o Rev. John Bertrand  an
2 CONC d Charlotte de Joli. John Bertrand with his brother Paul, had escaped from  France after the Revocation of the Edict of Nantes. John Bertrand had settled  in Rappahanock County, VA. John had two other daughters, Marianna and  Char
2 CONC lotte as well as a son, William. Paul Bertrand had settled in Calvert  County, MD. on a farm called by him 'Cox Hayes'. Rev. John Bertrand had  married Charlotte De Joli, the daughter of the Comte de Joli who had escaped  from Fra
2 CONC nce with the Bertrands. Charles Ewell, Sr. in his published will  mentioned his two daughters and three sons. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT James/Charles Ewell
2 CONT
2 CONT The information on this family was primarily obtained from the documentation found in the Ghotes website in The Miles Files
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Miles Files show no posterity for Charles Ewell (born circa 1675), son of James Ewell''', but in this website we have the descendants of Charles Ewell and Mary Ann Bertrand. Discrepancies have arisen between the conclusions
2 CONC  of various researchers attempting to discern the lineage of Charles Ewell from the fragmented records. For that reason, we have not linked Charles as the son of James Ewell and Ann. However, there have been some researchers who w
2 CONC ere confident that the Charles, son of James, in Accomack County, Virginia, is in fact the same Charles that moved to Lancaster County, Virginia. Also, that he was the father of the Charles in Prince William, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT This site with citations clearly shows that the Charles who was son of James Ewelll was not the same as this Charles Ewell:  http://espl-genealogy.org/MilesFiles/site/p242.htm - their son is listed as Charles born circa 1675 at Ac
2 CONC comack Co, VA. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT https://freepages.rootsweb.com/~bell/genealogy/a10.htm#i432226
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. Charles "The Immigrant" Ewell (Ule, Yole, Yowell) 	Born:	1660	In:	England, London 	Died:	Apr 11 1722 (at age ‎~62‏)	In:	  Lancaster Co., Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT Marianne (Mary Anne) Ewell (Ule, Yole, Yowell, Yule) (born Bertrand) His wife	
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Ann (Maryann) White (born Ewell) His daughter	
2 CONT
2 CONT Capt. Charles Ewell II His son
2 CONT
2 CONT Maj. Bertrand Ewell His son	
2 CONT
2 CONT Solomon Ewell (Ule, Yole, Yowell) His son	
2 CONT
2 CONT James "The Immigrant" Ewell (Ule, Yole, Yowell) His father [NO]
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne Ewell (Ule, Yole, Yowell)  (born Smith) His mother	[NO]
2 CONT
2 CONT brickmaker [NO]
2 CONT
2 CONT Three Brothers Migration Traditions We have come across several stories about three Ewell brothers who came to America. Three Brothers Tradition, as mentioned in "Ewells in America and Some Allied Families, 1635-1990" (pg. ix)
2 CONT
2 CONT "One of the main sources of information about the Virginia Ewells has been the book on "Virginia Genealogies" by the Rev. Richard Hayden. Hayden obtained much of his information about the Eolder family from the grandson of Charles
2 CONC  Ewell of Lancaster Co., Va. Charles was given this information by his father, Bertrand, about the arrival of the brothers from England."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Three sons of Charles Ewell of London, England; Charles being the oldest, along with his brothers, James and Solomon, arrived in Virginia about 1690,''' with the contract from the King to build the new capital for Virginia at W
2 CONC illiamsburg. At the conclusion of the contract, Charles settled in Lancaster, while James and Solomon retired to the eastern shore because of Indian troubles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Additional Source: Hayden, "Virginia Genealogies" p.333. Data entered by Paul A. Jensen 7 Dec 2003: "Dr. Jesse Ewell of Dumblane, P.W. Co., Va., aged 87 in 1889, wrote in 1869 that Charles Ewell came to Va. from England, 1690, wit
2 CONC h two brothers, names unknown, having obtained from the Proprietory Government in London a contract to build the first capitol at Williamsburg, to which place the seat of Colonial government was, about that time, to be removed fro
2 CONC m Jamestown. Of the two brothers of Charles, one settled south of the James R. in Princess Ann or Norfolk Co., and the other in Accomac or Northumberland Co. Charles located in Lancaster Co. Col. Benjamin S. Ewell, LL. D., thinks 
2 CONC this Charles was son of John Ewell."
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a noted architect. Major Charles Ewell, son of Bertrand, was told that his grandfather was an English gentleman by the name of Charles Ewell; that 3 of his sons came to VA during the reign of Charles II. On account of the I
2 CONC ndians, Solomon & James retired to the Eastern Shore, and Charles, the eldest son, remained in VA and married Miss Bertrand. She was the daughter of Rev. Bertrand, a french gentleman
2 CONT
2 CONT Ewell, Charles (b. 1660, d. 1722)
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Charles Ewell was probably Scottish. He came to Williamsburg in 1690 with two brothers with the contract to build the Capitol in Williamsburg, VA. during the reign of Charles, the Second.
2 CONT
2 CONT His name is sometimes spelled "Yuille." Charles and his brothers, Solomon and James, lived in Princess Anne (Norfolk County), Northumberland and Lancaster Counties, Virginia. Several of the houses that he designed are still in Vir
2 CONC ginia today. He died before MaryAnn and Isaac were married.
2 CONT
2 CONT SOUR: PAGE 334 QUAY 3 QUAY 2 QUAY 2
2 CONT
2 CONT BIRTH: PAGE page 334 QUAY 2
2 CONT
2 CONT DEATH: PAGE page 334 QUAY 2
2 CONT
2 CONT OCCU: Builder of the Williamsburg Capitol DATE 1690 PLAC Williamsburg, Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT PAGE page 334 QUAY 3
2 CONT
2 CONT PROP: Transfer of Property to Heirs DATE 1779 PLAC Williamsburg, Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT The following involves the transfer of property involving the heirs of Charles of Ewell.
2 CONT
2 CONT A mortgage by way of indenture to the said James Lawson in greater security of these debts, by which he sold etc. to the said James Lawson, his heirs and assigns, all his lands, tenements, hereditaments, negroes, and other slaves,
2 CONC  horses and utensils, for Lawson's own use, under provision of cancellation by payment of the sums due, with interest. The indenture further narrates that (e) James Lawson being in the position of not being able to satisfy his cre
2 CONC ditors, they met on 18 August, 1779, in Glasgow, and it was agreed that Lawson should convey his whole estate to John Alsont, Jr. and Alexr. Grindlay in trust for the creditors, and this indenture is part of that conveyance.
2 CONT
2 CONT This document therefore transfers to these trustees all the lands in Prince William and Fairfax Counties, Virginia, purchased by John Semple from John Ballandine, Bertrand Ewell, heirs of Charles Ewell, Robert Carter, George Mason
2 CONC , John Grigg, heirs of Valentine Payton, and others, belonging to or convenient to the Forges of the said John Semple at River Occaquhan in Prince William County, with 26 negroes employed at the forges, 10 negro women and other sl
2 CONC aves employed there.
2 CONT
2 CONT It also conveys to them the lands in Frederick County purchased from Gersham Keys, Joseph McCames alias Mackermock, and Jacob Friend, on which the Keep-tryst Furnace and ore-banks were situated, and other lands pertaining to the f
2 CONC urnace, with 13 negro men, 2 negro boys, 1 mulatto man, 1 negro girl and other slaves, and everything belonging to the Forges and Furnaces, to be administered by the trustees under the terms of disposition of Lawson's Scottish est
2 CONC ate as set out. On the left of the document are the stamps for duty paid. @S27@PAGE Survey Report No. E.43 Page 2 #06798 QUAY 2 1000 acres DATE 1693 PLAC Lancaster County, Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT He settled in St. Mary's Parish, Lancaster county, Va., bought land near Corotoman Creek of Matthew Meres, and 1000 acres or more of Tolliver's Mount form Thomas Chitwood.
2 CONT
2 CONT PAGE page 334 QUAY 2
2 CONT
2 CONT WILL: DATE 13 JAN 1721/22 PLAC Lancaster County, Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT The will of Charles Ewell of Lancaster County gives to son "Charles all that tract of land near the mouth of Corittoman, which I purchased of Thos. Chitwood." " To my son Bertrand half of this tract I now live on, with half the or
2 CONC chard not including any part of the homeing during wife's life, and after her decease the whole" to Bertrand; also 250 acres of the Tolliver Mount tract.
2 CONT
2 CONT "To my son Solomon 350 acres of the Tolliver Mount tract, he to his his first choice of the whole tract. " To my daughter Mary Anne negro girl Sarah. To daughter Sharlott negro girl Betty. The other negro bricklayer to assist his 
2 CONC three children, Solomon, Mary Anne and Sharlott, in building a brick house or chimney as they see fit.
2 CONT
2 CONT To my loving wife negroes, Jenny and Letty and the remainder of the plantation whereon I now dwell not before given. All the rest, real and personal estate, to be equally divided between his wife and children.
2 CONT
2 CONT My wife to enjoy the whole estate during her widowhood. Nominates "my loving wife executrix and my loving friends, Mr. William Bertrand and Mr. Bryan Pully, ex'rs. Witness, James Ball, Daniel McDonald and Susannah Bertrand. Codici
2 CONC l orders that his 3 sons may demand their estate at the age of 18 and his two daughters at 16.
2 CONT
2 CONT PAGE page 334 QUAY 2
2 CONT
2 CONT MARRIAGE: DATE BET 1705 AND 1710 PAGE page 334 QUAY 2
2 CONT
