2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT https://archive.org/stream/EwellTheEwellsInAmericaAndSomeAlliedFamilies16351990/Ewell+-+The+Ewells+in+America+and+Some+Allied+Families%2C+1635-1990_djvu.txt
2 CONT
2 CONT CHARLES EWELL (3,15,31,42,146), s/o Charles Ewell (£1), b.ca. 1660-65 in England, d. 1722. Charles Ewell is referred in the referenced book as as a bricklayer. He m.ca. 1705/10 Mary Ann Bertrand , the di/o Rev. John Bertrand and C
2 CONC harlotte de Joli. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Bertrand with his brother Paul, had escaped from France after the Revocation of the Edict of Nantes. John Bertrand had settled in Rappahanock County, VA. John had two other daughters, Marianna and Charlotte as well as a son, 
2 CONC William. Paul Bertrand had settled in Calvert County, MD. on a farm called by him 'Cox Hayes'. Rev. John Bertrand had married Charlotte De Joli, the daughter of the Comte de Joli who had escaped from France with the Bertrands. Cha
2 CONC rles Ewell, Sr. in his published will mentioned his two daughters and three sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT - - - 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''FROM WIKI:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Bertrand-208
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT John Bertrand was a Huguenot emigrant (1540-1790).
2 CONT flag of the Jamestowne Society
2 CONT John Bertrand is a Qualifying Ancestor of the Jamestowne Society
2 CONT U.S. Southern Colonies Project logo
2 CONT John Bertrand was a Virginia colonist.
2 CONT Rev. Jean (John) Bertrand [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT John Bertrand, possible son of Jean Bertrand and Marguerite Robert, was born about 1660 in France. (Parents uncertain, need confirmation.)
2 CONT
2 CONT John, his future wife Charlotte Dejolie and John's brother Paul Bertrand fled France as Huguenots during the persecution of Lewis XIV following his revocation of the Edict of Nantes in 1685. They went first to London, England, whe
2 CONC re John and Charlotte were married and thence to the British Colonies in America. John and Charlotte settled first about 1692[2] in Old Rappahannock County, Virginia (formed from Lancaster County in 1656). John's brother Paul Bert
2 CONC rand settled in Calvert County, Maryland, west of Patuxant River. Paul died soon after his marriage leaving a widow and one son Paul who returned to England.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT John Bertrand, fled from France as a Huguenot under Louis XIV; ord. in Eng. sett. in King George and Lancaster counties, Va.; sett. Sittingbourne Par. (King George) Va., 1701; Ep.; d. Sittingbourne Parish, 1701.[4]
2 CONT John Bertrand is listed as a qualifying ancestor by The Huguenot Society of America. [5]
2 CONT John Bertrand married Charlotte Dejolie on 29 September 1686 in Saint Paul Covent Garden, Westminster, London, England.[3] [6] Known children include:
2 CONT
2 CONT William Bertrand, abt.1687-abt.1760
2 CONT Maryann Bertrand, 1690-1750
2 CONT Charlotte Bertrand[7]
2 CONT John was Rector at Saint Mary's Whitechapel Episcopal Church, Sittingbourne Parish, from 1692 until his death in 1701.[8] Sittingbourne Parish was part of Richmond County, Virginia, from 1692 to 1732.
2 CONT
2 CONT Belle Isle State Park
2 CONT Belle Isle State Park is located in Lancaster County, Virginia, on the Rappahannock River.[9] The property was acquired in 1692 by John Bertrand, a Huguenot. The center or main portion of the Belle Isle residence was built by his 
2 CONC son William Bertrand prior to 1759, when William's will was written mentioning the residence. It was inherited by his grandson Thomas Bertrand Griffin. It was added on to in later years and suffered some removal of interior paneli
2 CONC ng, etc. It was restored after 1940. It is today "an excellent example of a small but formal Tidewater plantation complex. The residence itself is a classic essay in Virginia Georgian architecture.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Note on Places John Bertrand Lived
2 CONT Following the records for where John Bertrand lives can be confusing. The Colonial Clergy of Virginia, North Carolina, and South Carolina states John settled in Sittingbourne Parish, King George County, Virginia, where he died in 
2 CONC 1701. King George did not exist until it was formed from Richmond County in 1720-1721, twenty years after John died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources also say John was in Old Rappahanock County and/or Lancaster County. Lancaster was formed from Northumberland in 1651 and Old Rappahanock County from Lancaster in 1656. Richmond was formed from Old Rappahanock County in 16
2 CONC 92 and Old Rappahanock ceased to exist.
2 CONT
2 CONT To clarify a bit, I think it is safe to say that John settled originally in Sittingbourne Parish, Old Rappahanock County, sometime between 1686 and 1692 when the County became Richmond County. He then lived in Sittingbourne Parish
2 CONC , Richmond County, from 1692 until his death in 1701.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
