2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Estate 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1780-1782 Record Book #11; Northumberland Co Va, (Lewis & Booker): Page 200
2 CONT *Boyd, Margaret, cf St. Stephens Parish W. W. 22 April 1782---W. P. 10 June 1782
2 CONT * Daughter Millie Hall-two beds and furniture, one a field bed and English tick, the other Virginia tick, one easy chair, one chest of drawers, one night table, one dressing table, one large blue and white china bowl, one set of t
2 CONC ea cups and saussers of china, one tea pot, one milk pot, all the trunks, and my wearing cloaths.
2 CONT * Grandaughter Mary Jackqulen Smith-two beds and furniture, the looking glass, chairs, tables, hand irons, tongs, shovel and poaker in the hall, and one mourning ring that was formerly Alexander Boyds.
2 CONT * All my religious books-to Molly Jackqulen Smith.
2 CONT * Grandson Augustine Jackqulen Smith-one large gilt looking glass in the chamber, the mourning ring that was formerly my father's, Thomas Pinckard, and the rest of my china after my daughter Millie Hall receives hers.
2 CONT * Four grandchildren Mary Jackqulen Smith, Augustine Jackqulen Smith, Millie Pinckard Hall, and Horatio Gates Hall-remainder of my estate, and all the slaves given to me by my father Thomas Pinckard by deed of gift, to wit:- Humph
2 CONC ry, David, Lettie, Jesse, Adam, Isbell, Solomon, Aaron, and Ezekiel, to be equally divided between
2 CONT * them. 
2 CONT * If grandaughter Mary Jackqulen Smith, should die before her brother Augustine Jackqulen Smith, or marriage, her part of my estate to go to her brother and his heirs, and if the said Augustine Jackqulen Smith, should die before h
2 CONC is sister Mary Jackqulen Smith, or marriage, his part of my estate to go to his sister and her heirs.
2 CONT * If they both should die before marriage, their parts of my estate to return to my two grandchildren Millie Pinckard Hall, and Horatio Gates Hall, to be equally divided between them. 
2 CONT * If grandaughter Millie Pinckard Hall, should die before her brother Horatio Gates Hall, or marriage, her part of my estate to go to her brother and his heirs, If Horatio Gates Hall should die before his sister or marriage, his p
2 CONC art of my estate to go to his sister and her heirs.
2 CONT * If grandaughter Millie Pinckard Hall, should die before marriage, her part of my estate not to go to Horatio Gates Hall, but to any of the other brothers and sisters Mr. Hall and his wife may think is most dutyfull to them.
2 CONT * To Nancy Haynie-one best cow and calf.
2 CONT * To Bridgar Haynie and Sarah his wife-each ten pounds specie.
2 CONT * Friends Thomas Gaskins Senr., James Gordon, Edwin Conway, and Bridgar Haynie, executors.
2 CONT * Witness: Bridgar Haynie, William Lansdell, and Enock Potts.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Northern Neck Warrants and Surveys Vol IV, Fairfax Co. Va {Peggy Joyner} 
2 CONT DR DAVID BOYD of Northumberland; 18 Nov. 1777 -10 Dec. 1777 as directed by George Minor; 20 a. of sunken ground on Poto & Muddy Crk ca. 2 miles from Alexandria; adj. his own, Matthews pat. of 1,567 a. CC - Author Buckhanon & Richa
2 CONC rd Truman. Surv. John Hough.
2 CONT ===
2 CONT 1780-1782 Record Book #11; Northumberland Co Va, (Lewis & Booker): Page 118
2 CONT *Boyd, David, of St. Stephens Parish W. W. 7 May 1781---W. P. 10 December 1781
2 CONT * I do not consider myself indebted to the estate of the late John Yerby, for the land and other things I purchased of him.
2 CONT * I confirm my late precious daughter Margaret Smith's will, in every part of it. That the negroes Joe, and Joshua, and black George(son of Sarah), pass thereby, the first two I long since sent to Middlesex, the other I gave her f
2 CONC or raising him and saving him from death by her own personal care. Also two mares, the gray one called Polly, and the bay called Phillis, which I let her have in the autumn of 1774, and they have remained in Middlesex at "Shooters
2 CONC  Hill," the gray 'till last spring, the bay 'till this, having brought them over for more care, the overseer not taking good care of them, and the increase to go with the mares.
2 CONT * Grandson Augustine Jacqulin Smith, son of my said daughter-all the lands in Fairfax County which I purchased of John and George Yerby, to him and his heirs lawfully begotten of his body, and for want of such to David McCullock, 
2 CONC son of John McCullock of Torhouse, and Mary his wife, my youngest sister of the shire of
2 CONT * Wigton in North Britain, and the lawfully begotten heirs of his body, and should he die before me, to James McCullock, son of John McCullock and Agnes his wife, late of the Isle of Man.
2 CONT * To grandson with the same remainder, a small tract of land in Fairfax County containing about twenty acres, which I lately patented, and joining the other tract.
2 CONT * To grandson-my land in Loudon County, to him and the heirs of his body.
2 CONT * Grandaughter Mary Jacqulin Smith-my dwelling plantation, and a small tract of land I purchased of the widow of John Nichols, the land Henry Self now lives on.
2 CONT * Two thirds of my slaves to my above grandson.
2 CONT * One third of my slaves to my above grandaughter.
2 CONT * Grandaughter-to have all my plate, because her mother gave her brother Augustine, her plate.
2 CONT * Grandaughter-my gold watch, chain, hook and. seal, in gold.
2 CONT * I promised my daughter a gold watch, and suitable furniture, which I now order to be bought at the expense of my estate, and which I give to my grandaughter, 
2 CONT * Grandson-my books, and my musket which I long since gave him.
2 CONT * Wife-use of my home plantation and where Henry Self now lives, and the negroes thereon except Eve, Jesse, and a yellow fellow George the Carpenter, as they are at present boiling salt with Peter Cox as by agreement, and as many 
2 CONC black cattle as she can maintain vizt: ten cows and calves, four yoke of oxen, ten barren young cattle, all the stock of hogs and sheep and horses, present years crop, provisions in the house udring her natural life or widowhood, 
2 CONC also use of land I rent of Robert Carter, Esq., of "Nominy Hall," except the part I rented to Frances Walker, use of my gold watch, hook chain and seal, during her widowhood, and a neat plain chariot with harness, be purchased for
2 CONC  the use of my wife and grandchildren.
2 CONT * My estate in."North Britain" to my sister Mary McCullock, and her eldest surviving son and his heirs, and to the eldest child, male or female, of my late sister Agness McCullock.
2 CONT * Grandaughter-to be gentelly educated.
2 CONT * Grandson-may be brought to the profession of a physician and liberally educated, and to be sent to Scotland for that purpose, to one of the colleges in Aberdeen for his academical studies, and then to Edinburgh to complete his m
2 CONC edical studies, and he to visit London, Ligden, and Paris, before he leaves Europe.
2 CONT * My wife having perused my will, and not being satisfied, I hereby void every gift to her.
2 CONT * Rest of my estate real and personal-to my two grandchildren and their heirs, and if either should die without heirs, to the survivor, and if both die before marriage or arriving to the age of twenty one, I give my whole estate t
2 CONC o my two sisters Agnes McCullock, and Mary McCullock, and their heirs.
2 CONT * My land in Loudon County containing fifteen hundred acres,(but the courses will give seventeen hundred and fifty acres), for tobacco rent 'till grandson comes of age.
2 CONT * The heirs of Colonel John West of Fairfax County, to be sued for the high land and Pocoson joining on Potomack River, Fairfax County. The said West took away twenty acres of high land, including a little pocoson, tho the jury fo
2 CONC und it against him.
2 CONT * Philip Alexander, and the papers I have, will give light into this dispute.
2 CONT * The ten acres Thomas Marsh promised me in presence of his Cousin Charles Marsh, adjoining the land I bought of Betty Nicholls- to my grandaughter.
2 CONT * Grandson to carry my bones, those of his mother, and his Uncle Alexander Boyd, to Scotland, and have them buried by my father and mother in the churchyard of the Town of Wigtown, and my executors and his guardian to pay the expe
2 CONC nce out of my estate.
2 CONT * Colonel John Smith of Frederick County, the Reverend M. Thomas Smith of Westmoreland County, guardian to my grandchildren, and with Richard Mitchell of Lancaster County, Captain David Ball and Onesiphorus Harvey of Northumberlan
2 CONC d County, John Sydnor, Captain Williamson Ball, William Miskell, and Major Charles McCarty of Richmond County, and William Taylor of Northumberland County, executors.
2 CONT * No witness.
2 CONT * Presented in Court by the widow Margaret Boyd, the executors refusing to act, administration granted to the widow
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT 1750-1751 Record Book #1; Northumberland Co Va, (Lewis & Booker): Page 282 
2 CONT *Ball, John W.W. 12 October 1750 W. P. 8 April 1751 Codicil 6 March 1750/1---
2 CONT * Daughter Sarah Ball-eight negroes Humphries, Davey, Jesse, George9 Isabell, Betty, Letty and Silles and five shillings sterling money and no more of my estate. 
2 CONT * To the boy that may be called my son as being born in Wedlock that is said to be born in June 1741 and to be christened Mark Anthony the son of my wife Margarett one shilling sterling and no more of my estate; the reason I have 
2 CONC given the boy Mark Anthony no more than one shilling of my estate is because his mother my wife Margarett has absented herself from me about ten years as much from August last past and its about six years since the said Mark Antho
2 CONC ny was born, and it is impossible he should be my son; I have not for near these eleven years last past had any carnall knowledge of any kind whatever with my wife Margarett which is said to be the mother of Mark Anthony.  
2 CONT * To the two children that my wife Margarett delivered of(as I an informed)in the month of December 1743 and said to be christened Mille and Landon-one shilling sterling money each and no more of my estate, the child that my wife 
2 CONC Margarett delivered of(as I an informed)in the month 
2 CONT * of September 1747 and said to be christened Anna-one shilling sterling money and no more. 
2 CONT * Whereas my father Capt. George Ball deceased, in his will gave me the use of six negroes viz: Sam, Fillis, Ben, Humphries, Tom and Rose and their increase to me during my natural life, and to prevent any suit that may arise on a
2 CONC ccount of my being possest of the above negroes before my fathers death, I give all the right I have if any of the said six negroes to my four brothers to be equally divided between them as my father left in his will. 
2 CONT * I give a large common Prayer Book to the use of Wiccomoco Church. 
2 CONT * After all debts, legacies are paid all my personal estate to be equally divided amongst all my brothers and sisters. 
2 CONT * Three brothers David, Richard and Joseph Ball, executors and guardian to my daughter Sarah Ball. 
2 CONT * Witness: Josias Gaskins, Josiah Mayes and James Waddey  
2 CONT * CODICIL: Daughter Sarah Ball to have 550 pounds tobacco to be laid out for her cloathing by her guardian. 
2 CONT * Witness: George Ball. 
2 CONT * In order of probate Margarett Ball widow of said John Ball came into court and dissented to said will. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins 
2 CONT Abstracts of Lancaster County, Virginia Wills 1653-1800; {Ida J. Lee}
2 CONT * Pinckard, Thos. 29 Aug. 1740. Rec. 10 Oct. 1740, w.b. 13, P. 183. "Wife : Elizabeth. Sons: Thos., all land I now live on; John. Daus: Elizabeth Brent and Margaret Ball. Exors: Sons and Cousin Wm. Stepto. Wits: Thos. Stepto and T
2 CONC hos. Bridgford. "That my daughter Margaret Ball may be maintained out of my estate as long as she behaves herself well and lives from her husband John Ball."
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
