1 NOTE {geni:about_me} After Ann Corbin's death in 1694, William Tayloe married another Anne, with whom he had two more daughters, 
2 CONT
2 CONT * one named Anna Catherine, who married Samuel Ball, 
2 CONT * and the second named Mary, who married John Lyell/Lyall/Lile. 
2 CONT
2 CONT source King, George Harrison Sanford. 1986. Marriages of Richmond County, Virginia, 1668-1853. Easley, S.C.: Southern Historical Press. p. 205 (Available from FamilySearch.org at a Family History
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Unknown-355621
2 CONT
2 CONT >Ann was tried for "bastardy and infanticide" in Sep 1714 after being seen in the vicinity of an unearthed infant's leg. The story is told in a book chapter entitled "A Parcell of Murdering Bitches". [https://books.google.com/book
2 CONC s?id=4LuYNtek7CYC&lpg=PA93&dq=anne%20tayloe%20burn&pg=PA87#v=onepage&q&f=false link]
2 CONT
2 CONT >The chapter notes mistakenly assumes that Anne was the widow of Joseph Tayloe, when he was actually William Tayloe. In Oct 1715, Joseph complained in Lancaster Co., VA court that his brother William TAYLOE had died, and William's
2 CONC  widow, Anne TAYLOE, had cohabited with James BURN and they were "vehemently disposed of fornication and incontinency." James Burn is the same one featured in the book chapter, who she later married.Surprisingly, she was named in 
2 CONC Joseph Tayloe's will, as Anne Burn, in 1716.
2 CONT
2 CONT >On 11 Jun 1729, Mary Tayloe was given permission by her mother, Anne Burn, to marry John Loyal [Lyill, Lyall, Lile] Another daughter is Anna Catherine who married Samuel Ball. She and Samuel Ball were administrators of James Burn
2 CONC /Bourne's estate in 1721.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT * http://www.genealogy.com/ftm/l/i/s/Karen-K-Lisle/WEBSITE-0001/UHP-0434.html
