2 CONC ving in 1706.  (1,3,8a) "Said to have been a Londoner." (5) Of London. (1,8a) Immigrated to VA in the last half of the 17th century and settled in that part of the old county of Rappahannock which is now Richmond Co. (2) Came to V
2 CONC A in 1650. [NOTE: If b. 1645, would have been only 5 years old. Came with someone?] (5) Migrated to VA in the time of Cromwell (about 1650?) and settled in the county of Lancaster. (7) Sailed from London sometime before 1682. (11)
2 CONC  Migrated to VA in the time of CROMWELL (1649-1659). (3,7) 1682: (3) Purchased from William FAUNTLEROY land on the north side of the Rappahannock River. This property was referred to at first as "Tayloe's Quarter," but later was g
2 CONC iven the name of "Mount Airy." (1,8a) Land patented by him included the present day Mount Airy, in Richmond Co., VA, still owned by TAYLOEs. (7) Purchased land on which the first Mt. Airy, "the old house," was built. (3) 1683: Bui
2 CONC lt a house on "Tayloe's Quarter" that has been tentatively dated 1683. (1,8a) 1687: Sheriff of Rappahannock Co. (1) ca. 1692: (1,8a) One of the 1st Justices of Richmond Co. (NOTE: Richmond Co. was created from Old Rappahannock Co.
2 CONC  in 1692.) (1a) 1693, 23 Nov: Col. William TAYLOE of Richmond Co., VA, nephew and heir of Col. William TAYLOE, late of King's Creek in York Co., deceased, deeds to Lewis BURWELL, 1200 acres between King's Creek and Queen's Creek o
2 CONC n York River, formerly the property of Col. William TAYLOE, the elder. (1,8a) 1700, Dec: A Burgess for Richmond Co. (1,8a) 1701, Aug: A Burgess for Richmond Co. (1,8a) 1702, May: A Burgess for Richmond Co. (9a) 1703, 19 May: Col. 
2 CONC William TAYLOE, Col. George TAYLOR, Mr. Samuel PEACHEY, Capt. John DEANE, and Capt. John TARPLEY were justices, Richmond Co., VA. (1,8a) 1704: As Colonel and Commander in Chief of the militia of Richmond Co., he subdued an attempt
2 CONC ed uprising of the Indians. (10) 1704/5, 6 Mar: William TAYLOE, Colonel and Commander-in-chief of Richmond Co., in behalf of himself and the Militia within said county, showeth several charges for services in August and September.
2 CONC  Payments to Captain Thomas BEALE, Captain John CROOKE, Captain William BARBER and Captain Henry PRERETON their four companies on duty 33 days. Captain John TARPLEY and Charles BARBER sent out two squadrons of 12 men each under qu
2 CONC artermasters. Also claim of William UNDERWOOD, Captain of a company of foot Oct 1704; Captain Alexansder DONOPHAN, Captain of troop of horse in the upper parts of Richmond County and Captain Nicholas SMITH's claim for the troops u
2 CONC nder his command. (9b) 1705: Col. William TAYLOE, Lt. Col. Samuel PEACHEY, Maj. William ROBINSON, Mr. Joshua DAVIS, Capt. Nicholas SMITH, Mr. Edward BARROW and Mr. Francis SLAUGHTER were justices, Richmond Co., VA. (1,8a) 1706, Ap
2 CONC r: A Burgess for Richmond Co. (9b) 1706: Col. William TAYLOE and Major William ROBINSON voted their burgesses expenses, each 9,980 pounds tobacco, Richmond Co., VA. (8a,9c) 1710, 7 Feb: (8a) Richmond Co., VA court ordered the appr
2 CONC aisement of his personal estate. Valued at 702.8.8 pounds. (9c) Richmond Co., VA court granted Mr. John TAYLOE administration of the estate of his father, Col. William TAYLOE, late of Richmond Co., deceased. (8b) 1715, Oct: Joseph
2 CONC  TAYLOE complained in Lancaster Co., VA court that his brother William TAYLOE had died, and William's widow, Anne TAYLOE, had cohabited with James BURN and they were "vehemently disposed of fornication and incontinency."
2 CONT
2 CONT William Tayloe married Anne Corbin. The births of two of their 3 children were recorded in the parish register: Elizabeth in July 1686 and John in Feb 1687/8. (King, 182)  William Tayloe was the sheriff of Rappahannock Co VA in 16
2 CONC 87; he was one of the first judges of Richmond Co VA; he served as a colonel and commander of the militia; he served as a Burgess for Richmond County in Dec 1700, Aug 1701, May 1702, June 1702, April 1706. (Casey, 66; VMH)   On No
2 CONC v 24 1693, William Taylor "of Richmond Co VA, Gent, nephew & heir at law to Col. William Taloe late of Kings Creek in York Co VA deceased. Aforesaid uncle, Col. Wm Taylor dyed seized of 1200 acres situated in York Co [on] Kings Cr
2 CONC eek, Queens Creek, Utamaria Point, according to a survey made Dec 9 1691. In a codicil of his will, said uncle gave to his wife Elizabeth who after intermarried with Nathaniel Bacon Esq. Upon death of said Elizabeth, wife of said 
2 CONC uncle, Major Lewis Burwell of Gloucester Co procured a warrant of escheat. For £200 paid by Maj. Lewis Burwell, William Tayloe releases all claims to said lands of said uncle Col. William Tayloe deceased. Signed William Tayloe." (
2 CONC Casey, 58, 59)  William's wife Anne died in 1694. (King, 205) He married another Anne and they had two daughters.  William Tayloe died about age 50 without a will in 1710. (Casey, 66) However, an inventory of his estate was presen
2 CONC ted at the Mar 7 1710/11 Richmond County Court. The record states that "the appraisers met at the house of the deceased in Richmond County on Feb 9 and 10, 1710/1 and together with Mr. John Tayloe, were sworn by Col. Samuel Peache
2 CONC y." The estate included 21 slaves; furniture £22; 1 large feather bed and furniture; a quilt rug, blankets, and sheets; 6 new cane chairs; 5 old leather chairs, 1 elbow cushion chair; 55 pictures and maps great and small, £8.12; 1
2 CONC  large glass £2; 1 close stool and pan; 1 pair andirons and tongues, £1.2.6; 1 suite of old callico curtains and vallins, £1; 128.25 lbs of ozenbriggs £4.16.2 1/4; 3 pair yarn hose, 3s; 21 yards of narrow diaper, £1; 45 books, £6;
2 CONC  other fabrics; 1 pair small steelyards; 4 spinning wheels; 13 cows, 9 yearlings, 12 heifers, 10 steer, £38; 1 bull, 15s; coopers tools, handsaw, broad ax, £1.10; 3 horses, £11; 2 mares, 2 horses, £9; totalling £701. The appraiser
2 CONC s were Moore Fantleroy, William Fantleroy, William Smith, Aust. Brokenbrough. (Casey, 141) The administrator of the estate was John Tayloe.  William's widow was left with two young daughters. In Oct 1715, William's brother Joseph 
2 CONC complained to the Lancaster County court that William's widow Anne Tayloe "had cohaibted with James Burne." (King, 206) By Aug 1716, Anne did marry Burne because Joseph mentioned Anne Burne in his will.  [how are the children know
2 CONC n?]  References VA Magazine of History, Vol 17, 369-375. Casey, Albert E. Southern Taylor Families 1607-1830, 1958. York Co VA Records Wills, Deeds Bk 1633-1694, p 218. Richmond Co VA Wills & Inventories 1709-1717, p 41-44. Encycl
2 CONC opedia of VA Biography, p336. King, George H. S. Marriage of Richmond County [VA] 1658-1853. p 205. King, George H. S. The Registers of North Farnham Parish 1663-1814 and Lunenburg Parish 1783-1800, Richmond County VA records Rese
2 CONC arched and written by Ann Blomquist. This narrative is copyrighted material and may not be posted or published except by the author.
2 CONT Note:
2 CONT William and Mary College Quarterly Historical Papers, Vol. 1, No. 2. (Oct., 1892), pp. 79-80.  http://xroads.virginia.edu/~HYPER/VAGuide/tour16.html  VIRGINIA THREADS FOR THE FUTURE HISTORIAN John Tailoe, in 1673, owing to financi
2 CONC al embarrassment, left England and purchased a plantation in Virginia. At 1.6 m. on US 360 is another private road; R. here 0.5 m. to MOUNT AIRY (Private), most noted of the several houses built by the Tayloes. The lane leads thro
2 CONC ugh park, meadow, and lawn to the house that looks across terraced lawns toward the river. Dwarf boxwood of giant proportions still takes its undulating course among the remains of a formal garden.  Built of brown stone, with ligh
2 CONC t stone trim, Mount Airy consists of a massive central block, curving passageways, and two large dependencies set forward. The broad hip roof of the main unit is pierced by four chimneys near the ridge. The walls, three feet thick
2 CONC , are broken by slightly projecting pavilions, the one in front on the first floor square-columned in antis and that at the rear arcaded. These pavilions, the framing of every opening, the wide string course, and the deeply emphas
2 CONC ized water table are all constructed of light Aquia stone.  A fire in 1844 swept away the fine interior woodwork but left the walls undisturbed. The wide central hall opens into a lateral hall that leads to front and back parlors 
2 CONC and into the dining room, to which food is still carried from the kitchen dependency, as in the days of myriad servants. Old furniture, rarest of which perhaps is a cylinder piano played by turning a handle, and valuable pieces of
2 CONC  glass, china, and silver are cherished ere. The walls are lined with a large collection of family portraits, including work of Gilbert Stuart, Thomas Sully, Thomas Hudson, and John Wollaston, and etchings by St. Memin.  The estat
2 CONC e, acquired by William Tayloe, who built the first house, descended first to his son, Colonel John Tayloe (1687-1747), and then to Colonel John Tayloe II (1721-79), who built the present house about 1758. The formal setting and ch
2 CONC aracter of this Georgian mansion and the monumental scale of the original garden suggest a European designer. On Colonel Tayloe's death in 1779, Mount Airy passed to his only son, Colonel John Tayloe III (1771-1824), builder of th
2 CONC e Octagon House in Washington.  John Tayloe II had a deer park and maintained a band of musicians among his servants. He was an importer and breeder of fine race horses. Philip Vickers Fithian, a visitor here in 1774, wrote, 'Fish
2 CONC , Feasts, and Fillies! . . . Loud disputes concerning the Excellence of each others Colts-concerning their Fathers, Mothers (for so they call the Dams), Brothers, Sisters, Uncles, Aunts, Nephews, Neices, and Cousins to the fourth 
2 CONC Degree! All the Evening Toddy constantly circulating. Supper came in, and at Supper I had a full, broad, satisfying view of Miss Sally Panton. I wanted to hear her converse, but, poor Girl, anything she attempted to say was drowne
2 CONC d in the more polite and useful jargon about Dogs and Horses . . . In the Dining-Room, beside many other fine Pieces, are twenty four of the most celebrated among the English RaceHorses, Drawn masterly, and set in elegant gilt Fra
2 CONC mes.'  On the grounds are the tombs of Francis Lightfoot Lee and Rebecca, his wife, who was second daughter of the builder of Mount Airy.
2 CONT
2 CONT  1. Hon. William1 Tayloe married Ann Corbin , daughter of Hon. Henry Corbin and Alice Eltonhead, in 1685. He died in 1710. He resided at London, England. He resided at Lancaster Co., VA. He resided at 'Mt. Airy', Richmond Co., VA.
2 CONC  He was a member of the House of Burgesses from 1700 to 1706 at Virginia. Ann Corbin was born on 9 Feb 1664. She died in 1694. Known children of Hon. William1 Tayloe and Ann Corbin were as follows:
2 CONT i. Elizabeth2 Tayloe was born on 26 Jul 1686.
2 CONT 2. ii. Hon. John Tayloe, born 15 Feb 1688; married Elizabeth Gwynn.
2 CONT iii. William Tayloe was born on 30 Jul 1694. He married Letetia Wormeley, daughter of Col. Ralph Wormeley and Elizabeth Armistead. He died in May 1770 at age 75.
