2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT All the Hackley's of early Virginia appear to be decended  from James, even though there were others that emigrated at  about the same time.  James was a landowner in Essex County as late as 1719, but  appears in King George Count
2 CONC y, Hanover Parish, in 1724. The  land he owned in King George was about 15 miles below  Fredericksburg on the Rappahannock River. Hackley's Ferry  operated from his land, with a route across the reiver to  Moss Neck plantation in 
2 CONC Caroline County.  On 30 Sep 1724, James was granted 740 acres in King George  County by Robert "King" Carter who was acting as an agent  for Lord Fairfax. This land grant lay in that part of King  George which is now a part of Fan
2 CONC quier County on the  western side of Summerduck Run close to the Rappahannock  River. For this land James paid 10 shillings sterling for  each 50 acres and each year therafter a quit rent of 1  shilling for each 50 acres, payable 
2 CONC on the Feast of St.  Michael the Archangel. On 18 Apr 1741, james obtained  another grant for 126 acres adjoining the first tract.  These tow tracts were located on what is now the right side  of the road leading from highway 17 b
2 CONC y way of Summerduck to  Remington, just beyond Summerduck Run in southeastern  Fauquier County. His 5 sons eventually inherited equal  parts of the plantation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of John Hackley King George County Will Book #1 pp. 123-5 In the name of God, I, John Hackley of King George County, being very sick and weak of body, but of perfect sence (sic) and memory Doe make this my Last Will and Testa
2 CONC ment in manner and form following; I give and bequeathnto my Loving wife Judith Hackley the use of my whole personal Estate, Negroes, etc., in this manner: that whereas I am indebted to several Persons, my will is that my whole Es
2 CONC tate be kept together for Eight years to come the better to enable my loving wife to discharge my several Debts and after the expiration of the said eight years that my Estate be equally divided amongst my loving wife and all my c
2 CONC hildren as they shall come of age. And as they come to age or Marry after the term aforesaid to receive their equal parts to them and their heirs forever. And when my first child receives his or her parts that then the remainder b
2 CONC e kept in my said wife's hands to raise and bring up the rest of my children and so to continye (sic) on untill my last chile comes of age or marrys to receive his or her part as above. I leave the use of whole Lands and Lotts to 
2 CONC my loving wife Judith Hackley during her natural life & that if my wife can't discharge my several debts without selling my Personal Estate or Negroes that then she shall have full power to sell and dispose of my land in King Geor
2 CONC ge County whereon I now live and that the money arising from the said sale after discharging my several debts shall be put out to interest or laid out in buying slaves as my said wife should think fit which said slaves I give to m
2 CONC y sons and their heirs forever to be Equally divided amongst them at the decease of my lovely wife Judith Hackley. I leave the use of my Lott and Lands in Culpeper to my loving wife during her natural life and after her decease th
2 CONC e aforesaid Lotts and Lands to be sold to any person my wife at her decease may appoint. And the money arising by the sale thereof to be Equally divided between my sons James Hackley, Samuel Hackley, John Hackley, and as my said w
2 CONC ife is now with child it is my further desire if it should prove to be a son that then and in that case the said child to have an equal part of the money arising from the sale of the said Lands and Lotts with the other sons before
2 CONC  mentioned together with an equal part of my Personal Estate, Negroes, etc., above mentioned and if a Girl that to have an equal part with my other daughters before mentioned with respective parts I give to them and their heirs fo
2 CONC rever. And lastly I leave to my loving wife Judith Hackley my whole and sole estate not to be appraised. In witness whereof I have hereunto set my hand and seale the 8th day of August , 1760. John Hackley (seale)  Signed, sealed, 
2 CONC and acknowledged in the presence of -- George MortonBenjamin Ficklin Robt. ArmisteadSamuel Reeds
