1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John LILLARD Sr. (Captain) died in 1802.
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse: Susannah BALL. John LILLARD Sr. (Captain) and Susannah BALL were married. Children were: Elizabeth LILLARD, David LILLARD, Anna LILLARD
2 CONT
2 CONT * Reference: [http://sneakers.pair.com/roots//b108.htm#P5089 Sneakers]
2 CONT
2 CONT Revolutionary Soldier
2 CONT
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=29068384 Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Oct 31 2017, 2:31:06 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT A Patriot of the American Revolution for VIRGINIA with the rank of CAPTAIN.  DAR Ancestor #: A070356 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://kentuckydar.org/cpt.-john-lillard.html
2 CONT
2 CONT The chapter takes its name from John Lillard, the son of Benjamin and Elizabeth Lightfoot Lillard. John was born on November 3, 1737, in Culpeper County, Virginia. During the American Revolution he formed, under the direction of G
2 CONC eorge Washington, his own Company known as the "Culpeper Minutemen," and he became its Captain. His wife, Susannah Ball Lillard, was the niece of Mary Ball Washington. She died at the birth of their thirteenth child, David, on Nov
2 CONC ember 18, 1782, while in route to the Kentucky District of Virginia. Captain John Lillard died on August 5, 1801, in Mercer County, Kentucky. He is buried on a farm near Harrodsburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Lillard-19 WikiTree]
2 CONT
2 CONT John was married twice, first to Susannah (surname frequently given as Ball, though no authoritative source has been found to substantiate this) and second to Anne Moore (widow of Robert Thomas and daughter of Francis Moore, both 
2 CONC of Orange, Virginia) about 1791 (sources still needed to confirm).
2 CONT
2 CONT * Reference: [http://www.seekingmyroots.com/members/files/G003932.pdf Seeking My Roots]
2 CONT
2 CONT Lillard, A Family of Colonial America.  Stout Lillard.  Page 191
2 CONT
2 CONT >Susanna (Ball) Lillard, wife of Captain John, died either in child- birth or shortly afterward, when their youngest child David Lillard was born on November 18, 1782. Whether this was enroute to Kentucky from Virginia or whether 
2 CONC it was shortly after their arrival at Harrodsburg is not known. However Captain John Lillard married a second time to Mrs. Anne (Moore) Thomas, widow of Capt. Robert Thomas, who had eleven children. Anne Moore Thomas was the daugh
2 CONC ter of Colonel Francis Moore, of Orange County, Virginia, who was a Colonial officer commis- sioned under King George of England. Anne Moore Thomas Lillard was a sister of Sarah Moore Spencer, wife of Joseph Spencer, also a Colone
2 CONC l of Orange County, Virginia. Capt. Robert Thomas and Anne Moore were married in Orange County, Va., on April 9, 1757. Another of her sisters, Mary Moore married William Price. Her brothers were Reuben, Francis and Bernard Moore .
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT === Verified Children of Benjamin Lillard, Sr.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following children are heavily documented in contemporary Virginia Piedmont records (land deeds, probate, and marriage bonds) and are firmly established as his true historical heirs. They have been preserved on his profile, wh
2 CONC ile unverified or highly anachronistic lines from the 1928 pedigree have been isolated:
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''Captain John Lillard''' (c. 1737 – 1801) — Revolutionary War officer who migrated to Mercer County, Kentucky, leaving a detailed will. His timeline completely disproves the 1723 marriage narrative.
2 CONT # '''Elizabeth Lillard''' (c. 1748 – ?) — Married John Bradley in Culpeper County; validated through local marriage and family land associations.
2 CONT # '''Captain James L. Lillard, Sr.''' (c. 1755 – c. 1838) — Served in the Culpeper militia; remained in the Culpeper/Madison County area and is heavily documented in local land transactions.
2 CONT # '''William Jackson Lillard''' (c. 1759 – 1800) — Documented in Culpeper County records; married Anne Flippin.
2 CONT # '''Sarah Lillard''' (c. 1760 – ?) — Married a Bradley (often cited as William Bradley); tracks with the younger cluster of children.
2 CONT # '''Benjamin Lillard, Jr.''' (c. 1762 – 1823) — Remained in Culpeper/Madison County, establishing a clear, verified line of descent.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
