1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://tao221.wordpress.com/i-your-next-graphical-user-interface-coould-be-a-bot-how-vo-r-can-make-your-words-perfect-welcome-to-the-grid-an-emulator-card-can-be-your-next-videogame-console-make-waves-on-ocean/
2 CONT
2 CONT This article shows one viewpoint as to ancestry of Katherine Dale who married Thomas Carter.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://newsgroups.derkeiler.com/Archive/Soc/soc.genealogy.medieval/2006-04/msg00162.html The opposing view, which believes Katherine was the daughter of Edward and Diana.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Info copied from [http://www.tonycoxhome.com/cox2005-p/p7.htm#i330 Anthony W. Cox Geneology].  See also ''[http://www.archive.org/stream/descendantsofcap00mill#page/n7/mode/2up The Decendents of CAPT Thomas Carter]''
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Reference: [http://person.ancestry.com/tree/56715500/person/30449555085 Ancestry Genealogy] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Mar 10 2016, 2:50:40 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/40282104/person/360089694035/facts Ancestry Genealogy] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Mar 13 2018, 3:46:38 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Skipwith is Katherine's mothers maiden name.  There is no source to prove it was a middle name other than FAG which is not a reliable source for information.
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT === Little Barford ===
2 CONT Little Barford From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Little Barford is a hamlet and civil parish in the Borough of Bedford in Bedfordshire, England about 7 miles (11 km) northeast of the county town of Bedford.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Geography Little Barford is 2 miles (3 km) south of St Neots, 17 miles (27 km) west of Cambridge and 48 miles (77 km) north of Central London. The East Coast Mainline railway runs north-south through the parish.
2 CONT
2 CONT Area The civil parish covers an area of about 481 hectares (1,189 acres). The parish's northern boundary is the A428 road, Cambridgeshire is to the east and Central Bedfordshire to the south. The River Great Ouse forms its western
2 CONC  boundary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Landscape The hamlet lies within the Bedfordshire and Cambridgeshire Claylands as designated by Natural England.[3] Bedford Borough Council classifies the local landscape as the Great Ouse Clay Valley around and west of the villag
2 CONC e, and the eastern part of the parish as within the Biggin Wood Clay Vale that extends northwards from Central Bedfordshire. The surrounding area is mostly arable farmland. The northernmost part of the parish between the railway a
2 CONC nd the Great Ouse is taken up by the power station and industrial units.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cottages A row of four Grade II listed 18th century rough cast, thatched cottages stand by the roadside. One is detached, the remainder are terraced.[4] At the northern end of the hamlet are four pairs of late 19th century semi-de
2 CONC tached estate cottages having yellow brickwork with red brick dressings.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Elevation The hamlet is 20 metres (66 ft) above sea level. The whole parish is mainly low lying and flat, although the land does rise to 60 metres (197 ft) in the southeast corner of the parish.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Geology and soil type The hamlet lies mainly on first and second terrace river gravel and glacial gravel. Boulder clay is to the east. Alluvium borders the Great Ouse. Underlying these superficial deposits and also in part at the 
2 CONC surface is Oxford clay and Kellaways beds.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Around the hamlet the soil has low fertility, is freely draining and slightly acid with a loamy texture. The eastern part of the parish has highly fertile, lime-rich loamy and clayey soils with impeded drainage. By the Great Ouse 
2 CONC are loamy and clayey floodplain soils with naturally high groundwater.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT The manor of Barford was held by the de Leyham family from about 1194, and then passed by inheritance into the de la Dale family in about 1316:[12] the de la Dales owned Barford for two centuries, after which it passed by inherita
2 CONC nce to the Fettiplace family, and subsequently to the Brownes.
2 CONT
2 CONT A deserted medieval settlement in fields near to St Deny’s Church is visible as earthworks on historic aerial photographs.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT The parish church is dedicated to Saint Denys. The church became redundant in 1972 and is now in the care of the Churches Conservation Trust.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notable residents Thomas de la Dale, Lord Chief Justice of Ireland, was born here in about 1317
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT === Life Sketch ===
2 CONT Birth: 	1652, England Death: 	May 10, 1703 Lancaster County Virginia, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT Katherine Dale married Capt Thomas Carter in the British Colony of Virginia after the death of his first wife They lived and raised their children on their Barford Plantation.. They attended the Church of England and names and dat
2 CONC es of their twelve children are listed in his Capt Thomas Carter Prayer Book now in the possession of the Historical Society of Richmond. Both Katherine and Thomas died and are buried on their Barford Plantation, Lancaster County,
2 CONC  Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: 1. Edward Carter I 1671-1743 2. Thomas Carter, Jr. 1672-1733 3. John Carter 1674-1750 4. Henry Skipworth Carter 1675-1743 5. Diane Carter 1678-1680 6. Nicholas Carter 1679-1680 7. William Carter 1679-1680 8. Elizabeth Ca
2 CONC rter 1680- 9. Daniel Carter 1682-1682 10. James Carter 1684- 11. Katherine Carter 1686- 12. Joseph Carter 1690-1764 - Blanche Keating Collie, descendant and family researcher 
2 CONT
2 CONT Family links:  Parents: Edward Dale (1624 - 1695) Diana Skipwith Dale (1621 - 1695)
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse: Thomas Carter (1630 - 1700)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: Edward Carter (1671 - 1743)* Thomas Carter (1672 - 1733)* John Carter (1674 - 1750)* Henry Skipwith Carter (1676 - 1743)* Diane Carter (1678 - 1680)* Joseph Carter (1690 - 1765)*
2 CONT
2 CONT Siblings: Katherine Skipwith Dale Carter (1652 - 1703) Mary Dale Jones (1653 - 1683)* Elizabeth Dale Rogers (1654 - 1728)*
2 CONT
2 CONT *Calculated relationship
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: Body lost or destroyed Specifically: Burial site unk
2 CONT
2 CONT Created by: Blanche Keating Collie Record added: Jun 03, 2014 Find A Grave Memorial# 130824685
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT === Children of Thomas Carter ===
2 CONT “I have heard said there was two brothers of them the other being a John Carter who settled south of the river in Essex but further I can’t say.  And I have heard said we are kin to old Robert Carter who is buried at old Christ Ch
2 CONC urch in this County bur nave never found out how.  He was very rich -some say the richest man in Virginia. Our old ancestor Thomas Carter was about 21 years old when he came to Lancaster and he was a man of substance and position 
2 CONC as a planter and tobacco trader.  He was married twice.  First to an English woman whose name I’ve never heard, they had 2 or 3 children who all died young.  She died and he married a Miss Dale of good connections and had seven so
2 CONC ns and two daughters named Thomas, Edward, James, John, Henry, Peter Joseph and the girls was Betty and Katy.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Katherine was the daughter of Major Edward Dale and Lady Diana Skipwith, who was the daughter of Sir Henry Skipwith, Baronet of Prestwoold, Leicester Co. England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Major Edward Dale was apparently wealthy.  He gave Captain Thomas Carter, Katherine's husband about 3200 acres in Lancaster County, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT From a note added to the image:
2 CONT
2 CONT PLEASE refer to "Diana Skipwith - Fact and Fantasy" by Martin Wood (Jan. 24, 2012.  There is absolutely no evidence to support "Lady" Diana Skipwith as the biological mother of Katherine Dale.  Diana Skipwith married Katherine's f
2 CONC ather Edward Dale in 1664, twelve years AFTER Katherine's birth in 1652.
