1 NOTE !BIRTH - THE WASHINGTONS AND THEIR HOMES by John W. Wayland, p. 87
2 CONT Lawrence Washington, son of Augustine Washington I and his first wife, Jane Butler was born about 1716, in Westmoreland Co.,VA
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR - Ibid
2 CONT Lawrence Washington, soon after his father's death in April 1743 married Ann, eldest daughter of William Fairfax of Belvoir, on July 12, 1743, and lived at Mount Vernon.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first house there, it is said, had burned in or about 1739.  A new one, the nucleus of the present mansion, was built when Lawrence Washington and Ann Fairfax were married, or shortly before, and in it they made their home.  L
2 CONC awrence named the place Mount Vernon in honor of Admiral Vernon, under whom he had served in the West Indies.  By 1747 or thereabouts George Washington, a lad of 15, was occasionally at Mount Vernon, where he became acquainted wit
2 CONC h Thomas Lord Fairfax, who soon employed him as an explorer and surveyor.  In 1751, when Lawrence went to Barbadoes in a vain search for health, George went with him.  Lawrence went from Barbadoes to Bermuda, but soon returned to 
2 CONC Virginia.  - Ibid
2 CONT
2 CONT !DEATH - Ibid
2 CONT Lawrence made his will on June 20 (O.S.), 1752, and died on August 6 (N.S.)  His will, which is recorded at Fairfax, VA. provided for the payment of his debts and the construction at Mount Vernon of a "proper vault" for the interm
2 CONC ent of himself and family.
2 CONT
2 CONT All his lands in Fairfax Co., with improvements thereon, after the decease of his wife, were to go to his brother, George; and during her life, George was to have an equal share of the lands given to his brothers Samuel, John and 
2 CONT Charles. - Ibid
2 CONT
2 CONT George Washington in 1754 bought our the right, title, and interest of his sister-in-law and thus became the full owner of Mount Vernon.  There he and his brother, John Augustine, kept "bachelor's hall" until 1756 when John marrie
2 CONC d Hannah Bushrod of Westmoreland and brought her to his brother's home.
2 CONT Ibid
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a large landowner; had stock in 5 iron works in VA. and MD.; was a member of the Ohio Co.; died July 26, 1752; will probated Sept. 26 following.
2 CONT Ibid
