1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Mildred Warner
2 CONT * Born 1671 Gloucester County, Virginia
2 CONT * Died	January 30, 1701 (aged 29–30) Whitehaven, England
2 CONT * Resting place	St Nicholas Church in Whitehaven
2 CONT *Nationality	subjects of England
2 CONT * Parent(s)	Augustine Warner Jr.  & Mildred Reade
2 CONT *Relatives	Thomas Warner (great-grandfather) 
2 CONT
2 CONT *Spouse(s)	Lawrence Washington (1st), George Gale (2nd) Children	
2 CONT
2 CONT # John Washington, III
2 CONT # Augustine Washington
2 CONT # Mildred Washington
2 CONT
2 CONT Married her step brother, not biologically kin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT Mildred Warner was the child of Mildred Reade and Augustine Warner, Jr..  The grandmother of George Washington the first President of the United States, was born in Virginia about 1671. She was also born into a very prestigious Vi
2 CONC rginian family. Her grandfather had been acting governor of Virginia and her father Speaker of its House of Burgesses. She married into another important family in 1686 with the marriage to Lawrence Washington with whom she had th
2 CONC ree children John, Augustine and Mildred. Upon his death Lawrence gave Mildred and his children shares of his estate with the profits from their lands to be administered by Mildred for their education and upbringing. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1700 Mildred Washington then married George Gale who was part of a family of Whitehaven merchants trading with Virginia. The Gales were Irish dissenters who had fled Tralee and after suffering in Newcastle had set up in Whiteha
2 CONC ven about 1665 under the more sympathetic wing of John Lowther. In fact John Gale appears to have lived on land originally occupied by the Wyberghs known as the Old Hall which probably became Christopher Lowther's first residence 
2 CONC in Whitehaven. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Mildred Warner is the grandmother of George Washington, the first president of the United States. Mildred married [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=21512 Lawrence Washington]. It was from this marriage in whic
2 CONC h Augustine Washington, the father of George Washington, was born. After the death of Lawrence Washington, she married  [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=43407925 George Gale] and is buried in  [http://www.fin
2 CONC dagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=21925 St. Nicholas Church Cem., Whitehaven, England]. Maintained by: Find A Grave Record added: Apr 17, 2001
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Mildred's will was made January 24th, 1700-1, and it was proved in the Archdeaconry Court of Richmond (Copeland Deanery) the following March on the 18th. There she was described as wife of George Gale of Whitehaven, Cumberland, "b
2 CONC eing doubtful of the recovery of my present sickness;" she states that "by an Indenture of Marriage made and executed by and between John Washington one of the executors of my late husband's will of the one part, and my present hu
2 CONC sband George Gale with my own consent and approbation thereof of the other part bearing date 16 May in the present year 1700* I am empowered to demise by will or other instrument the estate and legacys of my late husband to the us
2 CONC es and purposes therein mentioned "
2 CONT *N.B. May 16th preceded January 24th in 1700 because before 1752 new year's day was 25th March.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT PLEASE NOTE: This entry represents a cenotaph. Mildred Warner Washington is NOT BURIED HERE but there is a bronze plaque on the wall commemorating her at her Warner family's ancestral home cemetery. She is actually buried at St. N
2 CONC icholas Church Cemetery, Whitehaven, England,  Find A Grave Memorial# 21925 http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=21925
2 CONT
2 CONT INSCRIPTION: [Seal: National Daughters of the American Colonists]
2 CONT
2 CONT Mildred Warner Daughter of  Augustine Warner II  and  Mildred Reade  Married Lawrence Washington  Grandmother of General George Washington  Died at Whitehaven, England 1701  Her Body Rests in  St. Nicholas Churchyard  Whitehaven, 
2 CONC England
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000204664534828&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT This Tablet Placed in  Her Memory  By the Pocahontas Chapter  Virginia Society Daughters of The American Colonists  1960
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT * Updated from [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&amp;GRid=117265765 Find A Grave Memorial] via  [http://www.geni.com/profile-2668998 Lawrence Washington] by [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]:
2 CONC  ''Dec 12 2015, 10:16:50 UTC''
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/28728481/person/27334338573 Ancestry Genealogy] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Dec 18 2017, 0:41:22 UTC''
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/120228381/person/390197023965 Ancestry Genealogy] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Apr 25 2018, 19:31:56 UTC''
2 CONT *http://www.whitehavenandwesternlakeland.co.uk/people/mildredgale.htm
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mildred_Gale
2 CONT *  GEDCOM Note [g675.ftw]  "Geoge Washington's Family": A descendant of the medieval Lords Kyme.
2 CONT
2 CONT ————————
2 CONT
2 CONT Mildred Gale (1671-1701)
2 CONT
2 CONT aka Mildred Warner, Mildred Washington
2 CONT
2 CONT Mildred Warner, Grandmother of George Washington the first President of the United States, was born in Virginia about 1671. She was also born into a very prestigious Virginian family. Her grandfather had been acting governor of Vi
2 CONC rginia and her father Speaker of its House of Burgesses. She married into another important family in 1686 with the marriage to Lawrence Washington with whom she had three children John, Augustine and Mildred. Upon his death Lawre
2 CONC nce gave Mildred and his children shares of his estate with the profits from their lands to be administered by Mildred for their education and upbringing.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1700 Mildred Washington then married George Gale who was part of a family of Whitehaven merchants trading with Virginia. The Gales were Irish dissenters who had fled Tralee and after suffering in Newcastle had set up in Whiteha
2 CONC ven about 1665 under the more sympathetic wing of John Lowther. In fact John Gale appears to have lived on land originally occupied by the Wyberghs known as the Old Hall which probably became Christopher Lowther's first residence 
2 CONC in Whitehaven.
2 CONT
2 CONT {The exact location of his home is vague but in 1878 William Jackson, FSA. speaking to the Antiquarian Society described the Old Hall as occupying the triangular plot of land South of the Market hall and bounded by Poe Street and 
2 CONC Swing-pump lane (formerly the road to St. Bees). Its gardens were on the west of the road stretching up to the foot of the hill. On the plan of 1699, by Andrew Pellin, plot 17 occupies buildings in the Market place and more across
2 CONC  the road with gardens extending to the foot of the hill. It seems to me that John Gale had his house there on the site of what is now the multi-storey car park. The plots to the north west were owned by Elisha Gale and Ebenezer G
2 CONC ale.}
2 CONT
2 CONT It would seem that this is the house that Mildred Gale stayed in when she and her husband came to Whitehaven just before Christmas. She was pregnant but possibly already had fever because she made her will before her baby's baptis
2 CONC m on 25th Jan 1701. Her Negro servant was also baptised Jane on the 7th Jan 1701. Mildred was buried on the 30th January 1701, her maid, Jane, on the 20th February and the baby Mildred on the 26th May. It seems strange to me that 
2 CONC her baby was named Mildred as she already had a daughter by that name from her first marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mildred's will was made January 24th, I700-1, and it was proved in the Archdeaconry Court of Richmond (Copeland Deanery) the following March on the 18th. There she was described as wife of George Gale of Whitehaven, Cumberland, "b
2 CONC eing doubtful of the recovery of my present sickness;" she states that "by an Indenture of Marriage made and executed by and between John Washington one of the executors of my late husband's will of the one part, and my present hu
2 CONC sband George Gale with my own consent and approbation thereof of the other part bearing date 16 May in the present year 1700 I am empowered to demise by will or other instrument the estate and legacys of my late husband to the use
2 CONC s and purposes therein mentioned "
2 CONT
2 CONT It is clear she felt she had the right to leave the care of her children and their estates in the hands of her new husband. In a similar way to how Lawrence had left his estate she gave £1000 to her husband and divided the rest of
2 CONC  her estate between him and the children. N.B. May 16th preceded January 24th in 1700 because before 1752 new years day was 25th March.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her will also put him in charge of the care of the Washington children - John and Augustine he obtained place for at Appleby School. Augustine was later to send his own sons from his first marriage to the same school so he must ha
2 CONC ve approved of its educational abilities. When George returned to Virginia, Mildred's will was challenged by John Washington, Lawrence's cousin, who failed but won on appeal no doubt aided by the fact that his family were better c
2 CONC onnected than the foreigner, George. The children were handed over to John in 1704 to provide for their care.
2 CONT
2 CONT Augustine Washington firstly married Jane Butler with whom he had four children and then married Mary Ball who gave him the future president George Washington (1731-1799) and five others.
2 CONT
2 CONT With a new wife Betty Denwood, George Gale founded the town of Whitehaven, Maryland but died in 1712 leaving four sons Levyn, George, John and Matthias.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mildred was buried in the grounds of the Chapel of St.Nicholas in the centre of Whitehaven. The grave of Mildred Gale was probably destroyed by the re-arrangement of graves in a small churchyard that became overcrowded by several 
2 CONC epidemics in a rapidly increased population over a period of 160 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1886 J.C.C. Smith stated this:- "there is not any extant memorial to her in either the church or churchyard". Despite this the gravestone has been seen in living memory. Smith doesn't seem to be in any doubt and Muriel Cinnamon
2 CONC  recalls it was in such good condition it could have been a copy: she also mentions anecdotal evidence of the earliest gravestones being laid flat as paving in the 1780's. Further, verbal evidence indicates the stone was seen in t
2 CONC he 1930's then buried about 1935 to prevent theft and then seen again about 1970.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1971 a great fire destroyed St. Nicholas church except the tower. Since then, the graveyard has been re-modelled into gardens, that are a great asset to the town, but in so doing the gravestone disappeared. It probably lies ben
2 CONC eath one of the paths or beds. This seems like a tragedy but it could protect it from weathering of the friable sandstone and thus it might actually reappear at some later date still intact.
2 CONT
2 CONT To make amends there are two plaques to commemorate the grave: - one made of brass was unveiled by Lady Nancy Astor at a ceremony in 1955, the other made of polished stone is on the wall of the church. There is another plaque in t
2 CONC he family graveyard at Warner Hall, Gloucester, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is also interesting to note at the time of the Gales were other Washingtons resident in Whitehaven but it isn't known if there was a family connection.
2 CONT
2 CONT Return to Whitehaven People
2 CONT
2 CONT Much information comes from a small booklet recently published by Muriel Cinnamon available from Michael Moon's bookshop.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref: Daniel Hay - Whitehaven an Illustrated History. (1979)
2 CONT Royal Commission ... - Whitehaven 1660-1800 (1991)
2 CONT Muriel Cinnamon - The Grave and Gravestone of Mildred Gale... (2005)
2 CONT William Jackson - Whitehaven: Its Streets, Principal Houses... (1878)
2 CONT J.C.C Smith - New notes on the ancestry of George Washington ... (1886)
2 CONT Gilpin - Correspondance of Sir John Lowther (1693-1698)
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:
2 CONT http://www.whitehavenandwesternlakeland.co.uk/people/mildredgale.htm
