1 NOTE !BIRTH - THE WASHINGTONS AND THEIR HOMES by John W. Wayland, p. 51
2 CONT According to Dr. Lyon G. Tyler, he was born July 7, 1725 the son of Col. John Lewis of Warner Hall, Gloucester Co., and his wife Frances Fielding.  He removed to Fredericksburg early in life, was a merchant of high standing and we
2 CONC alth, a vestryman and a magistrate.  In 1758 he took oath as county lieutenant of Spotsylvania Co. and from 1760 to 1768 he was a member of the Virginia House of Burgesses.
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR #1 - Ibid
2 CONT His first wife was Catherine Washington, a daughter of John Washington and Catherine Whiting.  This John was a brother to Augustine Washington, George's father.  Accordingly both wives of Col. Lewis were near relatives of General 
2 CONC Washington, the first being his cousin, the second his sister.
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR #2 - Ibid
2 CONT Betty Washington on May 7, 1750, married Col. Fielding Lewis of Fredericksburg.  She was then nearly seventeen; he was widower of 25 or thereabouts.  
2 CONT
2 CONT !DEATH - VIRGINIA LINEAGES, LETTERS & MEMORIES by Alice Jean Nelson, p. 178
2 CONT Col. Fielding Lewis, known as the "Gunmaker of the Revolution" as he furnished powder and cannon for the Continental Army.
2 CONT
2 CONT VIRGINIA CO. RECORDS VOL I, SPOTSYLVANIA CO. 1721-1800 Edited by William Armstrong Crozier, p. 37
2 CONT Fielding Lewis, St. George's Parish, Spotsylvania Co., died Oct. 19, 1781.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT VIRGINIA COUNTY RECORDS VOL I, SPOTSYLVANIA COUNTY 1721-1800 Edited by William Armstrong Crozier, p. 37
2 CONT LEWIS, FIELDING, St. George's Parish, Spotsylvania Co. died Oct. 19, 1781, probated Jan. 17, 1782, Wit.  George Noble, Benj. Ledwick, John Butler, Gerard Alexander, Will. Booth, William Carpenter.  Exor. Wife and my sons John, Fie
2 CONC lding and George.  Legacy - Wife, Betty Lewis, during life use of all my lands in Spots. Co. except that part rented to my son John; son John, after death of his mother, all my lands in Spots. Co. and in Fredericksburg; son Fieldi
2 CONC ng, 1000 acres of my Frederick Co. lands, on which he lives; son George, remainder of Frederick Co. lands bought of Robert Carter Nicholas, except 1000 acres to my son Lawrence, son-in-law Charles Carter, Esqr; son Lawrence, 1000 
2 CONC acres of land in Frederick Co.; son Robert, one-half of 10,000 acres of land located for me in the County of KY. by Mr. Hancock Lee, and one half of 20,000 acres located or to have been located for me by Natnaniel Randolph in the 
2 CONC County of KY; son Howell, the remaining half of the above lands in KY; all my lands purchased at the Land Office except what is already disposed of, to my sons Robert and Howell equally; my share in the Dismal Swamp Co. my land bo
2 CONC ught of Marmaduke Naughflett in partnership in with Genl. Washington; my lands bought of Dr. Wright and Jones in Nansemond Co. in partnership with Genl. Washington and Dr. Thomas Walkerr and the 320 acres of land in Frederick Co. 
2 CONC bought of George Mercer's estate, also my share in the Chatham Rope Walk at Richmond to be sold by executors and the money arising to be paid to my 6 sons before mentioned, in equal portions.
2 CONT Codicil dated Dec. 10,  1781, witnessed by Francis Willis, Jr., Gerard Alexander, Fr. Keyes, George Ogelvie, John Butler and William Carpenter; to son Robert Lewis a tract of land purchased of Francis Willis, Jr., adjoining the tr
2 CONC act of 320 acres of land in Frederick Co. also the said tract of 320 acres to son Howell Lewis, and the whole tract of 10,000 acres in Kentucky to son John.  (p. 434)
2 CONT
2 CONT Fielding Lewis is 2nd cousin, 6 generations removed to Robert Bruce Carter.
2 CONT Their common ancestor is Augustine Warner, Jr.
