1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Mildred Thornton Washington
2 CONT BIRTH
2 CONT 1741
2 CONT DEATH
2 CONT 1762 (aged 20–21)
2 CONT Stafford County, Virginia, USA
2 CONT BURIAL
2 CONT Burial Details Unknown. Specifically: probably buried on husband's farm in the Chotank Creek region of northern Stafford County, Va.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/244487428/mildred-washington
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Mildred Thornton (about 1741–1763) – possibly died during or shortly after childbirth. Her cousin, also named Mildred Thornton, married Samuel's younger brother Charles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children 
2 CONT #Thornton Washington (1760–1787)
2 CONT #Tristram Washington (born 1763) birth date is wrong, Samuel's third wife Lucy died in 1762
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Mildred Thornton (c1741-c62) was the second wife of Col. Samuel Washington (1734-81). Their children were Thornton (c1758-87) and Tristram Washington (c1760-ante1768).
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonel Samuel Washington (1734-81), second son of Capt. Augustine (c1694-1743) and Mary Ball Washington (1708/9-89), was born at "Wakefield," Popes Creek, Westmoreland Co., Va. In 1769-70, he built "Harewood" of native limstone, 
2 CONC three miles west of Charles Town, [W.]Va. He was a justice in Stafford, Frederick and Berkeley Counties and sheriff of Berkeley County. At the time of his death, he owned over 3,800 acres of land. Of his wives, (1) Jane Champe (c1
2 CONC 735-c54), daughter of Col. John Champe, of King George County, and (3) Louisa Chapman (1743-63), daughter of Nathaniel and Constantia Pearson Chapman, of Fairfax County, did not leave any children. His (2) wife was a cousin, Mildr
2 CONC ed Thornton (c1741-c62), of Caroline County, Va. Colonel Washington's (4) wife was Anne Steptoe (1737-77), daughter of Col. James Steptoe, of "Hominy Hall," Westmoreland County, Va. Their six children were Ferdinand (1767-88), Fre
2 CONC derick Augustus (1768-69), Lucinda (1769-70), Capt. George Steptoe (1771-1809), Lawrence Augustine (1774-1824), and Harriot Washington (1776-1822). Colonel Washington married his (5) wife c1778, Susannah Perrin (1753-83), daughter
2 CONC  of John Perrin, of Gloucester County, Va. Their son was John Perrin Washington (c1779-83). "Harewood" is on the National Register of Historic Places and has remained in the Washington family throughout its history. Its parlor was
2 CONC  the site of the marriage of future president James Madison and Dolly Payne Todd. Today it is owned by attorney Samuel Walter Washington IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT See Albert Welles, The Pedigree and History of the Washington Family (New York, 1879), 164-165; John W. Wayland, The Washingtons and Their Homes (Genealogical Pub., 1944), 341; Stella Pickett Hardy, Colonial Families of the Southe
2 CONC rn States of America (Genealogical Publishing Co., 1958), 522-523; John B. Burke, Burke's Presidential Families of the United States (London, 1975), 51, 53-55; Dr. Justin M. Glenn, The Washingtons, A Family History, Volume One, Se
2 CONC ven Generations of the Presidential Branch (Savas Beatie, El Dorado Hills, Ca. 2014), I: 33-36, 81-86; James Houston Barr III, Lt. Colonel Nathaniel Pope, c1610-1660, of Virginia, Ancestor of Washington, Governors and Legislators,
2 CONC  History of His Descendants (Louisville, Ky. 2018), 23.
