1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Hannah Bushrod''', daughter of John Bushrod (d. 1760); &  Jane Corbin, his 1st wife,  was born about 1738 in Bushfield, Westmoreland Co., VA. She died in 1804 in Bushfield, Westmoreland Co., VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married  on 14 April 1756 to John Augustine Washington (Augustine, Lawrence, John, Lawrence  ) was born on 13 Jan 1736 in Stafford Co., VA. He died on 8 Jan 1787 in Bushfield, Westmoreland Co., VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT John and Hannah Washington had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Mary Washington was born about 1757 in Mount Vernon, VA. She died about 1762.
2 CONT # Jane (Jenny) Washington was born about 1759 in Bushfield, Westmoreland Co., VA and died 1791.  She married about 1779 to William Augustine Washington (1747-1810).  He was the son of Augustine "Austin" Washington and Anne Aylett.
2 CONT # Bushrod Washington was born on 5 Jun 1762 in Bushfield, Westmoreland Co., VA. He died on 26 Nov 1829 in Philadelphia, PA.  Bushrod married Ann Blackburn in Oct 1785. Ann died on 28 Nov 1829 in Philadelphia, PA.
2 CONT # Corbin Washington
2 CONT # William Augustine Washington was born about 1767 in Bushfield, Westmoreland Co., VA. He died in Feb 1784.
2 CONT # Mildred C. Washington was born about 1769 in Bushfield, Westmoreland Co., VA. She died about 1797. Mildred married Thomas Lee, son of Richard Henry Lee and Anne Aylett on 15 Oct 1788 in Westmoreland, VA. Thomas was born on 20 Oc
2 CONC t 1758 in Westmoreland, VA. He died on 7 Sep 1805.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT === Hannah Washington's Will of Bushfield
2 CONT
2 CONT 1799-1803 Westmoreland County, Virginia Deed & Will Book 20 [Mike Marshall]; Page 211.
2 CONT
2 CONT * I Hannah Washington of Bushfield in the Parish of Cople and County of Westmoreland being sound in mind though weak in body and health make this my last will and testament.
2 CONT * I am very conscious of my great inability in drawing up any instrument of writing yet as none except my dearest friends will be at all concerned about it. I trust that they will make every allowance for the defects which they ma
2 CONC y be with here. I shall try to explain my desire which I hope will be sufficient. The cruel custom in this county of hurrying a poor creature into a coffin as soon as the managers of the business (who are generally indeed people q
2 CONC uite indifferent about the deceased or the most ignorant) suppose them dead; the friends at that awful moment quit the room and have their dear friend to the discretion of these creature, who tired of setting up and confinement ha
2 CONC ve them hurried into the coffin, no physician in the world can possibly tell whether or not a person is dead until putrefaction takes place and many have most absurdly been hurried away before they were dead. As I ever had a most 
2 CONC horrid idea of such usage I most earnestly intreat my friends to act with me in the following manner, that when it is thought I am dead that I remain in my bed quite undisturbed in every respect, my face to be uncovered not even t
2 CONC he thinnest thing to be laid over it, also I do requests that not one thing shall be attempted about washing or dressing me, no laying out as it is called, I beg I therefore most earnestly pray that I may be allowed to remain in m
2 CONC y bed just as I did whilst living until putrefaction by every known sign justifies my being put into the coffin. It is my will to be laid by my ever dear husband.
2 CONT * It is my will and desire that after all my just and lawful debts are paid which indeed are but few, that my estate shall be disposed of in the following manner.
2 CONT * I give and bequeath to my dearest and most tenderly beloved son Bushrod Washington all my stock of horses cattle cheap hogs and horses except what may hereafter dispose of all the corn and the 8 hogsheads of tobacco now on the p
2 CONC lantation at least 3 are here, and 5 at Nominy warehouse, all the plantation utensils, card and turnbrel, the furniture of the large dining parlor, the china press and its contents excepted, the bedstead bed bolster and pillars wh
2 CONC ich stands in the green room, the green morun bed and window curtains belonging to, one single bed bedstead bolster and one pillar, one pair small sheets and one pillar case, hemstiched tops made for it, one pair small calico curt
2 CONC ains belonging to it, one pair small blankets and one small quilt, one English and two M&O coverlets, the M&O not fringed, one pair fine sheets with two seams in them, one pair pillar cases, one pair pillar cases with one seam, on
2 CONC e pair pillar cases shades two pair not quite so fine, one pair course and one pair cotton, and one pair rose blankets which was imported from Blane, one pair but single, one large new damask tablecloth, one large diaper to cover 
2 CONC two tables, three diaper for one table, one white bedstead, one white rushia tick whole of my very fine new feathers, one large and elegant punch bowl which is in the china press this bowl belonging to my father before my memory, 
2 CONC so that my beloved son will no doubt value it much, my two green settees, the English carpet bought by me two years ago in Alexandria, also the green bed carpet belonging to the green curtains, the following furniture in this hous
2 CONC e belonging to Mrs. Hannah Washington of Shelby, four small gilt oval looking glasses, two with mahogany frames, 14 mahogany chairs, four mahogany card tables, a large mahogany dining table, which she was so good as to lend me dur
2 CONC ing my life, one silver, a small roundtable and a desk and bookcase.
2 CONT * I give and bequeath to my dearest and greatly beloved granddaughter Ann Aylett Robinson the whole of my drawing room furniture, the chairs, sofa, screen, ornamental mantle piece, china, that handsome set of breakfast chairs impo
2 CONC rted from London, the tea table belonging to it, the brass andirons and irons, the white window curtains, sofa cover and chair covers trimmed with netting of my own work, the 12 calico chairs covers trimmed with green ribbon, the 
2 CONC calico sofa cover with a deep handsome fringe, this cover was imported with the sofa, the tea and coffee urns, the teaboard belonging to the above china, the china press which stands in the large dining parlor with every article i
2 CONC t contains, except the largest china bowl it is a very richly enameled with gold and colors, and a blue and white china bowl both of which I give to my dearest and only son Bushrod Washington.
2 CONT * I also give my dear granddaughter Ann Aylett Robinson all my Japan ware, the two looking glasses and prints hanging in the drawing room, my imported Wilton carpet, the five bed holsters and pillar not already disposed of, four b
2 CONC edsteads, one pair rose blankets, one pair new bought of Mr. Hutt, one pair belonging to the small bed, three single large blankets, one pair smaller, the chintz curtain which I ripped to make up new and the white lining with it, 
2 CONC one pair calico belonging to the small bed, one pair checked Virginia ditto, which I always used, one pair white M&O, and Indian chintz coverlet bought in Alexandria, one quilt lined with dark blue striped cotton, one quilt lined 
2 CONC with a lining of many sorts of country cloth and not new yet quite good, one M&O coverlet fringed belonging to the curtains of the same cloth, one old M&O, 2 belonging to the checked Virginia curtains, one do damask and one old ta
2 CONC blecloth, three diaper, one larger diaper, to cotton fine and new, 16 birds eye diaper napkins, 9 old damask, 12 imported diaper which I got here at the division of my husband’s estate, and should have divided all the above napkin
2 CONC s but have been told that my dear son had a great number in his house, I also give my granddaughter Anne Aylett Robinson my large washing copper in my small prinus metal preserver which I keep for preserving fruit, all my toilets 
2 CONC and the tables to them, my large black walnut cabinet, the large trunk which now stands in my present chamber with all it contains, coffee, sugars, candles etc., the mattress belonging to the white curtains bed.
2 CONT * In the next place I give and bequeath to my ever darling grandson Richard Henry Lee Washington, all my silver plate of every description, that is to say, 13 large silver spoons, 12 dessert and 12 teaspoons, one pair sugar tongs,
2 CONC  one soup ladle, one dish rim to stand on the middle of the table, four salts with salt spoons to them, one punch ladle, one new elegant stand and cruets, I daily expect some plate which was sent for to the federal city to estimat
2 CONC e 30 pounds being a legacy from my worthy brother-in-law general Washington, this plate I include, should it not be bought, the 30 pounds will be paid my dear daughter-in-law in trust for her son Richard Henry Lee Washington to be
2 CONC  laid out by my daughter-in-law Hannah Washington in such plate as it will purchase for my grandson Richard Henry Lee Washington. I also give him 56 pounds all in gold pieces, this money to be put to interest until he marries or a
2 CONC rrives at the age of 21 years, a Negro woman called Judy who is a very good cook. My daughter-in-law Hannah Washington is by me left at liberty to sell the said Judy if she thinks it most for her son’s advantage and put the money 
2 CONC received for her to interest as I above observed that she was a very good cook. Her age is supposed to be about 40 and a very healthy party woman, she also spins, washes and irons extremely well. Also a new bed quilt lined with co
2 CONC untry white cotton bound round with green silk ferret in the middle piece of the quilt is a shawl, this quilt is laid in my lowest large cabinet drawer where is also the plate and gold which I have bequeath to my dearest grandson 
2 CONC Richard Henry Lee Washington. Should my grandson die before he arrives to the age of 21 years, I then desire all I have given him to be the property of my truly beloved (now an infant) grandson Corbin Washington of Shelby, and in 
2 CONC case of his death under age, his brother John Augustine Washington and Bushrod Corbin Washington to have it.
2 CONT * I give to my dearest daughter-in-law Hannah Washington all my spun and unspun cotton, white English chamber color ground bought by me in Richmond town, also my flowerpots, gin rumber? purchased by me of merchant Neale at Nominy 
2 CONC ferry, should my daughter-in-law Hannah Washington, after having her choice, prefer my [carriage] to her own and harness, she is to take it, hers must be sold towards paying any debt my estate may owe, my carriage being so complet
2 CONC ely repaired, that it is said to be so much stronger than it ever was.
2 CONT * I direct to my dearest daughter-in-law Anne Washington a mourning ring to cost 2 pounds current money of Virginia, and one half of my little library, the other half to Anne Aylett Robinson.
2 CONT * I give my two sorrel carriage horses and the young sorrel five years old which was intended also for the carriage to my dear daughter-in-law Hannah Washington, to my dearest son Bushrod Washington I give every other horse and co
2 CONC lt which are in my estate, except my little riding horse Sift, that with my blue plush saddle and bridle I give my dear good friend Mrs. Mary Harrington, wife to Dr. Timothy Carrington
2 CONT * The 18 green passage and dining room chairs I divide equally between my dearest son Bushrod Washington and my dear granddaughter Anne Aylett Robinson.
2 CONT * I give my son Bushrod Washington the mattress belonging to the green morun bed.
2 CONT * I give my daughter-in-law Hannah Washington my black walnut physic cupboard.
2 CONT * I give my dear granddaughter Anne Aylett Robinson my large black walnut cabinet which stands in my summer chamber.
2 CONT * I give my mahogany dressing table and a small cabinet which stands on it with a looking glass door to my dear granddaughter Jane Mildred Washington.
2 CONT * I give my beloved Mary Lee Washington my handsome chamber table and the dressing glass with the three drawers which stands on it.
2 CONT * My dear husband left me in his will the following slaves to dispose of as I choose at my death provided I gave them to our own children, the slaves are as follows; Billy (who is dead since his wife Jenny, their daughter Venus wh
2 CONC o has brought a daughter since called Betty), these three slaves I give my beloved grandson Richard Henry Lee Washington, a lad called West, son of Venus who was born before my husband’s will was made and not there in mentioned, I
2 CONC  offered to buy him of my dear sons Bushrod Washington and Corbin Washington, but they generously refused to sell him but presented the boys to me as a gift. It is my most earnest wish and desire this lad West may be as soon as po
2 CONC ssible inoculated for the smallpox after which to be bound to a good tradesmen until the age of 21 years, after which he is to be freed for the rest of his life.
2 CONT * If Anne Aylett Robinson has no gold watch, I give her mine with all the different cases and trinkets of every description, chains etc. should she have a gold watch in that case I give mine with everything belonging to it to my d
2 CONC ear Jane Mildred Washington.
2 CONT * I give my dear granddaughter Anne Aylett Washington and the miniature of my ever darling Mildred Lee Washington. My other trinkets I desire may be equally divided between my two granddaughters Jane Mildred Washington and Mary Le
2 CONC e Washington.
2 CONT * I give to my three granddaughters, vizt; Anne Aylett Robinson, Jane Mildred Washington and Mary Lee Washington all my wearing apparel of every sort to be entirely and equally divided among the three, some of my most indifferent 
2 CONC things, my daughter-in-law Hannah Washington and granddaughter Anne Aylett Robinson may at their own discretion give [Negroes] Letty, Jenny, Suck and Venus, through the two last mentioned treated me with great disrespect in my las
2 CONC t hours. Everything which I have not mentioned here must equally be divided between: my small black walnut rum case I long since gave my dear Richard Henry Lee Washington and here confirm, should I owe anything at my death the deb
2 CONC ts must be discharged by selling that part of my property which I have not hear disposed of.
2 CONT * A mourning ring of 5 pounds value currency of Virginia to my beloved grandson Bushrod Washington son of William Augustine Washington and my dear daughter Jenny Washington.
2 CONT * My hand is so very well known that I trust this my last will and testament needs no vouchers.
2 CONT * Hannah Washington
2 CONT * I constitute my darling son Bushrod Washington and William Robinson the latter who married my dear granddaughter Anne Aylett Washington by executors.
2 CONT * At a court held for Westmoreland County the 26th day of April 1801 the aforegoing last will and testament of Hannah Washington, deceased of Bushfield, being presented to the court and it appearing to the satisfaction of the cour
2 CONC t from the testimony of Willoughby Newton that it is wholy the handwriting of the said testatrix, the same is ordered to be recorded; and on the motion of Bushrod Washington one of the executors named in the said will and he havin
2 CONC g performed what is usual in such cases, certificate is granted him for obtaining a probate thereof in due form.
2 CONT * Teste Joseph Fox, Jr. CWC
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT # [http://www.familysearch.org/Eng/Search/af/individual_record.asp?recid=7872297&frompage=0 (AFN:9PJZ-W2)] FamilySearch™ Ancestral File v4.19
