2 TITL Bushrod Washington (1762-1829) memorial
1 NOTE !BIRTH - THE WASHINGTONS AND THEIR HOMES by John W. Wayland
2 CONT Bushrod Washington, the son of John Augustine Washington and his wife, Hannah Bushrod was born 5 June 1762 in Westmoreland Co.,VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR - Ibid
2 CONT In 1785 he married Anne Blackburn, daughter of Col. Thomas Blackburn of Rippon Lodge.  Because the early marriage records of Fairfax Co. have been destroyed, the day and month of the wedding at Rippon Lodge have not been ascertain
2 CONC ed, but the date was prior to Oct. 9, 1785; for on that day, when Gen. Washington reached home a little after noon, as he notes in his diary, he found Bushrod and his wife there.
2 CONT
2 CONT The young lawyer, Bushrod Washington, began practice in his native county of Westmoreland, in Virginia, but soon located in the town of Alexandria.  Here he was only 7 miles from Mt. Vernon. - Ibid
2 CONT
2 CONT in 1787 he was a member of the Virginia House of Delegates, and shortly thereafter he sat in the state convention in Richmond.  In 1790 he settled in Richmond and 8 years later President John Adams appointed him an associate justi
2 CONC ce of the U.S. Supreme Court to succeed his old teacher, James Wilson, who had died.  Bushrod Washington is given high rank as a jurist.  His colleagues regarded him as one of the greatest nisi prius judges in English history, not
2 CONC  excepting Chief Justice Holt or Lord Mansfield.  Judge Washington had no children of his own. - Ibid
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Martha Washington on May 22, 1802.  Thereupon the Mt. Vernon estate, in accordance with the terms of the General's will, descended to his nephew, Judge Bushrod Washington, the older son of the General's second f
2 CONC ull brother, John Augustine Washington and his wife, Hannah Bushrod. - Ibid
2 CONT
2 CONT !DEATH - Ibid
2 CONT Judge Washington died in Philadelphia on Nov. 26, 1829 and his wife died 2 days later, before she reached Mt. Vernon with his body.
