1 NAME Charles /Burgess/
2 GIVN Charles
2 SURN Burgess
2 _MARNM Burgess
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1682
2 ADDR
3 STAE England
1 DEAT
2 DATE 14 MAR 1732
2 PLAC 	St Mary's White Chapel Parish
2 ADDR
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY British Colonial America
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT Contributed by: James Hughes
2 CONT
2 CONT URL: http://vagenweb.rootsweb.com/hening/vol04-23.htm#page_452
2 CONT URL title: Hening's Statutes at Large
2 CONT Note:
2 CONT An Act for the better enabling the Executors of the last Will and Testament of Charles Burges, gent. deceased, to pay his Debts, and Legacies.
2 CONT
2 CONT I. WHEREAS Charles Burges, late of the county of Lancaster, gent. deceased, was in his life time, and at the time of his death, seised in fee simple, of divers plantations, and parcels of land, with the appurtenances, lying and be
2 CONC ing in the counties of Prince-William, and Spotsylvania; that is to say, Three thousand two hundred and thirty acres, lying and being in the said county of Prince-William, near the mountains, called the Coblers, granted to him by 
2 CONC right honourable Thomas, lord Fairfax, proprietor of the Northern Neck, in Virginia, by his certain deed poll, bearing date the fifteenth day of June, in the year of our lord one thousand seven hundred and thirty one; three thousa
2 CONC nd and forty six acres, lying and being near the mountains, in the said county of Prince-William, granted to him by the said lord Fairfax, by one other deed poll, bearing date the same fifteenth day of June, in the year aforesaid;
2 CONC  eleven hundred and seventy six acres, lying and being on Goose-Creek in the said county of Prince-William, granted to him by the said lord Fairfax, by one other deed poll, bearing date the seventeenth day of June, in the year of 
2 CONC our lord one thousand seven hundred and thirty one; and two thousand nine hundred and twenty five acres, lying and being upon Goose Creek, aforesaid, in the said county of Executors of Charles Burges, empowered to sell his lands i
2 CONC n Prince-William and Spotsylvania.   .... 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Frances Fox, daughter of Samuel Fox and Anne Dangerfield, was born about 1704 in Lancaster County, and died between 1737 and 1741 in St. Mary's White Chapel Parish, Lancaster County, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married 
2 CONT #  Charles Burgess (b. 1682-d 14 March 1732) - married 5 Oct 1721,  St Mary's White Chapel Parish, Lancaster County, Virginia.
2 CONT # Jesse Ball (b. 27 Jun 1716-d. 14 Aug 1747) - married 10 Dec 1737 Lancaster County, Virginia.  Jesse Ball was a son of James Ball and Mary Conway.  He married 14 February 1741 to Mary Smith, so Frances had died before that date.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Charles Burgess and Frances Fox:
2 CONT * 1. Frances Burgess,   b. Abt 1718, St Mary's White Chapel Parish, Lancaster County, Virginia d. 1750, Lancaster County, Virginia.  Married Baldwin Matthews Smith.
2 CONT *  	2. Margaret Burgess,   b. Abt 1721, St Mary's White Chapel Parish, Lancaster County, Virginia.  Married James Ball (1718 - 1789), son of James Ball and Mary Conway.
2 CONT *  	3. Elizabeth Burgess,   b. 1732, St. Mary's White Chapel, Lancaster County, Virginia d. Between 1754 and 1758, St. Mary's White Chapel, Lancaster County, Virginia. Married 1) Jeduthan Ball (1725-1749), son of  James Ball & Mar
2 CONC y Conway 2) Robert Armistead.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT # MARRIAGE BONDS IN LANCASTER COUNTY. 5 Oct., 1721, Chas Burges & Frances Fox, daughter of Anne Fox. Sec.+David Ball.  4 Oct., 1721, Anne Fox consents to marriage of daughter, Frances Fox, to Charles Burges. Witnesses, David Ball,
2 CONC  Richard Cooper.
2 CONT # https://www.colonial-settlers-md-va.us/getperson.php?personID=I123624&tree=Tree1
2 CONT #In Christ Church Vestry Book I find that "May 5, 1741, Mr. Jesse Ball and Mrs. Margaret Ball, wife of Mr. James Ball, Jun'r, Mrs. Frances Burgess and Mrs. Elizabeth Burgess, daughters of Charles Burgess, Gent., dec'd, by his wife
2 CONC  Frances, daughter of Samuel Fox, Gent, dec'd, have liberty to build Galleries on the East side of the South wing of the new Church."
