1 NAME Peter /Foster/ of Wendover
2 GIVN Peter
2 NSFX of Wendover
2 _MARNM Foster
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC St. Mary the Virgin
2 ADDR
3 CITY Aylesbury
3 STAE England
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1547
2 ADDR
3 CITY Wendover, Aylesbury
3 CTRY England
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1609
2 ADDR
3 CITY Wendover, Aylesbury
3 CTRY England
1 OCCU Fuller
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *Peter was not married to https://www.geni.com/people/Helen-Foster/6000000003166551855?through=6000000016670862342. Helene and Ellen were two different women conflated together, along with their children.
2 CONT *Not to be confused with https://www.geni.com/people/Patrick-Foster-of-Wing/6000000225887903929. They are two completely different people. Patrick was the spouse of Helene.
2 CONT
2 CONT Factual Biographical Profile
2 CONT Birth: Estimated circa 1547 to 1550. His birthplace is traditionally attributed to the borough of Wendover, Buckinghamshire, England, based on his established residency and business there as an adult.
2 CONT
2 CONT Occupation: He is explicitly documented as a fuller. In the 16th century, a fuller played a critical role in the English textile market. He was responsible for the mechanical fulling process—cleansing, scouring, and thickening fre
2 CONC shly woven wool cloth using water, fuller's earth, and wooden mallets at a local mill.
2 CONT
2 CONT Residency: He lived in the borough of Wendover, a market town situated in the Chiltern Hills of Buckinghamshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: He married a woman named Ellen Unknown (c. 1576) in Wendover, Buckinghamshire, England. Her maiden name and their exact date of marriage are lost to history due to missing 16th-century parish records for Wendover.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT Peter and Ellen Foster had seven documented children. Six of these children were living and explicitly named in Peter's April 20, 1609 last will and testament, while a seventh deceased daughter is confirmed by the mention of her s
2 CONC urviving child.
2 CONT Birth Order
2 CONT 1. Richard Foster (c. 1575)
2 CONT Status: Eldest or co-eldest son.Context: He was already an adult and married when his father wrote his will in 1609.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Katharen Foster (c. 1577)
2 CONT Status: Likely the eldest daughter.Context: She married Ralph Tompkins prior to 1609, making her one of the earliest children to establish her own household.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Unknown Foster (c. 1579)
2 CONT Status: Deceased before April 1609.
2 CONT Context: She married a man named Skinner and had a daughter named Ales (Alice) Skinner before passing away.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Thomas Foster (c. 1580)
2 CONT Status: Younger son.
2 CONT Context: He took over his father’s fulling business in Wendover and married Elizabeth Seamer around 1606.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Margery Foster (c. 1586)
2 CONT Status: Married daughter.
2 CONT Context: She is listed in the 1609 will as Margery Cooke, indicating she married shortly before her father’s death.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Elizabeth Foster (c. 1588)
2 CONT Status: Unmarried daughter.
2 CONT Context: She was still living at home and single in April 1609, placing her birth toward the end of the family timeline.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. Isabel Foster (c. 1593)
2 CONT Status: Youngest daughter.Context: Listed last among the daughters in the will and designated as unmarried, indicating she was likely a teenager or young adult in 1609.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter’s Death: He died between April 20, 1609, and July 13, 1609. His exact burial date is unrecorded because the 1609 Bishop's Transcripts (the local church register backups sent to the bishop) for Wendover are missing.
2 CONT
2 CONT Primary Source Documentation
2 CONT The cornerstone of Peter Foster’s historical validity is his probate record filed in the Archdeaconry of Buckingham.Document Reference: Archdeaconry of Buckingham, Wills, Register Wf, volume 18, folio 37A.Execution Date: Written b
2 CONC y Peter Foster on April 20, 1609.Probate Date: Formally proved in court on July 13, 1609.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Will of Peter Foster
2 CONT The will of Peter Foster of Wendover Borough, fuller, was dated 20 April and proved on 13 July 1609. In it, he named his wife Ellen, his daughter Isabel Foster, his daughter Elizabeth Foster, his son Thomas Foster, his daughter Ka
2 CONC tharen Tomkins, and his daughter Ales Skinner. (Arch. of Budkingham, Wf/18/37A.) This takes the ancestry of the Fosters back one generation to Peter (and perhaps Ellen). Unfortunately, the 1609 Bishop's Transcripts for Wendover, i
2 CONC n which Peter Foster's burial would have been recorded, are among those that are missing.
2 CONT Updated: 06/2026
2 CONT —-
2 CONT http://genforum.genealogy.com/tompkins/messages/1790.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Regarding fulling mills:
2 CONT
2 CONT They appear to have originated in 9th or 10th century in the Islamic world, either in the Middle East or North Africa. Mechanical fulling was subsequently disseminated into Western Europe through Islamic Spain and Italy in the 11t
2 CONC h and 12th centuries.
2 CONT
2 CONT The earliest known reference to a fulling mill in France, which dates from about 1086, was discovered in Normandy. Nearby towns: Aylesbury,( seat of the ancient government) Wycombe, Amersham, Marlow.  Of 223 parishes,there are sev
2 CONC en deaconries in Buckinhamshire, which is in the diocese of Lincoln, except for four, which are peculiarly under the Archbishopric of Canterbury, four under London, and four under St. Albans. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Aylesbury contain many magnificent seats including Stowe (the seat of the Marquis of Buckingham), Bulstrode (formerly of the Rutland family, now under the Duke of Somerset), Dropmore (the seat of Lord Grenville), Taploe House (the
2 CONC  seat of the Marquis of Thomond), Wycombe Abbey (seat of Lord Carrington). The whole of the Chiltern district is said to have formerly been a forest, the western part occupied by the forest of Bernwood, and was deforested during t
2 CONC he reign of James I.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nearby areas are still forested. Nearby are also Stoney Stratford, Newport Pagnell, and Olney. Nearby also is the Vale of Aylesbury, the village of Ickford, and on the borders of Bedfordshire are the celebrated Fuller's Earth Pits
2 CONC .Nearby Hanslope was the center of paper and bone- lace manufacturing. The church of Stewkley is the most ancient and important church still standing and is one of the most complete examples of Norman architecture in England, not 
2 CONC having been altered from its original state. It is believed to date from 1106- 1150 and its building '''to have been ordered by one Geoffrey de Clinton.''' Geoffrey’s father was a great lord in the court of Henry I. The family, in
2 CONC evitably, hailed from Normandy but it was not one of the great Norman families. Geoffrey rose from relative obscurity to become first Henry’s treasurer, then Sheriff of Warwickshire. This latter position seems to have been partly 
2 CONC due to Henry’s growing mistrust of one of his other advisors, Roger de Beaumont  the Earl of Warwick. Geoffrey proceeded to  initiate the building of the mighty Kenilworth Castle just two miles from Warwick itself! Warwick reasser
2 CONC ted himself with Henry and it was possibly his hand that led to Geoffrey himself being tried - but acquitted - of treason in 1130 after which Geoffrey’s influence declined before his death between 1130 and 1133.
2 CONT
2 CONT His son, also Geoffrey, inherited and also got involved with quarrels with the Earl of Warwick - this time in King Stephen’s reign - and almost ruined the family. They settled their differences, however, and Geoffrey married Warwi
2 CONC ck’s daughter, Agnes. It isn’t clear whether it was the elder or younger 	
2 CONT Geoffrey who commissioned Stewkley Church but we know that the younger Geoffrey passed custodianship of the church to Kenilworth Priory.In the centre of a doorway (see pictures) is a small tympanum with its unusual shape. Here, ei
2 CONC ther side of the keystone, we see dragon figures, one of the few semi-pagan icons to be seen in this church. This shape would have been rather more common in France and, again, much more so in the Early English period.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernwood Forest was one of several forests of the ancient Kingdom of England and was a Royal hunting forest. It is thought to have been set aside as Royal hunting land when the Anglo-Saxon kings had a palace at Brill and church in
2 CONC  Oakley, in the 10th century and was a particularly favoured place of Edward the Confessor, who was born in nearby Islip. William the Conquerer loved hunting, and one of the first things he did post invasion was to re-organize the
2 CONC  Royal hunting forests, including Bernwood as one of his most valuable assets.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://greatenglishchurches.co.uk/html/stewkley.html
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.sarahwoodbury.com/forest-laws/
2 CONT
2 CONT (3)E. M. Carus-Wilson, 'An Industrial Revolution of the Thirteenth Century' Economic History Review, Old Series, 11(1) (1941), 39–60.
2 CONT
2 CONT (4) Reginald Lennard, 'Early English Fulling Mills: additional examples' Economic History Review, New Series, 3(3) (1951), 342–343
2 CONT
