1 NAME David /Morrow/
2 GIVN David
2 SURN Morrow
2 _MARNM Morrow
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1715
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Colonial America
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Unclear where the assumptions as to this David Morrow's parents comes from.
2 CONT
2 CONT Re David Morrow b. c. 1715: from [http://boards.ancestry.com/thread.aspx?mv=flat&m=1664&p=surnames.morrow '''Morrow'''] boards, Ancestry.com (Bill Morrow):
2 CONT
2 CONT David Morrow b. c1715- c1795 & wife Mary Morrow left the Baltimore area in early 1760's moving to the Fishing Creek area in present day Chester Co SC where in 1765 David obtained a 100ac land grant next to his brother "Old Samuel 
2 CONC Morrow" d.1796 SC. Family lore is that David Morrow fought at Braddock's Defeat in 1755.
2 CONT
2 CONT David & Mary Morrow's children:
2 CONT # John Morrow c1750-1781 m. Margaret Kelso, died Rev War
2 CONT # Robert Morrow 1752-1823 m. widow Jane (Peden) Morton
2 CONT # Samuel Morrow 1760-1842 m. Janet Nelson, became a Major in SC Militia, stated in his pension papers he was born near Baltimore, Md.
2 CONT # Sarah Morrow b. bef 1775
2 CONT # Mary Morrow b. bef 1775
2 CONT
2 CONT The entire David & Mary Morrow family above are listed on page 130 in Rev John Simpson's "Visitation Journal" 16 Jan 1775 living in the Fishing Creek area. Rev John Simpson was pastor of the Fishing Creek Presbyterian Church locat
2 CONC ed near the York/Chester county line.
2 CONT
2 CONT "I found an 1821 deed where Major Samuel Morrow 1760-1842 was actually a nephew of [https://www.geni.com/people/Old-Samuel-Morrow/6000000011048324232 ''' "Old" Samuel Morrow'''] rather than a son. We knew that Lt John was a brothe
2 CONC r of Major Sam, thru 1847 court testimony by the widow of Major Sam. In SC land grants and census records, discovered a new David Morrow, previously missed by researchers. He appeared in the 1790 census along with Old Sam and Majo
2 CONC r - then Capt Sam, spelled Murrow on pages 25 & 26 of Spartanburg census. Land grants showed this David Morrow living adjacent to Old Samuel in 1765-85 in the Fishing Creek area of present day Chester Co SC. Old Sam and David and 
2 CONC Major Sam also appear in the Spartanburg area in the 1780's as neighbors, a strong indication of kinship. 
2 CONT
2 CONT David Morrow sold 150ac in the Fishing Creek area with Samuel Morrow witnessing and then in 1786 proving the deed, noted as "Samuel Morrow of Spartanburg". This ties David & Samuel to Fishing Creek and to the Spartanburg area. Str
2 CONC ong indication they were kin.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1981 Morrow research sent me by Jean Agee on Chester Co records [shows that] the family of David & Mary Morrow were visited 16 Jan 1775 by Rev John Simpson, pastor of the Fishing Creek Presbyterian Church. The children noted were:
2 CONC  Robert, Saml, Sarah, Mary, (page ended, top of next page: John & Mary Morrow). This is my Robert age 22, Major Sam age 14, Sarah & Mary, and Lt John Morrow and his wife Mary Kelso."
2 CONT
2 CONT See also [http://www.fgs-project.com/maryland/groups/m/morrow-david-.txt Morrow Family Group Sheet] posted by Bill Morrow.
