1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the father of https://www.geni.com/people/William-Simmons-Sr-of-Bruton-Parish/5056009563640051552?through=6000000082945379139
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Disambiguation ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Tingley, Raymon Meyers. Some Ancestral Lines, p. 371-2 fraudulently created records to state that Elizabeth was daughter of Thomas Simmons (Symonds) of Plymouth who in turn married Elizabeth Nash and that Thomas Symonds of Plymout
2 CONC h was son of Moses. The supposed family of Thomas Symmonds of Plymouth is a known fraud. See Anderson's [https://www.americanancestors.org/databases/great-migration-begins-immigrants-to-ne-1620-1633-vols-i-iii/image/?pageName=1790
2 CONC &filterQuery=category:Great%20Migration%20Study%20Project Great Migration Begins 1790] and [https://www.americanancestors.org/databases/great-migration-begins-immigrants-to-ne-1620-1633-vols-i-iii/image/?pageName=1683&filterQuery=
2 CONC category:Great%20Migration%20Study%20Project Great Migration Begins 1683] and [https://www.americanancestors.org/databases/great-migration-immigrants-to-new-england-1634-1635-volume-i-a-b/image/?pageName=411&volumeId=7051&rId=2207
2 CONC 4595 Great Migration 2:1:411] Thomas Symonds of Plymouth was a real person but has no known family. But there was also another Thomas Symonds of Braintree/Bost that seems be be conflated here.
2 CONT
2 CONT There were two men in New England before 1640 named Thomas Simmons or Symonds. The first was Thomas Symonds of Plymouth and Scituate. He arrived by 1633 when Samuel Fuller named him in his will "my two servants Thomas Symons & Rob
2 CONC ert Cowles." [https://www.americanancestors.org/databases/mayflower-descendant-the/image/?volumeId=12165&pageName=26&rId=137474011 Mayflower Descendants 1:26]. The only other records of him includes a court record with Edward Doty
2 CONC  in 1641, a right to bear arms in Scituate in 1643 a land transfer in in Scituate on 26 Jan 1649 to Gilbert Brooks. All that is known is he died after that last date, 26 Jan 1649 probably in Scituate with no family. See [https://c
2 CONC atalog.hathitrust.org/Record/006784512 Records of the Colony of New Plymouth in New England, Nathaniel B. Shurtleff and David Pulsifer, eds]., 12 volumes in 10 (Boston 1855 – 1861) 2:33. 8:191, 12:217-18. This is the man who was t
2 CONC he subject of the Tingley-Meyers fraud. His origins are unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT There was a second Thomas Simmons/Symonds of Braintree/Boston. He arrived in 1638 and was in Suffolk County when he received a land grant in Braintree see [https://www.americanancestors.org/databases/great-migration-newsletter-v1-
2 CONC 20/image/?pageName=20&volumeId=21154&filterQuery=category:Great%20Migration%20Study%20Project Great Migration Newsletter 10:20]. But he is not identical to the man above. Evidence for him can be found in the vital records in see [
2 CONC https://www.americanancestors.org/databases/new-england-historical-and-genealogical-register/image/?pageName=248&volumeId=11713 NEHGR 3:248 link]:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Joan Symons the daughter of Thomas Symons born 8 Nov 1640 died 30 May 1642
2 CONT * Thomas Symond died on 15 Jun 1642
2 CONT
2 CONT Possibly he was related to the John Symons who has a daughter Anne who died in Jun of 1640?
2 CONT
2 CONT All we seem to know about his family based on vital records is that he had a daughter Joan and he (or a son of the same name died in Boston 15 Jun 1642. The name of his wife is not recorded. Thomas Symonds of Braintree/Boston was 
2 CONC listed in a mortgage to Richard Parker in 1640 see [https://archive.org/stream/notebookkeptby07lechrich#page/351/mode/1up  Letchford 351]. Thomas Symonds of Braintree/Boston was listed in a court case in Colony Record p 282 regard
2 CONC ing Mr Eaton's house and "the college" see [https://archive.org/stream/recordsofgoverno01mass#page/281/mode/1up BCR 1:282] Wikipedia reports that Thomas Symonds of Boston/Braintree was a carpenter who had apparently assisted in th
2 CONC e building of the college at Cambridge in 1639 and afterwards - was soon found to be in debt to one of the creditors of the college, John Cogan.The college building itself was poorly erected - Thomas Symonds being the responsible 
2 CONC party after Nathaniel Eaton left - and eventually Symonds and at least one of his assistants were thrown into debtor's prison. See: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nathaniel_Eaton Nathaniel Eaton in Wikipedia]. If true this may exp
2 CONC lain the deaths listed in vital records for he may very well be the man who died 15 Jun 1642 in debtors prison. 
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Simmons/Symmonds did *not* marry Elizabeth Nash (1609-1680). That is a fraud see above. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Caution from [http://willisolandergenealogy.com/1695.htm Anderson's Great Migration Project] 
2 CONT
2 CONT There was a THOMAS SYMONS in Plymouth by 1633, and several authors have attempted to squeeze him into the family of Moses Simonson, but there is no evidence that he belongs. The most egregious attempt in this direction was perpetr
2 CONC ated in 1935 by Raymon Meyers Tingley, who fabricated documents, including an alleged deposition made by Thomas, and managed to make Thomas both son and brother of Moses [ Tingley-Meyers 371-72].
2 CONT
2 CONT "It have been said that the Immigrant, https://www.geni.com/people/Moses-Simmons-of-Duxbury/6000000000792368240?through=6000000025361695898 had a son Thomas. In a forty years' study of the Moses Simmons family, I have failed to fi
2 CONC nd any reliable record that the first Moses Simmons had a son Thomas."
2 CONT
2 CONT He has no relationship to Moses Simmons:
2 CONT
2 CONT from https://archive.org/stream/ancestryofjohnsi00rowe#page/11/mode/1up
2 CONT
2 CONT >Moses Simmons, the Immigrant, died in Duxbury, in 1689. His will, dated June 17, 1689, was as follows .... etc. This will was filed September 15, 1691. .... etc. His oldest son, Moses, died in 1676, before the will was made. His 
2 CONC oldest child, Rebecca, who married John Soule in 1655, must have died before 1680, when John Soule married a second wife, so was living at the time the will was made.
2 CONT
2 CONT This burial in FindAGrave is based on erroneous data:
2 CONT
2 CONT * http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=73134757
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
