1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/33379073/gerard-spencer
2 CONT
2 CONT >'''Wikitree: [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Spencer-165 Gerard Spencer (bef. 1614 –bef. 1685)]'''
2 CONT >'''Note 1:''' The timeline produced is helpful. Click the link to read details. 
2 CONT >'''Note 2:''' Where Wikitree presently fails, to some degree, is accepting birthplaces for some children occurring in Connecticut before the family removed from Lynn. Tertiary sources and Find A Grave are at the root of such erro
2 CONC rs at Wikitree and on Geni.
2 CONT >'''Note 3:''' All children born before Nathaniel probably were born in Lynn, MA, though there are no extant birth records for them.
2 CONT >'''Note 4:''' The earliest family record in Hartford is for the birth of Nathaniel on 21 Dec 1658. '''—''' ''Connecticut, U.S., Town Birth Records, pre-1870 (Barbour Collection): Hartford Vital Records 1635-1855''. Available at: 
2 CONC [https://www.ancestry.com/discoveryui-content/view/318234:1034?tid=174604283&pid=132312131890&hid=1037356590723 Ancestry.com].
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT >'''Robert Charles Anderson's Suggested Birth Order of Children'''
2 CONT >''The Great Migration: Immigrants to New England, 1634-1635'', Vol. 6, R-S. [https://www.ancestry.com/imageviewer/collections/2496/images/42521_b158317-00536?ssrc=&backlabel=Return&ranMID=50138&ranEAID=4026539&ranSiteID=Xib7NfnK1
2 CONC 1s-Iyygn4qAjW7G_njsPkhsQA&o_xid=4026539&o_lid=4026539&o_sch=Affiliate+External Ancestry.com], pp. 423-425. ([https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000206777764863 '''document attached''']).  
2 CONT >'''i.''' JOHN SPENCER, b. say 1638.
2 CONT >'''ii.''' MEHITABEL SPENCER, b. say 1640.
2 CONT >'''iii.''' MARAH SPENCER, b. say 1642.
2 CONT >'''iv.''' HANNAH SPENCER, b. say 1644.
2 CONT >'''v.''' SARAH SPENCER, b. say 1646.
2 CONT >'''vi.''' THOMAS SPENCER, b. say 1648.
2 CONT >'''vii.''' SAMUEL SPENCER, b. say 1650.
2 CONT >'''viii.''' TIMOTHY SPENCER, b. say 1652.
2 CONT >'''ix.''' RUTH SPENCER, b. about 1654.
2 CONT >'''x.''' WILLIAM SPENCER, b. say 1656.
2 CONT >'''xi.''' NATHANIEL SPENCER, b. say 1658.
2 CONT >'''xii.''' REBECCA SPENCER, b. say 1660.
2 CONT >'''Page [https://www.ancestry.com/imageviewer/collections/2496/images/42521_b158317-00539?ssrc=&backlabel=Return&ranMID=50138&ranEAID=4026539&ranSiteID=Xib7NfnK11s-Iyygn4qAjW7G_njsPkhsQA&o_xid=4026539&o_lid=4026539&o_sch=Affiliat
2 CONC e+External p. 248].'''— “Nathaniel Goodwin, the first to publish a compiled account of this family, did not include Sarah and '''Elizabeth''' in the list of children [Goodwin Notes 197]. Jacobus pointed out the document that demon
2 CONC strated that Sarah, wife of Stephen Backus, was a daughter of this immigrant [TAG 27:164]. The earliest known reference to a daughter '''Elizabeth''', who married John Stannard, was published in 1907, without supporting evidence, 
2 CONC by William Henry Spencer [''Spencer Family Record'' (New York 1907), 21]. Most later treatments of the family include this daughter, also without evidence, and so we do not include her in the list of children above [Dawes-Gates 2:
2 CONC 775; TAG 27:165; McCormick-Hamilton 909]; the birth-order chronology is crowded enough with twelve children.”
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT "Gerard (1) Spencer (1614-1685) along with his older brothers, William (1601-1640), Thomas (1607-1687), and Michael (1611-1653) were among the very first settlers in New England under the auspices of the Massachusetts Bay Company.
2 CONC  Their sister, Elizabeth Spencer, also was a pioneer as the wife of Timothy Tomlins. Almost certainly all were among the first passengers of the Winthrop Fleet when the first contingent of eleven ships reached New England in 1630.
2 CONC " [Jack T. & Edith W. Spencer, ''Gerard Spencer, A Pioneer of Cambridge'', Lynn & Haddam; Connecticut Nutmegger, Sept 1997, pgs 188-614, as cited in http://ketchum.bobaddleman.com/spencer_family.html]
2 CONT
2 CONT "GERRARD CAME TO AMERICA with the first Winthrop Fleet in 1630. He was under the leadership of John Winthrop who established the Massachusetts Bay Colony in Cambridge. The first mention of Gerrard is in the Cambridge Town records 
2 CONC "in the prime of September 1634, Lots granted one west side River - Gerrad Spencer 4 ackrs." He moved to Lynn, MA with his brother Michael in 1638 and ran the ferry there. The ferry ran from Needham's Landing in Lynn to Biards Lan
2 CONC ding in Saugas. In 1661 he was one of the 28 purchasers of the town of Haddam. He was commissioned an Ensign for the town of Haddam on September 14, 1675. A pewter flagon and Urim Basin are in the glass case in the rear of the chu
2 CONC rch sancturary at Haddam.
2 CONT
2 CONT Links 
2 CONT * http://bobaddleman.com/ketchum/spencer_family.html#Gerard%201614
2 CONT
2 CONT =============================
2 CONT *'''Founders and Patriots of America Index By National Society of Daughters of Founders and Patriots of America 
2 CONT *Pg.206 
2 CONT *http://books.google.com/books?id=7dGPyld17G4C&pg=PA206&lpg=PA206&dq=Jared+Gerrad+Spencer+1614&source=bl&ots=ykeSPwU_QM&sig=8VonCN9aRKIeTmpbzSgS1JGfyNc&hl=en&ei=SgmMTOTYHI26sQPKirnSBA&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=6&ved=0CC
2 CONC EQ6AEwBTgy#v=onepage&q=spencer&f=false
2 CONT *'''Gerard (Jared) Spencer (bpt. Apr. 25, 1614-Sept. 3, 1685) married 1st 1637 Hannah Pratt; p. 1677 2nd Rebecca (Porter) Clark   CONN.
2 CONT *_____________________
2 CONT *'''Our New England ancestors and their descendants, 1620-1900: historical ... By Henry Whittemore
2 CONT *http://archive.org/details/ournewenglandanc00whit
2 CONT **Spencer and Allied Families
2 CONT *Pg. 49-84
2 CONT *http://archive.org/stream/ournewenglandanc00whit#page/104/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg. 53
2 CONT *   Prof. Ray T. Spencer while making researches in his own line, discovered among the church records of St. George's Parish, Stotfold, Bedfordshire, England. much interesting data, concerning the line of Gerrard Spencer, the fath
2 CONC er of William, Thomas, Michael and '''Gerrard''' the emigrants, from which it appears that the above named Gerrard was the son of Michael who also had a brother Gerrard, and they were the sons of John. Thus three generations prece
2 CONC ding the emigrants are definitely established. The church records of St. George's Parish, above referred to, show that "June 9, 1558, John Spencer, Sen., was buried." 
2 CONT *   There is evidence to show that this John was the father of Michael and of the first Gerrard mentioned. 
2 CONT **		FROM NOTES OF GEO. T. SPENCER. 
2 CONT *   The ancestry of Gerard Spencer and his brothers William, Thomas and Michael Spencer, appears from the parish registers of Edworth and Stotfield in Bedfordshire, Eng., and other contemporaneous documents. 
2 CONT *Jan. 25, 1555, Michael Spencer and Annis Liuur were married (the last name of Annis is so poorly written in the record that is cannot be definitely determined.)
2 CONT *April 20, 1557, John, son of Michael Spencer baptized.
2 CONT *May 27, 1558, was baptized Michael, son of Michael Spencer.
2 CONT *April 15, 1560, buried Michael, son of Michael Spencer.
2 CONT *23 Feb. 1561, Annis, the wife of Michael Spencer, was buried.
2 CONT *20 Aug., 1564, Joan, the daughter of Michael Spencer, was baptized.
2 CONT *30 Aug., 1566, Alice, daughter of Michael Spencer, was baptized. }Twins.
2 CONT *30 Aug., 1566, Michael Spencer, was baptized,} Twins
2 CONT *24 July, 156?, Ann, daughter of Michael Spencer baptized.
2 CONT *30 July, 1568, Jarratt Spencer and Ellen Whitson were married, whose will was proved at London, 20 May, 1577; he was brother of Michael the elder.
2 CONT *http://archive.org/stream/ournewenglandanc00whit#page/106/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg. 54
2 CONT *12 March, 1571, Thomas, son of Michael Spencer was baptized.
2 CONT *20 May, 1576, Gerat Spencer, son of Michael Spencer, and Elizabeth his wife, baptized.
2 CONT *9 July, 1580, Richard, son of Michael and Elizabeth Spencer, baptized.
2 CONT *18 Nov., 1599, Elizabeth, wife of Michael Spencer, was buried.
2 CONT *It is quite probable that Michael Spencer of Edworth, and Gerrard Spencer of Biggleswade, were sons of John Spencer, Senior, and Ann Spencer who burials are recorded in Edworth register, the former June 9, 1558, and the later Jun
2 CONC e 16, 1569: Nothing more is known of them than what is disclosed by these records .... etc.
2 CONT *http://archive.org/stream/ournewenglandanc00whit#page/108/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.55
2 CONT *   Richard Spencer, gent, 17 March, 1645, with a codicil bearing date 20 May, 1646, proved 8 June, 1646. To Thomas Spencer, son of my brother, Thomas Spencer, all my copyhold lands and tenements by me purchased of the creditors o
2 CONC f Walter Maiston, situate in Kingsbury street, near St. Albans, in the county of Hertford, in the parish of St. Michael. To Daniel Spencer, of London, grocer, son of my brother John Spencer, deceased, all of those eight messuages 
2 CONC or tenements, etc . lately by me purchased of John Gearing, grocer, * * * *  To Sarah Bland and Hannah Bland, daughter of my sister Katharine Bland, deceased, and to Elizabeth Tomlyns, widow, daughter of my brother, Jerrard Spence
2 CONC , deceased, my messauges or tenements * * * *  To the said Daniel Spencer all my land and tenements in the counties of Kent and Essex, he to pay unto Anthony Spencer and Jarrard Spencer, sons, of my brother Thomas Spencer, decease
2 CONC d, and unto two children of Margaret Spencer, deceased, now in or near London, and at the desposing of Elizabeth Carter, their aunt, the sum of thirty pounds yearly, 
2 CONT *http://archive.org/stream/ournewenglandanc00whit#page/110/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.56
2 CONT *during their natural lives * * * * and ten pounds per annum to the said two children, and ten pounds per annum to the said two children, and ten pounds apiece to the said  Anthony Spencer and Jerrard Spencer, to be paid unto them
2 CONC  and to their guardians by half yearly payments, as the rent of said lands and tenements shall grow to after my decease. 
2 CONT *   "Item. I give and bequeath unto '''Jerrard Spencer''', Thomas Spencer and Michael Spencer, sons of my brother, Jarrard Spencer, deceased, the sum of fifty pounds apiece, and unto the children of William Spencer, son of my brot
2 CONC her, Jarrard Spencer, deceased, to he diviided equally hetween them the sum of fifty pounds, to he paid unto their guardians, and within two years next after my decease." 
2 CONT *   All of these brothers above mentioned were living in New England at the time of their uncle's death. 
2 CONT *http://archive.org/stream/ournewenglandanc00whit#page/112/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.57
2 CONT *'''   Gerrard or Jerrard Spencer, the founder of the Haddam and Saybrook, Connecticut families of this name, born in Stotfold, England, bap. April 28, 1614, was the son of Gerrard Spencer and nephew of Sir Richard. He came to New
2 CONC  England in 1632, and was admitted March 9, 1637, a freeman of the Massachusetts Bay Colony March 9, 1637. He was a resident of Cambridge 1634, and owned land on the south side of the river. He removed to Lynn, and at 
2 CONT *'''   "A .... etc. ... In June, 1656, he was chosen Ensign of the train band of Lynn, and in 1659 Grand Juror. He was in Connecticut the following year, as Simon Lobdell sued him and his daughter Hannah for damages because she re
2 CONC fused to marry Lobdell. 
2 CONT *'''He was probably in Hartford for a short time. In .... He died in 1685, his will bearing date two years earlier. 
2 CONT **'''The Last Will and Testament of Jarrard Spencer, of Haddam. 
2 CONT *'''   "In the first place it is my will that all my lawful debts shall be payd concerning my estate that God hath left me. It is my will that my sons have after tbe rate of twenty and my daughters fifteen'''. I give unto my son W
2 CONC illiam that land which I bought of Stephen Laxford's estate ; how I came by it the Court Records will show, I give unto my son William the third part of forty-eight acres, be it more or less, lying by that which is commonly called
2 CONC  Mills his brook ; likewise I give unto my son William an acre of swamp mowing land adjoining to my house meadow lot, which particulars mentioned shall be all his part of my estate. 
2 CONT *   I give to my son Nathaniel, and to his heirs, my now dwelling house in the lot with an addition lying by the side of it, granted by the committee : likewise I give my son Nathaniel one-third part of that lot formerly mentioned
2 CONC  lying by Wells his brook, likewise I give unto my son Nathaniel an acre of swamp lying at the end of my meadow lott, and joining to his brother William formerly given ; likewise I give unto my son Nathaniel forty acres, which is 
2 CONC a third part of six score acres, and this is to be his portion. 
2 CONT *   "I give unto my daughter Rebecca that house lot I bought of Thomas Smith ; likewise I 
2 CONT *http://archive.org/stream/ournewenglandanc00whit#page/114/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.58
2 CONT *give unto my daughter Rebecca one-third part of that lott by Wells his brook, and this is to be her portion if she will accept it; if not to take what falls to her in the distribution.
2 CONT *   I give unto my son Thomas forty acres on Matchamoodus side, being part of that lot whereof his brother Nathaniel hath a share. I give unto my son Thomas his son, Jarrard Spencer, my rapier. 
2 CONT *   "I give unto my son Timothy Spencer the remaynder of that six acre lot whereof his two brothers had their shares before, which six acres are those my sons shall choose. The other six acre-lot I dispose of as followeth, to Grac
2 CONC e Spencer, the daughter of my son John Spencer, forty acres; to Alice Brooke, the daughter of my daughter Brooke, forty acres; to Grace Spencer, the dauughter of my son Samuel Spencer. I give the other forty acres. I give unto Jar
2 CONC rard Cone, the son of my daughter Cone, my carbine. A pewter flagon and rim bayson I give unto the chuch at Haddam, if there be one within five years after the date hereof. It is my will that my son John, his children, and my son-
2 CONC in law. Daniel Cone, his children, have an equal proportion of my estate with my other children. It is my will that however my estate falls out for portion to my children that my daughter Ruth Clark's portion shall be fifteen poun
2 CONC ds, which was my covenant with her father at her marriage, which fifteen pounds she hath received some part of it as my books will testify. One thing I forgot : one feather bed which I give to my son Nathaniel. 
2 CONT *'''Dated Sept. 17, 1683. 		JERRARD SPENCER.
2 CONT * .... etc.
