2 CONT
2 CONT The Will of Gerrard / Jarrad Spencer
2 CONT
2 CONT "Spencer, Jarrad, Ensign, Haddam. Invt Pounds: 124-12-00 of Estate not disposed of by Will Taken 29 June, 1685, by Joseph Arnot & Alexander Rollo Will dated 17, September, 1683.
2 CONT
2 CONT The last will of Ensign Jarrad Spencer of Haddam:
2 CONT
2 CONT I give unto my son William the Land which I bought of Steven Luxford's Estate. How I come by it the Court Record will show.
2 CONT
2 CONT I give unto my son William 1/3 part of 48 acres lying by that wch was commonly called Welles his Brook.
2 CONT
2 CONT I give to my son Nathaniel my now Dwelling house with the Lott that was the Houselott, with an Addition lying by the side of it, granted by the Committee.
2 CONT
2 CONT I give unto my daughter Rebekah that Houselott I bought of Thomas Smith.
2 CONT
2 CONT Likewise I give unto my daughter Rebeckah 1/3 part of the Lott by Welles his Brook.
2 CONT
2 CONT I give unto my son Thomas 40 acres on Matchamodus Side.
2 CONT
2 CONT I give unto my son Thomas his son, Jarrad Spencer by name, my Rapier.
2 CONT
2 CONT I give unto my son Timothy Spencer the remainder of that 6 score acre lott whereof his 2 brothers had their shares.
2 CONT
2 CONT The other 6 score thereof I dispose of as followeth: to Grace Spencer, the daughter of my son John Spencer, 40 acres; to Alice Brooks, the daughter of my daughter Brooks, 40 acres; to Grace Spencer, the daughter of my son Samuel S
2 CONC pencer, I give the other 40 acres.
2 CONT
2 CONT I give unto Jarred Cone, the son of my daughter Cone, my Carbine.
2 CONT
2 CONT A pewter Flagon and Urim Bason I give to the church at Haddam, if there be one within five years.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is my will that my son John Spencer his Children and my son-in-law Daniel Cone his Children have an equal proportion of my Estate with my other Children. It is my Will however my Estate falls out for portions to my Children, th
2 CONC at my daughter Ruth Clarke's portion shall be 15 pounds, which was my Covenant with her father at her marriage, which 15 pounds she hath received some part thereof, as my Books will testify; and to son Joseph Clarke I give him 40 
2 CONC acres of land at Matchemodus. It is the humble request of Jarrad Spencer that the honoured Major John Talcott and Capt. John Allyn would be pleased to oversee this his Will. I appoint my two sons Daniel Brainard and William Spence
2 CONC r Adms to the Estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jarrard Spencer
2 CONT
2 CONT Witness: John James, Joseph Arnot Court Record, Page 111-3, Sept, 1685: Adms to Daniel Braynard and William Spencer, with the Will annexed.
2 CONT *______________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of Jarrad Spencer, Ensign: A Digest of Early Connecticut Probate Records, Compiled by Charles William Manwaring, Vol 1, 1685-1700, p. 363-4.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gerrard had a son named Gerrard b. 1614 and died 1685. This son came to America in 1638 from Stotfold, England, settled in Cambridge, then Hartford Connecticut. He was a member of General Court of Commonwealth in 1674, 1675, 1678,
2 CONC  1679, 1680, and 1683. He also served in King Philips War (Colonial Families of the United States of America, edited by George Norbury Mackenzie, Volume III, page 536).
2 CONT
2 CONT One of East Haddam's sons, who became prominent in the Colony and later as a soldier, was the Hon. Joseph Spencer. The first American ancestor of this family was Jared Spencer, ,who came to America and settled in Newtown (Cambridg
2 CONC e), Massachusetts, and a few years later moved to Lynn. Still later, he became one of the first settlers of Haddam and was made an ensign there by the General Court at Hartford, in September, 1675. His great-grandson, the lion. Jo
2 CONC seph Spencer, married Martha Brainard, daughter of the Hon. Hezekiah Brainard, in August, 1738. His public service began as judge of probate in 1753, and he filled this office till his death. In 1758, he was a major in the norther
2 CONC n army against the French. In 1775, a short time before the signing of the Declaration of Independence, he was appointed a brigadier-general, and in 1776, he was made a major-general in the army of the United States.
2 CONT *_____________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Ensign Gerard SPENCER was christened on 25 Apr 1614 in St. Mary's Parish, Stotfold, Bedford, England.(8362) (8363)(8364) He emigrated about 1630 to Cambridge, Massachusetts Bay Colony. (8365)(8366) According to Jack Spencer (600),
2 CONC  there is no known record of the SPENCERs amoung any passenger list, but the 5 Spencer brothers were "almost certainly" among the passengers of the 1630 Winthrop fleet. he asumes that they came together. It is known that siblings 
2 CONC Eliabeth and Timothy were in Saugus (Lynn) in 1630. William was in Newe Towne (Cambridge) in 1631 being one of the first 8 settlerd there. If so, Gerard, the youngest, would have been only 16 when he arrived. "They probably were a
2 CONC mong the thousands of Englishmen who had no strong ties to the land simply because the did not belong to the landed gentry. They had few hopes for advancing their situation in life by remaining in England. They were, in fact, reli
2 CONC cs of the centuries of domination by the nobility and the landed gentry." Jack Spencer includes an 8-page chapter on "Why and how the Spencer siblings of Bedfordshire come to America," in which he cites four motivations. The first
2 CONC  was economic. Gerard Sr leased his property in 1615. Jack Spencer hypothesizes that the Spencer family began to run into tough times beginning with his father Michael. The remaining 6 pages of the chapter address three additional
2 CONC  motivations: religious, "turmoil," and [association with] "leaders of the Great Migration." Dispute being an interesting read on the times in England, this part of the chapter is highly speculative and provides no specific eviden
2 CONC ce or connections to the SPENCERs. He suggests that the SPENCER brothers were Puritans based on them being the "right age" to be dissenters and that brother William was reported to be "closely connected" with Rev. Thomas Hooker, w
2 CONC hom he MAY have met at Cambridge University (Massachusetts). He granted a four acre plot in Sep 1634 in Cambridge, Massachusetts Bay Colony. (8367)(8368) He moved in 1637 to Lynn, Essex, MA.(8369) (8370) Harold Spencer (357) says 
2 CONC the move took place "soon after 1631 and in 1634 he was a Journeyman in Hartford whereas as Jack Spencer (600) say the move took place in 1637 (600, page 213) and says he had been grated land in Cambridge in 1635. He goes on to de
2 CONC scribe a "great turmoil" which occurred at Cambridge in the mid 1630s during which nearly ALL of the population removed to Hartford under Reverend Thomas Hooker and sold their property to followers of Reverend Thomas Shepard. The 
2 CONC move to Lynn allowed Gerard to increase his property from 4 to 30 acres. He took the oath of a freeman on 9 Mar 1637 in Lynn, Essex, MA. (8371)(8372) He granted 30 acres in 1638 in Lynn, Massachusetts Bay Colony. (8373) He served 
2 CONC as a made Ensign of trainband in 1638 in Lynn, Massachusetts Bay Colony.(8374) He Gerard acts as financer: loans neighbors money and signs bonds as security between 1639 and 1644 in Lynn, Massachusetts Bay Colony.(8375) In Sep 163
2 CONC 9, Gerard signed a bond (together with Joseph Armitage) to provide security to Daniel Salmon. Before Feb 1644, Gerard loans money to Abraham Belkap, who notes the debt in his will. He was a Ferry boat keeper between Mar 1639 and 1
2 CONC 9 Mar 1641 in Lynn, Essex, MA.(8376) (8377) (8378)(8379) On the first date he is awarded a license to operate a ferry at Lynn for two years. It operated between Needham's Landing in Lynn and Biards landing in Saugus. Also in 1639,
2 CONC  the General Court allocates £50 towards and builds a bridge across the Saugus River. He appeared in court between 1641 and 1659 in Lynn, Massachusetts Bay Colony.(8380) During this period, Gerard appeared in court many times.  16
2 CONC 41: Testified as to the indent of William Ballard, deceased, regarding his estate.  1643: Testified that a neighbor woman spoke contemptuously about infant baptism.  1643: Testified that Joseph Armitage sold wine without a license
2 CONC .  1643: Testified that he saw Edward Richard drunk.  1643: Awarded £3 and a "dead beast" in a case involving the premature death of a cow.  1643: Awarded damages from men who let their cattle graze in his fields.  1639: Served on
2 CONC  jury  1652: Served on jury  1653: Awarded small estate of brother Michael, deceased, for raising Michael's children.  1657: Served on jury  1658: Served on jury  1659: Served on jury He hired Watertown and London attorneys to col
2 CONC lect his 50£ inheritance left by uncle Richard of London in 1648. (8381) He moved in 1659 to Hartford, Hartford, CT. (8382)(8383) Genealogist Ruth Ellsworth Richardson calls Gerard "the founder of Hartford, Conn.," however, this s
2 CONC eems to be an error. Genealogist Jack Spencer seems certain that Gerard did not arrive until in Hartford until 1659 or 1660, where his BROTHER Thomas SPENCER had been living for about 24 years. By then, his siblings in Lynn had di
2 CONC ed. Gerald was among only 28 men who founded and sellted in Haddam. The other men included He Purchased a number of Hartford-area properties between 1659 and 1664 in Hartford, Connecticut Colony. (8384) In 1659, he purchased 9 par
2 CONC cels from John Norton for £122, but had to sign a bond of indebtedness (IOU) to obtain the property. John lost the note but reported that it was paid for by 1664. The 9 parcels ranged in size from 1 to 30 acres and one included a 
2 CONC house and barn. He took the oath of a freeman in 1660 in Hartford, Connecticut Colony. (8385) In this year made "an inhabitant" of Hartford" (I assume this means freeman). He appeared in court in 1660 in Hartford, Connecticut Colo
2 CONC ny.(8386) Simon Lobdell sued Gerard and his daughter Hannah (age 19) because she refused to marry Lobdell as she promised. I do not know the outcome. He moved in 1662 to Haddam, Middlesex, CT.(8387) (8388)(8389) (8390)(8391) Accor
2 CONC ding to Jac
