1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/175464318/edmund_underwood
2 CONT
2 CONT PARENTAGE, BIRTH, & CHRISTENING:
2 CONT Edmund UNDERWOOD, Grocer and Citizen of London, was the 7th child and 6th son of George UNDERWOOD, Gentleman of Weston (1541-1596) and his only wife Alice PAPWORTH, of Hitchin (c.1542-1607), who had been previously wed to William 
2 CONC BROCKET, II, of Hitchin, to whom she bore 2 sons, who were raised with the UNDERWOOD children in Weston. Edmund was born 9 Feb 1576/77 in Weston, Herts, and died on or about 11 Jun 1631 in his home [called "The Woolsack"] in the c
2 CONC entral east end of old Bucklersbury [Lane], London. He was christened on 10 Feb 1576/77 at Holy Trinity, the parish church of Weston, and the Parish ledger of Baptisms also record his actual birthdate. Edmund had several namesakes
2 CONC , starting with his mother's paternal grandfather Edmund PAPWORTH of Hitchin (c.1480-1540); and two uncles, Edmond UNDERWOOD (1543-c.1599?) and Edmund PAPWORTH, II (1545-____).
2 CONT
2 CONT DEATH & BURIAL:
2 CONT He was buried in old St. Christopher-Le-Stocks, on 22 Jun 1631, although it is this researcher's personal hypothesis that he was actually buried on 12 Jun 1631, and either one of two things occurred to produce the recorded date as
2 CONC  22 June rather than 12 June: 1) The Rector's or Vicar's original notebook of events (Baptisms, Marriages, & Burials, etc.) was mis-read when copied over to the official parish church records; or 2) Edmund was buried on the 12th, 
2 CONC but due to his prominence in the Grocer's Company of London, he may have had an official funeral service in the church with its pomp and regalia some ten days later. The truth may never be known if the Rector's original personal n
2 CONC otebooks are not extant to compare with the original parish records. But the fact that Edmund's will was probated on 14 June makes it certain that he died before then, and either way it is parsed, an actual burial 8 or 10 days lat
2 CONC er would have been unusual due to a probable lack of embalming---though there is also a very remote possibility that he was very expensively embalmed, so as to actually preserve his body in the June heat of the City a good 8 or 10
2 CONC  days. However, there seems to be nothing in his last wishes that reflects such an elaborate burial or funeral plan, and certainly nothing that his last will and testament says about it. The Rector of St. Christopher-Le-Stocks in 
2 CONC June 1631 was Rev. John MacArness, who, on 14 June, was present at the Probate of Edmund's will, and personally oversaw the swearing in of Edmund's Executrix---Rose UNDERWOOD, his widow---sworn on the Holy Gospels that she would f
2 CONC aithfully administer her duties as Executrix of her late husband's will. Doubtless, he conducted the burial service for Edmund just days earlier.
2 CONT
2 CONT MARRIAGE & EARLY APPRENTICESHIP:
2 CONT Edmund UNDERWOOD (Sr.), married Rose FAIRCLOUGH, of Fairclough Hall in Weston, Herts, on 18 Feb 1601/02 in Weston. Rose was born circa Sep 1581 at Fairclough Hall in Weston, but was christened 17 Sep 1581 at St. Mary's, Goldington
2 CONC , Co. Bedford, as was tradition in this family. She was the 6th child and 5th daughter of John FAIRCLOUGH (1544-1630) and Anne SPENCER (1543-1634) of the famous SPENCERs of Cople, Bedfordshire, kin to the SPENCERs of Althorp in No
2 CONC rthamptonshire, and Wormleighton in Warwickshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT They moved to London, where Edmund was apparently already apprenticed as a Grocer and apothecary, and was just beginning to be established in the Grocer's Company in his own right. In fact, in Edmund's father George's last will an
2 CONC d testament, dated 16 Oct 1596 in Weston, Herts., he mentions his son Edmund still being in his Apprenticeship, though he never mentions the place. At that time, Edmund would have been 19 years old. A deeper investigation into the
2 CONC  records of the Grocers' guild in London would likely reveal the details of Edmund's apprenticeship, including the date he was made a freeman, which seems likely to have been around 1599.
2 CONT
2 CONT CHILDREN:
2 CONT Rose bore Edmund 15 known children, the summary list given here: Rose, John [1st], Edmund, Millicent, William, John [2nd], George, Thomas, Jacob, Anne, Elizabeth, Lawrence, Abigail, Benjamin, & Abraham UNDERWOOD.
2 CONT
2 CONT In more detail, here are the 15 children, most of whom lived to adulthood (all christened at St. Stephen Walbrook, London [abbreviated StSW], except where noted): …
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT >V) William UNDERWOOD, b: c. Dec 1606 - [Shorne?,] Co. Kent? (?during one of London's plague years); chr: 19 Apr 1612-StSW (5-1/2 years after his birth, possibly to correct the oversight of skipping it earlier); m: Margaret DEWE (
2 CONC c.1605-aft.1673, dau. of Thomas DEWE, London Stationer, & Annie HELMES) c.1624-London? [Margaret m:2) LtCol. John UPTON, c.1642; m:3) Thomas LUCAS]; d: betw. 1637-1642-Isle of Wight Co., VA?. For a variety of reasons, a good case 
2 CONC is made that this is the William UNDERWOOD that is the best known fit for the identity of the William who settled in Isle of Wight Co., VA, and began one of the UNDERWOOD lines in America. It is ultimately believed that the UNDERW
2 CONC OOD family grocery business in London needed a forward tobacco buyer in the New World (and eventually a tobacco planter and supplier), and this William, being the 2nd oldest surviving son of Edmund, was perhaps asked to be this ke
2 CONC y person in the business, or he just volunteered at the opportunity. William & Margaret had 6 known children: …
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT BROTHER AS BUSINESS PARTNER:
2 CONT Edmund's younger brother Robert UNDERWOOD married Rose's younger sister Dorothy FAIRCLOUGH, and these brothers became full business partners in the grocery business in Bucklersbury in the heart of the old City of London. Dorothy a
2 CONC lso bore Robert 15 known children.
2 CONT
2 CONT HISTORIC LONDON RESIDENCE:
2 CONT These two families, with a combined 30 children (though not all at once, with a few infant deaths and timely marriages), shared two large, adjoining houses in Bucklersbury, as well as the use of some large warehouses which apparen
2 CONC tly were comprised of a few cavernous large rooms in the actual house complex. These houses and this property sat on the north side of Bucklersbury lane and the west side of the Walbrook, at about where Bucklersbury takes a slight
2 CONC  angle northwest and Soper's Lane departs to the west at St. Benet Sherehog's churchyard. Of course, all was destroyed in the Great Fire of London in Sep 1666, and the entire parcel of land is now under the pavement of Queen Victo
2 CONC ria Street
2 CONT
2 CONT These two adjoining houses incorporated a large and very historic stone and mortar building at their center, which was literally the old renowned "Cernet's Tower" or "Servat's Tower", built in 1305 by William SERVAT (or CERVAT), w
2 CONC hich was where the King's Royal Exchequer was established, and where gold coin was minted and housed during the reign of Kings Edward II & III and afterward, in the 1300's. It is the origin of the London financial district, first 
2 CONC in Bucklersbury among the grocers, spicers, and apothecaries, and eventually shifting a block eastward to the adjoining Stocks Yard, where eventually would be built the Stocks Market, the London Exchange, the Bank of England, and 
2 CONC the Mansion House. William SERVAT died in 1317/18, and Edward II granted Queen Isabella Servat's Tower and its houses to live in for her lifetime, which lasted long into the reign of King Edward III, in whose day these premises we
2 CONC re also known as "The Queen's Tower". Ultimately, the upper part of the stone and crenellated tower either was torn down or was built up around it by extensions of the building itself.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Underwood brothers' time, these houses were probably 4 or 5 stories above the ground, and likely had 2 basement levels. The ground floor of at least one of these houses, at street level, served as their grocery store front.
2 CONC  The same floor of the other house was likely connected by large door openings and may have been an extension of the store space, or may have served as a stock room, and each of these may have held a partitioned kitchen space, con
2 CONC nected upstairs with their own respective staircases. The cellars likely served as warehousing space, and the upper floors served as dining, living, and sleeping quarters. These were common arrangements in these houses---built in 
2 CONC the early 1400's probably, if not earlier---and these were the domain of the lady of the house, and these two sisters seem to have run their houses well.
2 CONT
2 CONT The combined business space of these two adjoining houses was called "The Woolsack", and had been purchased by the Underwood brothers in 1616 from Mary VESEY, widow of Thomas, a fellow Grocer, and had before that belonged to Mary'
2 CONC s brother John BODENHAM (a.k.a. BODNAM) and the BODENHAM family for about 80 years. The name harkens back to the days when the business dealt in the livestock and wool trade, which was centered in London just a few hundred feet N/
2 CONC E of "The Woolsack", in the Stocks Yard, almost at the geographic center of the old City of London. Edging the south side of this open space was the church of St. Mary Woolchurch Haw, next to which was London's great wool scales. 
2 CONC In the far S/E corner of the yard and a few dozen feet down Lombard Street was the parish church of St. Mary Woolnoth, demonstrating the once-common use of the word "wool" in many of the nearby locales.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Mansion House---traditional home of the Lord Mayor of London since about 1739---now occupies the entire south side of the old Stocks Yard. The parish church of St. Christopher-Le-Stocks, and the old London Stock Exchange was o
2 CONC n the north and N/E side of the Yard, showing how the name "Stocks" also was common in this area. The trading of livestock gave way to trading in other interests, and eventually to a pure financial trading in "stocks" and bonds, e
2 CONC tc. In the heart of the old city, this area became the center of London's financial district. The Bank of London---eventually the Bank of England---was established here in 1694.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Will
2 CONT
2 CONT WILL, PROBATE and POST MORTEM INQUISITIONS:
2 CONT Edmund's Will, dated 21 Mar 1626, was probated on 14 Jun 1631. Post-Mortem Inquisitions in Counties Middlesex and Kent, reveals some of the extensive property holdings of Edmund UNDERWOOD and his widow Rose [nee FAIRCLOUGH], inclu
2 CONC ding his Bucklersbury house; Halliwick Hall in Friern Barnet, northern Middlesex; land in East Greenwich parish, western Kent; and extensive lands and houses in Shorne, Kent. Other known properties were in Moreton, South Riding, Y
2 CONC orks, and at least one other place in Yorkshire; these had been held in join ownership with Edmund's business partner, his younger brother Robert UNDERWOOD.
2 CONT
2 CONT Here is the full text of Edmund's Will, originally written in the English of the period of King James I, but here given using modern spelling and paragraph breaks, but otherwise the same (I also can provide the exact transcript of
2 CONC  the original spellings). Here also is the Probate of his Will, in its original abbreviated Latin, expanded Latin, and English translation of the expanded Latin:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Probate of the Last Will of Edmund UNDERWOOD , Citizen & Grocer of London, 14 June 1631, Prerogative Court of Canterbury
2 CONT
2 CONT [Original, abbreviated Latin Text]
2 CONT
2 CONT Probatum fuit testum suprascr. =
2 CONT apud London coram vene'bil's Dm'o Henrico Marten milite legum Dco
2 CONT Curie Prerogative Cantuaren' Custode sive Comissarus l'time constituto decimo quarto
2 CONT die Mensis Juny anno Dm' mill'imo sex centesimo tricesimo primo Iuramento Rose UNDER //
2 CONT WOOD Relicte Dicti Defuncti et Executrice in hu'mod' testam'o nominat' Cui' comissa fuit ad'co
2 CONT omni' et singuloru' bonoru' Iuriu' et creditorum eiusdam defuncti de bene et fideli' administrando x
2 CONT eadem ad sancta Dei Evangelia coram Mag'ro Johanes Macarnesse Rectore Ecclis paroch'lis x
2 CONT s.ti Christofori prope le Stocks London vig're Comissionis ea p'te al's emanate iurate / ex'd
2 CONT
2 CONT [Expanded or unabbreviated Latin Text]
2 CONT
2 CONT Probatum fuit testamentum suprascriptum apud London, coram venerabilis Domino Henrico Marten Milite Legum Doctore,
2 CONT Curie Prerogative Cantuariensis Custode sive Comissarus legitime constituto decimo quarto die Mensis Juny Anno Domini millesimo sex centesimo tricesimo primo, Iuramento Rose UNDERWOOD Relicte Dicti Defuncti et Executrice in huiusm
2 CONC odi testamento nomniatorum, Cuibus Comissa fuit Administracio omnium et singulorum bonorum Iurium et creditorum eiusdam defuncti, De bene et fideliter Administrando eadem, ad sancta dei Evangelia coram Magistro Johanes Macarnesse 
2 CONC Rectore Ecclisiasticus parochialis sancti Christofori prope le Stocks London vigore Comissionis ea parte alias emanate Jurate. / Examinatur
2 CONT
2 CONT [English translation]
2 CONT
2 CONT The above-written will was proved at London, before the Worshipful Sir Henry Marten, Knight, Doctor of the Laws, Keeper or Commissary of the Prerogative Court of Canterbury, lawfully constituted on the fourteenth of the month of J
2 CONC une in the year of Our Lord one thousand six hundred thirty one, by the oath of Rose Underwood, widow of the said Deceased and Executrix named in the same Testament, to whom was granted administration of all and singular of the go
2 CONC ods, rights and credits of the same deceased, for well and faithfully to administer the same, she being sworn on the Holy Gospels before Master John MacArness Rector of the parish Church of St. Christopher in Le Stocks, London, by
2 CONC  the power of a Commission in that part otherwise issued. / Examined.
2 CONT
2 CONT +++++++++++++++++++++++++
2 CONT
2 CONT [This biography is a work in progress....]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT # https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/175464318/edmund_underwood
2 CONT ## comment: in 1867, the remains of those in St Christopher's churchyard were moved to Nunhead Cemetery in South East London. In the 1933, further relocations were made in the cemetery. Today, the churchyard no longer exists.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/175712620/alice-underwood
2 CONT
2 CONT George UNDERWOOD Sr. and Alice (PAPWORTH) left an impressive number of immediate descendants, the total of which may never be known. Here are the brief family groups of their 7 sons and 4 daughters (all those 11 children were born
2 CONC  and christened at Weston).
2 CONT
2 CONT I.) George UNDERWOOD, Jr. (chr: 1 Apr 1565, d: May 1617); m: Jul 1589 at Baldock, to Grace SPURLING (b: c.1570-Baldock, d: 1644; dau. of Henry SPURLING Sr. & Joan GLANFIELD; who m: 2nd, on 28 Dec 1641 to Rev. John HOPKINS of Berkh
2 CONC amstead St. Mary's, Herts). George UNDERWOOD, Jr. inherited the UNDERWOOD lands in Weston, Herts. George Jr. & Grace had 11 known children:
