2 CONT Sparkman Family Branch
2 CONT Early Sparkmans of Virginia
2 CONT 1635-1650
2 CONT Last updated 30 Jan 2000
2 CONT ________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: underlining is by the editor to highlight members of the Sparkman families.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Sparkman
2 CONT Pattened land in Virgina, 1635 brought wife & 2 daughters over in 1635 "Early Immigrants" - Greer, 1623-1666 North Carolina State Library, Raleigh, N.C. John Spackman (Sparkman) 300 acres, Warrasquinoake, County 13 July 1635, p. 2
2 CONC 28, 2 miles up on west side of Pagan Point Creek, 100 acres for per adv. of himself and Dorothy Spackman, his wife, and 200 acres for trans of his children, Joyce and Rosemon and two servants. John Evans, John Wilsonn. Land Grant 
2 CONC to John Moone, Gentleman near land of John Spackman & Captain John Upton dated 20 Nov 1642
2 CONT Note: During the first century fifty acres were granted for the importation of every emigrant. The names of these "head-rights" generally appear under the grants.
2 CONT Abstracts of Virginia Land Patents, page 180
2 CONT John Sparksman, 300 acres in the county of Warrasquioake, lying two miles up Pagan Point Creek on the west side, due 50 acres for the personal adventure of his wife Dorothy, and 200 for two of his children, Joyce and Rosamond Spar
2 CONC kman, and two servants, Jno. Evans and Jno. Wilson. granted July 13, 1635.
2 CONT Note: John's name given as Sparksman, but children's names as Sparkman!
2 CONT Abstracts of Virginia Land Patents, page 502
2 CONT (209) John Sparkman, 300 acres in the county of Warrosquoiacke, lying two miles up Pagan Point Creek[1] on the west side - due 50 acres for the personal adventure of his wife Dorothy, and 200 for two of his children, Joyce and Ros
2 CONC amond Sparkman, and two servants, Jno. Evans, and Jno. Wilsoon. By West July 13th, 1635.
2 CONT Note: spellings have been preserved as presented
2 CONT Note [1]: Now called Pagan Creek, a navigable streem on which the town of Smithfield is situated. (pg 527)
2 CONT Note [2]: Warrosquoiacke was changed to Isle of Wright.
2 CONT (Believe he may tie into the Sparkmans of Bristol, England)
2 CONT ________________________________________
2 CONT Richard Sparkman
2 CONT From land grant to Leift. John Upton 7 Jul 1635 pg 210 Warresquioake County, Virgina for transportation of 33 persons including Richard "Spackman". Not claimed until 25 aug 1637.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abstracts of Virginia Land Patents, page 180
2 CONT John Upton, 1650 acres in the county of Warrosquoiacke, about three miles up Pagan Point Creek. Due for the importation of 33 persons (names) below: Rich. Young, Antho, a negro, Mary, a negro, Florence Richards, Roger Bagnal, Ralp
2 CONC h Harwood, Thomas reeves, Rich. Sparkman, Edwd. Burr, Savage Nerrie, Wm. Scott, Rich. Jones, Fr. Savage, Owen Howell, Nich. Bushell, James Parsons, Jon. Parker, Lewis Phillips, Morgan Roerts, Wm. Davis, John Fitchett, Morgan Evans
2 CONC , Christopher Lewis, Phillip Kennesley, Eliz. King, Martha Swann, Mary Johnson, Jonas Sadlington, Anth. Tyler, Peter Heyes, Rich. Jackson, Wm. Pincher, Eliz. Larkin. Granted July 7, 1635.
2 CONT Abstracts of Virginia Land Patents, page 557
2 CONT (194) John Upton [1], 1,650 acres in the County of Warrosquoiake, about three miles up Pagan Point Creek. Due for the importation of 33 persons (names below). By John West, July 7, 1635. Rich. Young, Antho., a negro, Mary, a negro
2 CONC , Florence Richards, Roger Bagnal, Ralph Harwood, Thomas reeves, Rich. Sparkman, Edwd. Burr, Savage Nerrie, Wm. Scott, Rich. Jones, Fr. Savage, Owne Howell, Nich. Bushnell, James Parsons, Jon. Parker, Lewis Phillips, Morgan Robert
2 CONC s, Wm Davis, John Fitchett, Morgan Evans, Christopher Lewis, Phillip Kennusley Eliz. King, Martha Iwan, Mary Johnson, Jonas Sadlington, Anth. Tyler, Peter Heyes, Rich. Jackson, Wm. Pincher, Eliz. Larkin.
2 CONT Note. [1] Captain John Upton was long one of the leading men of Worrosquoiacke, or Isle of Wight county, as it soon was called. He was a member of the House of Burgesses March, 1629-30; commissioner (justice) of Warrosquoiacke in 
2 CONC November, 1627, and again from February, 1631-2; Burgess February, 1632-3, January 1639 (Robinson's notes), April 1642, November, 1645, March 1645-6, and November, 1647 (Hening I). At the session of 1645 the Assembly provided for 
2 CONC a mint, and enacted that "Captain John Upton is hereby confirmed Mint Master Generall. Wee reposing much confidence in his care, ability and trust for the preformance of said office" (Hening I, 209). April 13th, ...
2 CONT ________________________________________
2 CONT Nath. Sparkman
2 CONT Land Grant to Capt. Thomas Osborne, 1000 acs. Henrico Co. 6 Feb 1637 p. 519 for transport of 20 persons including Nath. Spackman
2 CONT ________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicho. Sparkman
2 CONT Immigrant to Virginia 1650 by Capt. Moore, Fauntleroy County, Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT (Genealogical tradition (re Castleberry) suggests that John, Richard, and Nicho. Sparkman may have been with the group that floated down the Chowan river into North Carolina in 1650.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Research Note: Parallel research in England suggests that most of the Sparkman families were concentrated around Hampshire County, England and in specific the town of Southport. Captain John Upton was the most important man in the
2 CONC  picture, and paid for Richard to immigrate. This sugests that the Upton line might lead us to where in England these people were coming from
2 CONT __________________________________________________.
