1 NOTE He was killed by Indians
1 NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Colonel
2 CONT John Catlett, the great-great grandfather of President James Madison, was the first white man to explore and set foot on the summit of the Blue Ridge Mountains.  He was born 1625 in Sittingbourne, Kent, England to John Catlett, Ye
2 CONC  Younger (1599-1624) and Sarah Hawkins (b.1600).  He was christened March 12, 1625 in Canterbury and married his first wife before immigrating to America, she died before 1663.
2 CONT
2 CONT He immigrated to America in the 1640's (1653 at the latest) and brought over Nicholas, the oldest son from his first marriage, to Virginia in 1650.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Elizabeth Underwood, born 1632in Isle of Wight, Virginia, the widow of Captain Francis Slaughter and daughter of Captain William Underwood and Margaret ?,  prior to January 5, 1663 in Rappahannock Co. Virginia.  In 1666
2 CONC  he was granted 500 acres on the North side ofRappahannock River.  He became Presiding Justice of Rappahannock County and wasa planter for many years in Rappahannock County, which became Essex county.  He was also known to be a go
2 CONC od mathematician.  He was killed by Indians while defending a fort at Port Royal, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth remarried the following year to Reverend Amory Butler and died the following year May 7, 1673 in Rappahannock, VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT His home place was called "Green Hill" at the mouth of the GoldenVale Creek (now Essex Co.).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1668 Gov. Berkeley wrote to Maj. Gen. RobertSmith commanding his militia to destroy all Indians and sell women and children to slavery  to defray costs.  Response:  "We intend with the assistance of Almighty God, by the strengt
2 CONC h of our Northern part, utterly to destroy and eradicate without further incroachment then the "spoyles of our enemies".   Signed byJohn Catlett, John Weire, Thomas Goodrich, and Humphry Booth  (C&BF)
2 CONT
2 CONT Rev. Fras Doughtery refused to administer Holy Sacrament to Col. Catlett and Mr. Humphrey Booth in 1668-1669, stating "his conscience would not suffer him to so do."
2 CONT
2 CONT Earliest mention of Catlett family:  "Catts Place situated in Brenchley Parish, a mile northward from Brenchely Town, within the 100 of Twyford." The mansion was anciently residence of Hugh de Catte, from whom it acquired its name
2 CONC .His descendants possessed it until reign of Henry VI (1442).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1464 a Johncatelot bequeathed 4d to Bearsted Church in County Kent.  In 1499 the Will ofJohn Catlott bequeathed 20s to repair Bearsted Church. (Info appears in Hx of Kent Co.)  In 1469 Lawrence Catlote of parish of Great Chart 
2 CONC and wife Joane mentioned.  In 1562 William Catlett levied fine of his estate at Sittingbourne.  Hedied the following year (1563), survived by son Thomas.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his will Catlettdirected his estate in England to be devoted to the education of his childrenin that country.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Catlett was a surveyor, vestryman, soldier, and president of the court
2 CONT in Old Rappahannock County, Virginia. Catlett was literate as his one
2 CONT surviving letter now the property of The Colonial Williamsburg Foundation
2 CONT demonstrates. John was the son of John A Catlett, who at age 24 married Sarah
2 CONT Hawkins, age 19, 10 April 1623. In his last will and testament John Catlett
2 CONT devised to Sarah his wife two cherry gardens in Sittingbourne containing 4
2 CONT acres for her natural life. He stated if my wife is with child at the time
2 CONT of my decease then the two cherry gardens to child forever after the decease
2 CONT of my wife. If not with child the two cherry gardens to my brother Thomas
2 CONT Catlett and his heirs. Catlett went on to provide for his wife and the child
2 CONT in esse, with other provisions in his will. It is the cherry garden that is
2 CONT of interest because it is mentioned in two documents in Essex County,
2 CONT Virginia. These cherry gardens were the property of John2 Catlett in June
2 CONT 1701 whenhe gave his power of attorney to Jno Mumford in London, Gentl. My
2 CONT true & lawful Attorney to sell all my Estate & inheritances of land houseing
2 CONT orchards &appurtenances thereto belonging in Cittingbourne
