1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT === Joan "Joane" Allen
2 CONT *Born 1629 / 1633 in England
2 CONT * Died 1711 in Isle of Wight, Virginia
2 CONT * Daughter of	Capt. Arthur Allen, of Droitwich & Bacon's Castle and Alice (Tucker) Hardy
2 CONT * Wife of Robert Williamson Sr — married 1651 (to 1670) in Surry County, Colony of Virginia
2 CONT * Wife of Robert Burnett — married 1671 (to before 1679) in Isle of Wight, Isle of Wight, Virginia
2 CONT * Wife of Reuben Proctor — married 1691 (to 1711) in Isle of Wight County, Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT Extracts on 24 November 2024 from https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Allen-2416
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan Allen was born about 1629 in England. It is not certain when the family arrived in Virginia, however father Arthur Allen recorded a patent for 200 acres in James City County, 14 May 1649:
2 CONT
2 CONT >Lyeing bet. Lawnes & Chipoaks Crks., bounded N. upon land of Capt. Peirce now in possession of Edward Blane, E. towards Lawnes Cr. joyning with his Eastermost angle on the land of Mr. Rennolds. Trans. of 4 pers: Ailce Tucker [his
2 CONC  wife], Wm. Eyres, Wm. Mors, Thomas Rastill.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages & Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan married Robert Williamson Sr about 1652/54 probably in Surry County, Virginia, where she was living with her parents. Or the marriage may have taken place in Isle of Wight County where her new husband made his home. No record
2 CONC  has been found for their marriage. However, in his will dated 16 February 1669/70, Robert Williamson states his oldest son Robert is not yet age 18, indicating a birth after 1652. [2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Known children (named in will):
2 CONT
2 CONT # Robert Williamson
2 CONT # George Williamson
2 CONT # Arthur Williamson
2 CONT # Francis Williamson
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan married second Robert Burnett about 1671/72. On 2 November 1672 Burnett "as marrying the relict of Robert Williamson" gave bond for the settlement of Robert Williamson's estate.[2] They had one child, daughter Ann Burnett, bo
2 CONC rn about 1672/73. She died unmarried in 1729. Her will was dated 8 April 1729 and probated on 28 April 1729. It mentioned brother Francis Williamson and cousin Joseph Williamson.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan married third Reuben Proctor of Isle of Wight County. The marriage date is unknown. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1710 Joan's brother Major Arthur Allen named sister "Jane Proctor" in his will.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.encyclopediavirginia.org/Allen_Arthur_1608-1669
2 CONT
2 CONT Arthur Allen became an agent for tobacco merchants in Bristol, England, and arrived in Virginia '''during the 1640s.''' He amassed one of the largest plantations in Surry County by the 1660s. There, he built a three story brick ho
2 CONC use that reflected his status as one of the county's wealthiest men. Allen died in 1669; his house became known as Bacon's Castle after Nathaniel Bacon's followers occupied it during the 1676 insurrection.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT # https://www.encyclopediavirginia.org/Allen_Arthur_1608-1669
2 CONT # https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bacon%27s_Castle Soon after Surry County was formed in the English colony of Virginia in 1652, Arthur Allen built a high style Jacobean brick house in 1665 near the James River, where he and his wif
2 CONC e Alice (née Tucker) Allen lived. He was a wealthy merchant and a Justice of the Peace in Surry County. Allen died in 1669, but his son, Major Arthur Allen II, inherited the house and property. Major Allen was a member of the Virg
2 CONC inia House of Burgesses.
2 CONT # WikiTree contributors, "Joan (Allen) Proctor (1629-aft.1710)," WikiTree: The Free Family Tree, (https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Allen-2416 : accessed 25 November 2024). cites
2 CONT ## Nugent, Nell Marion, Abstracted and Indexed by. Cavaliers and Pioneers: Abstracts of Virginia Land Patents and Grants, 1623-1800. In Five Volumes. Richmond, VA.: Press of the Dietz Printing Co., 1935. page 187
2 CONT ##WILLS AND ADMINISTRATIONS OF ISLE OF WIGHT COUNTY, VIRGINIA, 1647-1800, BLANCHE ADAMS CHAPMAN, Smithfield, Virginia, 1938, Page 9
2 CONT ## Boddie, John Bennett. Historical Southern Families. Copyright John Bennett Boddie, 1959. Reprinted for Clearfield Company, Inc. by Genealogical Publishing Co., Inc. Baltimore, Maryland, 1993, 1994. Ancestry Image, Volume III, p
2 CONC age 160.
2 CONT ##Boddie, John Bennett. Southside Virginia Families. in 2 volumes. Redwood City, Calif., Pacific Coast Publisers, 1955-Vol. 1, page 2
2 CONT ## “Isle of Wight County Records.” The William and Mary Quarterly, vol. 7, no. 4, Omohundro Institute of Early American History and Culture, 1899, pp. 205–315, https://doi.org/10.2307/1919740. Pages 234, 237
2 CONT ## WILLS AND ADMINISTRATIONS OF ISLE OF WIGHT COUNTY, VIRGINIA, 1647-1800, BLANCHE ADAMS CHAPMAN, Smithfield, Virginia, 1938, Page 9
2 CONT ## WILLS AND ADMINISTRATIONS OF ISLE OF WIGHT COUNTY, VIRGINIA, 1647-1800, BLANCHE ADAMS CHAPMAN, Smithfield, Virginia, 1938, Page 12
2 CONT ## WILLS AND ADMINISTRATIONS OF ISLE OF WIGHT COUNTY, VIRGINIA, 1647-1800, BLANCHE ADAMS CHAPMAN, :Smithfield, Virginia, 1938 Page 15
2 CONT ## “Isle of Wight County Records.” The William and Mary Quarterly, vol. 7, no. 4, Omohundro Institute of Early American History and Culture, 1899, pp. 205–315, https://doi.org/10.2307/1919740. Pages 239-240
2 CONT ## WILLS AND ADMINISTRATIONS OF ISLE OF WIGHT COUNTY, VIRGINIA, 1647-1800, BLANCHE ADAMS CHAPMAN, Smithfield, Virginia, 1938, Page 20
2 CONT ## WILLS AND ADMINISTRATIONS OF ISLE OF WIGHT COUNTY, VIRGINIA, 1647-1800, BLANCHE ADAMS CHAPMAN, Smithfield, Virginia, 1938, Page 88
