2 CONT Subject: Re: Philemon Bird, Sr. Author: PatrickSaturday13 Date: Tuesday, April 17, 2007 Classification: Query Surnames: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Philemon Bird, Sr. was born around 1740, in Middlesex County, Virginia. He was one of seven known children born to Robert Bird and Adicia "Dicey" Williamson.
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 2, 1762, Philemon married Mary Lee in Middlesex County, Virginia. Mary, the daughter of George Lee and Mary Buford, was born April 22, 1747. The Last Will and Testament of Mary's father, George Lee, reveals that the Le
2 CONC e's resided in Christ Church Parish in Middlesex County. Beneficiaries listed are George's wife, Mary, and children James, Elizabeth, Mary, Dorothy, Rachel, and Easter. Items mentioned include pots, pewter, slaves, furniture, hors
2 CONC es, and two unnamed plantations, causing one to believe that George was a fairly wealthy person. The Will, which may be read in its entirety on page 00, was presented in court on August 2, 1757. This relation was obviously the beg
2 CONC inning of the widespread use of the names George Lee and Buford within the Bird Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Philemon reportedly served as a captain in the Virginia Infantry during the Revolutionary War in 1776. In the early 1780's, we find Philemon residing in King & Queen County, Virginia. In a book entitled "Virginia Tax Payers" by Fo
2 CONC thergill, Philemon is listed in the year 1782 in King & Queen County, Virginia, and has fourteen slaves.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the first United States Federal Census of Prince Edwards County, Virginia, which was taken in 1785, Philemon is listed as the head of a family of eleven "white souls", which would probably have been Philemon, Mary, and the youn
2 CONC ger nine of their twelve known children. The Census also shows Philemon as the owner of one "dwelling" and four "other buildings", probably a rather large estate. Philemon's brother, Williamson, can also be found as the head of th
2 CONC irteen "white souls", one "dwelling", and four "other buildings".
2 CONT
2 CONT Philemon and wife, Mary, had twelve known children - Job (c. 1764), Lee (1765), Philemon Jr. (c. 1767), Mary "Molly" (1769), Susanna Diana (1771), Robert (1772), Williamson (1774), James (1776), Catherine (1778), Buford (1780), El
2 CONC izabeth (1782), and George (1784).
2 CONT
2 CONT Williamson Bird, Philemon's older brother, purchased property in Wilkes County, Georgia in 1785, and presumably moved to Georgia shortly thereafter. The deed is recorded as Joseph Williams and wife, Frankey, of Wilkes Co., having 
2 CONC sold to Williamson Bird of Prince Edward County, Virginia, 400 acres of land on Upton's Creek, adjoining the property of Gideon Anderson and William Thorton. The transaction was recorded January 26, 1785.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philemon migrated southward to Wilkes County, Georgia, around the late 1780's, joining his brother, Williamson. The earliest record of Philemon's arrival in Wilkes County is recorded in Deed Book CC, and is dated January 1787. The
2 CONC  land transaction was recorded as William Bentley selling 300 acres of land to Philemon Bird. The land is described as 300 acres on the Little River, adjoining John Bell. Testators were listed as Daniel Price, Balaam Bentley, and 
2 CONC Job Bird.
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 16, 1802, the Creek Indians ceded their lands in Georgia in a treaty, which was signed at Fort Wilkinson. This land included "the Territory south of the Oconee and Altamaha rivers", and was divided into three separate coun
2 CONC ties - Wayne, Wilkinson, and Baldwin. The land was divided into various sized lots, depending on in which county they were located, and given away in lotteries to settlers who met specific qualifications. The Lottery of 1803, list
2 CONC s Philemon Bird, Sr. as qualifying for two draws of this lottery. To qualify for these two draws, Philemon was a free white male, twenty-one years or older, and resident of the state for at least one-year prior the passage of the 
2 CONC lottery act, who had paid tax; having a wife and one or more legitimate children. Philemon's brother, Williamson, is also listed as receiving two draws. Another Philemon Bird is listed as receiving two draws, and is probably Phile
2 CONC mon's son, Philemon Jr.
2 CONT
2 CONT A similar lottery in 1806 gave settlers of the same qualifications lots of 202.5 acres each. In this lottery, Philemon, Sr. received two draws for himself, and one draw for his deceased son, Philemon, Jr. Philemon, Sr. is listed a
2 CONC s being grandfather of the orphans of the deceased Philemon, Jr. Philemon, Sr.'s brother, Williamson, is once again listed near Philemon, and received two draws.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philemon lived out the rest of his days in and around Wilkes County, Georgia, owning, buying, and selling much property along the way. It is safely assumed that Philemon was a man of great wealth and prestige in the area. Some twe
2 CONC nty-three years after his arrival and settlement in Georgia, Philemon Bird, Sr. died. His exact death date is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 21, 1809, Philemon Bird set his hand to his Last Will and Testament in Wilkes County, Georgia. The Will simply lists Philemon's wife, Mary, as the sole beneficiary, and states that after her death, all properties are t
2 CONC o be divided among their children, which are listed as Dianna Evering, Lee Bird, Job Bird, Molly Ogletree, Robert Bird, Williamson Bird, James Bird, Caty Heard, Buford Bird, Elizabeth Jourdan, and George Bird. It also lists the he
2 CONC irs of the deceased Philemon Bird Junior. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Will, which may be read in its entirety on page 00, was presented in the Wilkes County Court on May 7, 1810. So we know that Philemon died sometime between November 21, 1809, and May 7, 1810. Mary Lee Bird died sometime after.
2 CONT
2 CONT -The Children of Philemon Bird, Sr. & Mary Lee-
2 CONT
2 CONT Job Bird was born c. 1764, presumably in Virginia. He is said to have married Judith Scott, and had children - Judia, Vashti, Job S., and Lee. Job settled in Wilkes County with his father's family, but apparently moved to Carroll 
2 CONC County, Georgia, where he died in 1818.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lee Bird was born September 13, 1765, in Virginia. He married Polly Caldwell on May 31, 1796, in Prince Edward County, Virginia, and later moved to Kentucky. Lee and Polly had three known children - John (1797), Sarah (1799), and 
2 CONC Philemon (1801), all born in Kentucky. Most researchers feel that after Polly's death, Lee married Nancy Ann Glass. Lee Bird died Mary 3, 1838, in Shelbyville, Kentucky (Shelby County).
2 CONT
2 CONT Philemon Bird, Jr. was born c. 1767, in Virginia. (Dot)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary "Molly" Bird was born March 18, 1769, in Virginia. She married William Ogletree c. 1785. William and Molly settled in Wilkes County, Georgia, probably around the same time that Philemon and Mary did. William and Molly had ele
2 CONC ven known children - Sarah Frances (1789), Philemon (1791), Nancy (1793), William (1795), John B. (1798), James B. (1800), Elizabeth (1803), Buford B. (1806), David (1809), Absalom (1811), and Mary (1814). Mary "Molly" Bird Ogletr
2 CONC ee died May 25, 1829, presumably in Wilkes County, Georgia, or nearby.
2 CONT
2 CONT Susanna Diana Bird was born c. 1771, in Virginia. She married Samuel Evering in Prince Edward County, Virginia, and had children. She is last found mention in her fathers Will in 1809. This family possibly settled in Kentucky.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Bird was born c. 1772, in Virginia. He married Elizabeth Matthews on October 16, 1792, in Wilkes County, Georgia. He was apparently dead by 1819, as Charles Smith is listed in Land Lottery records as being the "next friend"
2 CONC  to the orphans of Robert Bird.
2 CONT
2 CONT Williamson Bird was born c. 1774, in Virginia. No further information can be found to separate this Williamson from that of the other Williamson Bird's, brother and nephew to Philemon Bird, Sr. He is mentioned in his father's Will
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT James Bird was born c. 1776, in Virginia. No further information has been found regarding James. He is mention in his father's Will.
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherine Bird was born c. 1778, in Virginia. She married William Heard. She is last mentioned in her father's Will as "Caty Heard". No further information is available at this time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Buford Bird was born c. 1780, in Virginia. More information on his family comes later.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Bird was born c. 1782, in Virginia. She married a Jourdan and is last mention in her father's Will as Elizabeth Jourdan.
2 CONT
2 CONT George Bird was born c. 1784, in Virginia. No further information is available. He is mention in his father's Will.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
