1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Captain William Shelton==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Captain John Shelton and Jane Chilton 
2 CONT * William Shelton was born 1676 in Westmoreland County, Virginia, and died 1734 in York County, Virginia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Married==
2 CONT
2 CONT * He married (1): '''Hannah Armistead,''' on  10 Dec 1698 in Elizabeth, York Co., VA, daughter of Anthony Armistead and Hannah Ellison. She was born 1674 in Elizabeth, York, Virginia, and died Abt 1748. - https://www.geni.com/phot
2 CONC o/view/6000000006769168799?album_type=photos_of_me&photo_id=6000000083134768992&position=0
2 CONT * He married (2): '''Katherine Nutting,''' daughter of Thomas Nutting and Elizabeth Booth. She was born Abt 1680.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===notes===
2 CONT
2 CONT From the files of Terry Esvelt: There is an article in the William and Mary Quarterly, reproduced in "Genealogies o f Virginia Families, Vol. IV," Genealogical Publishing Co., 1982, p. 410, by Mrs. A.E. Whitaker entitled "The Shel
2 CONC tons." In it, she mentions that "In 1681 Wm. Shelton had a grant of 150 acres in Gloucester Co., Va., and on December 10
2 CONT 1698, he married Hannah Armistead, daughter of Capt. Anthony Armistead, who was born in Elizabeth City Co., in 1648. " She doesn't give the parentage of this William Shelton, but she does provide a great deal of information about 
2 CONC the Shelton family. The first emigrant was James Shelton, who came to Jamestown, Virginia with Lord Delaware in 1610. In addition, she states that according to Greer's "Early Immigrants," a Fr. Shelton and a Richard Shelton, two b
2 CONC rothers, came to Virginia in 1638. It's not known whether they were related to the first immigrant James Shelton.
2 CONT
2 CONT Plantation Currioman built by John Shelton in Westmoreland County, Virginia about the year 1660. In 1672, he bought another plantation called "Carotoman" in Lancaster County, Virginia. In the year 1670, he built a family home in H
2 CONC anover County, Virginia and called it "Rural Plains" which is still owned by make descendants. 
2 CONT
2 CONT These homes were inherited by John Shelton's children-- Capt. Thomas Shelton inherited "Currioman" and '''William Shelton''' inherited "Rural Plains". (Wikipedia) Rural Plains, also informally known as Shelton House, is a historic
2 CONC  farm house dating to the 1720s in Mechanicsville, Virginia, Hanover County; it is one of the sites included within the Richmond National Battlefield Park.[3] The building was added to the National Register of Historic Places in 1
2 CONC 975. - https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rural_Plains
2 CONT
