1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mordecai Cooke was born 1649 / ABT 1660 in , Gloucester Co., Virginia, and died 1718 in , Gloucester Co., Virginia. He was the son of Thomas Cooke and  Mary Hubbard 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married
2 CONT # Joan Constable died ABT 1704 in , Gloucester Co., Virginia.  She is said to have been a red headed Jewess.  
2 CONT # Elizabeth Buckner, daughter of Richard Buckner and Elizabeth Sampson. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Joan Constable and Mordecai Cooke is:
2 CONT * John Cooke was born ABT 1678 in "Wareham", Gloucester Co., Virginia, and died in "Wareham", Gloucester Co., Virginia. He married Ann Todd ABT 1700, daughter of Thomas Todd and Elizabeth Bernard. She was born 9 Nov 1682 in Ware P
2 CONC arish, Gloucester Co., Virginia, and died 18 Jul 1720 in "Wareham", Gloucester Co., Virginia. He married Mary Smith, daughter of John Smith Esq. and Elizabeth. She was born 14 Apr 1691 in Ware Parish, Gloucester Co., Virginia, and
2 CONC  died 15 Mar 1724 in "Wareham", Gloucester Co., Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Elizabeth Buckner and Mordecai Cooke are:
2 CONT # Elizabeth Cooke was born ABT 1712 in , Gloucester Co., Virginia, and died 1800 in Hamilton Parish, Fauquier Co., Virginia. She married William Pickett 1728 in Cople Parish, Westmoreland Co., Virginia, son of John Pickett and Mar
2 CONC y. He was born ABT 1700 in ,, Virginia, and died 1766 in Hamilton Parish, Fauquier Co., Virginia.
2 CONT # Jemima Cooke. She married Thomas Chilton 1723, son of John Chilton II. He was born 1699, and died 1765/1775 in , Fauquier Co., Virginia.
2 CONT # iii.   Mordecai Cooke was born circa 1718; he married Mary Hayes. 
2 CONT He resided at Gloucester Co., VA.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Supporting data
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonial Families of the Southern States of America: A History and Genealogy ...” (1911) By Stella Pickett Hardy.  “Cooke Family.”  Page 164-165.  <[https://books.google.com/books?id=e1VlAAAAMAAJ&lpg=PP19&ots=D8erhZ4kcW&dq=stella%
2 CONC 20picket%20hardy%20cooke%20family&pg=PA164#v=twopage&q&f=true GoogleBooks]>
2 CONT
2 CONT MORDECAI COOKE, of “Mordecai's Mount,” Gloucester Co., Va., was a staunch loyalist and devoted to his king and to the House of Stuart; a staunch and liberal supporter of the Established Church; the name of his wife is unknown, but
2 CONC  generally believed to have been of the Giles family, as that name is used in every branch of the family, His known issue:
2 CONT * 2-I. MORDECAI, b. about 1664; of whom later.
2 CONT * 2—2. Thomas, of "Warham," Gloucester Co., Va. He was appointed Surveyor of Gloucester and Middlesex, in 1707, by Col. Miles Cary, Surveyor General for William and Mary College. In his contract he agrees to give one-sixth of his 
2 CONC receipts to said College. In 1717 he was reappointed to the same position by Peter Beverly, the successor of Miles Cary; he m. Mary, of what family is unknown, and had issue.
2 CONT * 2—3. Giles, who was the Tobacco Agent in 1714. Tradition says that he was a member of the expedition under Gov. Spottswood, which first crossed the mountain and descended to the banks of the Shenandoah. Since this expedition too
2 CONC k place in 1716, it is possible that he may have been one of the “Knights of the Golden Horse Shoe," and was so charmed with the country that he remained in the valley. At all events, the Cookes and Cooks of Jefferson Co., W. Va.,
2 CONC  are descendants of the Cookes of Gloucester Co., and since we can find no descendants of Giles living in Gloucester later than 1714, it is safe to say that the Tobacco Agent was either the father or grandfather of this line of Co
2 CONC okes and Cooks, for some of the family have dropped the "E.” The said Giles m, unknown, and had issue.
2 CONT * 2—4. John, of “Beechley,” Gloucester Co., Va., m. (first) Ann Todd, b. 1682; d. 1720; dau. of Capt. Thomas and Elizabeth Todd, of “Toddsbury;" (second) Mary Smith, d. 1724; dau. of John and Elizabeth Smith, of Ware Parish, Glouc
2 CONC ester Co., Va., and had issue.
2 CONT * 2-5. Mary, d. 1723; m. Thomas Booth, of Gloucester Co., Va., b. 1663, in Lancashire, England; d. 1736, in Virginia. He was the progenitor of the family in America; he was the son of St. John Booth, of Barton, Lancashire, England
2 CONC , and had issue.
2 CONT * 246. Frances, m. 1690, Hon. Gabriel Throckmorton, of Ware Parish, Gloucester Co., Va., son of John and Frances (Mason) Throckmorton, and had issue.
2 CONT * 2—7. Susannah, m. Henry Fitzhugh, of King George Co., Va., son of Hon. William and Sarah (Tucker) Fitzhugh, of "Bedford," and had issue. (See Fitzhugh lineage.)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From [https://books.google.com/books?id=e1VlAAAAMAAJ&lpg=PA166&ots=D7alg-3mdY&dq=elizabeth%20scrosby%20mordecai%20cooke&pg=PA166#v=onepage&q=elizabeth%20scrosby%20mordecai%20cooke&f=false page 166] of Colonial Families of the Sout
2 CONC hern States of America: A History and Genealogy ... By Stella Pickett Hardy
2 CONT
2 CONT * HON. MORDECAI COOKE, (2.1), of Gloucester Co., Va., b. about 1664; served as High Sheriff of Gloucester, 1698; Justice of the Peace and a Burgess, 1702 to 1714. He m. Elizabeth Buckner. And had among other issue:
2 CONT * 3.1. Jemima, b. 1707; m. 1723, Thomas Chilton, of Cople Parish, Westmoreland Co., Va., son of John Chilton, of "Currioman," and had issue. (See Chilton lineage.)
2 CONT * 3.2. Elizabeth, b. 1712; m. 1728, William Pickett, of Hamilton Parish, Fauquier Co., Va., son of George and Ida (Martin) Pickett, of Westmoreland Co., Va., and had issue. (See Pickett lineage.)
2 CONT * 3.3. MORDECAI, b. about 1718; of whom later
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT * MORDECAI COOKE, (3.3), of Gloucester Co., Va., b. about 1718; he was educated at William and Mary College; he was a prominent, influential citizen in affairs of Church and State. He m. his cousin, Mary Booth, dau. of Thomas and 
2 CONC Mary (Cooke) Booth, of Gloucester Co., Va. Issue:
2 CONT * 4.1. MORDECAI, b. about 1755; of whom later. 
2 CONT *4.2. Giles, of Gloucester Co., Va., m. Miss Lawson, and had issue.
2 CONT *4.3 Thomas Booth, M. D., m. Christina Helen Meredith; his wid. m. (second) Mr. Morris.
2 CONT * 4.4. Elizabeth, m. Thomas Buckner, son of Baldwen M. Buckner, and had issue.
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT * COL. MORDECAI COOKE, (4.1), of "The Cedars," Gloucesterter Co., Va., b. about 1755, a prominent, influential citizen in affairs of Church and State; a staunch and liberal supporter of the Established Church; and a Burgess. He m.
2 CONC  (first) 1781, Elizabeth Scrosby, d. 1795; (second) Sarah Smith Cooke, dau. of Francis Whiting and Mary (Baytop) Cooke. Issue by first marriage:
2 CONT * 5.1 John, b. 1782; d. 1798. 
2 CONT * 5.2 MORDECAI; of whom later
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT COOKE, Mordecai, b 1785 was the son of Revolutionary War Lieutenant, Mordecai Cooke and Elizabeth Scrosby
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Thomas Cooke and Mary Hubbard.  Married Elizabeth Buchner
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Buckner, adopted son of Richard Buckner and Lydia Topping of Buckinghamshire England married ... Elizabeth Sampson, the daughter of Thomas Sampson and his first wife, Elizabeth Johnson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Buckner died before 7 May 1734 when the Inventory of his estate was submitted before distribution could take place. Richard's wife, Elizabeth, she died after 7 May 1734, and before 1735. Richard and Elizabeth had the follo
2 CONC wing children validated and proven:
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Buckner married Mordecai Cooke, son of Thomas Cooke and his wife, Mary Hubbard
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
