1 TITL Captain
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} married about 1685 in St. Stephens Parish, Northumberland, Virginia to Jane Presley 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://www.colonial-settlers-md-va.us/getperson.php?personID=I027907&tree=Tree1
2 CONT
2 CONT -- 1672: "Ursula Bysshe", book by Alice Algood, Part II, pg 57
2 CONT
2 CONT QUOTE: Seated on the court in August 1672 when Charles Ashton's estate was being settled were Major Thomas Brereton, who married Jane Claiborne, and Capt John Rogers whose son, Richard, married Jane Presly, daughter of Elizabeth T
2 CONC hompson Presly. Rogers and William Keene lived at Cherry Point, the same area as the Ashtons. William Keene married Elizabeth Rogers, who later married a Banks and whose Will is probated in Northumberland in 1720. [Northumberland 
2 CONC County Record Bk, 1718-16, pg 127 ] William Keene was the son of Thomas Keene (Keyne), a head-right of (Wm) Claiborne, who had been on the Isle of Kent with him. [ Garner-Keene Families of Northern Neck of Virginia, by Ruth Richee
2 CONC  & Sudie Rucker Wood.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Alice Allgood's book, "Ursula Bishe", pg. 91.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Rogers died in 1697. Will was proven 18 Aug 1697 by Mrs. Jane Rogers and Mr. Robert Banks, executors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Will: 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===== 
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Rogers' will was not duplicated in the book.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Jane Rodgers, b. 12 Apr 1686, Recorded St Stephens Parish.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard, b. abt 1688-90
2 CONT
2 CONT John, b. Abt 1693, Probably named for a deceased son of the same name.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hannah, b. abt 1695
2 CONT
2 CONT His children, Richard, John & Hannah were named on 20 May 1703, when a commission of administration was granted to Christopher Neale and Jane, his wife on the estate of Richard Rogers. (S: Jane Presley Rogers remarried shortly aft
2 CONC er Richard's death)
2 CONT
2 CONT None of the children listed as christened in St Stephens Parish records, as the children of Richard Rogers, were named in his will. They must have been born to a first wife, for Jane would have been too young. (S: Perhaps they had
2 CONC  their inheritance before his Will. This was common, though many recorded all children's names without mention of a bequest.)
