1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Richard Lee''' I (before about 1619 – 1664), later nicknamed "The Immigrant", was the first member of the Lee family to live in America; he emigrated from Shropshire, England to Virginia in 1639. Lee was a lawyer, planter, sold
2 CONC ier, politician, and Member of the Virginia House of Burgesses. By the time of his death, Lee was the largest landholder in Virginia, with 13,000 acres, and perhaps the richest man in Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Lee_I retrieved 18 May 2019
2 CONT
2 CONT Not long after his arrival he married '''Anne Constable''', a ward of the Governor, Sir Francis Wyatt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Lee I and his wife Anne raised eight children:
2 CONT * 1. '''John Lee''' (1643–1673) of "Mount Pleasant", who never married
2 CONT * 2. '''Col. Richard Lee II''' of "Paradise", "the Scholar" (1647–1715), who married Laetitia Corbin (c.1657–1706), daughter of Hon. Henry Corbin, Sr. (1629–1676) and Alice (Eltonhead) Burnham (c.1627–1684). Great-Great-grandfathe
2 CONC r of General Robert E. Lee.
2 CONT * 3. '''Francis Lee''' (1648–1714) a merchant in England, who married Tamar.
2 CONT * 4.'''Capt. William Lee''' (1651–1696), apparently never married.
2 CONT * 5. '''Capt. Hancock Lee''', Hon. (1653–1709) of "Ditchley", who married 1) Mary Kendall (1661–1694); 2) Sarah Elizabeth Allerton (1671–1731), daughter of Col. Isaac Allerton, Jr. (1630–1702) (son of Isaac Allerton of the Mayflow
2 CONC er) and (grandson of William Brewster of the Mayflower) and his second wife, Elizabeth (Willoughby) Grandfather of President Zachary Taylor.
2 CONT * 6. '''Elizabeth (Betsey) Lee''' (1654–1714), who married 1) Leonard Howson Sr (1648–1704); 2) John Turberville (1650–1728), son of George Turberville IV (1638-c.1659) and Bridget
2 CONT * 7. '''Anne Lee''' (1654–1701), who married Maj. Thomas Youell Jr (1644–1695), son of Thomas Youell (1615–1655) and Anne Sturman (d. 1672)
2 CONT * 8. '''Capt. Charles Lee''' Sr (1655–1701) of "Cobbs Hall", who married Elizabeth Medstand, daughter of Thomas Medstand (−1675). Ancestor of the Manning family of Eli Manning and Peyton Manning.
2 CONT
2 CONT His widow Anna (or Anne) married again to Edmund Lister before 24 September 1666.  The date of her death is unknown, although legend has it that she was buried beside Lee near the house at "Dividing Creeks".
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Will written 6 February 1663/4
2 CONT
2 CONT From  [http://www.thesocietyoftheleesofva.org/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=4&Itemid=543 Society of the Lees of Virginia] ([https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000093512392978& full text])
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Lee’s will directed that his property at Stratford, England be sold, and that the proceeds be used to discharge his debts, to complete the education of John and Richard at Oxford, and to provide dowries for his daughters, 
2 CONC Elizabeth and Anne. In Virginia, he left the Machodoc plantation to his son John, the Paradise plantation to Richard, "War Captain’s Neck" to Francis, and the Maryland plantation to William. The Dividing Creek plantation he left t
2 CONC o his widow for her lifetime and afterwards to be divided among his younger sons, William, Hancock, and Charles.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Disputed Origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents speculated as:
2 CONT * https://www.geni.com/people/John-Lee-Sr/6000000001672218645?through=6000000002700598061 and https://www.geni.com/people/Jane-Lee/6000000005388584166?through=6000000002700598061
2 CONT * https://www.geni.com/people/Richard-Lee-of-Coton-Hall/654803 and https://www.geni.com/people/Elizabeth-Lee/6000000002240763466
2 CONT * https://www.geni.com/people/Walter-Lee/6000000039227597870
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===English property
2 CONT
2 CONT 'West Ham: Introduction', in A History of the County of Essex: Volume 6, ed. W R Powell (London, 1973), pp. 43-50. British History Online http://www.british-history.ac.uk/vch/essex/vol6/pp43-50 [accessed 20 August 2021].
2 CONT
2 CONT > About 1700 there was a spurt of growth at Stratford. Defoe reported in 1722, no doubt with exaggeration, that it had more than doubled in size during the previous 20 or 30 years. He also stated that two new hamlets had grown up 
2 CONC on the forest side of the village, namely Maryland Point, on the Woodford Road, and the Gravel Pits on the Ilford Road. (fn. 35) Maryland Point is shown on a map of 1696. (fn. 36) The first house there is said to have been built b
2 CONC y a rich merchant who returned to England from Maryland. (fn. 37) Various attempts have been made to identify the merchant. The most likely candidate is Richard Lee (d. 1664), who emigrated to Virginia about 1640. (fn. 38) Among h
2 CONC is estates was land on the Maryland side of the Potomac river, near a place known in 1676 as Maryland Point. (fn. 39) He returned to England in 1658, and in 16589 bought properties at Stratford Langthorne. In 1662 he had a house t
2 CONC here with 9 hearths. (fn. 40) Whatever its origin, Maryland Point became a permanent place-name in West Ham. The Gravel Pits, the other new hamlet mentioned by Defoe, is not named on any map. (fn. 41) It was probably the settlemen
2 CONC t, north of Stratford Common (or Green), shown on later maps.
2 CONT
2 CONT >>38. W.H.L., File on Maryland Point. Much of the inf. on Ric. Lee comes from: Ludwell L. Montagu, 'Richard Lee the Emigrant', Virginia Magazine of Hist. and Biog. lxii (1954) 1 sqq., the writer of which does not, however, accept 
2 CONC the identification of Lee with the merchant mentioned by Morant.
2 CONT
