1 NOTE Col. Richard Henry Lee
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=4dbccda4-4990-4d74-9059-d949bb539d45&tid=904323&pid=-1144988936
1 NOTE !BIRTH - THE LEES OF VIRGINIA published by The Society of the Lees of Virginia
2 CONT Richard Lee was the grandson of John Lee of Coton Hall, lord of the manor of Nordley Regis in southeastern Shropshire where he was born perhaps in 1613, but as the son of a younger son, he had no prospects there.  His father, Rich
2 CONC ard, had only a life tenure in one of the farms of the manor.
2 CONT
2 CONT !CHRIS - ANCESTORS OF AMERICAN PRESIDENTS compiled by Gary Boyd Roberts, p.23
2 CONT Richard Lee was bp. Worcester St. Martin, Worcestershire 22 March 1617/8.
2 CONT
2 CONT At an early age he was sent to London to seek his fortune under the tutelage of his first cousin, John Lee, an established merchant in hides and pelts.
2 CONT Richard Lee came to Virginia in 1639 to be Clerk of the Quarter Court at Jamestown.
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR - Ibid
2 CONT Not long after his arrival he married Anna Constable, a ward of the Governor, Sir Francis Wiatt.  But his principal occupation in Virginia was as a factor for his cousin, John Lee of London, in the fur trade with the Indians.  He 
2 CONC took his young bride away from the colonial capital to live near Gloucester Point.  They must have narrowly escaped death in the Great Massacre of 1644, when some 500 English were slain by the Indians in a sudden onslaught.
2 CONT
2 CONT ANCESTORS OF AMERICAN PRESIDENTS compiled by Gary Boyd Roberts, p. 23
2 CONT He married Anne Constable abt. 1641, prob. in Jamestown, VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1652 onwards Richard Lee spent almost as much time in London as he did in Virginia becoming as much a London merchant trading to Virginia as he was a great planter in Virginia.  Disposing of several lesser properties, he cons
2 CONC olidated and developed 4 major plantations, two in Gloucester Co. ("War Captain's Neck" and "Paradise") and 2 in Northumberland ("Dividing Creek" and Machodoc").  He also acquired a plantation ("Lee's Purchase") in Maryland. - Ibi
2 CONC d
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1658 Richard Lee Acquired a residence at Stratford Langton, then a pleasant suburb of London, and in 1661 he moved his family thither.  He did that for the better education of his younger children - his 2 older sons, John and R
2 CONC ichard, were already students at Oxford - but he always intended that their future should lie in Virginia, except for Francis, who was designated to become a London merchant. - Ibid
2 CONT
2 CONT !DEATH - Ibid
2 CONT Richard Lee died at his Dividing Creek plantation in 1664 and his family returned to Virginia soon thereafter.  His will directed that his property at Stratford Langton be sold and that the proceeds be used to discharge his debts,
2 CONC  to complete the education of John and Richard at Oxford, and to provide dowries for his daughters, Elizabeth and Anne.  He left the Machodoc plantation to his son John, the Paradise plantation to Richard, "War Captain's Neck" to 
2 CONC Francis and the Maryland plantation to William.  The Dividing Creek plantation he left to his widow for her lifetime and afterwards to be divided among his younger sons, William, Hancock and Charles.
2 CONT
2 CONT ANCESTORS OF AMERICAN PRESIDENTS compiled by Gary Boyd Roberts, p. 23
2 CONT Richard Lee died at Cobbs Hall, Northumberland Co.,VA between 6 Feb. 1663/4 & 20 April 1664.
2 CONT Note:  I think the Cobbs Hall location is correct since he was burried at Cobbs Hall. - JMC
2 CONT
2 CONT !BURIAL - Ibid
2 CONT Richard Lee, the emigrant and Anna Constable, his wife are buried in the Cobbs Hall Burying Ground.
1 NOTE deposition May 1641 Norfolk Co gives age as 32, hence born 1608/9
2 CONT
2 CONT Depositoin London 1654 Admiralty Court. Richard Lee Gentleman age 34 or thereabouts deposes of things seen on York River in Virgina the prior January. (pub 1984) hence born 1618
2 CONT
2 CONT Coton Hall lies on main road between  Shrewsbury  and Brostol.  Along that road going south you find Coton Hall,  then Worcester, then  Twining. Starting from the south, Twining in Worcestershire is about  12 miles north of Worces
2 CONC ter, and it is another 20 miles from Worcester  to Coton Hall.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard baptized 22 march 1617/1618 ST Martin's  Worcester England
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Will says"of Virginia, late of Stratford-Langton in the County of Essex"
2 CONT this was part of the Abby of Stratford-Langthorne founded 1135 disbanded 1539 and Westsham Mannor or Parish was divided into four parts one of which was Stratford-Langton, or one ward of the parish/mannor.
2 CONT *****************
2 CONT Subj:Re: Lee
2 CONT Date:11/6/2005 9:08:33 AM Central Standard Time
2 CONT From: dnca2@earthlink.net Reply-to: dnca2@earthlink.net To: Jhlawr@wmconnect.com (Jhlawr@wmconnect.com)
2 CONT *****************
2 CONT New Research on the Wraysbury Deeds
2 CONT by Alan Nicholls.
2 CONT In 1999 I gave a presentation to the Lee Society of Virginia at their AGM concerning the English ancestry of Colonel Richard Lee, the founder of the Virginia Lee family. This presentation was placed on my webpage on 24th May 1999.
2 CONT In that presentation I included evidence that Colonel Richard Lee had signed a series of three indentures relating to a purchase by John Lee of Coton of a property at Wraysbury in Buckinghamshire, England in 1652. Colonel Richard'
2 CONC s signature on these deeds had been used by all previous researchers, with other evidence, to show a direct connection between the Lee family of Virginia and the Coton Lee family of Alveley, Shropshire. However since the 1999 pres
2 CONC entation my research has revealed evidence using only original contemporary documents that these indentures were in fact signed by Richard Lee, fishmonger, son of Lancelot Lee of Coton. This Richard was the apprentice of John Lee,
2 CONC  fishmonger, citizen of London and gentleman. I have shown previously that this John Lee (1600-1682), son of Thomas Lee of Coton (1560-1620) was mistakenly identified as a saddler by all earlier researchers.
2 CONT As I reported in the 1999 presentation the signatures on the Wraysbury deeds seemed to match extremely well with those few other original signatures of Colonel Richard Lee that I had found. He signed himself Rich: Lee in all cases
2 CONC  and I found these signatures the most persuasive evidence to authenticate his claim to a Coton ancestry. However this new evidence has overturned those conclusions. The new research is detailed below.
2 CONT The research undertaken since the 1999 presentation has not found the English ancestor of Colonel Richard Lee but it is challenging the fundamental assumptions made by earlier researchers. I am continuing to follow my lines of res
2 CONC earch and hopefully will carry it through to a successful conclusion. I will post articles on my webpage as I complete each phase of the research. As with all research of this nature there are gains and losses but I view each reve
2 CONC rsal with a positive attitude that will propel me forward with new knowledge to more gains and, hopefully, less losses.
2 CONT ****************************************
2 CONT
2 CONT Jim,
2 CONT I have not done English research on Richard and Henry Lee of York. They were obviously related, probably brothers. Since Richard was born c. 1619, and appears to have been the older, it would appear that Henry was born c. 1620. Si
2 CONC nce his first child was b. 1645, this fits well.
2 CONT There were several Richard Lees and several Henry Lees on the early emigration records. To my knowledge, no one has proven whence they came or which county they settled in.
2 CONT The first problem in research is that there is no proof that Richard Lee of York was the same person as Col. Richard.
2 CONT The research that has been done has been aimed at the origin of Col. Richard Lee. Two of the most recognized articles are: Article by Wm. Thorndale, National Genealogical Society Qtrly, Dec 1988
2 CONT Article by Ludwell Lee Montague, Va. Magazine, Jan. 1954.
2 CONT The descendants of Col. Richard have not enthusiastically accepted any of the research done. One of them has recently engaged an English researcher, Alan Nicholls. Nicholls has a web page which you can Google up.
2 CONT Hope this helps. Will send another message on the Henry Lee descendants.
2 CONT Cary Adams
2 CONT ***************my replyThis one got settled very well William and Mary Quarterly about 25 yrs ago sorry exact ref not in hand -it took some doing by the way very good work.. but they found Col. Richard baptized 22 march 1617/1618 
2 CONC ST Martin's Worcester England, d 1 MArch 1664 Northumberland Co Va but will probate London andhis papa was John /Lee or Lyes/ Clothier b 1588 of, Nordley Regis, Coton Hall, Salop, England burried 23 Feb 1629/1630 St Martin parish,
2 CONC  Worcestershire, Englandwife Jane Hancock (hence all the Hancocks in his line).Grandfather Col Richard was RIchard Lee 1564-1621 md Elizabeth BentleyGGfather John Lee 1528 - will 14 May 1605 will proven, Shropshire, Englandburried
2 CONC  Chesham, Buckinghamshire, , England wife Joyce or Jocota /ROMNEY/ b abt 1533 died 4 Dec 1609 Alveley Parish, Shropshire, England (burr same place)and finally GGG was Humphrey 1506 wife Katherine /Blount/ and this is the common li
2 CONC nk to Henry of York.They were cousins to be precise Henry Jr 1627 was 3rd cousin to Col Richard.Jim Lawler
2 CONT **************
