1 OCCU Colonel, Educated at Oxford, emigrated 1641, "the scholar", House of Burgesses
1 TITL Col.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Col. Richard Lee II, Esq.''' (1647-1715) was a Colonel, planter, member of the Upper House and the King's Council. He was known as  "Richard the Scholar".  Richard was the son of Col. Richard Lee I, Esq., "the I
2 CONC mmigrant" (1618-1664) and Anne Constable (ca. 1621-1666).
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard was born at "Paradise", in Northumberland County, Virginia, the estate he inherited from his father when he died in 1664. This estate consisted of 1,350 acres, and was later part of Gloucester County. He was educated at Ox
2 CONC ford in England and may have studied law at the London “Inns”. He seemed destined for a career in the church, but he elected rather to return to the life of a Virginia gentleman, residing at “Paradise”. In 1673, when his older bro
2 CONC ther John died unmarried, Richard inherited the estate, “Machodoc”. Richard left “Paradise” to overseers and removed to his new estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard married Laetitia Corbin (ca. 1657-1706), daughter of Richard’s neighbor and, Councillor, Hon. Henry Corbin, Sr. (1629-1676) and Alice (Eltonhead) Burnham (ca. 1627-1684)
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon after his marriage, Richard was elected to the House of Burgesses. In 1676 Richard became a member of the King’s Council and he served in this capacity off and on until 1698. On one such absence in 1690 he had lost his seat b
2 CONC ecause of his refusal to take the oath of allegiance to William III, King of England (“William of Orange”) (1650-1702). However, he was reinstated within a year. Richard was forced to retire from this position because of ill healt
2 CONC h. The Council was a body that served as the Governor’s privy council, the Upper House of the Colonial Legislature, and the Colonial Supreme Court. As early as 1680 he was Colonel of Horse in the counties of Westmoreland, Northumb
2 CONC erland and Stafford. He was appointed by Sir Gov. Edmund Andros (1637-1714) to be “Naval Officer and Receiver of Virginia Dutys for the River Potomac”. Richard II, had one of the largest libraries in the Colony. He spent almost hi
2 CONC s whole life in study, and usually wrote his notes in Greek, Hebrew, or Latin. It was because of this that he was termed “the scholar”. Richard was a supporter of the Established Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard died March 12, 1714 at “Machodoc”, Westmoreland County. His will was probated on April 27, 1715. He was buried at the old “Burnt House Fields”, located near “Mount Pleasant”. Laetitia died on October 6, 1706 at “Machodoc”,
2 CONC  and her tombstone can still be seen at “Mount Pleasant”.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard established his residence at the “Machodoc” plantation, which was located on the Potomac River, near the town of Hague, in Westmoreland County. This was a large brick house, largely inclosed by a brick wall. The estate was
2 CONC  inherited by his son Hon. Richard Lee III (1679-1718) who was at the time residing in London as a tobacco merchant with his family. Richard III leased his estate in Virginia to his brothers, Col. Thomas Lee, Hon. (1690-1750) and 
2 CONC Capt. Henry Lee I (1691-1747), for “an annual rent of one peppercorn only, payable on Christmas Day”. After Richard’s death in 1718, the estate was sold by his wife Martha Silk (d.ca. 1734), who sold it to her brother-in-law, Col.
2 CONC  Thomas Lee, (1690-1750). Thomas resided here until it burned down January 29, 1729, and removed to his newly built “Stratford Hall”. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The fire was so serious that the field near the old mansion at “Machodoc” where the Lee burial ground was situated, that it  became forever known as the old “Burnt House Fields”. The land was sold to Richard Lee III’s only son Col
2 CONC . George Lee (1714-1761), who had come over from England. A new plantation was built by George who called his new estate “Mount Pleasant”. The new house was built further back from the river and upon higher ground. This house also
2 CONC  burned down.
2 CONT
2 CONT The inscription on Richard Lee's tombstone at Burned House cemetery reads (translated from the Latin):
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Here lieth the body of Richard Lee, Esq., born in Virginia, son of Richard Lee, Gentleman, descended of an ancient family of Merton- Regis, in Shropshire. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >"While he exercised the office of a magistrate he was a zealous promoter of the public good.  He was very skillful in the Greek and Latin languages and other parts of polite learning.  He quietly resigned his soul to God, who he 
2 CONC always devoutly worshipped, on the 12th day of March, in the year 1714, in the 68th year of his age."  
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Nearby is interred the body of Lettuce, his faithful wife, daughter of Henry Corbyn, Gentleman.  A most affectionate mother, she was also distinguished by piety toward God, charity to the poor, and kindness to all.  She died on 
2 CONC the 6th day of October, 1706, in the 49th year of her age." 
2 CONT
2 CONT The 7 children of Richard Lee and wife Laetitia Corbin were:
2 CONT #John Lee (21 May 1678-1679), who died in infancy. 
2 CONT #Hon. Richard Lee III (1679-1718), who married Martha Silk (1680-ca. 1734). 
2 CONT #Capt. Philip Lee, Sr., Hon., Esq. (1681-1744) of "Blenheim", who married 1) Sarah (Brooke) Dent (1683-1724), widow of her uncle, Col. William Dent, Sr., Gent. (1660-1705). Sarah was the daughter of Col. Thomas Brooke, Jr., Hon. (
2 CONC 1660-1730) and Barbara Dent (1676-1754). 2) 
2 CONT #Ann Lee (1683-1732), who married 1) Capt. Daniel McCarthy, Sr., Esq. (1679-1724), son of Dennis (MacCartee) McCarthy, Sr. (d. 1694) and Elizabeth Billington. 2) Hon. William Fitzhugh, Jr. (1679-1713) of "Eagle's Nest", King Georg
2 CONC e Co., Virginia
2 CONT #Francis Lee (1685-aft. 1754), who died childless, and is NOT the father of the South Carolina Lee Family
2 CONT #Col. Thomas Lee, Hon. (1690-1750) of "Stratford Hall", Westmoreland Co., Virginia. Thomas married Hannah Harrison Ludwell (1701-1750), daughter of Col. Philip Ludwell II (1672-1726) of "Greenspring", and Hannah Harrison (1679-173
2 CONC 1).
2 CONT #Capt. Henry Lee I (1691-1747) of "Lee Hall", Westmoreland Co., Virginia. Henry married Mary Bland (1704-1764), daughter of Hon. Richard Bland, Sr. (1665-1720) and his second wife, Elizabeth Randolph (d. 1719). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Not a known child:
2 CONT * Arthur Lee (1693-1756), who married an unknown Sherrad .
2 CONT * John Lee (1678-1742) who married Dorothy Guythernew
2 CONT
