1 NOTE !BIRTH - SOME PROMINENT VIRGINIA FAMILIES, VOL 4 by Louise duBellet, p. 84
2 CONT Richard Lee, son of Col. Richard Lee and Anna ___?, his wife, was born 1647 at "Paradise" in Gloucester Co.,VA.  He was known as the scholar.
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR - Ibid
2 CONT He married Laetitia Corbin, the eldest daughter of Henry Corbin and Alice Eltonhead, his wife about 1674.
2 CONT
2 CONT !DEATH - Ibid
2 CONT Richard Lee died March 12, 1714 at his home, Mt Pleasant, Westmoreland Co., VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT !BURIAL - THE LEES OF VIRGINIA by Paul C. Nagel
2 CONT He was buried in the garden at Machodoc.
2 CONT
2 CONT THE LEES OF VIRGINIA published by the Society of the Lees of Virginia
2 CONT When John Lee died in 1673, Richard Lee II (1646-1714) established his residence at the Machodoc plantation, which by that time was in Westmoreland Co.  There he married Letitia, the daughter of his neighbor, Councillor Henry Corb
2 CONC in.  Soon afterward he was himself appointed to the Council, a body which served as the Governor's privy council, the upper house of the colonial legislature, and the colonial supreme court. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Lee II left half of the Machodoc plantation, including the family residence there, to his eldest son, Richard (1678-1718), but this third Rechard Lee resided in London as a merchant trading to Virginia.  He leased his esta
2 CONC te in Virginia to his brothers, Thomas and Henry, for an annual rent of one peppercorn only, payable on Christmas Day.
2 CONT
2 CONT LEE OF VIRGINIA 1642-1892 Edited and Published by Edmund Jennings Lee, p. 74
2 CONT Mt. Pleasant in Westmoreland Co. estate consisted of about 2,600 acres and had been bequeathed by the immigrant to his eldest son John and was inherited by Richard as heir in law to their father.  An old manuscript has stated that
2 CONC  "the eldest of the two sons of the (Immigrant) afterwards removed to Westmoreland and established himself at "Mt. Pleasant" on the River Potomac.  The large brick house, largely inclosed by a brick wall, was burned down and anoth
2 CONC er was built on the surrounding heights of the Potomac."  The date of this fire is not known but it must have occurred between 1716 and 1730.  Thomas Lee obtained a lease of this estate in 1716 and apparently lived there until he 
2 CONC built the Stratford mansion.  It seems likely that the loss by fire, mentioned by William Lee, occurred at "Mt. Pleasant," not at Stratford, as has been generally supposed.  There is no record of a fire ever having occurred at the
2 CONC  latter place; while frequent mention has been made "of the burnt house fields," at the former, evidently showing that fire there had been so serious that the field had been nameddd as a record of the disaster.
2 CONT
2 CONT !BURIAL - Ibid
2 CONT From a tombstone in the Burnt House Fields, at Mt. Pleasant, Westmoreland Co, where are yet to be seen the foundations of large buildings are the following:"
2 CONT "Here lieth the body of Richard Lee, Esq., born in Virginia, son of Richard Lee, Gent'eman, descended of an ancient family of Merton-Regis in Shropshire.  While he exercised the office of magistrate he was a zealous promoter of th
2 CONC e public good.  He was bery skillful in the Greek and Lating languages and other parts of polite leaning.  He quietly resigned his soul to God, whom he always edvoutly worshiped, on the 12th day of March, in the year 1714, in the 
2 CONC 68th year of his age.
