1 NOTE !BIRTH - LEE OF VIRGINIA 1642-1892 Edited and Published by Edmund Jennings Lee, M.D., p. 72
2 CONT William the fourth son of Richard and Anna Lee was born about 1651.  Under date of the 25th of Feb., 1673-4, "aged about twenty years.
2 CONT
2 CONT !DEATH - THE LEES OF VIRGINIA published by The Society of the Lees of Virginia
2 CONT William Lee died in 1696 and left his estate to one Mary, the widow of Thomas Heath and wife of Bartholomew Schreever.  It has been supposed that she was William Lee's daughter.  Richard Lee II, however, claimed William Lee's land
2 CONC s as heir-at-law of their father, contending that William had had only a life estate in them, since the first Richard's will had contained, with reference to them, no "words of inheritance" - i.e., "and his heirs forever".  This c
2 CONC ontention was upheld by the courts of both Virginia and Maryland, and the Schreevers were dispossessed.
1 NOTE 3. William Lee b. 1678 or 1682, Northampton, Northumberland Co, VA
2 CONT m. Dorothy Taylor, ca 1703, Westmoreland Co, VA d/o Thomas Taylor & Elizabeth Harwood b. 1681, Surry,
2 CONT King and Queen Co, VA
2 CONT Information on Dorothy Taylor comes from "Log Cabins to White House" by Brewer. 13 Apr 1745
2 CONT
2 CONT Dorothy was witness to will of Henry Williams in Richmond Co.,VA. signed as Dorothy Croucher. Dorothy and her brother Thomas were administrators of her husband William Lee's estate. She was left wearing apparel in her mother's wil
2 CONC l. "Dorothy to have my Coyas" d. 1717, Richmond Co, VA (Source: Amelia Co. Order Book 7, p. 226, Letters of Administration of Estate of William Lee to Dorothy Lee, relict of William Lee on 12/4/1717.).
2 CONT
2 CONT Documentation from "Log Cabins to White House" by Brewer. On page 275 "On 19 Jan 1668 William and wife Alice acknowledged debt of 250 acres of land to William Heath.
2 CONT On 5 Sep 1668 William and wife Alice sold their plantation to Thomas Adams (Surry Co. records p.54).
2 CONT
2 CONT From "Log Cabin" book on p.276 it gives William Lee( who married Dorothy Taylor) as one of his children. On p.273, Richard Henry Lee and Anna Constable are given as the parents of William (husband of Alice) but other sources state
2 CONC  that Richard Lee's son William did not marry. Other sources say he married and had a daughter Mary who married a Heath and that he left his estate to her -- which was contested by his brother and returned to the Lee family.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a William Lee and Mary Lee, witnesses in Richard Jesper's will, dated 1698 in North Farnham Parish, VA. Need to identify them. Dorothy and brother Thomas Taylor were administrators of his estate. They left to William's gr
2 CONC andchildren (children of Wm.3rd) the legacy left to him by his grandmother Elizabeth Taylor. Thomas Hanks received 9 lbs 11 shillings 8 pence; Joseph Hanks 2 lbs2 shillings 6 pence; Richard Lee 4 shillings; none was listed for Joh
2 CONC n Lee who had probably died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT **************************
2 CONT alt version below probable error confused
2 CONT ID: I16208 Name: William COLONEL Lee Leigh
2 CONT Sex: M Birth: 1654 in Surry County, , Virginia
2 CONT Death: 14 JUL 1703Father: William Lee Leigh  b: ABT 1588 in England Mother: Catherine  b: ABT 1592Marriage 1 Mary Green  b: ABT 1659 in King & Queen County, VA
2 CONT Married: 1675 in King & Queen County, VA
2 CONT Children
2 CONT John CAPTAIN Lee Leigh b: 1677 in Of Leigh Hall, , Cheshire, England
2 CONT William Lee Leigh  b: 1682 in New Kent County, VA
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT Title: Family Group Record FamilySearchØ Ancestral File v4.19
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT *********************
2 CONT : William Lee Leigh 1 Sex: M Birth: ABT 1588 in England Death: 1653 in , James, VirginiaMarriage 1 Mary Dawkes  b: ABT 1578 in < , Charles City County, Virginia>
2 CONT Marriage 2 Catherine  b: ABT 1592
2 CONT Children
2 CONT William COLONEL Lee Leigh  b: 1654 in Surry County, , Virginia
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT Title: Family Group Record FamilySearchØ Ancestral File v4.19
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------************************
2 CONT Edmund Jennings Lee had not found any record of what happened to one son William Lee and blew it off finally saying he appears to have died young.
2 CONT Weelllll sort of.
2 CONT He did die earlier, 1694-5, than other siblings, leaving a 18 year old daughter and three younger minor sons. The daughter Mary had married William Heath at age 15 had two sons, Thomas and Samuel the last being born after his fath
2 CONC er died. She re married to Bartholomew Schriever who is the villain of this story.  He was  of age so grabbed the estate and paid out yearly  a gentlemans living to the three boys; Wm, John, and Richard.  What he paid was far less
2 CONC  than the estate could manage; and they deserved more; but it was legally large enough that they could not sue their guardian until ALL of them were of age. The instant the youngest turned 21 they did sue.
2 CONT BUT
2 CONT Not in probate court    but common pleas court   to recover their inheritance.
2 CONT And not in the original county (Westmoreland) but the county had been split and then re-split in the mean time (at this time it was Surry Co) and worse the judge (a VIP ) did not travel to them he stayed put and they had to come t
2 CONC o him in King and Queen County. (This was a MAJOR estate -and why they had a superior judge in a higher court- in todays terms it would be  multi millionaire stuff).
2 CONT AND
2 CONT It was 7-8 years out of timeS7 -8 years later than expected.
2 CONT Wrong place, wrong time, wrong court, and why E J Lee missed it.
2 CONT Bart and Mary got her furniture and what money he had sequestered (the court knew he had milked estate, said so, but did not bother to try to find where he had squirreled money away--{Bermuda}), just gave Mary HER  furniture, and 
2 CONC kicked them off land and divided it 3 ways each boy getting roughly 1/3.   Bart packed up in high dudgeon and went back to England (assume they stopped at Bermuda where $$ were) - and lived life of rich ex colonial gentleman.
2 CONT
2 CONT OK first problem solved. This I got from two separate books /people who had done work independently, and I cross-checked just to be sure. (A Weavin' and Log Cabin to White House
2 CONT Next problem
2 CONT William (ii) son Wm (i) and grandson of Co Richard died 1717 (about 13 years after court bruhoo); leaving 4 sons. And I will stick to our line his son Charles b 1706 moved to new land at North Farnham Parish, Richmond Co., Va.
2 CONT Charles moves again dies 1799 at-really near just out of town- Leesburg, Cumberland Co., Va. leaving cash to a son William (others got land) and just down the road, land adjoining Charles is a William Lee that the previous genealo
2 CONC gists had assumed was his son.
2 CONT BooBoo!
2 CONT It was a reasonable assumption.  Just wrong. Charles died in 1799 Cumberland Co Va and had a son William. There is a William Lee (md Ave Noel) whose land adjoins Charles land so they assume he is the son. Unfortunately they happen
2 CONC ed to buy adjoining lands at about the same time and they were cousins, but unwarranted assumption as to being his son.
2 CONT Then I found the full name of James Garnett Early Lee b. 23 feb 1777 known as Garnett Lee usually, AND known to be son of William and Ave Noel and that triggered my memory that James Lee had md Mary Clark Garnett and I cross conne
2 CONC cted Wm and Ave to James Lee and Mary Garnett who also had known son William but unknown what happened to him if we connect Wm and Ave to Charles. To add to the surety I was right,  Wm and Ave moved about 1788 to Evington Road Bed
2 CONC ford Co Va. just a bit down the road from James and Mary Garnett  -- (Ah Ha!)----- so this time making the assumption that the William who was close to James was his son works. James died by the way 1791  Probate  Bedford Co Va.
2 CONT With that I was able to disconnect he wrong William (md Ave) and get him to correct papa- and so was left connecting MY William (1738-1808) to Charles. Reason for cash was my William had gone on to Hardy Co Va. (now West Va) at th
2 CONC e same time, abt 1760, his father Charles had moved from Richmond Co where he was born (The residual of the original estate was now in Surry Co.-but had been split up so much the land left to Charles was too small to make him well
2 CONC  off - or as well off as he wished) then on to Leesburgh in Cumberland Co and so our William  had no use for land- he had his own- but hundreds of miles away. SO cash.
2 CONT Oh me oh my long story. I also jumped 4 years work in getting all of the above. I followed Loads of dead ends.
1 NOTE !BIRTH - ANCESTORS OF AMERICAN PRESIDENTS compiled by Gary Boyd Roberts, p.22
2 CONT Hancock Lee was born in Northumberland Co. abt 1653.
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR - THE LEES OF VIRGINIA Published by The Society of the Lees of Virginia.
2 CONT He married 1st Mary Kendall.
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR #2 - ANCESTORS OF AMERICAN PRESIDENTS compiled by Gary Boyd Roberts, p.22
2 CONT He married 2nd Sarah Allerton of Westmoreland Co., prob widow of ___ Newton.
2 CONT about 1700.
2 CONT
2 CONT !DEATH - Ibid
2 CONT He died 25 May 1729 in Wicomico Parish, Northumberland Co.,VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hancock Lee inherited the southern third of the Dividing Creek plantation and became the progenitor of the Ditchley branch of the family - THE LEES OF VIRGINIA published by The Society of the Lees of Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT DITCHLEY - Hancock Lee (1653-1709) eventually inherited the southern third of the Dividing Creek plantation, which he called Hancock's Neck.  He married first, Mary Kendall, and second, Sarah Allerton, a great-granddaughter of Eld
2 CONC er William Brewster, the leader of the Plymouth Colony.  Richard Lee (1691-1740), Hancock Lee's eldest son, inherited Hancock's Neck and renamed it Ditchley after Ditchley Park in Oxfordshire, England.  The present Ditchley mansio
2 CONC n was built about 1762 by this Richard Lee's son, Kendall Lee.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hancock Lee's grandsons, Willis and Hancock Lee, and his grand-nephew, Hancock Taylor, went to Kentucky in 1771 as surveyors for the Ohio Company founded by Thomas Lee of Stratford.  Hancock Taylor was killed there by the Indians 
2 CONC in 1774, Willis Lee in 1776.
2 CONT
2 CONT The most notable member of this branch of the family was General Zachary Taylor (1784-1850) a grandson of Elizabeth Lee, a daughter of the first Hancock Lee.  He distinguished himself in the Mexican War and was afterwards Presiden
2 CONC t of the United States. - VIRGINIA HOMES OF THE LEES by Eleanor Lee Templeman
1 NOTE Occupation: Burgess
1 NOTE Justice Northampton Co. 1667
1 NOTE moved to Northumberland Co. 1688
1 NOTE will dated 1706
1 NOTE built "Ditchley", Northumberland Co. in 1688
