2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT [[Category:Lee]]
2 CONT {{US Southern Colonist}} [[Category:Northumberland County, Virginia Colony]] [[Category:Northern Neck, Virginia]] [[Category: Christ Church Parish, Virginia Colony]]
2 CONT == Biography == 
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Lee
2 CONT
2 CONT === Origin ===Charles Lee, youngest son of [[Lee-318|Richard Lee]] and [[Constable-37|Anne Constable]], was probably born about 1656, at "Dividing Creeks"(later known as "Cobbs Hall") in the Northern Neck, in NorthumberlandCounty.
2 CONC <ref name="Lee">Lee, Edmund Jennings, 1895 [https://archive.org/stream/leeofvirginia16400inleee#page/1199/mode/2up ''Lee of Virginia, 1642-1892: biographical and genealogical sketches ...''] Philadelphia: [Franklin Printing Compan
2 CONC y], Archive.org accessed 12 February 2016.</ref>  Formerly, the family had lived at "Paradise" in Gloucester County, but removed to "Dividing Creeks," near what is today the town of Kilmarnock.<ref name="Wik">Wikipedia contributor
2 CONC s, [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Lee_I Richard Lee I.] 20 June 2015, accessed 12 February 2016.</ref>  The date of this move is uncertain, but Richard Lee served as burgess for Northumberland County in 1651 and began pate
2 CONC nting land there in the same year (p 65).<ref name="Lee"/>   
2 CONT On 6 February 1663/4, Charles' father executed his will in London.  Hewas preparing for the return voyage to Virginia, and by 20 April 1664, he had died (p 64).<ref name="Lee"/>
2 CONT
2 CONT === Marriage and Children ===He married c. 1676-1678 to [[Medstand-6|Elizabeth Medstand]], daughterof Thomas Medstand, of Lancaster County (p 558).<ref name="Lee"/>  Onthe date of Charles' will in 1700, only his oldest child was o
2 CONC f age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children, from will (pp 560-62):<ref name="Lee"/>
2 CONT # Thomas Lee, b c. 1679, d 1735; mar Unknown and had chidren
2 CONT # [[Lee-4571|Charles Lee]] (Major), d 1740/1; mar Elizabeth Pinckard
2 CONT # Leeanna Lee; mar prob. William Jones
2 CONT # Elizabeth Lee; poss. mar Capt. John Howson
2 CONT
2 CONT === Property and Positions ===Charles Lee and his wife, Elizabeth, on 21 December 1684 sold 203 acres in Christ Church Parish acres to Robert Scholfield (p 558).  This was land inherited by Elizabeth from her father, Thomas Medsta
2 CONC nd.<ref name="Lee"/>  
2 CONT Charles and his brother, Richard, owned the plantation known later as "Cobbs Hall."  In 1707, brother Richard assigned his portion to his nephew, Charles' son by the same name (p 559).  The Cobbs Hall plantation (600 acres) was th
2 CONC e family homestead of Charles and his family.<ref name="Lee"/> Charles was a Justice for Northumberland from 1687 until 1699.<ref name="Lee"/> Charles' name was included in a 1702 list of civil officers,showing he was still in pub
2 CONC lic service until the time of his death.<ref name="Lee"/>
2 CONT
2 CONT === Last Will & Testament ===His will was dated the 13th of July, 1700, and probated at Northumberland on the 17th of December 1701. From this will it is learned that his wife survived him, and four children are named in the will 
2 CONC -- Thomas, Charles, Leeanna, and Elizabeth. 
2 CONT Younger son, Charles received the 600 acre homestead, Cobbs Hall.  Leanna received the 200 acres across Andrews' creek and on the south of the "Ditchy Creek" property.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Death and Legacy ===Charles died before 17 December 1701 (when his will was proved), at "Dividing Creeks" (later known as "Cobbs Hall") in the Northern Neck, inNorthumberland County, Virginia.<ref name="Lee"/>  He was buried a
2 CONC t the Lee Family Graveyard, Dividing Creek, Northumberland County with his parents, wife, and other family members.<ref name="Lee"/>
2 CONT Inscription at Cobb's Hall Burying Ground for Charles' father, RichardLee: <blockquote>Within this enclosure was buried ~ Richard Lee ~ of an ancient family of Nordley Regis in Shropshire, who emigrated from London to Virginia in 
2 CONC 1639. He was successively clerk of the quarter court at Jamestown, Attorney General of Virginia, Secretary of State, and a member of the council. Having long traded with the nearby Wicomico Indians, in 1656 he established his home
2 CONC  on Dividing Creek, at a point 400 feet to the Northeast of this stone. He died there in 1664 and was buried here in his garden. ~ Also buried within this enclosure were Anne Constable, the wife of Richard Lee; their son, Charles 
2 CONC Lee (1656-1701), and his wife Elizabeth Medstand; their son, Charles Lee of Cobbs Hall (1684-1734); and his son, Charles Lee (1722-1747), and his wives, Mary Lee of Ditchley and Leeanna Jones of Hickory Neck. In 1761 this Leeanna 
2 CONC Lee ordered the erection of the original enclosing wall. Later members of theCobbs Hall family, Lees and Harveys, were buried nearby outside the wall. ~ Erected by The Society of the Lees of Virginia ~ 1958.<ref name="Lee"/> </blo
2 CONC ckquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT == Research Notes ==This profile reported an exact birth date May 21, 1656, without evidence, citation: Find a Grave memorial - which cites Edmund J. Lee, but Lee does not report this exact birth date for Charles Lee.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Charles''' Lee. <ref>Source: [[#S1659598983]]   </ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Born''' 
2 CONT 21 May 1656. Cobbs Hall, Northampton, Virginia, United States. <ref>Source: [[#S1659598983]]   </ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Died'''  17 December 1701. Cobbs Hall, Northampton, Virginia, United States. <ref>Source: [[#S1659598983]]   </ref> 1700 Cobbs Hall, Northampton, Virginia, United States.  ''Found multiple copies of death date. Using 17 Decembe
2 CONC r 1701'' 
