1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == DOCTOR JAMES CRAIK ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===British Army Surgeon (1751) General Practitioner (1751-54) Surgeon in Colonel Joshua Fry's Virginia Provincial Regiment (1754) Physician General of the US Army (1798)===
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Doctor James Craik is the illegitimate son of https://www.geni.com/people/William-Craik-of-Arbigland/6000000024860080920?through=6000000017955991758. The name of his mother has not been identified. [https://www.findagrave.com/m
2 CONC emorial/86525930/william-craik Find A Grave Memorial]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Craik Biographical Summary by Wikipedia] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT James Craik (/kreɪk/; 1730 – 6 February 1814) was Physician General (precursor of the Surgeon General) of the United States Army, as well as George Washington's personal physician and close friend.
2 CONT
2 CONT Education and emigration to America
2 CONT
2 CONT Born on the estate of Arbigland in the parish of Kirkbean, County of Kirkcudbright, Scotland, Craik was the son of Robert Craik, a member of Parliament. He studied medicine at the University of Edinburgh, then joined the British A
2 CONC rmy after graduation and served as an army surgeon in the West Indies until 1751. Craik then opened up a private medical practice in Norfolk, Virginia, and shortly thereafter relocated to Winchester, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT French and Indian War career
2 CONT
2 CONT On 7 March 1754, Craik resumed his military career, accepting a commission as a surgeon in Colonel Joshua Fry's Virginia Provincial Regiment. While with this force, he became good friends with George Washington, at that time a lie
2 CONC utenant colonel in the regiment. Craik saw a great deal of action in various battles of the French and Indian War. He fought at the Battle of the Great Meadows and participated in the surrender of Fort Necessity, then accompanied 
2 CONC General Edward Braddock on Braddock's unsuccessful attempt to recapture the region in 1755, treating Braddock's ultimately fatal wounds. Craik then served under Washington in actions in Virginia and Maryland, during various engage
2 CONC ments with Indians.
2 CONT
2 CONT Inter War years
2 CONT
2 CONT After the war's end, Craik opened another medical practice at Port Tobacco, Maryland, and on 13 November 1760, he married Mariamne Ewell at her family's estate, Bel Air, located in Prince William County, Virginia. Marriamne would 
2 CONC later become the great-aunt of Richard S. Ewell. They had six sons and three daughters. In 1760, he moved to Charles County, Maryland, where in 1765, he built La Grange near La Plata, Maryland. In both 1770 and 1784 he went on sur
2 CONC veying expeditions with Washington, examining military claims in Pennsylvania and what is now West Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Revolutionary War career
2 CONT
2 CONT With the outbreak of hostilities during the American Revolution, Craik once more rejoined the army. He served as an army surgeon, ultimately advancing to the second-highest post in army medicine. Craik warned Washington about the 
2 CONC plots of the Conway Cabal, and treated the wounds of General Hugh Mercer at the Battle of Princeton and Gilbert du Motier, Marquis de La Fayette at the Battle of Brandywine. Mercer died of his wounds, but La Fayette was more fortu
2 CONC nate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Washington persuaded him to move his practice to Alexandria, Virginia, where he built his house Vaucluse (plantation), where he died. He also had a town house at 117 South Fairfax Street, 209 Prince Street, and then 210 Duke Stree
2 CONC t.
2 CONT
2 CONT Washington summoned Craik out of private practice in 1798 in connection with the Quasi-War against France, installing him as Physician General of the Army on 19 June of that year. After the conclusion of hostilities, Craik mustere
2 CONC d out on 15 June 1800.
2 CONT
2 CONT At Washington's death
2 CONT
2 CONT As Washington's personal physician, Craik was one of three doctors to attend on him during his final illness on 14 December 1799. Washington complained of respiratory distress, described by Craik as "cynanche trachealis". When Was
2 CONC hington proved unable to swallow medicines orally, Craik and the other two physicians (Dr. Elisha C. Dick and Dr. Gustavus Richard Brown) treated his condition with bloodletting, the application of various poultices, and a rectal 
2 CONC solution of calomel and tartar. Washington's condition continued to deteriorate, but Craik and Brown decided against Dick's suggestion of a tracheotomy (which might have been lifesaving, but likely would have spread the infection 
2 CONC and caused sepsis), and Washington died at 10:10 p.m. Brown and Craik co-published an account of their treatment in December 1800. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Craik died in Alexandria in 1814; he is buried in the graveyard of the Old Presbyterian Meeting House in that city. [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Craik Wikipedia: James Craik]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Commentary by https://www.geni.com/people/Janet-Louise-Milburn-Schultz/6000000011913809768 ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''An online book titled, "Monongahela of old, or, Historical sketches of south-western Pennsylvania to the year 1800.". Among other surgeons were Dr. James Kraik and Hugh Mercer, men of imperishable fame. They were both Scotchman
2 CONC  and the latter having fled to Virginia  from the service of the Pretender on the fatal field of the Culloden....There is more about these two men and how they both settled in Fayette county, Pennsylvania pg 49 of 255. Added by Ja
2 CONC net Milburn 10/24/22 while searching for my Stewart ancestors who came to
2 CONT America and settled in Virginia and died before 1830.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source: [https://archive.org/details/cu31924028854359/page/n12/mode/1up The Monongahela of Old: or Historical Sketches of South-Western Pennsylvania to the year 1800, By James Veech (Printed for Private Distribution Only, Pitts
2 CONC burgh, 1858-1892), For mention of Doctor James Craik see pp. 44, 57 and 58]'''
