1 NOTE !BIRTH - SOME PROMINENT VA. FAMILIES VOL 3 p. 7 by Louise Du Bellet
2 CONT John Smith born Nov. 13, 1715.
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR - Ibid - p. 8
2 CONT Married Nov. 17, 1737 Mary Jaquelin by the Rev. William Dawson of Jamestown.
2 CONT
2 CONT !DEATH - Ibid - p. 8
2 CONT John Smith of Shooter's Hill, d. November 19, 1777 at 3 o'clock in the morning, aged 56 years at "Hackwood" in Jefferson Co., WV, the home of his second cousin, Samuel Washington, Esq.
2 CONT Note:  The date of 1777 is incorrect as his will was written 7 Jan 1771 and
2 CONT probated 28 Apr 1772.   - JMC
2 CONT
2 CONT Maj. John Smith was called upon to pay a large security debt for his friend and relation, Speaker John Robinson. To meet this debt, he was compelled to sell a large amount of property including his home.
2 CONT SOME PROMINENT VA. FAMILIES VOL 3, p. 13 by Louise du Bellet
2 CONT
2 CONT WILLS OF WESTMORELAND CO., VA. 1654-1800 by Augusta B. Fothergill
2 CONT SMITH, JOHN, gent. 7 Jan. 1771 - 28 Apr 1772
2 CONT Entire estate to sons John, Edward and Mathew Smith and their executors.
2 CONT Note:  Mathew died 25 Oct 1777 and if John Smith died 19 Nov 1777, Mathew would not have been mentioned in his will.  As it was, there was very little to be left to anyone. - JMC
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a member of the House of Burgesses from Middlesex Co., VA from 1737 to
2 CONT 1768, a period of 31 years. - SOME PROMINENT VA. FAMILIES VOL 3 p. 8.
2 CONT
2 CONT VIRGINIA NORTHERN NECK LAND GRANTS 1694-1742 compiled by Gertrude E. Gray, p. 108
2 CONT C-144 - John Smith, Jr. of Northumberland Co. Wrnt. for surplus in 36 A. now in his possession, granted Stephen Wills 9 July 1696.  Deed to Smith for  97 A. in Northumberland Co. adj. land formerly Robertson's now Coltston's, Main
2 CONC  Br. of Yeocomoco R., Jeremiah Rust's mill pond. 14 May 1731.
2 CONT
2 CONT GENEALOGICAL ABSTRACTS FROM 18TH CENTURY VIRGINIA NEWSPAPERS by Robert K. Headley, Jr., p. 314
2 CONT SMITH, John of Northumberland Co., deceased, his tract in that county known as Fleet's Bay, containing 1,611 acres to be sold pursuant to a decree of the General Court (VIRGINIA GAZETTE 12 June 1772).
