1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Union Co. S.C. Will Book A Pp.22-23 : Will Of Isaac White proved in Ordinaries Ofﬁce 2 April. 1801 by Ricldtson Lipsey
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of Isaac White, Sr. ...To my wife Mary,her choise of my beds with every necessary furniture thereunto belonging,cattle, etc: one negro woman Dilse during her life or widowhood, at her death or marriage to be equally divided a
2 CONC mong the children; To my daughter Sarah Smith, negro woman Mall; To my son, William White, Negro woman Alce; To my son Thomas White, Negro woman Lud; To my Isabel Cooper, Negro girl Mimey; To my son Thomas White land I now live on
2 CONC  after my wifes decease; To my son Isaac White, tract where he did formal' live, also the land where my son John White now lives I lend to him during his lifetime;  To my grandsons and daughters, my son Johns children, all my stoc
2 CONC k of cattle; my son William White and Samuel Smith, Exrs. 14 April 1801. Iscca White(+) (Seal). Wit: Rickitson B Lipsey, William Sharp (X)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://familytreemaker.genealogy.com/users/p/a/c/Janie-H-Pace/GENE2-0003.html#CHILD3
2 CONT
2 CONT Info from link above:
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac and his wife Mary left Philadelphia County, Pennsylvania and traveled south to establish a plantation in South Carolina.  According to the Union County World Wide Web Site the following information is available on Union Coun
2 CONC ty Early Settlements and Families: The earliest land grant in the Brown's Creek area, located approximately four miles northeast of what is now the town of Union, was issued in 1752.  The area was settled by a group of Scotch-Iris
2 CONC h Presbyterians. By 1755, there were enough families to organize Brown's Creek Church, which soon became known as Union Church because it was used by several denominations. Some of these families later moved elsewhere, but descend
2 CONC ants of many of these families remained in Union County for decades.  Families that settled along Brown's Creek, after the Revolutionary War include the family of Isaac White.  Source: American Trails and Roads Reference Cards, Ka
2 CONC nsas. Title: Missouri Genealogy Tutor, by Beverly Whitaker, 1995. Reference Cards, Kansas City, MO. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac White enlisted while residing in Charleston, under Captain Philip Pricleau. In 1780 and 1781, he was under Capt. Anthony Toomer and Major Gilball in the Charleston Battalion of Artillery. After being wounded in the hip and t
2 CONC aken prisoner during this fall of Charleston, he was paroled for one year. Thereafter, he was put on a prison ship for refusing to swear allegiance to the King. He was held prisoner for another four months and then exchanged. (Mov
2 CONC ed to R.I. and N.Y.)  In May 1781, the following person were sent on board the prison ship Forbay and the Schooner Packhorse: The list of names include that of Isaac White.  The following is evidence that Isaac served in the milit
2 CONC ia.  The following is a letter from Stephen Moore and John Barnwell to General N. Greene  (From North Carolina State Records)  PRISON SHIP FORBAY, CHARLES TOWN HARBOUR, 18TH MAY, 1781  We have the honor of inclosing (sic) you a co
2 CONC py of a letter from Colonel Balfour commandant of Charlestown, which was handed us immediately on our being put on board this ship. The letter speaking for itself needs no comment; your wisdom will best dictate the notice it merit
2 CONC s. We just beg leave to observe that should it fall to the lost of all, or any of us, to be made victims, agreeable to them enaces therein contained, we have only to regret that our blood cannot be disposed of more to the advancem
2 CONC ent of the glorious cause to which we have adhered. A separate Roll of our names attends this letter.. With the greatest respect, we are, Sir, Your most obedient and most H'ble Servants,  Stephen Moore, Lieut. Colonel. North Carol
2 CONC ina Militia  John Barnwell, Major South Carolina Militia  For ourselves and one hundred and thirty other Prisoners.  To Major Gen'l N. Greene  FORBAY PRISON SHIP, CHARLES TOWN,HARBOUR, 18TH May, 1781  Roll of the Militia Prisoners
2 CONC  on board said Ship:  listed among them; White, Isaac  Note: These were doubtless prisoners mostly of the battle of Camden, Aug, 1780.  Source: South Carolina Patriots in the American Revolution, Author: Moss, Genreal PUblishing C
2 CONC o, Baltimore, MD 1983. Pg S11757. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1781 Census - Charleston, SC  Source: Union Co Heritage Book 1981. This book is a compilation of histories of the pioneer families of Union Co. by Minnie Lee Mabry. Union County Historical Society, Union Co, SC 1981. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Last Will and Testament of Isaac White, Sr.  South Carolina Union District  In the name of God, Amen, I, Isaac White, Sr., of the above State and District, being weak in body, but of sound and perfect mind and memory, and cons
2 CONC idering the uncertainty of this mortal life, and blessed be Almighty God, for the same, do make and publish this my Last Will and Testament in manner and form following, that is to say, first,  ITEM. I give and bequeath unto my be
2 CONC loved wife, Mary White, he her choice of my beds with every necessary furniture, thou unto belonging, also her choice of my mares, which I have, also two choice cows and calves out of my stock, also one pot and one oven to her and
2 CONC  heares (Sic) forever. Also, I lend unto my beloved wife, Negro woman named Dilse and her increase during her life or widowhood, and also one chest and one flat iron, and also one sow and pigges (Sic), and also three choice hoges 
2 CONC (Sic) at her death or marriage to be equally divided among the children, that I give the remaining number of my Negroes to.  ITEM. I give and bequeath unto my daughter, Sary (Sic) Smith, one Negro woman named Moll, to have her and
2 CONC  her hears (Sic) for every (Sic).  ITEM. I give and bequeath unto my son, William White one Negro woman named Alice (Sic) to him and his hears (Sic) for every (Sic).  ITEM. I give and bequeath unto my son, Thomas White, one Negro 
2 CONC woman named Eeed to him and his hears (Sic) for every (Sic), on condition she will and all the rest that I give Negroes to will make up a (Sic) equal part unto the rest that I give Negroes to will make up a (Sic) equal part unto t
2 CONC he rest of my children that I don't give no Negroes unto.  ITEM. I give unto my daughter, Isabel Cooper, one Negro garle (Sic) named Mimey, to her and her hears (Sic) for every (Sic).  ITEM. I give and bequeath, unto my son, Thoma
