2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT White, Isaac (b. 1729, d. 1801)
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Isaac and his wife Mary left Philadelphia County, Pennsylvania and traveled south to establish a plantation in South Carolina.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Union County World Wide Web Site the following information is available on Union County Early Settlements and Families: The earliest land grant in the Brown's Creek area, located approximately four miles northeast
2 CONC  of what is now the town of Union, was issued in 1752.
2 CONT
2 CONT The area was settled by a group of Scotch-Irish Presbyterians. By 1755, there were enough families to organize Brown's Creek Church, which soon became known as Union Church because it was used as several denominations. Some of the
2 CONC se families later moved elsewhere, but descendants of many of these families remained in Union County for decades.
2 CONT
2 CONT Families that settled along Brown's Creek, after the Revolutionary War include the family of Isaac White.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac White enlisted while residing in Charleston, under Captain Philip Pricleau. In 1780 and 1781, he was under Capt. Anthony Toomer and Major Gilball in the Charleston Battalion of Artillery. After being wounded in the hip and t
2 CONC aken prisoner during this fall of Charleston, he was paroled for one year. Thereafter, he was put on a prison ship for refusing to swear allegiance to the King. He was held prisoner for another four months and then exchanged. (Mov
2 CONC ed to R.I. and N.Y.)
2 CONT
2 CONT In May 1781, the following person were sent on board the prison ship Forbay and the Schooner Packhorse: The list of names include that of Isaac White.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following is evidence that Isaac served in the militia.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following is a letter from Stephen Moore and John Barnwell to General N. Greene (From North Carolina State Records) PRISON SHIP FORBAY, CHARLES TOWN HARBOUR, 18TH MAY, 1781
2 CONT
2 CONT We have the honor of inclosing (sic) you a copy of a letter from Colonel Balfour commandant of Charlestown, which was handed us immediately on our being put on board this ship. The letter speaking for itself needs no comment; your
2 CONC  wisdom will best dictate the notice it merits. We just beg leave to observe that should it fall to the lost of all, or any of us, to be made victims, agreeable to the menaces therein contained, we have only to regret that our blo
2 CONC od cannot be disposed of more to the advancement of the glorius cause to which we have adhered. A separate Roll of our names attends this letter.. With the greatest respect, we are, Sir, Your most obedient and most H'ble Servants,
2 CONC  Stephen Moore, Lieut. Colonel. North Carolina Militia John Barnwell, Major South Carolina Militia For ourselves and one hundred and thirty other Prisoners. To Major Gen'l N. Greene
2 CONT
2 CONT FORBAY PRISON SHIP, CHARLES TOWN,HARBOUR, 18TH May, 1781 Roll of the Militia Prisoners on board said Ship: listed among them; White, Isaac Note: These were doubtless prisoners mostly of the battle of Camden, Aug, 1780. @S11@The La
2 CONC st Will and Testament of Isaac White, Sr. South Carolina Union District
2 CONT
2 CONT In the name of God, Amen, I, Isaac White, Sr., of the above State and District, being weak in body, but of sound and perfect mind and memory, and considering the uncertainty of this mortal life, and blessed be Almighty God, for th
2 CONC e same, do make and publish this my Last Will and Testament in manner and form following, that is to say, first,
2 CONT
2 CONT ITEM. I give and bequeath unto my beloved wife, Mary White, her choice of my beds with every necessary furniture, thou unto belonging, also her choice of my mares, which I have, also two choice cows and calves out of my stock, als
2 CONC o one pot and one oven to her and heares (Sic) forever. Also, I lend unto my beloved wife, negro woman named Dilse and her increase during her life or widowhood, and also one chest and one flat iron, and also one sow and pigges (S
2 CONC ic), and also three choice hoges (Sic) at her death or marriage to be equally divided among the children, that I give the remaining number of my negroes to.
2 CONT
2 CONT ITEM. I give and bequeath unto my daughter, Sary (Sic) Smith, one negro woman named Moll, to have her and her hears(Sic) for every (Sic).
2 CONT
2 CONT ITEM. I give and bequeath unto my son, William White one negro woman named Alice (Sic) to him and his hears(Sic) for every (Sic).
2 CONT
2 CONT ITEM. I give and bequeath unto my son, Thomas White, one negro woman named Eeed to him and his hears (Sic) for every (Sic), on conditions he will and all the rest that I give negroes to will make up a (Sic) equal part unto the res
2 CONC t that I give negroes to will make up a (Sic) equal part unto the rest of my children that I don't give no negroes unto.
2 CONT
2 CONT ITEM. I give unto my daughter, Isabel Cooper, one negro garle (Sic) named Mimey, to her and her hears (Sic) for every (Sic).
2 CONT
2 CONT ITEM. I give and bequeath, unto my son, Thomas White, my land that I now live on, after my wife('s) decease, to him and his heirs for every (Sic).
2 CONT
2 CONT ITEM. I give and Bequeath unto my son, Isaac White, that tract of land where he did formerly live to him and his heirs for every (Sic); also the land where my son, John White now lives, I lend unto him and his hears(Sic) during th
2 CONC is life.
2 CONT
2 CONT ITEM. I give unto my grandsons and daughters, my son, John's children, all my stock of cattle to them and theirs for every (Sic). My will is at my death what things I have not given away may be equally divided, and every (Sic) and
2 CONC  those that have got no negroes may have it made up to them in money, and lastly, I hereby appoint my son, William White and Samuel Smith shall be joint executors of this my Last Will and Testament, hereby revoking all former will
2 CONC s by me made in witness whereof I have hereunto set my hand and seal this fifth (?) day of April, One Thousand Eight Hundred and One, in the presence of us,
2 CONT
2 CONT Jas ? Rickerson Lipsey (Signed) Isaac White William Sharp (by Mark)Given Name: IsaacChange: Date: 11 NOV 2010
2 CONT
2 CONT  Isaac b 1729 Either Phila, PA or Lancaster Co VA. married Mary ___ b ca 1734 . Married ca 1750 prob Phila Co PA. Children
