1 OCCU Clerk of Lancaster County
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Joseph Tayloe died after 23 August 1716, when he wrote his will,  and before it was filed in Lancaster Co., VA court on 14 Nov 1716.
2 CONT
2 CONT In one record, his name was written as "Gentleman" Joseph William Taylor.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===family===
2 CONT
2 CONT The names of his parents are not known, but he was a nephew of William Tayloe, sheriff of York County, Virginia (d abt 1655)
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Barbara Billington BEF 22 May 1710 in Virginia, daughter of Luke Billington (Bullington) and Barbara "Barbary". She died 1726/1727 in , Lancaster Co., Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their children
2 CONT #  Joseph Tayloe b: in , Lancaster Co., Virginia
2 CONT #  Anne Tayloe b: in , Lancaster Co., Virginia
2 CONT #  Elizabeth Tayloe b: in , Lancaster Co., Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From [https://books.google.com/books?id=UCgSAAAAYAAJ&vq=TayLoe&pg=PA336#v=onepage&q&f=false page 336] of Encyclopedia of Virginia Biography, Under the Editorial ..., Volume 1 edited by Lyon Gardiner Tyler
2 CONT
2 CONT >Tayloe, William, was nephew of [https://www.geni.com/people/Col-William-Tayloe-Gent-of-Chisliake/6000000003177576035 '''Colonel William Tayloe'''], of the Virginia council. He emigrated to Virginia and in 1687 settled in Richmond
2 CONC  county, then part of Rappahannock county. He was one of the first justices of Richmond county, and in 1704, as colonel and commander-in-chief of the county militia, subdued an attempted uprising of the Indians. He was burgess for
2 CONC  Richmond county in 1700, 1701, 1702 and 1706, and died in 1710.  ....  Colonel William Tayloe had at least two brothers, '''Joseph, clerk of Lancaster county''', and Robert, a ship captain, who died in 1705.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Unknown-355621
2 CONT >Ann was tried for "bastardy and infanticide" in Sep 1714 after being seen in the vicinity of an unearthed infant's leg. The story is told in a book chapter entitled "A Parcell of Murdering Bitches".
2 CONT
2 CONT >The chapter notes mistakenly assumes that Anne was the widow of Joseph Tayloe, when he was actually William Tayloe. In Oct 1715, Joseph complained in Lancaster Co., VA court that his brother William TAYLOE had died, and William's
2 CONC  widow, Anne TAYLOE, had cohabited with James BURN and they were "vehemently disposed of fornication and incontinency." James Burn is the same one featured in the book chapter, who she later married.Surprisingly, she was named in 
2 CONC Joseph Tayloe's will, as Anne Burn, in 1716.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===supporting data===
2 CONT
2 CONT * ! Birth: (1) Brother of Col. William Tayloe of Richmond Co., VA.
2 CONT *  Marriage to Barbara Billington: (1) (2,3b) Prior to 22 May 1710.
2 CONT *  Death: (3h) Will dtd 23 Aug 1716, filed in Lancaster Co., VA court 14 Nov 1716.
2 CONT *  (1) Of Lancaster Co., VA.
2 CONT *  (2) 1696-1716: Was Clerk of Lancaster Co., VA.
2 CONT *  (3d) 1702: As Lancaster Co. Clerk, he petitioned the House of Burgesses for allowance for transmitting copies of the lists of tythables, muster rolls, etc. to the Council Office.
2 CONT *  (1) 1704, 10 Jul: (1,3a) Wrote to Mrs. Ruth TAYLOE, his sister-in-law, telling her of the death of her husband, Capt. Robert TAYLOE, addressed to her at Bassinghall St., London, under cover to Mr. Benjamin TAYLOE, Bedford St., 
2 CONC Covent Garden, London. (3a) 1705, 10 Jul: Recorded in Lancaster.
2 CONT *  (3d) 1705, 12 May: The House of Burgesses ordered that for failure to report number of tythables in their counties, Mr. Attorney General prosecute James COCK, Richard BLAND, Charles CHAPMAN, Thomas MILNER, Christopher COCK, Cha
2 CONC rles JENNINGS, Nathaniel POPE, James WISTCOMB, Joseph TAYLOE, Thomas HOBSON, Robert SNEAD and Hancock CUSTIS, Gentlemen.
2 CONT *  (3e) 1705, 10 Jul: Joseph TAYLOE wrote to Mrs. Ruth TAYLOE, "Dear Sister, Since my last to you by way of Bristoll wch gave you an acct of my Brothers death I have had no opertunity to write to you until this time dureing which 
2 CONC space I have made it my business to view ye state of your affaires in Virginia, and haveing made what progress I possibly could doe in ye times, I send you this to acquaint you therewith first then it may suffice to lett you know 
2 CONC that my brothers haveing contracted some debts in our parts on his own and the shipps account, wee durst not intermeddle with any of his Estate, but what could be done by the bye for fear of being made executors in our owne wrong 
2 CONC and soe bee made lyable for his debts here so that my brother William advised ye mate Mr. Richard CUDLIP to sell his estate att ye mast weh hee did to great advantage as you will find by ye enclosed accts. His wearinge clothes wer
2 CONC e sold for thirty pounds one shilling two pence and we find by Mr. CUDLIP his accts that he has sold other goods of his amounting to nineteen pounds three shillings and ten pence soe yt you will find CUDLIP his accts signed by his
2 CONC  own hand to amount to forty-nine pounds five shillings which he is actually your debtor for. I urged him all that I could with modesty to draw bills in your name for ye money, or to give me an obligation to pay you ye money in En
2 CONC gland when he arrived there, but could bring him to neither of those proposalls soe I gott him to sign ye accts all which I have gott recorded in our office and the originals I have herewith sent you as alsoe a memorandum: of what
2 CONC  goods are left unsold. Part of ye above sd goods were sold as you will find by ye accts signed by CUDLIP to one Thomas CLARKSON who was a seaman hired by ye month in our country and his wages were due here soe Mr. CUDLIP gave me 
2 CONC a note to Mr. Gawin CORBIN for his debts being nine pounds five shills: I can't yet advise you wither it will be paid or noe Mr. CORBIN being down att James River the shipp will come away before I can demand it. But if I gett it p
2 CONC aid it shall be sent by the next occasion. I have sent you a bill of Madam GRIFFIN for two pounds ten shills drawn on Mr. CORBIN being for goods sold by my brother in his lifetime. I find an account of Capt. Jno. TARPLEYs in my br
2 CONC others book the bal: being eleven pounds one shill: and one penny I demanded in your name and he owed but four pounds our money, wherefore I sent his account to my brother William to be recovered which you will hear on by the next
2 CONC  occasion also. Mr. John SHAW ye chyr of ye cabin to whom you are much obliged for his care in your affairs has sent me a memorandum to send you of sugar and money sent you from Jamaica which you will find inclosed comes to 10 pou
2 CONC nds, 10 shillings my Brother left sum goods with me to sell for him which was a cell of old shopkeepers vizt boots, shoes, shipers, etc. amounting to seven pounds ten shillings which I sold to a shoemaker att a lump butt am forced
2 CONC  to sue for ye money, and although I commence suit in my own name to blind ye world yett you may be sure of an honest account when recovered. You may informe yourself of Mr. SHAW ye chyrya most inteligible gent on board ye ship an
2 CONC d knows ye most of your concerns and of ye truth of all your affairs and will assist you in what lyes in his power. It is convenient to be private in what you receive lest it being known may oblidge you to pay more away than you w
2 CONC ould be willing. I sent last weeke to know my Brothers pleasure with Coz Charles who refuses to keep him here and if you don't send for him to bind him apprentice to ye sea I know no more I have to enlarge upon but recomending you
2 CONC  to ye protection of allmight God who best knows how to provide for ye widdow and fatherless. I conclude in all sincerity Dear Sister Your affectionate and loveing brother, Joseph TAYLOE." Addressed to Mrs. Ruth TAYLOE, Bassinghal
2 CONC l St., London, under cover to Mr. Benjamin TAYLOE, Bellford St., Covent Garden, London, recorded July 10, 1705, by Joseph TAYLOE, clerk.
2 CONT
2 CONT *  (2) 1710, 22 May: Joseph TAYLOE of Lancaster Co., Gentleman, and his wife Barbara, heiress of Luke BILLINGTON, Gentleman, late of Rappahannock Co., dec'd, made a deed for land patented by BILLINGTON.
2 CONT *  (3g) 1711, 12 Mar: Joseph TAYLOE with Thomas CARTER and John HEALE witnessed the verification of a deed from John WOODINGTON to William VAUGHN and VAUGHN's successors, which tract of land was patented by Joseph WOODINGTON 15 Ja
2 CONC n 1658.
2 CONT *  (3f) 1715, Oct: Joseph TAYLOE complained in Lancaster Co., VA court what his brother William TAYLOE had died, and William's widow, Anne TAYLOE, had cohabited with James BURN and they were "vehemently disposed of fornication and
2 CONC  incontinency."
2 CONT *  (3h) 1716, 23 Aug: Joseph TAYLOE wrote his will. Left his estate to wife Barbara and his two remaining children, giving his daughter his land. His sister-in-law, Ann BURNS, and Thomas THORNTON were witnesses. (3) His children w
2 CONC ere not named, but since his daughter Ann died in Oct 1716 and her inventory was taken 10 Oct and filed 14 Nov 1716, the two children of his will probably were Elizabeth and Joseph Jr., who, according to Tayloe family history, die
2 CONC d between 1714 and 1716. (NOTE: If his will was dtd 23 Aug 1716 and daughter Elizabeth was living after this date and therefore one of the two remaining children, Ann must have been the other one of the 2 remaining children if she
2 CONC  died in October, and son Joseph was deceased before that date. But if Ann died earlier than the date of the will in August and inventory of her estate was not taken until her father's decease, then son Joseph was one of the 2 rem
2 CONC aining children. Joseph was mentioned in the will of Daniel McCARTY in 1724, and therefore was probably one of the remaining children, and did not die by 1716. This would also be confirmed by the wording of the will if Joseph Sr. 
2 CONC left his lands to his daughter, which would only cause confusion if both remaining children were daughters, although it would seem strange that Joseph would will his lands to daughter Elizabeth and not his son.)
2 CONT *  (3h) 1716, 14 Nov: Will and inventory of estate filed, Lancaster Co., VA. His wife Barbara TAYLOE was executrix.
2 CONT *  (4) 1716, 14 Nov: At Lancaster Co., VA court, the county of Lancaster being indebted for 6,000 pounds of tobacco, ordered a levy of 5 3/4 pounds of tobacco by the poll to be collected by Capt. Richard BALL, Sheriff, from every 
2 CONC titheable person in the county, and he to pay the same to the several creditors, which included Mrs. Barbara TAYLOE, who was to be paid 1,000 pounds. On Mr. Thomas CARTER's list, Jos. TAYLOE was assessed for 6 titheables, and Jame
2 CONC s BURN for 1 titheable. (NOTE: Since Joseph was dead, was this his son, or was this his estate?)
2 CONT *  (3c) 1717, 1 May: Barbara TAYLOE, widow of Joseph TAYLOE of Lancaster Co., mentions slaves bought of John and Moses TAYLOR, executors of the last will of John TAYLOR deceased of Northumberland Co., VA and 300 acres of land in C
2 CONC hrist Church Parish, Lancaster Co., VA, bought by John and Moses TAYLOR of Joseph TAYLOE, late husband of Barbara.
2 CONT *  (2) 1724, 29 Mar: Daniel McCARTY, Esq., of the Parish of Cople, Westmoreland Co., VA wrote his will. Names his uncle, Mr. Joseph TAYLOE, late Clerk of Lancaster Co., his cousin Col. John TAYLOE, and aunt Barbara TAYLOE and her 
2 CONC son Joseph.
2 CONT *  (1) Probably the ancestor of the TAYLOEs of NC, TN and TX. 
2 CONT * (3) His only son died young. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===sources===
2 CONT * (1) "The Tayloes of Virginia and Allied Families," by W. Randolph Tayloe (Berryville, VA, 1963), p.1. FHL #929.273 T211t.
2 CONT *  (2) "Virginia Genealogies - A Genealogy of the Glassell Family," by Rev. Horace Edwin Hayden (Genealogical Pub. Co., Baltimore, 1966) p.84B,86.
2 CONT *  (3) Cynthia Snider, 13003 Bellevue St., Beltsville, MD 20705. (301) 572-2224. E-mail:csnider@tomco.net (1998). Cites: (a) "Virginia Historical Magazine," "Virginia Legislative Papers," Vol. 17 (1909) p.369-375. (b) "Virginia Ma
2 CONC rriages," by Wolfeck, p.17. (c) "Virginia Historical Magazine," Vol. 35, p.216. (d) "Virginia Historical Magazine," "Public Officers in Virginia, 1702, 1714," Vol. 2, p.8-9. (e) "William and Mary Quarterly," Vol. 11 (1902-1903), "
2 CONC Old Letters from Virginia County Records," p.171-174. (f) Marriages of Richmond Co., VA, p.205-206. (g) Lnacaster Co., VA Deeds, Vol. 9, p.382. (h) Lancaster Co., VA Will Bk 10, p.188,197.
2 CONT *  (4) "Virginia Tax Records from The Virginia Magazine of History and Biography, the William and Mary College Quarterly, and Tyler's Quarterly" (Genealogical Pub. Co., Baltimore, 1983; reproduced on Broderbund Software's Family A
2 CONC rchive CD#174, Virginia Vital Records #1, 1600s-1800s), Tithables in Lancaster Co., 1716, p.268,271.
2 CONT
