1 NOTE Will of James Hackley:
2 CONT Pages 203-204
2 CONT In the name of God Amen.  I James Hackley of the Parish of Brunswick in the County of King George being sick and weak of body but of perfect mind and memory do make and ordain this my last will and Testament in manner and form fol
2 CONC lowing.
2 CONT Item:  I give and bequeath to my loving son John Hackley all that dividend of land lying in Essex County to himand his heirs forever, and if any dispute should arise and the said land be taken away then I give my loving son John H
2 CONC ackley and equal share of my land in Prince William with muyother five sons to him and his heirs forever.
2 CONT Item:  I give and bequeath unto mylopving sons Joseph, Lott, Richard, James and francis Hackley all my land lying in Prince William to be equally divided between the, to them and their heirs forever, but if it chance that any of m
2 CONC y aforesaid sons should dye before they arrive to the age of Twenty One years their parts so dying shall be equally divided amonghst the survivors.
2 CONT Item:  It is my desire that my loving wife Elizabeth Hackleyhave the use and advantage of all my labouring Negroes (viz) Captain, Dinah and Pender during her natural life and after her decease them and all the rest of my Negroes t
2 CONC o be equally divided amongst all my children then living.
2 CONT Item:  It is my desire that my loving wife Elizageth Hackleyhave the use and advantage of all my stock of hogs, cattle and sheep upon both my plantations furing her natural life and after to be divided amongst all my chldren then 
2 CONC living.
2 CONT Item:  And as to the horses and mares which I have not yet given to my children, I leave to the disposal of my loving wife Elizabeth Hackley.
2 CONT Item:  It is my will and desire that my loving wife Elizabeth Hackley have the use and advantage of all the rest of my personal estate during her natural life and after her decease to be equally divided amongst all my children the
2 CONC n living, butas to the ready cash which I have by me with which I intended to buy two young Negro boys, I desire it may be put to the same use by my  Executors and Executrix herafter mentioned.  It is my desire that my loving wife
2 CONC  Elizabeth Hackley, my sons John and Joseph Hackley be Exexutrix and Executors of this my last will and Testament, utterly revoking all former wills by me at any time made.  I do hereby acknowledge this my last Will and Testament.
2 CONC   As witness my hand and seale this second day of November 1741.  It is mywill and desire that my estate may not be appraised.  James Hackley (seal)  Signed  Sealed and Acknowledged in the Presence of us:  John Gilbert, Samuel Ree
2 CONC ds, John Carter
2 CONT
2 CONT Name: James HACKLEY Given Name: James Surname: HACKLEY Sex: M Birth: Abt 1690 in Rappahannock County, Virginia Death: 1748 in Brunswick Parish, King George County, Virginia
2 CONT All the Hackley's of early Virginia appear to be decended from James, even though there were others that emigrated at about the same time.  James was a landowner in Essex County as late as 1719, but appears in King George County, 
2 CONC Hanover Parish, in 1724. The land he owned in King George was about 15 miles below Fredericksburg on the Rappahannock River. Hackley's Ferry operated from his land, with a route across the river to Moss Neck plantation in Caroline
2 CONC  County.  On 30 Sep 1724, James was granted 740 acres in King George County by Robert "King" Carter who was acting as an agent for Lord Fairfax. This land grant lay in that part of King George which is now a part of Fanquier Count
2 CONC y on the western side of Summerduck Run close to the Rappahannock River. For this land James paid 10 shillings sterling for each 50 acres and each year thereafter a quit rent of 1 shilling for each 50 acres, payable on the Feast o
2 CONC f St. Michael the Archangel. On 18 Apr 1741,James obtained another grant for 126 acres adjoining the first tract. These tow tracts were located on what is now the right side of the road leading from highway 17 by way of Summerduck
2 CONC  to Remington, just beyond Summerduck Run in southeastern Fauquier County. His 5 sons eventually inherited equal parts of the plantation.
2 CONT SOURCE: Woodford B. Hackley, NOTES ON THE DESCENDANTS OF JOHN HACKLEY OF OLD RAPPAHANNOCK AND MIDDLESEX COUNTIES, VIRGINIA (Richmond: author, 1968), 28 BUR: Kentucky DAR Bible and Cemetery Records, 1962, v 1 pt 2, 49. Visited 1961
2 CONC ; not found 1994 DEATH: Will dated 2 Nov 1741, proved 6 May 1748. King George Will Book A-1, n.p., in Sparacio, 42. 
2 CONT   Father: John HACKLEY  Mother: Elizabeth LAMBERT b: Abt 1665 in Rappahannock County, Virginia  Marriage 1 Elizabeth SHIPPEY  b: Abt 1703 in King George County, Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT John HACKLEY b: Bef 1720 in King George County, Virginia
2 CONT Joseph HACKLEY  b: Abt 1728 in King George County, Virginia
2 CONT Lott HACKLEY b: Abt 1730 in King George County, Virginia
2 CONT Ellen HACKLEY  b: Abt 1732 in Summerduck, Fauquer, Virginia
2 CONT Mary HACKLEY b: Abt 1734 in Summerduck, Fauquer, Virginia
2 CONT Francis HACKLEY  b: 16 May 1740 in Summerduck, Fauquer, Virginia
2 CONT James HACKLEY b: Abt 1742 in Summerduck, Fauquer, Virginia
2 CONT Lucy HACKLEY b: 10 Jun 1743 in King George County, Virginia
