1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Eleanor Dunn, widow of Robert Green, survived her husband 45 years, also four of her seven sons. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her seven sons were:
2 CONT *1. William Green
2 CONT *2. Duff Green
2 CONT *3. Robert Green
2 CONT *4. John Green
2 CONT *5. Nicholas Green
2 CONT *6. James Green
2 CONT *7. Moses Green
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/L8SM-HTB
2 CONT
2 CONT To identify herself in later years on account of having grandchildren bearing the same name, she frequently signed papers as "Eleanor, the aged." Following the death of her husband Culpeper County was formed and her will is found 
2 CONC there, probated Dec. 16,1793. It is as follows:                                           
2 CONT
2 CONT THE WILL OF ELEANOR DUNN GREEN
2 CONT
2 CONT In the name of God Amen, I, Eleanor Green of the Parish of St. Mark's and County of Culpeper in the State of Virginia, being aged and infirm but of sound mind and memory as usual, thanks to the Almighty God for that blessing, call
2 CONC ing to mind the uncertain duration of this life and knowing that it is appointed to all mankind, once to die, do make and ordain this my last will and testament in manner and form following, to wit:
2 CONT
2 CONT Principally and first of all, I do with all humility and conviction for my past offenses, command my soul to Almighty God who gave it. Trusting that through the merits and mediation of my blessed Lord and Savior Jesus Christ after
2 CONC  this mortal life is ended, I shall enjoy everlasting felicity. My body I bequeath to earth from whence it came, therein to be decently interred at the direction of my Executors and as for worldly goods and effects wherewith it ha
2 CONC th pleased God to bless me, I dispose of in the manner and form following:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1st. It is my desire that my funeral expenses and trust debts be first satisfied and paid.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2nd. I give and bequeath to my son, Robert Green, to him, his heirs and assigns forever all my part of land which myself and other plaintiffs are at law with the late Lord Fairfax and other debts on Shenando River and the waters t
2 CONC hereof when the said land shall be recovered together with all damages which may hereafter be recovered for grant of the said lands as all money that may hereafter appear to be due from the other parties from any sales that they m
2 CONC ay have made and converted the money to their use as also any other advantage or advantages that may hereafter appear to be due or arise from the said lands. I give and bequeath likewise to my foresaid son, Robert Green, his heirs
2 CONC  and assigns forever.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3rd. I give and bequeath to my foresaid son, Robert Green, all stock and hogs that be in prospect at the time of my death likewise all kind of crops that I may then have on the grounds unfinished to him my son, Robert Green, his h
2 CONC eirs and assigns forever.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4th. I give and bequeath to my granddaughter, Eleanor Duff Green, my riding mare called Snip, also my side saddle and mourning ring, describing the time of my son, Duff Green's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5th. I give and bequeath to my three sons now living, viz; Robert Green, John Green, and James Green, all the remaining part of my estate to be equally divided among them, their heirs and assigns forever.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6th. And lastly, I do hereby nominate and appoint my three sons, Robert Green, John Green, and James Green, Executors of this my Last Will and Testament, hereby revoking all other former wills by me made and declaring this to be m
2 CONC y Last Will and Testament. In witness whereof I have hereunto set my hand, assigned my seal this nineteenth day of May in the year of our Lord, 1784. Signed, sealed and acknowledged In the presence of us Eleanor Green (S.S.) John 
2 CONC Strode Isaac Herrin Elizabeth Herrin
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT No documentation found for dates of birth and death.  The branch (merge) of the line of Ann and George Reeves appears at least partially incorrect: the line has no documentation and has an improbable and undocumented history appen
2 CONC ded to it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Listed among the older children in their father's will, and therefore believed to be his child by his first wife. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Lancaster County VA records, Will Book #10 1709-1727, as researched by James D. Doggett, Doggettfam.org (http://doggettfam.org/100benjamin.htm).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Listed among the youngest children in their father's will, and therefore believed to have been his child by his second wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Lancaster County VA records, Will Book #10 1709-1727, as researched by James D. Doggett, Doggettfam.org (http://doggettfam.org/100benjamin.htm).
2 CONT BettyWoodulWalsh 
2 CONT Search
2 CONT Eleanor Dunn, immigrant
2 CONT 1698–1793
2 CONT Birth 20 MARCH 1698 • Dundonald, Ayrshire, Strathclyde, Scotland, United Kingdom
2 CONT Death 16 DECEMBER 1793 • Brandy Station, Culpeper County, Virginia, United States of America
2 CONT LifeStory Facts Gallery
2 CONT Summary
2 CONT When Eleanor Dunn, immigrant was born on March 20, 1698, in Dundonald, Ayrshire, Scotland, her father, John, was 36, and her mother, Mary, was 36. She married Robert Duff Green, Burgess, immigrant in 1720 in Orange, Virginia. They
2 CONC  had one child during their marriage. She died on December 16, 1793, in Brandy Station, Virginia, at the impressive age of 95, and was buried there.
