1 OCCU Carpenter, Private French&Indian War, Revolutionary War. Story: Lewis Green and the bear, told by Daniel Boone, Draper Manuscripts., in French and Indian War, Dinwiddie, Virginia, USA   Carpenter by trade
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''https://www.familysearch.org/tree/pedigree/landscape/L1Y4-S3S
2 CONT
2 CONT '''A Patriot of the American Revolution for VIRGINIA.  [http://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A047197  DAR Ancestor #  A047197]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a Private who served under Captain Robert McKenzie in Tennessee and Kentucky. He was stationed at Fort Nashboro in Nashville,Tennessee. He served in the French and Indian War.
2 CONT
2 CONT He enlisted in Capt Robert McKenzie's Co from Dinwiddie Co., VA at age 30 at the rank of Private in the French and Indian War and also served in the Rev War in VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a vestryman in the church at Kilgore Station (Church of England? ) and was very brown in complexion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lewis owned 41 acres of land adjacent to Porter's Fort in March of 1774.
2 CONT
2 CONT A story about Lewis Green, Sr. told by Daniel Boone
2 CONT
2 CONT Occurred near the Clinch River
2 CONT
2 CONT Lewis and a brother-in-law, who resided near Blackmore's, on Clinch, about fifteen miles below Captain Gass' place, where Boone was sojourning, went out some considerable distance among the mountains to hunt. They selected a good 
2 CONC hunting range, erected a cabin, and laid up in store's some jerked bear meat. One day when Green was alone, his companion being absent on the chase, a large bear made his appearance near camp, upon which Green shot and wounded the
2 CONC  animal, which at the moment chanced to be in a sort of sink-hole at the base of a hill. Taking a circuit to get above and head the bear there being a slight snow upon the ground covered with sleet, Green's feet slipped from under
2 CONC  him, and in spite of all his efforts to stop, himself, he partly slid and partly rolled down the declivity till he found himself in the sink-hole, where the wounded bear, enraged by his pain, flew at poor Green, tore and mangled 
2 CONC his body in a shocking manner, totally destroying one of his eyes. When the bear had sufficiently gratified his revenge by gnawing his unresisting victim as as he wished, he suddenly departed, leaving the unfortunate hunter in a h
2 CONC elpless and deplorable condition, all exposed, with his clothing torn in tatters, to the severities of the season.
2 CONT
2 CONT His comrade at length returning, found and took him to camp. After awhile, thinking it impossible for Green to recover, his companion went out on pretense of hunting for fresh meat, and unfeelingly abandoned poor Green to his fate
2 CONC , reporting in the settlements that he had been killed by a bear. His [Green] little fire soon died away from his inability to provide fuel. Digging, with his knife, a hole or nest beside him in the ground-floor of his cabin, he m
2 CONC anaged to reach some wild turkey which had been saved, and with them lined the excavation and made himself quite a comfortable bed; and with the knife fastened to the end of a stick, he cut down, from time to time, bits of dried b
2 CONC ear meat hanging over head, and upon this he sparingly subsisted. Recovering slowly, he could at length manage to get about. When spring opened, a party, of whom Boone is believed to have been on, went from Blackmore's settlement 
2 CONC to bury Green's remains, with the brute of a brother-in-law for a guide; and to their utter astonishment, they met Green plodding his way towards home, and learned the sad story of his sufferings and desertion. The party were so i
2 CONC ndignant that they could scarcely refrain from laying violent hands on a wretch guilty of so much inhumanity to a helpless companion. Green though greatly disfigured lived many years.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT 1) - Lewis Green, Sr. was born ca 1710, place unknown; the Green family
2 CONT
2 CONT settled in Washington County, VA when it was still a part of Botetourte
2 CONT
2 CONT County; he may have been the grandson of Lewis Burwell Green, who, in
2 CONT
2 CONT 1720 was the head of a prominent Gloucester County, VA family, or he may
2 CONT
2 CONT have been related to the Green family of Culpepper, VA; he made his will on
2 CONT
2 CONT 9 Jan 1784, while moving with his daughter, Sarah Payne, wife
2 CONT
2 CONT of Josiah Payne, to Nasbore (Nashville), TN.
2 CONT
2 CONT The wife of Lewis Green, Sr. (surname said to have been
2 CONT
2 CONT Lauderdale)seems to have been have been the "Elizabeth" who was the
2 CONT
2 CONT mother-in-law of Moses Foley, who moved to Knox County, KY ca 1810,
2 CONT
2 CONT and who performed many marriages as a minister and as a Justice of the
2 CONT
2 CONT Peace.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) - Note: Info received in 1995 identifies wife "Elizabeth" as Elizabeth
2 CONT
2 CONT Lauderdale, daughter of James Maitland Lauderdale, who was the 3rd son of
2 CONT
2 CONT the 5th Earl of Lauderdale, of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3) - Here is the "Will" (e-mail from David Green <
2 CONT
2 CONT d..green@worldnet.att.net > 4/04/97):
2 CONT
2 CONT Note #1 - - A testimony filled in support of a nuncupative will, filed
2 CONT
2 CONT in Davidson County, North Carolina/ Tennessee on 4 April 1785.  Page 26 of
2 CONT
2 CONT the actual early Probate and Death Records.  Source:  "Tennessee
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestors the Brave and the Dead, Probate and Death Records of Early
2 CONT
2 CONT Middle Tennessee 1780 - 1805", Page 6.  (Note:  Tennessee did not
2 CONT
2 CONT become a state until 1796.  Prior to this, Davidson County was part of
2 CONT
2 CONT the "Mero District" of North Carolina.)
2 CONT
2 CONT "The Testimony of Sundry Person Concerning a Nuncupative Will made by
2 CONT
2 CONT Lewis Green, Deceased.  Sarah Payne, Daughter of the Desd. and Wife of
2 CONT
2 CONT Josiah Payne of full Age Deposeth That her father the Testator made his
2 CONT
2 CONT abode in Washington County on Clinch River wt her and her husband; And
2 CONT
2 CONT while he lived wt them made a Written Will As the Testator told her in which
2 CONT
2 CONT he Bequeath a certain Negro fellow named Charles, to be Sold and the
2 CONT
2 CONT Money to be Equally Divided Among all his Children That Sometime
2 CONT
2 CONT Afterwards in the Month of October last:  Her father the testator And
2 CONT
2 CONT all her husband's family Removed to the Settlement on Cumberland
2 CONT
2 CONT Before her father left Clinch:  (About Eighteen Months before) he left the
2 CONT
2 CONT Written Will wt their (Neighbor (?) Benjamin Nicholas:  and not Long
2 CONT
2 CONT before their Removal gave the sd Nicholas a Charge not to make any use
2 CONT
2 CONT of this Will Untill he heard from the Testor; for when he got to
2 CONT
2 CONT Cumberland he should Alter it and leave this Negro (to) his Son Zachariah
2 CONT
2 CONT Green because all his other Children were Married and gone from him:
2 CONT
2 CONT And had Moreover Received the Portion which he Allotted them all but
2 CONT
2 CONT his Son Zachariah.  That on the Road to Cumberland at the place called
2 CONT
2 CONT the Blue Spring her father was Very Sick; he called up the Most of the
2 CONT
2 CONT Grown people that were Encamped wt them And after Asking this
2 CONT
2 CONT (deponent) if she was Willing her Brother Zachariah Should have the
2 CONT
2 CONT Negro, and being answered in the Affirmative Declared before the Deponent
2 CONT
2 CONT and Sundry other persons Particularly Mary Payne her Mother in Law. Geo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Payne her brother in law And Jno. Payne:  That the sd Negro Charles Should
2 CONT
2 CONT be his Son's Zachariah And Should Serve no other person. And that his son
2 CONT
2 CONT Zachariah Should Pay all his Debts:  That this Would
2 CONT
2 CONT then have been put in writting at the Time, but there was no Materials to
2 CONT
2 CONT write wt in the Camp:  And in about Three Days Afterwards her father
2 CONT
2 CONT the Testor Dies:  And further saith not:  Which was subscribed by the
2 CONT
2 CONT Mark of the sd Sarah Payne.  Mary Payne Wife of Mathew Payne of full
2 CONT
2 CONT Age Deposeth and Saith That She did for a long time live a Neighbour to
2 CONT
2 CONT Lewis Green The Testator, Was Acquainted with his Making the Written
2 CONT
2 CONT Will and Was present When he made the nuncupative Will and Knows that
2 CONT
2 CONT What Daughter in law Sarah Payne Deposed Above; is the Truth:  And
2 CONT
2 CONT further Saith not.  Subscribed by Mary Payne........
2 CONT
2 CONT Roland Miracle note:  In the next to last line above Mary Payne must have
2 CONT
2 CONT meant to say "Daughter Sarah Payne" in her  statement, rather than
2 CONT
2 CONT "Daughter in law Sarah Payne" since there is no question as to the father
2 CONT
2 CONT daughter relationship.
2 CONT
