2 CONT
2 CONT Francis Slaughter I [Parents] 1, 2, 3 was born about 1701 in Essex or Culpeper County, Virginia. He died before 1766 in Culpeper County, Virginia. He married Anne Lightfoot in 1729.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1Slaughter Genforum.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2Family Tree Maker #4 #3439. FICKLING FAMILY HISTORY.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3GEDCOM file imported on 24 Sep 2000.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Children 	
2 CONT 1. Francis Slaughter,   b. 1730, Spotsylvania County, Virginia     d. 1805, Elizabethtown, Hardin County, Kentucky  
2 CONT 2. John Slaughter,   b. 1732, Spotsylvania County, Virginia     d. 29 Aug 1796, St. Mark's Parish, Culpeper County, Virginia   	 
2 CONT 3. Reuben Slaughter,   b. 1733, Spotsylvania County, Virginia    d. Aft 1800, Bedford County, Virginia  
2 CONT 4. Cadwallader Slaughter,   b. Abt 1735, Spotsylvania County, Virginia    d. 6 Mar 1798, Louisville, Jefferson County, Kentucky   	
2 CONT 5. Frances Slaughter,   b. 1737, St. Mark's Parish, Spotsylvania County, Virginia     d. Aft 1766, St. Mark's Parish, Culpeper County, Virginia  
2 CONT 6. Mary Slaughter,   b. Bef 1739, Culpeper County, Virginia   d. Aft 1769, St. Thomas Parish, Orange County, Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT *****************************************
2 CONT Francis Slaughter and his brother Robert were the sons of Robert Slaughter and his wife, Frances Ann Jones. The sons were the first church wardens of St. Mark's Parish, Spottsylvania County, Va., in 1731. That portion of the count
2 CONC y became Culpepper County, Virginia. in 1748. According to a deed recorded in Hardin Co., Kentucky, in 1804, Francis was the eldest son of Robert &amp;amp;amp; Frances Slaughter. Francis became a vestryman and remained in that pos
2 CONC ition until his death in 1766.
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis was married June 3, 1729, to Ann Lightfoot of Spottsylvania County. She was the daughter of Major Goodrich Lightfoot and Mary Chew, born September 22, 1708. Francis and Anne had six children. Francis was commissioned Capta
2 CONC in of Militia on Feb 2, 1730, and later rose to the rank of Colonel. Francis was a prominent man in the county, and owned a large tract of land, including the old glebe, near what is know as "Brandy Station", adjoining the land of
2 CONC  his sons, Reuben and Cadwallader. Francis' will was dated September 22, 1765, and was proved in May, 1766. Anne was alive at the date of the will.
