1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Cadwallader Slaughter was original owner of the Slaughter farm in the Cherokee Triangle neighborhood of Louisville. His Land Grant from 1779 was for 800 acres, some of which was given for the completion of Cherokee
2 CONC  Park over 100  years later. It fronted on Bardstown Road roughly between Slaughter's Lane (Ave.) now known as Patterson Ave. and Cherokee Parkway extending back to beyond Ray Ave., approximately half of the entire neighborhood. T
2 CONC he original log cabin is said to have been on Willow Ave. In 1907 a map shows the land (about 16 acres at this point) behind the cabin as belonging to his Great Granddaughter Fanny L. Slaughter, who passed in 1919. Almost the enti
2 CONC rety of Glenmary Ave. was then developed (originally called Mary's Glen after Fanny's mother), as well as Bassett Ave. (originally known as Chigger Hill). Ransdell Ave. is likely named for Cad's wife Margaret or his son Francis Ra
2 CONC nsdell Slaughter. Bassett Ave. is likely named for his Great Granddaughter Elizabeth Frances Bassett, wife of Squire Bassett (ancestors of Ted Bassett of Keeneland). Granddaughter Matilda married Joseph Longest and they acquired a
2 CONC  portion of the farm, now Longest Ave. and Cherokee Parkway.
2 CONT
