1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Evidence needed to support https://www.geni.com/people/Benjamin-Lillard-Sr/1668366 as son of https://www.geni.com/people/Jean-Lillard/1668558?through=1668366 and https://www.geni.com/people/Mildred-Lillard/1668560?
2 CONC through=1668366.  They’ve been detached.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === Biography: Benjamin Lillard, Sr. (c. 1715 – c. 1793)
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin Lillard, Sr. was an 18th-century colonial planter settled in the Virginia Piedmont region. He lived and operated primarily within Culpeper County (formed from Orange County in 1749), where he managed agricultural holdings
2 CONC  and raised a family that formed a distinct genealogical cluster in the area.
2 CONT
2 CONT Based on the chronological timeline of his verified children and his appearances in local county dockets, his life span is estimated from circa 1715 to circa 1793. His estate and primary family associations remained centered in Cu
2 CONC lpeper County until his death, after which several of his children migrated westward into Kentucky and Tennessee, while others remained on the ancestral lands in Madison and Culpeper counties.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT === Identity and Spurious Pedigree Warning
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The traditional data fields and older biographical narratives for Benjamin Lillard, Sr. are heavily corrupted by unverified claims originating in David Hicks Lillard’s 1928 book, *Lillard: A Family of England and Virginia*.'
2 CONT
2 CONT Specific assertions that he was born on November 30, 1700, died in Isle of Wight County in 1781, and had a decorated colonial military career under George Washington at Fort Duquesne, William Fleming in Lord Dunmore's War, and Mic
2 CONC hael Cresap at Fort Pitt are total fabrications unsupported by any primary records.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT === The 1723 Marriage Myth and Generation Slippage
2 CONT
2 CONT Traditional pedigrees assert that Benjamin Lillard married "Elizabeth Ann Lightfoot" on '''November 30, 1723'''. Curatorial auditing reveals that this date is an architectural impossibility for the following reasons:
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1. '''Total Absence of Record:''' No parish register, marriage bond, or civil court record exists in the Province of Virginia to document a Lillard-Lightfoot marriage on this date, or any other date in the 18th century.
2 CONT * 2. '''Biological and Chronological Contradiction:''' Forcing a marriage date of November 30, 1723, creates a severe identity smash-up when aligned with the birth years of Benjamin's actual, verified children. Several of his chil
2 CONC dren—such as William Jackson Lillard, Capt. James Lillard, and others—were born well into the late 1750s and 1760s. A single wife married in 1723 cannot continuously bear children across a span of forty years.
2 CONT * 3. '''The Purpose of the Date:''' This precise date was invented in the early 20th century to create a neat, mathematical alignment with the fabricated 1700 birth date assigned to Benjamin and the 1704 birth date assigned to the
2 CONC  fictional Elizabeth. It functions purely as name conflation designed to anchor a spurious pedigree.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT === Disputed Parentage: The Jean Lillard Legend
2 CONT
2 CONT The traditional assignment of Benjamin's parents as '''Jean Lillard''' (alleged French Huguenot refugee) and '''Mildred Jones''' is an unproven genealogical narrative that collapses under archival scrutiny.
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1. '''Zero Primary Evidence:''' There is no "Jean Lillard" matching this description in any surviving Virginia ship passenger list, naturalization record, land patent, or tithable list for the early 18th century. The name is com
2 CONC pletely absent from the detailed registers of known Virginia Huguenot settlements like Manakin Town.
2 CONT * 2. '''Onomastic Patterns:''' The name "Jean" appears to have been retroactively applied by 20th-century researchers seeking a romantic French origin for the family. In authentic colonial records, the family names are thoroughly 
2 CONC English (John, Benjamin, James, William).
2 CONT * 3. '''Current Status:''' Until a contemporary court record, deed, or probate document links Benjamin, Sr. to an older generation, his parentage remains **historically unknown**.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT === Children Audit
2 CONT
2 CONT The traditional pedigree packed the immediate family tree with unproven assertions, creating severe generation slippage. Curatorial logic divides the traditional slate into two distinct groups:
2 CONT
2 CONT === Verified Children
2 CONT These individuals are well-documented in Virginia Piedmont records (land deeds, probate, and marriage bonds) and are firmly established in this generation:
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1. '''Captain John Lillard''' (c. 1737 – 1801) — Revolutionary War officer who migrated to Mercer County, Kentucky, leaving a detailed will. His timeline completely disproves the 1723 marriage narrative.
2 CONT * 2. '''Captain James L. Lillard, Sr.''' (c. 1755 – c. 1838) — Served in the Culpeper militia; remained in the Culpeper/Madison County area and is heavily documented in local land transactions.
2 CONT * 3. '''Elizabeth Lillard''' (c. 1748 – ?) — Married John Bradley in Culpeper County; validated through local marriage and family land associations.
2 CONT * 4. '''William Jackson Lillard''' (c. 1759 – 1800) — Documented in Culpeper County records; married Anne Flippin.
2 CONT * 5. '''Sarah Lillard''' (c. 1760 – ?) — Married a Bradley (often cited as William Bradley); tracks with the younger cluster of children.
2 CONT * 6. '''Benjamin Lillard, Jr.''' (c. 1762 – 1823) — Remained in Culpeper/Madison County, establishing a clear, verified line of descent.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Unverified, Misidentified, or Duplicate Children
2 CONT These profiles lack contemporary primary documentation and represent name conflation or chronological fabrications from the 1928 narrative:
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1. '''Nancy Lillard (Garrett)''' — Placement in this exact generation lacks primary evidence; frequently conflated with a granddaughter.
2 CONT * 2. '''Moses Lillard, Sr.''' — Claims of direct parentage by Benjamin, Sr. rely on circular pedigree reasoning; direct primary land or probate ties linking the North Carolina/Tennessee Moses Lillard to Culpeper are missing.
2 CONT * 3. '''Thomas Jefferson Lillard, Sr.''' — A blatant chronological anachronism. The use of "Thomas Jefferson" as a given name would not occur in the 1730s or 1740s before the author of the Declaration of Independence achieved prom
2 CONC inence. This profile is a later descendant dragged back two generations.
2 CONT * 4. '''Clara Lillard''' (c. 1740) — Her existence rests entirely on the 1928 publication without a single supporting deed, tithable list, or court order.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT === Curatorial Actions for Profile Maintenance
2 CONT
2 CONT To resolve this identity conflict and preserve site cleanliness, curators must enforce the following structural limits:
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1. '''Purge Vitals:''' All specific dates of November 30, 1700, and November 23, 1781, along with the Isle of Wight and Warsaw locations, must be wiped from the data fields.
2 CONT * 2. '''Purge Marriage Data:''' The marriage date of November 30, 1723, must be deleted from the primary timeline events.
2 CONT * 3. '''Spouse and Parent Fields:''' Ensure the spouse profile remains renamed to "Unknown Wife" and completely detached from any historical Lightfoot lineages. Parent fields for Jean Lillard and Mildred Jones should be detached o
2 CONC r heavily flagged as unproven.
2 CONT * 4. '''Unverified Offspring Isolation:''' A dedicated [https://www.geni.com/people/Placeholder-Lillard/6000000225904780901?through=6000000217319823010 placeholder profile] representing the unverified branches from the 1928 narrat
2 CONC ive has been created. The traditional, unproven children (Nancy, Moses, Thomas Jefferson, and Clara) must be attached to this placeholder lineage rather than allowed to clutter or distort the verified descendant lines of Benjamin 
2 CONC Lillard, Sr.
