2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===  Biography: Unknown Wife of Benjamin Lillard, Sr.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Identity and Spurious Pedigree Status:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The identity of the wife (or wives) of Benjamin Lillard, Sr. (c. 1715 – c. 1793) remains historically unverified. In early 20th-century genealogical publications—most notably David Hicks Lillard’s *Lillard: A Family of England and
2 CONC  Virginia* (1928)—she was identified as "Elizabeth Ann Lightfoot" with specific birth and death dates (February 13, 1704 – November 23, 1787).
2 CONT
2 CONT Forensic auditing of contemporary 18th-century colonial records has proved this identity and its accompanying dates to be a total fabrication. No primary source—including parish registers, deeds, wills, or court records in Richmon
2 CONC d, Orange, or Culpeper counties—documents the existence of an Elizabeth Ann Lightfoot married to Benjamin Lillard.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin Lillard, Sr. was an 18th-century colonial planter settled in the Virginia Piedmont region, operating primarily within Culpeper County (formed from Orange County in 1749). Based on the chronological timeline of his verifie
2 CONC d children and his appearances in local land and court dockets, his life span is estimated from circa 1715 to circa 1793. His true parentage and the identity of his wife (or wives) remain historically unknown, as the traditional 1
2 CONC 928 Huguenot origins narrative and the ''Elizabeth Ann Lightfoot'' identity have been debunked by forensic archival analysis.
2 CONT
2 CONT The specific marriage date of '''November 30, 1723''' traditionally assigned to this couple is a total chronological fabrication. Forcing a 1723 marriage creates catastrophic generation slippage when aligned with the birth years o
2 CONC f Benjamin's actual children, who were born across a span of nearly thirty years.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Verified Children
2 CONT
2 CONT These children are well-documented in Virginia Piedmont records (land deeds, probate, and marriage bonds) and are clearly identified as part of the immediate Culpeper County Lillard cluster:
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Captain John Lillard''' (c. 1737 – 1801) — A prominent Revolutionary War officer. He migrated to Kentucky (Mercer County) and left a highly detailed will naming his children. His birth year completely destroys the validity of
2 CONC  the 1724/1723 marriage narrative.
2 CONT * '''Captain James Lillard, Sr.''' (c. 1755 – c. 1838) — Remained in the Culpeper/Madison County area. He served in the Culpeper militia during the Revolution and is heavily documented in local land transactions.
2 CONT * '''William Jackson Lillard''' (c. 1759 – 1800) — Documented in Culpeper County records; married Anne Flippin. He is frequently confused with later generations due to name repetition.
2 CONT * '''Elizabeth Lillard''' (c. 1748 – ?) — Married John Bradley in Culpeper County. Validated through local marriage and family land associations.
2 CONT * '''Sarah Lillard''' (c. 1760 – ?) — Married a Bradley (often cited as William Bradley). Her timeline tracks with the younger cluster of Benjamin’s children.
2 CONT * '''Benjamin Lillard, Jr.''' (c. 1762 – 1823) — Remained in Culpeper/Madison County, establishing a clear line of descent firmly rooted in Piedmont records.
2 CONT * 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === Curatorial Actions and Database Integrity
2 CONT
2 CONT To correct this name conflation and protect the integrity of both the Lillard and Lightfoot lineages, the following structural changes have been executed:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. '''Name and Dates Purged:''' The fictional name "Elizabeth Ann Lightfoot" and all specific dates/locations have been wiped from the data fields. The profile is now maintained as a placeholder for the '''Unknown Wife of Benjamin
2 CONC  Lillard, Sr.''' to prevent researchers from recreating the spurious profile.
2 CONT 2. '''Lineage Detachment:''' This profile must remain strictly detached from any historical Lightfoot families of Virginia. Attaching her to verified colonial lines introduces generation slippage and corrupts authentic ancestral t
2 CONC racks.
2 CONT 3. '''Maternal Conflation Warning:''' Because Benjamin Lillard, Sr.'s children were born across a wide span of years, forcing them into a single maternal block under an unverified wife creates severe chronological inconsistencies.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Lightfoot-38 Additional info found here.  7/27/2018 by Candice L.F. Tucker
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Nota Bene:   Added 29 Jun 2015 by Donald F Colvin
2 CONT
2 CONT There is verbal history passed down that Elizabeth Lee was first cousin to Gen Robert E Lee.
2 CONT
2 CONT This profile even gives the source of that information as the book:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Lillard, A Family of Colonial VA-1415 to 1928" by Jacques Ephraim Stout Lillard, published in 1928.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacques Lillard worked for decades at the Library of Congress and his credentials as a  researcher are clearly unimpeachable.
2 CONT
2 CONT However - upon obtaining the book from archives.org and reading it - it actually  gives no single reference to any connection between the Lee and Lillard families.
2 CONT
2 CONT The source is good - its use as a citation on this profile is incorrect as far as proving the connection between the Lee and Lillard families.
2 CONT
2 CONT At this point - there is no tangible known link between "The Lee's" and this Lillard family.
2 CONT
2 CONT If anyone finds such a connection - please document it here and feel free to remove this notation.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
