1 NAME William /Daniel/ Sr
2 GIVN William
2 SURN Daniel
2 NSFX Sr
2 _MARNM Daniel
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE BET 1710 AND 1714
2 ADDR
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY Colonial America
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1775
2 ADDR
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY Colonial America
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Born between 1710-1714, William Daniel was the son of James Daniel and Margaret Vivian.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Elizabeth (Watkins) Woodson — married May 1734 in Goochland, Cumberland, Virginia. Widow of Jacob [Benjamin] Woodson.
2 CONT
2 CONT “ 5. WILLIAM DANIEL, (James'), b. 1710–14 (?); d—; m. 1740 (?), ELIZABETH (WATKINS) WOODSON, widow of Jacob Woodson.
2 CONT
2 CONT By her first marriage Mrs. Daniel had Jacob, Joel and Susanna. Mr. D. was "tall, of fair skin, and blue eyes. His sons were all farmers and his daughters m. farmers. His wife was noted for industry and lived to be upwards of 90 ye
2 CONC ars old” (Mrs. Worthy Allen.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Father of Bettie (Daniel) Nelson, John Daniel, Mary (Daniel) Fuqua, Woodson Daniel, William Pride Daniel, Mary Daniel, William Daniel II, Benjamin Daniel, Sarah (Daniel) Hancock, Sallie (Daniel) Womack, Judith (Daniel) Fuqua, John
2 CONC  Daniel, Rhoda (Daniel) Nelson, Mary Polly Daniel and Hezekiah Daniel
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Until proof of the contrary, ...
2 CONT
2 CONT PROOF TO THE CONTRARY
2 CONT "Daniel Families of the Southern States" by Charles Brunk Heineman, 1934
2 CONT Mr Heineman is a descendant of Capt. William Daniel, the progenitor of the Midlesex County line of Daniel's. 
2 CONT In his book it lists this William Daniel as descended from John Daniel of York County, Virginia.
2 CONT He also comments on how this William Daniel was mistaken into the Middlesex County Daniel's.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Daniel is credited as son of James and Margaret Vivian for these reasons: First, While the vestry book of Middlesex Par. gives seven children of James, there is a gap of eight years between his sons Charles and James into 
2 CONC which William from his supposed age could legitimately be placed. Second, the vestry book is in such a dilapidated condition that it is difficult to decipher all it's records, and the entire family of James may not have been disco
2 CONC vered. Third, Maj. J.W. Daniel states that when a boy he "called with his father on Mr. Justice P.V. Daniel in Washington, soon after his wife was burnt, and well remembers that they each greeted the other as a kinsman." Mrs. W.P.
2 CONC  Conway states (1880) that Judge Wm. Daniel, Sr., the father of Judge Wm. D., Jr., was a near kinsman of her grandfather, Travers Daniel, 1741-1826, and that he attended dinners in Richmond given by Mr.s Gov. Wood and Mrs. Col. Ha
2 CONC y, the sister of Judge P.V. Daniel. Mrs. Hay wrote of him as presented on such an occasion that "he wore Virginia pressed cloth and although there were many who wore English broadcloth he looked, as was, the grandest gentleman at 
2 CONC the dinner."
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sources
2 CONT * https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Daniel-726 cites 
2 CONT * Virginia Genealogies, by Horace Edwin Hayden, pages 295, 301-302 [https://books.google.com/ebooks/reader?id=SJcsAAAAYAAJ&printsec=frontcover&output=reader&pg=GBS.PA295&hl=en#v=onepage&q&f=false GoogleBooks]
2 CONT *The Prominent Families of the United States of America edited by Arthur Meredyth Burke.  Page 388.  [https://books.google.com/books?id=3-78JbUTUewC&lpg=PA388&ots=gbKd5IWWWw&dq=Elizabeth%20(Watkins)%20Daniel&pg=PA388#v=onepage&q&f
2 CONC =false GoogleBooks]
