2 CONT The obituary of Travers Daniel, Senior, Esquire, appears in the
2 CONT Fredereicksburg “Virginia Herald” of 17 July 1824 in the Library of
2 CONT Congress.  From 1763 to 1792 he was the official surveyor of Stafford
2 CONT county.
2 CONT
2 CONT “This marble is erected in affectionate remembrance of the devoted wife, the
2 CONT tender mother, the humble and pious Christian.”
2 CONT
2 CONT FRANCES DANIEL
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife of
2 CONT
2 CONT TRAVERS DANIEL, SENR
2 CONT
2 CONT Born September
2 CONT
2 CONT Died
2 CONT
2 CONT (Broken in several pieces; remainder of this stone could not be located.)
2 CONT
2 CONT “Here lies the body of
2 CONT
2 CONT ELIZA TRAVERS DANIEL
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter of Travers &amp; Mildren Daniel who departed this life in her 21 year
2 CONT
2 CONT Oct. 29th 1823
2 CONT
2 CONT In the hour of death so strong was her faith in the Saviour of the world
2 CONT that her fondest friends in meditating on what they witnessed, forbore to
2 CONT weep at what they lost:  they communed with their own harts and were still.”
2 CONT
2 CONT “To the memory of Mrs. Mildred Daniel who was born in Charles County,
2 CONT Maryland Feby. 27th 1772 and died in the County of Stafford Virginia October
2 CONT 27th 1837.  She was the widow of Travers Daniel and the daughter of Thomas
2 CONT Stone of Maryland a signer of the Declaration of Independence.  Mrs. Daniel
2 CONT lived and died an examplary Christian.
2 CONT
2 CONT “Precious in the sight of the Lord is the death of his Saints., 1 Psa
2 CONT 116:15”
2 CONT Pages 690 – 693, Genealogies of Virginia Families – by George Harrison
2 CONT Harrison Sanford King, Fredericksburg, Virginia
