3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU General, soldier
1 TITL General
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for VIRGINIA with the rank of BRIGADIER GENERAL. [http://services.dar.org/public/dar_research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A082521  DAR Ancestor #  A082521]
2 CONT
2 CONT PARENTAGE UNVERIFIABLE
2 CONT
2 CONT '''General John Neville''' was a commandant of Fort Pitt. He was a man of wealth and education. John Neville was an American military officer, land speculator, and state official who served in the American Revolutionary War and, a
2 CONC s a tax collector, was a central figure in the Whiskey Rebellion. He was the father of Presley Neville.
2 CONT
2 CONT Woodville, the John and Presley Neville house, is Southwestern Pennsylvania’s principal link to the late 18th century, interpreting the time period of 1780-1820 and documenting the lives of the three families that resided there, t
2 CONC he Nevilles (1775-1815), the Cowans (1815-1835), and the Wrenshalls (1835-1975).
2 CONT
2 CONT John and Winifred Oldham Neville’s home was deemed “a temple of hospitality.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sources: '''
2 CONT * http://www.woodvilleplantation.org/significance
2 CONT * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Neville_%28general%29
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT On August 7, 1775, the Provincial "Convention of Virginia" ordered him to march with his company and take possession of Fort Pitt. On December 23, 1776, he was appointed a "justice" of Yohogania County Court, but considering the d
2 CONC istracted state of the country, occasioned by the boundary dispute and his position as commandant at Ft. Pitt, he declined the position. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Appointed Lieutenant Colonel November 12, 1776 he was ordered to join Washington's Army. Promoted to Colonel in 1777, Brigadier General in 1783. Subsequent to the "revolution", he was a member of the Board of Property, and of the 
2 CONC Supreme Executive Council of Pennsylvania, and of the Pennsylvania Convention which formed the Constitution of PA. in 1789-90. In 1791 at the request of the President and the Secretary of the Treasury, he accepted the appointment 
2 CONC of "Inspector of the Revenue" in the Fourth Survey of the District of PA., for the collection of the whiskey Tax in Western PA. (1792-95) which he held until after the suppression of the Whiskey Rebellion of 1774 and the establish
2 CONC ment of the supremacy of the laws of the United States. He was appointed Federal Agent, at Pittsburgh, for the sale of public lands, under the act of Congress passed May 18,1796, entitled "an act for the sale of lands of the Unite
2 CONC d States in the Territory northwest of the Ohio, etc." 
2 CONT
2 CONT During later years he lived on his estate on Montour Island, near Pittsburgh where he entertained visitors of note including Louis Philippe, the Duc D'Orleans (King of France 1830-48) and his two brothers the Duc de Montpensier an
2 CONC d the Count de Beaujolais. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on Montour's Island, now Neville Township, Allegheny County, PA. on July 29, 1803, and was buried in the First Presbyterian Churchyard, Pittsburgh. The inscription on his tombstone (now most probably illegible) was as foll
2 CONC ows: 
2 CONT
2 CONT "In memory of General John Neville, who departed this life July 29, 1803, aged 72 years and 3 days. During his long life, he filled many important offices, both Civil and Military. In the former, he was virtuous and disinterested.
2 CONC  In the latter, he was patriotic and brave. He enjoyed the friendship and confidence of the illustrious Washington. The day of his death witness and most pleasing tribute that can be paid to the memory of a mortal, the sincere reg
2 CONC rets of his friends and the tears of the neighboring poor."
2 CONT
2 CONT On August 7, 1775, the Provincial "Convention of Virginia" ordered him to march with his company and take possession of Fort Pitt. On December 23, 1776, he was appointed a "justice" of Yohogania County Court, but considering the d
2 CONC istracted state of the country, occasioned by the boundary dispute and his position as commandant at Ft. Pitt, he declined the position. Appointed Lieutenant Colonel November 12, 1776 he was ordered to join Washington's Army. Prom
2 CONC oted to Colonel in 1777, Brigadier General in 1783. Subsequent to the "revolution", he was a member of the Board of Property, and of the Supreme Executive Council of Pennsylvania, and of the Pennsylvania Convention which formed th
2 CONC e Constitution of PA. in 1789-90. In 1791 at the request of the President and the Secretary of the Treasury, he accepted the appointment of "Inspector of the Revenue" in the Fourth Survey of the District of PA., for the collection
2 CONC  of the whiskey Tax in Western PA. (1792-95) which he held until after the suppression of the Whiskey Rebellion of 1774 and the establishment of the supremacy of the laws of the United States. He was appointed Federal Agent, at Pi
2 CONC ttsburgh, for the sale of public lands, under the act of Congress passed May 18,1796, entitled "an act for the sale of lands of the United States in the Territory northwest of the Ohio, etc." During later years he lived on his est
2 CONC ate on Montour Island, near Pittsburgh where he entertained visitors of note including Louis Philippe, the Duc D'Orleans (King of France 1830-48) and his two brothers the Duc de Montpensier and the Count de Beaujolais. He died on 
2 CONC Montour's Island, now Neville Township, Allegheny County, PA. on July 29, 1803, and was buried in the First Presbyterian Churchyard, Pittsburgh. The inscription on his tombstone (now most probably illegible) was as follows: "In me
2 CONC mory of General John Neville, who departed this life July 29, 1803, aged 72 years and 3 days. During his long life, he filled many important offices, both Civil and Military. In the former, he was virtuous and disinterested. In th
2 CONC e latter, he was patriotic and brave. He enjoyed the friendship and confidence of the illustrious Washington. The day of his death witness and most pleasing tribute that can be paid to the memory of a mortal, the sincere regrets o
2 CONC f his friends and the tears of the neighboring poor."
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT General John Neville.
2 CONT
2 CONT Documentation: Beckley Public Library
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes & family history by Ruth Beckley.
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Winifred Oldham on 24 Aug 1754.
2 CONT
2 CONT General John Neville (originally spelled Nevill) was a member of St. Lukes church at the time of the Whiskey Rebellion.  Prior to his appointment as federal tax collector he was a popular figure in western Pennsylvania, expressing
2 CONC  opposition to the Commonwealth's whiskey excise tax, and initially opposing the federal version.  But when his childhood friend, George Washington, appointed him collector of federal revenue for the western survey, his neighbors 
2 CONC were first stunned, then outraged.  When asked if he had no concern for his reputation,  it is said that Neville replied that he "had no regard for (his neighbors') opinions," and that he had "an independent salary of 600 (is quot
2 CONC ed variously) per year."  Like many of the stories of the Whiskey Rebellion this one is hearsay.  But Neville was not without his supporters and friends.  He was reported to be generous to the poor and a loyal family man.  When Ne
2 CONC ville died in 1803 he was buried at Trinity Church Burial Ground.  In 1900 he was exhumed and moved to Allegheny Cemetery, Pittsburgh.  The following is a ledger from the Craig-Neville plot in Section 11.
2 CONT
2 CONT From an on-line article:
2 CONT
2 CONT Brigadier General John Neville 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Neville was born in Prince William (now Fauquier) County, Virginia, on July 26, 1731.  His father was Joseph Neville, Sr., and his mother was Elizabeth Bohannan.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1754 he married Winifred Oldham (1736-1797), and that same year, he served with George Washington in the ill-fated campaign against the French at Jumonville.  This defeat at Ft. Necessity marked the beginning of the French and 
2 CONC Indian War, and later, in Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1755 he served under General Edward Braddock in the unsuccessful attack on Fort Duquesne (Pittsburgh).  Presley Neville was born in 1755 at the family home in Winchester, Virginia.  There John became a landowner, a Justice of t
2 CONC he peace and sheriff of Frederick County, and Vestryman of the Episcopal Church.  Amelia was also born in Winchester, in 1763.  Three daughters did not survive.  Presley graduated in 1775 from the University of Pennsylvania with h
2 CONC igh honors in French classics.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Virginia Provincial Council set John to command Ft. Dunmore (Pitt) in 1775.  The dispute between Virginia and Pennsylvania over boundary lines was settled in 1777.  He remained there until 1777, when, as a colonel of the Fourt
2 CONC h Virginia Regiment, he served at the battles of Trenton, Princeton, Germantown and Monmouth (and Valley Forge?).  Presley served as Marquis Lafayette's aide-de-camp for two years.  Both John and Presley were captured in the Battl
2 CONC e of Charleston in 1780.  Following his release in 1782, Presley married Nancy Morgan, daughter of General Daniel Morgan.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1783 John was brevetted a Brigadier General by the Continental Congress, and he was elected to the Superior Executive Council of Pennsylvania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since there was no church structure for the Episcopal congregation, a frame church was erected in 1790 on William Lea's Kings-grant land, supported by the Neville family and other neighbors.  Neville also sponsored the seminary ed
2 CONC ucation of Francis Reno in nearby Canonsburg, which led to Reno's ordination in 1791, and his call to be the first resident Episcopal priest at St. Luke's Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Neville was called by President George Washington to be Inspector of Revenue for this district, to demonstrate that the excise tax on distilled spirits initiated in 1791 by Alexander Hamilton could be collected somewhere west
2 CONC  of the Allegheny Mountains.  Because frontier churches were also community meeting places, perhaps St. Luke's Church was a meeting place for Federalist supporters, "The Friends of Order."  The anti-Federalists, "The Friends of Li
2 CONC berty," largely Scotch-Irish Presbyterians, did utilize Mingo Creek Presbyterian Church for their meetings.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1794, Neville was rebuffed by irate farmers on Tuesday, July 15 when he and Marshal David Lenox tried to arrest William Miller.  Angry farmers on July 16 challenged Neville to resign his commission and destroy all tax records. 
2 CONC  Young Oliver Miller was killed.  On Thursday, July 17, 500 Anti-Federalist farmers attack Neville's mansion, named Bower Hill, and after their leader, Major James McFarlane was killed, the mansion was burned down. This insurrecti
2 CONC on is called the Whiskey Rebellion. Neville and other plantation owners in the Chartiers Valley moved away.  Neville went to Pittsburgh and to Montour's Island in the Ohio River, which he renamed Neville's Island
2 CONT
2 CONT John Neville died July 29, 1803. He and Winifred (1797) were buried in the burial ground at Trinity Episcopal Church, Pittsburgh.  In 1900, their graves were moved to Allegheny Cemetery in Pittsburgh, due to commercial development
2 CONC  in the city.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT BRIGADIER GENERAL JOHN NEVILLE  
2 CONT
2 CONT 1731 - 1803
2 CONT
2 CONT By Rev. Richard W. Davies
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT It is appropriate to observe the milestone anniversaries of the living and the dead.  this is the privilege of history.  We take note in 2003 of the 200th anniversary of the death of an early member of Old St. Luke's Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Neville was born in Price William (now Fauquier) County, Virginia, July 26, 1731.  In 1755 he married Winifred Oldham, just before he served with George Washington in the skirmish against the French, at the location today nam
2 CONC ed Jumonville, just north of Uniontown, Pennsylvania.  This was the beginning of the French and Indian War.  He then served under Gen. Edward Braddock in the unsuccessful attack against the French at Fort Duquesne.  In 1755, Winif
2 CONC red gave birth to a son, Presley, in the family home in Winchester, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Winchester, as a neighbor of George Washington, John began his dream of being a significant landowner.  He was Justice of the Peace, Sheriff, and a Vestryman of the Episcopal Church.  A daughter Amelia was born in 1763, the sam
2 CONC e year the French and Indian War ended.  The Neville family lost three additional female children.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1775, the Virginia Provincial Council sent John, then a Colonel, to command Ft. Dunmore, which was the deserted Ft. Pitt, and renamed by Governor Dunmore to honor himself.  Neville built the first log house beside Chartiers Cre
2 CONC ek, naming it Woodville.  He remained at the Fort until 1777.  John and his son Presley served in the American Revolution until being captured in 1780.  The Woodville residence was expanded in 1781.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1783, John was brevetted Brigadier General by the Continental Congress.  He served in the Superior Executive Council of Pennsylvania.  By Virginia Certificate, John owned 400 acres at Woodville.  He represented Washington Count
2 CONC y to ratify the U.S. Constitution.  In 1787, the Church of England was reconstituted in the new United States as the Episcopal Church.  The mansion at Bower Hill was built.  John was a neighbor of the Col. William Lea family.  We 
2 CONC believe that the Lea and Neville families held Christian devotions in each other's homes.  In 1790, they led the call to build a frame church on Lea's land, named St. Luke's, and with a resident clergyman.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1794, Washington called Neville to be the Inspector of Revenue and to collect the excise tax on distilled spirits.  The result was the 1794 Whiskey Rebellion, and hatred was heaped on Neville by anti-federalists farmers.  Nevil
2 CONC le moved to Pittsburgh in 1794, and to Neville Island in 1801.  Winifred died in 1797.  At John's death, July 29, 1803, (click HERE to see his tombstone) he was buried in land donated by John Penn in 1787 as a church site, where t
2 CONC he second Trinity Episcopal Church, Pittsburgh was built on Sixth Avenue in 1825.  He was moved to Allegheny Cemetery in 1900 when the Oliver Building was built on a portion of the burial ground.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT John served under George Washington in Braddock's 1755 expedition and then settled near Winchester, VA., where he became sheriff.  He made large purchases of land on Chartiers Creek near Pittsburgh and received additional land the
2 CONC re for his services in Dunmore's War of 1774.  He commanded Fort Pitt from 1775 through the first two years of the Revolution.  He served as colonel of the 4th Virginia Regiment at Trenton, Princeton, Germantown, and Monmouth. He 
2 CONC was brevitted brigadier-general in 1783.  After the war, he held the office of U.S. Inspector under the excise law, in which capacity he was instrumental in suppressing the whiskey rebellion of 1794. He is listed in "Historical Re
2 CONC gister of Officers in the Continental Army During the War of the Revolution" by Heitman; "Dictionary of American Biography"; "History of Allegheny County"; and "Who Was Who in America".
