1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/116606374/elizabeth-gann
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Carter married Samuel Gann(I) in about 1726. They were early colonists in the northern neck of the Colony of Virginia. The area, Frederick County, extended from settlements on the Atlantic Coast to a vast uncharted wilde
2 CONC rness. Only sturdy, hardy people were brave enough to venture into the interior of this land and carve out a home so far from civilization. Elizabeth and Samuel(I) had six known children, five sons and one daughter. Older sons had
2 CONC  married and grandchildren arriving when Samuel(I) died in 1760. 
2 CONT 1764 - 22 Mar 1764 swore statement from widow, Elizabeth Gan, that she sold to Andrew Longacre of Frederick County, Virginia, a certain tract of land lying under the North Mountain on the drains of Cedar Creek, joining the propert
2 CONC y of John Blair. wit: Adam Gan, Joseoh Longacre. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT *1. Adam Carter Gann(I) 1727-1812
2 CONT *2. Nathan Gann(I) 1729-1832
2 CONT *3. Clement Gann 1733-1735
2 CONT *4. John Gann(I) 1736-1817
2 CONT *5. Samuel Gann(II) 1748-1845
2 CONT *6. Nancy Gann 1749-
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
