1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Triangulation atDna Family of Pendenako's 2  71 matchers, see Media section for those matchers.(Common to those who claim Nicati ie Sarah Goowin, and Niketti Hughes.
2 CONT
2 CONT History of the Corotoman Indian Town Trade Town:   James Nickens October 22, 2015 · Washington By 1640 settlers south of the James River were agitating for the right to settle the Chicacoan Indian District, the land of the Rappaha
2 CONC nnock Indian Nation. Prior to that time Chicacoan was barely mentioned in Virginia records and remained the unmentioned domain of Henry Fleet, the Indian trader, and probably other unknown and unlicensed traders. In 1642 patent wa
2 CONC s granted to''' John Carter for the Cutatawomen Indian Town''' by assignment from the trader Daniel Gookin. This was the first patent in the Chicacoan Indian District "on Cossatomen Cr. now Carter's Cr." on the Rappahannock River.
2 CONC  Prior to that time an unofficial settlement of traders had sprung up at the Chicacoan River Indian Town ruled by Machywap, father of William Taptico the first.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before any official settlement of the Rappahannock Indian Nation could ensue Opechankanough, the great emperor of the Powhatan Nation, launched repriasals against the English on the James River which set off the 1644 Anglo-Powhata
2 CONC n War, halting Chicacoan settlement before it had begun.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the conclusion of the 1644 war settlement north of the York River was permitted and in 1648 Northumberland County was created from the Rappahannock Indian Nation. Three years later the Chicacoan District was bisected by the cre
2 CONC ation of Lancaster County. Lancaster bracketed both shores of the Rappahannock River including Occupatia Creek on the Southside and Menokin, the capital town of the Rappahannock Indian Nation on the North side. Three trading broth
2 CONC ers, the Lawsons, patented lands in Lancaster on both sides of the River. They were agents of Simon Overzee the Dutch trader and financed by Richard Bennett the Puritan trader, both from south of the James River.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 22 May 1650 Epaphraditus Lawson patented 2,900 acres on Occupacia Creek in Lancaster County, now Essex County. Henry Berry is named as Lawson's headright. This trading colony, the precursor of the Indian People of the Broken Hi
2 CONC lls (Ancestors or Namesakes), later relocated downriver to Lawson's Island on the Corrotoman River across from John Carter's Corrotoman Indiantown.
