1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Burial record:
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/20747086/anne-washington_lee
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial not confirmed, but died here. She was an owner of Mt. Vernon and she and her 2nd husband George Lee leased Mt. Vernon to George Washington. George Washington inherited Mt. Vernon when Anne died. Anne's first Marriage was to
2 CONC  Lawrence Washington the elder 1/2 Brother of George Washington. When Lawrence died in July 1752, Anne wasted no time and remarried into the Lee Family. She and George Lee married Dec. 16, 1752.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT *[http://www.pbs.org/wgbh/pages/frontline/shows/secret/famous/washington.html The Fairfaxes and George Washington]
2 CONT
2 CONT *[http://www.keepapitchinin.org/2011/11/28/eminent-women-ann-fairfax-washington-lee-and-susanna-rogers-sangiovanni-pickett-keate-part-1/  Biography of Anne Fairfax]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Anne Fairfax''' was born in 1728 in the West Indies and moved with her family to Marblehead, Massachusetts. where she was  christened in 1730.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne married Lawrence WASHINGTON, son of Augustine WASHINGTON and Jane BUTLER, in July 1743 in Belvoir, Virginia. She was the daughter of Colonel William Fairfax of neighboring Belvoir, himself a land agent for his cousin, Thomas 
2 CONC Fairfax, 6th Lord Fairfax of Cameron. The marriage of the 15-year old Anne to the newly returned 25-year old army veteran appears to have been prompted by Anne's disclosure to her parents that the family's minister, the Reverend C
2 CONC harles Green of Truro Parish, had taken opportunities with her. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne was a woman of charm, grace, and culture. She and Lawrence had several children (none of whom survived childhood): 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Jane (1744-1745)
2 CONT * Fairfax (1747)
2 CONT * Mildred (1748-1749) 
2 CONT * Sarah (1750-1754)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah was her father's heir. Had she lived she would have inherited the property now known as Mount Vernon instead of her uncle George Washington.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne married second George LEE on 16 December 1752 in Westmoreland County, Virginia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne FAIRFAX died in 1761 in Virginia. She was buried in Burnt House Fields, Lee Family Estate, Coles Point, Westmoreland County, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents: 
2 CONT *   William Fairfax (1691 - 1757) 
2 CONT *   Sarah Walker (1695 - 1731) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouses: 
2 CONT *   Lawrence Washington (1718 - 1752) 
2 CONT *   George Lee (1714 - 1761) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Mount Vernon Ladies' Association, non-profit organization founded in 1853 by Ann Pamela Cunningham.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Widow of Lawrence Washington of Mt. Vernon - as quoted by "Lees of Virginia" by Edmund Jennings Lee.
2 CONT
2 CONT Find A Grave Memorial # 20747086.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Before becoming wife #2 to George Lee,  Anne Fairfax Washington was the widow of Lawrence Washington, half-brother of George Washington.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Fairfax
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Bahamas, Fairfax married Sarah Walker (c. 1700 – January 21, 1731), the daughter of a former justice of the Vice admiralty court and acting deputy governor of the Bahamas. They had a son, George William Fairfax, followed by
2 CONC  a daughter Anne (discussed below) and another daughter Sarah before Mrs. Sarah Fairfax died on January 21, 1731, in Marblehead, Massachusetts.
2 CONT
2 CONT In June 1743, the eldest Fairfax daughter, Anne (then aged 15) was hastily married to Lawrence Washington.[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT At age 25 in 1742, Washington had recently returned to Virginia from two years at war in the Caribbean. Washington was appointed Adjutant (commander) of the Virginia militia, at the colonial rank of major. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the spring of 1743, the young Anne disclosed to her parents that she had been sexually molested by Charles Green, the Anglican priest of Truro Parish.[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Surviving court documents suggest Lawrence Washington may have been staying with the Fairfax family at Belvoir before the marriage, awaiting the completion of his new home at nearby Little Hunting Creek. Washington named his home 
2 CONC Mount Vernon, to honor Admiral Edward Vernon, under whom he has served for two years as "Captain of the Soldiers acting as Marines" of the American Regiment, aboard the admiral's flagship HMS Princess Caroline (80 guns).[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1745 Washington took Green to court over his actions with Anne Fairfax; he and the senior Fairfax tried to have the priest deposed for the scandal, but were unsuccessful. Green rallied support in the county, and the trial was a
2 CONC borted.[5] Lawrence and Anne Washington had four children together. Only one, a daughter Sarah, survived to inherit that estate upon Lawrence's death in 1752. The widow remarried, to George Lee.[6] 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT * “George Washington's Family": Cousin of the Lords Fairfax.
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lawrence_Washington_(1718–1752). Lawrence Washington died of tuberculosis at his Mount Vernon home in July 1752. His widow Anne remarried into the Lee family shortly thereafter. Twenty-year-old Geor
2 CONC ge lived at, and managed, the Mount Vernon plantation. Upon the death of Lawrence's widow Anne, George Washington inherited the estate at Mount Vernon.
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Fairfax-166
2 CONT Privacy Level: Open (White)
2 CONT Ann (Fairfax) Lee (1728 - 1761)
2 CONT Ann (Nancy) Lee formerly Fairfax aka Washington
2 CONT Born 1728 in Salem, Essex, Massachusetts Bay Colonymap
2 CONT Ancestors ancestors
2 CONT Daughter of William Fairfax and Sarah (Walker) Fairfax
2 CONT Sister of George William Fairfax, Thomas Fairfax, Sarah (Fairfax) Carlyle, Bryan Fairfax [half], William Henry Fairfax [half] and Hannah (Fairfax) Washington [half]
2 CONT Wife of Lawrence Washington — married Jul 1743 [location unknown]
2 CONT Wife of George Lee — married 16 Dec 1752 [location unknown]
2 CONT Descendants descendants
2 CONT Mother of Jane Washington, Fairfax Washington, Mildred Washington, Sarah Washington, George Fairfax Lee, Lancelot Lee and William Fairfax Lee
2 CONT Died 14 Mar 1761 at about age 33 in Mt Pleasant, Westmoreland, Colony of Virginiamap
2 CONT Problems/QuestionsProfile manager: Jo Spicer private message [send private message]
2 CONT Profile last modified 5 Jul 2024 | Created 16 Mar 2013
2 CONT This page has been accessed 3,917 times.
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT George Washington is connected to this particular family of mixed race Bahamian heritage (read the story of the Fairfaxes and George Washington). Suspicions are that Anne Fairfax, Mount Vernon's first mistress and the wife of Lawr
2 CONC ence Washington, the President's brother, was a woman of colour whose mother was born in the Bahamas. A number of George Washington's historians have pointed out that when Anne's brother, George William, visited his Fairfax aunts 
2 CONC in England, he had been utterly humiliated by their curiosity over whether he would turn black at puberty. Since the Fairfaxes were a wealthy, titled English family whose members corresponded regularly with each other, there must 
2 CONC have been some foundation for this assumption regarding George William's race. Indeed, from one of his father's letters I came across, I discovered that his Fairfax relatives, in point of fact, actually knew his mother, Sarah Walk
2 CONC er, the daughter of the Chief Justice of the Bahamas, since she had resided with them in England while on a visit from the West Indies. It is all too obvious then that there must have been a substantial proportion of her ethnic he
2 CONC ritage evident, if her in laws expected George William to inherit these African traits as well.
2 CONT
2 CONT Besides their relationship to Mount Vernon, another historical point about this family is that because of their enormous wealth and their social position in the British Colonial government of the time, William, Sarah's (Walker Fai
2 CONC rfax) husband, was the first person for whom George Washington ever worked. George William, their son, would, for the rest of his life, be counted as the president's closest friend.
2 CONT
2 CONT Laurence Washington, like his brother George, did not father any children that survived more than a few years. However, Ann Fairfax's second marriage was to one of the Lees. With George Lee, Anne finally succeeded in leaving a lin
2 CONC e even if not a political or culturally dynamic one. From her sister, Sarah's marriage to Major John Carlyle, however, are descended some of the most politically influential families of the South.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne Fairfax was born in Salem, Massachusetts in 1728. [2] She was christened in Marblehead in 1730.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT She married first, on Jul 19, 1743, Lawrence Washington.[4] He was the elder brother of George Washington. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne married second Colonel George Lee. [2][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children were: [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Jane Washington.
2 CONT Fairfax Washington.
2 CONT Mildred Washington.
2 CONT Sarah Washington.
2 CONT Anne died about 1761.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
