1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT ===Anne Aylett
2 CONT * AKA Ann
2 CONT *Birth:  about 1727, Nomini Plantation, Westmoreland, Virginia; seen as 18 Feb 1726 
2 CONT * Death: Apr 1774 at age 48 in Chantilly, Washington Parish,Westmoreland, Virginia; seen as "Wakefield"
2 CONT *Parents: William Aylett and Anne Ashton
2 CONT *Sister of Elizabeth (Aylett) Booth, Anne (Aylett) Lee [half] and Mary (Ayelett) Lee [half]
2 CONT *Spouse: Augustine Washington Jr. — married before 1744 in Washington Parish, Westmoreland County, Virginia.  He was the son of Augustine Washington and Jane Butler, and half brother to Pres. George Washington.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT From https://www.colonial-settlers-md-va.us/getperson.php?personID=I22502&tree=Tree1
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Anne AYLETT and Augustine (Austin) WASHINGTON are:
2 CONT
2 CONT * i. Lawrence WASHINGTON was born 1745.
2 CONT * ii. Augustine WASHINGTON was born 1747.
2 CONT * iii.''' Elizabeth WASHINGTON''' was born 15 NOV 1749 in Westmoreland County, Virginia, and died 20 OCT 1814 in va. She married Alexander general SPOTSWOOD 15 FEB 1769 in Bridge Creek, Fairfax County, Virginia, son of John SPOTSW
2 CONC OOD and Mary DANDRIDGE. He was born 1751 in va, and died 20 DEC 1818 in Spotsylvania County, Virginia
2 CONT * iv. '''Anne WASHINGTON''' was born 2 APR 1752 in Westmoreland County, Virginia, and died 3 JUN 1777 in Westmoreland County, Virginia. She married '''Burdett col ASHTON''' 19 DEC 1768 in Westmoreland County, Virginia, son of Char
2 CONC les ASHTON and Sarah BUTLER. He was born 21 NOV 1747 in Chestnut Hill, Westmoreland County, Virginia, and died 8 MAR 1814 in Westmoreland County, Virginia.
2 CONT * v.''' Jane WASHINGTON''' was born APR 1756 in niece of Gen. George Washington-Westmoreland County, Virginia, and died OCT 1833 in va. She married''' John Spotswood THORNTON''' 1784, son of Francis III col THORNTON and Mary Franc
2 CONC es GREGORY. He was born ABT 1739 in Spotsylvania, Virginia, and died 1822.
2 CONT * vi.''' William Augustine WASHINGTON''' was born 25 NOV 1757 in Westmoreland County, Virginia, and died OCT 1810 in va-Bridges Creek Washington Family cemetery. He '''married Jane "Jenny" WASHINGTON''', daughter of John Augustine
2 CONC  WASHINGTON and Hannah BUSHROD. She was born 20 JUN 1759 in va, and died 10 AUG 1791 in Bridges Creek Washington Family cemetery. He married Mary LEE 5 JUL 1792, daughter of Richard Henry LEE and Anne AYLETT. She was born 28 JUL 1
2 CONC 764 in Chantilly, Westmoreland, Virginia, and died 1795 in Haywood, Madison, Virginia-.''' He married Sarah Sally TAYLOE''' 11 MAY 1799, daughter of John TAYLOE and Rebecca PLATER. She was born 5 MAR 1765 in Lancaster County, Virg
2 CONC inia., and died 3 SEP 1834.
2 CONT * vii. George WASHINGTON was born 1760.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: 
2 CONT
2 CONT ! (1) "Burke's Presidential Families of the United States of America" (Burke's Peerage Ltd., London, 1st ed.) p.48. Half-Brothers and Sister of President Washington.
2 CONT
2 CONT (2) "Virginia Vital Records from the Virginia Magazine of History and Biography, the William and Mary College Quarterly, and Tyler's Quarterly" (Baltimore: Genealogical Pub. Co., 1984), "Inscriptions on Old Tombstones in Westmorel
2 CONC and and Northumberland Counties," p.548.
2 CONT
2 CONT ! Birth: (1,2) d/o Col. William AYLETT/Anne ASHTON of King William CO., VA. (1) 1726.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage to W. Augustine WASHINGTON: (1) 1743. (2)
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: (1) Dec 1773, Wakefield, Westmoreland Co., VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Buried in the Washington Family cemetery, VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Mount Vernon Ladies' Association, non-profit organization founded in 1853 by Ann Pamela Cunningham.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Place of burial verifiable based on the original stone marker found at Bridges Creek grave site, and the "Original Indentures, Bonds, Agreements, etc. 1653-1760. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Birth: 	1724 Westmoreland County Virginia, USA Death: 	Apr., 1774
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann Aylett was the wife of Augustine Washington Jr. Augustine Jr is the half-brother of George Washington, the first president of the United States. Augustine Jr is the son of Augustine Washington Sr and Augustine Sr's first wife 
2 CONC Jane Butler. Augustine is also the brother of Lawrence Washington, the builder of Mt. Vernon. Augustine Sr. was married, for the second time, to Mary Ball. It was from this second marriage of Augustine Sr in which George Washingto
2 CONC n, the President, was born. Ann Aylett and her husband Augustine Jr are both buried is the same common grave. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne Aylett was the eldest daughter of Captain William Aylett III and his 1st wife, Ann Ashton:
2 CONT
2 CONT From "Aylett family of Virginia" < [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aylett_family_of_Virginia Wikipedia] >  
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT >Captain William Aylett III (c. 1703 - August 28, 1744) - Son of Col. William Aylett and Anne Aylett, Wm III married twice (Ann Ashton [7] and Elizabeth Eskridge, later Steptoe)[8][9] had four daughters, Elizabeth and Anne from hi
2 CONC s first marriage, and a second Anne and Mary from his second marriage. His Daughter Elizabeth, who was probably the oldest, married Col. William Booth and lived at the Aylett family's Nomini Plantation;[10] his''' first [sic] daug
2 CONC hter Anne married George Washington's half brother Augustine Washington Jr.['''10] and lived at Pope's Creek.
2 CONT
2 CONT 10.  https://founders.archives.gov/documents/Washington/01-02-02-0003-0013 
2 CONT
2 CONT > ... Col. William Aylett, who died in 1744. Aylett had married twice and had no sons, but four daughters. Elizabeth, who was probably the oldest, married Booth, and her sister Anne married GW’s half brother Augustine Washington (
2 CONC eubank, 47–49).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Named in the will of her grandfather, Henry Ashton:
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of Col. Henry Ashton:
2 CONT
2 CONT >"To wife and daughter Grace, 2200 acres of land; also land to granddaus. Elizabeth and Anne Aylett, daus. of Capt. William Aylett and Ann his wife who was my dau. deceased; 400 acres of land; granddau. Elizabeth Tuberville 800 ac
2 CONC res of land; son Henry my pistols, sword and holster; son John, dau. Elizabeth Ashton 1000 acres of land in Stafford; cousin Burdett Ashton 1000 acres of land in Stafford; Godson John, son of Charles Ashton, decd., land in the bac
2 CONC k woods on Broad Run, Stafford Co., and the other part of same tract to my sons Henry and John which lies near Col. Carter's copper mines; Sister Mrs. Sarah McGill; exrs. in trust Capt. George Tuberville; Capt. Burdett Ashton; Mr.
2 CONC  Andrew Munroe; Mr. Richard Watts."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT # https://www.colonial-settlers-md-va.us/getperson.php?personID=I22503&tree=Tree1
2 CONT # https://www.colonial-settlers-md-va.us/getperson.php?personID=I22502&tree=Tree1
2 CONT #https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/21505/ann-washington includes multiple pictures of her grave marker.
2 CONT # WikiTree contributors, "Ann (Aylett) Washington (1726-1774)," WikiTree: The Free Family Tree, (https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Aylett-38 : accessed 08 November 2024). 
2 CONT #Virginia, Marriages of the Northern Neck of Virginia, 1649-1800 Ancestry.com Publication: Ancestry.com Operations, Inc.
2 CONT #Westmoreland County, Virginia Wills, 1654-1800 Ancestry.com Publication: Ancestry.com Operations Inc.
2 CONT # "Aylett family of Virginia" < [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aylett_family_of_Virginia Wikipedia] > The Aylett family of Virginia was a prominent family in King William County in Colonial Virginia which also supplied several bri
2 CONC des to the Washington and Lee families. The family descended from Thomas Aylett (1570-1650) of Hovells, in Coggleshall, Essex, via his son William (1607- 1677) who became a merchant taylor in London. It was his son William (?1640-
2 CONC 1679) who moved to Virginia.[1] Aylett, Virginia is named for the family.[2]
2 CONT # https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Popes_Creek_(Virginia) Wakefield burned in 1779. "Wakefield was never rebuilt and in William Augustine's will of July 12, 1810, the property was described as "the Burnt house plantation a
2 CONC nd the Islands and Marsh in the Creek belonging to me."[21]"
2 CONT # "Augustine Washington Jr. (1720 – May 1762)" < [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augustine_Washington_Jr. Wikipedia] > Died	May 1762 Wakefield plantation, Westmoreland County, Colony of Virginia, British America Resting place	Popes
2 CONC  Creek, Colony of Virginia, British America Spouse	Anne Aylett Children	4, including William Augustine Washington.  According to the will of his father, Augustine Washington Sr., the land now known as Mount Vernon first was willed
2 CONC  to this man's elder brother Lawrence Washington. However, the will instructed that in the case Lawrence should die without an heir the property would go to Augustine Jr., provided that he gave the Popes Creek property, known as "
2 CONC Wakefield", to George Washington. Augustine decided instead to keep the Popes Creek property and so George got the property now known as Mount Vernon.
2 CONT # Wayland, John W. (2009). The Washingtons and Their Homes. Genealogical Publishing Com. p. 327. ISBN 9780806347752.
2 CONT # Tyler, Lyon Gardiner, ed. Encyclopedia of Virginia Biography. Volume 1. New York: Lewis Historical Publishing Company, 1915. OCLC 2576742. Page 175.  < [https://books.google.com/books?id=UCgSAAAAYAAJ&q=augustine#v=onepage&q&f=tr
2 CONC ue GoogleBooks] >.  Page 352.  < [https://books.google.com/books?id=UCgSAAAAYAAJ&vq=augustine&pg=PA352#v=onepage&q&f=true GoogleBooks] >. Washington, Augustine, married "Anne Aylett, daughter and coheiress of William Aylett, of We
2 CONC stmoreland county."
2 CONT # https://members.tripod.com/brian_skelton/d0000/g0000036.html#I0452
