2 CONT * Reference: [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augustine_Warner,_Jr. Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT Augustine Warner, Jr. (1642/3–June 19, 1681) was a Virginia politician and landowner. He served in the House of Burgesses 1666–77 and was its Speaker  in two separate sessions in 1676 and 1677, before and after Bacon's Rebellion. 
2 CONC He then served on the Governor's Council from about October 1677 until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT Warner's exact birth date is unclear; different sources cite July 3, 1642 and October 20, 1643. He was the only son of Augustine Warner, who immigrated from England in 1628 and patented the plantation called "Austin's Desire" in G
2 CONC loucester County, Virginia in 1642, building Warner Hall on the property. Warner Sr. served on the Council from 1659 until his death in 1674.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The younger Warner went to London in 1658 and attended the Merchant Taylors' School. He returned to Virginia after finishing his education and married Margaret Reade, daughter of George Reade, a member of the Council. They settled
2 CONC  on a farm in Gloucester County, living there until he inherited Warner Hall in 1674.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Bacon's Rebellion
2 CONT
2 CONT In March 1676 the General Assembly called by Governor Sir William Berkeley in 1661 held its last session. Warner was elected Speaker, replacing Robert Wynne, who died the previous year. On May 10, as the Nathaniel Bacon crisis was
2 CONC  building, Berkeley dissolved the House of Burgesses and called new elections. It is not known if Warner served in the new House that met in June.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Fighting began in late July. Warner remained loyal to Berkeley, joining his forces. Bacon captured Jamestown and burned it on September 19, then crossed the York River and seized Warner Hall. Bacon died in October, but the rebelli
2 CONC on continued until early January 1677.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Warner served on a court-martial headed by Berkeley on January 11, 1677. Berkeley called for elections, and Warner was elected Speaker of the new House when it convened in February. The Assembly met until early April. It revoked a
2 CONC ll acts of the June 1676 Assembly, and then reenacted some.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Later years
2 CONT
2 CONT In late September – early October 1677 Warner was appointed to the Council. Although he was aligned with the "Green Spring faction" of Berkeley loyalists after Berkeley's removal as governor, he was not removed from the Council, u
2 CONC nlike such diehards as Philip Ludwell and Thomas Ballard.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Warner sued William Byrd I, a sometime ally of Bacon, for the damage the rebels had done to Warner Hall. Byrd claimed in his defense that he was Bacon's captive, not his supporter, and was not responsible.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Warner died June 19, 1681, and was interred at Warner Hall.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Reference: [http://www.thepeerage.com/p228.htm#i2280 The Peer Age]
2 CONT
