2 TITL Col. Thomas Speke of Westmoreland County …. .pdf
1 NOTE !BIRTH - THE WASHINGTONS AND THEIR HOMES by John W. Wayland, p. 328
2 CONT John Washington, immigrant; born in England in 1632/33, son of Lawrence, who was born in 1602; great-grandfather of George; came to Virginia in 1656.  In 1664 he purchased land in Westmoreland Co, at Bridges Creek, where he lived 
2 CONC thereafter.
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR #1 - Ibid
2 CONT He married 1st Ann Pope who was the mother of all his children (5) two of whom died before him.
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR #2 - Ibid
2 CONT He married 2nd Widow Anne Brett.
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR #3 - Ibid
2 CONT He married 3rd Widow Frances Appleton.
2 CONT
2 CONT !DEATH - Ibid
2 CONT He made his will January 22, 1697 and it was probated February 22, 1697/98.
1 NOTE He sailed for Virginia in 1656 as mate and voyage partner of Edward Prescott, owner of te sea Horse of London, a ketch engaged in the tobacco trade. They arrived early in 1657. Having taken tobacco on board near Mattox Creek, they
2 CONC  weighed anchor and set sail again, but the ketch sank. He bought land at Bridges Creek and settled there in 1664. Eventually he owned over 6,000 acres in Verginia, including the nucleus of the Wakefield and Mount Vernon estates. 
2 CONC As a Colonel he led Virginian forces in the Indian War of 1675. Among civil offices, he was a member of the Virginian House of Burgeses for Westmoreland County. Married 2ndly December 1658 Anne Pope, widow of Walter Boadhurst and 
2 CONC dr. of Lt.Col. Nathaniel Pope.
1 NOTE Colonel
1 NOTE Dec 22, 2009
2 CONT It was only about nine months ago that I came to the conclusion that Joseph Harwood, 1630s-1681, is not the son of William and Elinor Harwood, (Who aren't Harwood's at all but Hayward's according to the original land patents.), bu
2 CONC t rather the brother of Samuel Hayward of Stafford Co, VA. This position breaks ranks with other Harwood researchers, but when I looked at the original documents it appears to be true.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Hayward married Martha Washington who is the great-aunt of George Washington. Samuel Hayward and Martha Washington are second cousins, through her mother Amphillis Hayward or Harwood. The Haywards, were neighbors of John Wa
2 CONC shington, George Washington's immigrant ancestor and great-grandfather. Nicholas Hayward, Sr., and Jr and Samuel Hayward had business dealings with John Washington.
2 CONT Source: Randy Harwood
1 NOTE He sailed for Virginia in 1656 as mate and voyage partner of Edward Prescott, owner of te sea Horse of London, a ketch engaged in the tobacco trade. They arrived early in 1657. Having taken tobacco on board near Mattox Creek, they
2 CONC  weighed anchor and set sail again, but the ketch sank. He bought land at Bridges Creek and settled there in 1664. Eventually he owned over 6,000 acres in Verginia, including the nucleus of the Wakefield and Mount Vernon estates. 
2 CONC As a Colonel he led Virginian forces in the Indian War of 1675. Among civil offices, he was a member of the Virginian House of Burgeses for Westmoreland County. Married 2ndly December 1658 Anne Pope, widow of Walter Boadhurst and 
2 CONC dr. of Lt.Col. Nathaniel Pope.
1 NOTE Dec 22, 2009
2 CONT It was only about nine months ago that I came to the conclusion that Joseph Harwood, 1630s-1681, is not the son of William and Elinor Harwood, (Who aren't Harwood's at all but Hayward's according to the original land patents.), bu
2 CONC t rather the brother of Samuel Hayward of Stafford Co, VA. This position breaks ranks with other Harwood researchers, but when I looked at the original documents it appears to be true.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Hayward married Martha Washington who is the great-aunt of George Washington. Samuel Hayward and Martha Washington are second cousins, through her mother Amphillis Hayward or Harwood. The Haywards, were neighbors of John Wa
2 CONC shington, George Washington's immigrant ancestor and great-grandfather. Nicholas Hayward, Sr., and Jr and Samuel Hayward had business dealings with John Washington.
2 CONT Source: Randy Harwood
