1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The Dandridges of Virginia https://archive.org/details/jstor-1921234/page/n3
2 CONT Her birth date can be found in the Henley Family Bible (which was hers), quoted in the 1896 publication "The Dandridges of Virginia" by Wilson Miles Cary. She also recorded birth and death dates for her siblings and parents, with 
2 CONC birth and death dates of her children with William Aylett and Leonard Henley. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Extract of a letter from Burwell Bassett, Jr., to President George Washington, dated 2 Dec 1799:
2 CONT "To communicate to our friends agreable incidents we are readily prompted by the pleasure it gives but it is duty alone which can lead us to be the communicators of uncomefortable tidings. Tis this that impels me to inform my frie
2 CONC nds at Mount Vernon that Mrs Henley was attacked about ten days since with a severe bilious pleurisy her weak constitution was unequal to the shock and in five days she yielded to the force of her disorder. As it relates to hersel
2 CONC f perhaps the change cannot be much regretted as she had seen the best days this life could afford her but to her children the loss will be great."
2 CONT
2 CONT Married first to John Aylett, having married Henley as "the widow Aylett".
2 CONT
2 CONT "Thomas Meekins Henley, another son [of young Leonard], wrote of visiting his family and relatives in James City County (Virginia) in 1845; and of visiting the graves of his father and mother, at the Henley estate, in James City C
2 CONC ounty, Virginia, then belonging to Dr. Hubbard, and noting that by the dates on the tombstones that he, Thomas, had lived (62) at least 5 years longer than any preceding Henley (Millennial Harbinger, page 515, 1845).* Reference: [
2 CONC https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/236209973/elizabeth-henley Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Aug 29 2023, 19:31:06 UTC''
