1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===John Bushrod, Gent. 
2 CONT * Gender:	Male
2 CONT * Birth:	1710  -  "Bushfield", Westmoreland County, Virginia [reached majority, age 19, in 1729] 
2 CONT * Death:	Between 14 Feb 1760 [will written] and 30 December 1760 [probate] - Cople Parish, Westmoreland County, Virginia 
2 CONT * Son of John Bushrod (d. 1719) & Hannah Keene, his 2nd wife (d. 1739).   [Hannah married 2nd to Willougby Allerton & 3rd to John Cooper]
2 CONT
2 CONT John Bushrod Jr. married first Jane Lane Corbin. His children, according to his will, were:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Hannah (married John Augustine Washington) and 
2 CONT # Elizabeth (married Philip Smith).
2 CONT
2 CONT A daughter Catherine (Bushrod) Taylor is seen in some pedigrees without substantiation.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Bushrod Jr. married secondly Mildred Washington. Mildred had been married once before, and some sources cite her married name as Seaton.
2 CONT
2 CONT No children by second wife. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.genealogy.com/forum/surnames/topics/bushrod/120/
2 CONT
2 CONT Hannah Bushrod was not the only child of John Bushrod (1663-1719) and Hannah Keene (1676-1739).They also had a son named '''Col. John Bushrod (1712-1760)''', whose third (??) wife was Mildred Corbin (1720-1785).Their daughter was 
2 CONC named Hannah Bushrod (1738-1804)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT 26 Jan 1719: Mentioned in Will of father, John Bushrod
2 CONT
2 CONT > My loving son John Bushrod all that land and plantation I bought of Mr. John Spencer in Washington Parish and not any of the land be cleared that is one the left hand of the road that leadeth from Nominy Ferry to the mouth of Po
2 CONC pe's Creek.<BR>
2 CONT >Also to my son John Bushrod thirteen Negro slaves, Tom, Guy, Sam,Cezar, Abraham, Virginia, Jack, George, Beck, Abraham, Moll, Dinah, Betty and young Rose. Also five young cowes, five calves and five heifers of four year old and f
2 CONC ive cattle of two yeares old and five steares of five yeares old and four steares of six year old and one bull, alsoe four young breeding sowes and sixteen piggs of four months old and ten spaid sowes or barrows of three yearesold
2 CONC  and alsoe twenty ewes and one ram, all to be young, and one young breeding mare, when he shall come to his respective age hereafter mentioned. Also 5000 pounds of tobacco. Also my least silver tankard and least silver tumbler and
2 CONC  one dozen of my largest silver spoones marked I H, when he shall attaine to the age of nineteen yeares old.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1729-1731: sues for his estate 
2 CONT
2 CONT Westmoreland County, Virginia Order Book [Amanda Gonzalez]; Page 392
2 CONT At a Court held for the sd Co. the 29th day of Sept. 1731
2 CONT
2 CONT > John Bushrod, son of John Bushrod, Gent., late of this Co., dec'd, proffered a petition to this Court therein setting forth that being he arrived to the age of 19 years, the time by his sd. father appointed for him to have posse
2 CONC ssion of the Estate he left him, also there being a tract of land about 1,000 in the forrest of Nomini, and seven Negroes given by his sd. father, to his son Thomas Bushrod, and the issue of his body, and in default, to be equally
2 CONC  divided betw. the petitioner, and his brother, Richard, and the sd. Thomas, being dead without such issue, whereby the sd. Estate now equally belongs to him and his sd. brother. Therefore, he prayed the Court's order for such Est
2 CONC ate, as it belongs to him by his sd. father's Will, but is now in the hands and possession of John Cooper, Gent., and Hannah his wife. The petitioner's mothers and Execx. of his sd. father's will also on order for the appointing p
2 CONC ersons to lay out and make an equal division of the afsd. land & negros betw. him, and his sd. brother, according to their sd. father's Will, which premises being considered by the Court, it is ordered that John Cooper, Gent., and
2 CONC  Hannah, his wife, the sd. John Cooper being present & consenting thereto, to deliver unto the petitioner all such Estate as of Right to him belongs by virtue of his sd. father's Will, in their custody & possession. And, Patrick S
2 CONC pence, John Footman & Geo. Turbervile, Gent., together with James Thomas, Gent., Co. Surveyor, are appointed to lay off and divide the land mentioned in the sd. petition, equally betw. the sd. John & Richard Bushrod, according to 
2 CONC the Will of their sd. father. And, also the sd. Spence, Footman & Turbervile are to divide the seven Negroes mentioned in the sd. petition according to the value of them, and agreeable to the sd. will, and to make a report of thei
2 CONC r proceedings herein to the next Co. Court.
2 CONT
2 CONT !26 Dec 1738: Mentioned in Will of mother Hannah Cooper.
2 CONT
2 CONT > Item. I give my Son, JOHN BUSHROD, a Mourning Ring of 15/ value and my Negro man named Clarke and the bed that used to stand in my Porch Chamber with all the furniture belonging to the said Bed
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 14 Feb 1760: Will of John Bushrod in Westmoreland Co. VA. Proved 30 Dec. 1760.
2 CONT
2 CONT >Wife Mildred part of est.; dau. Hannah Washington 35 slaves, furniture and land; grand daus. Mary and Jenny Washington 3 slaves each; dau. Elizabeth Bushrod land and 41 slaves; to Lydia Bushrod Pettit dau. of John £10 yearly if h
2 CONC er father spend her est.; friends Hon. Richard Corbin and John Washington exrs.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT “[Diary entry: 22 May 1769],” Founders Online, National Archives, https://founders.archives.gov/documents/Washington/01-02-02-0004-0013-0022. [Original source: The Diaries of George Washington, vol. 2, 14 January 1766 – 31 Decembe
2 CONC r 1770, ed. Donald Jackson. Charlottesville: University Press of Virginia, 1976, pp. 153–154.]
2 CONT
2 CONT >'''Mildred Washington Bushrod''' (c.1720–1785), of Gloucester County, a cousin of GW, was the sister of Warner Washington, Sr., and the widow of John Bushrod (d. 1760) of Westmoreland County. She had been his second wife, and he 
2 CONC her second husband, but she had no children by either of her two marriages, John Bushrod’s daughters Hannah and Elizabeth having been born to his first wife, '''Jenny Corbin Bushrod''' (kenner, 177–78; GW to Ruthey Jones, 25 Sept.
2 CONC  1783, DLC:GW). After John Bushrod died, Mildred apparently returned to Gloucester County, where she was listed on the tax roll for 1770 as owning 1,280 acres of land and a sedan chair (gloucester, 1:92). Her family on this visit 
2 CONC may be children of her other brother, Henry Washington of Middlesex County, who at his death in 1763 had left a son, Thacker, and three underage daughters, Elizabeth, Catherine, and Ann (wayland [1], 325).
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT * Will: 14 Feb. 1760 Westmoreland Co. VA, America.
2 CONT * Probate: 30 Dec. 1760 Westmoreland Co. VA, America.
2 CONT
2 CONT Westmoreland County Wills Book XIII. 
2 CONT * Name: John Bushrod 14 Feb 1760 30 Dec 1760 
2 CONT * Comment: gent. 
2 CONT * Wife Mildred ; granddau. Mary Washington ; dau. Hannah Washington ; granddaughter Jenny Washington ; dau. Elizabeth Bushrod ; to Lydia Bushrod Pettit dau. of Mr. John Pettit ; friends Honl. Richard Corbin and Major John Washingt
2 CONC on exors.
2 CONT *Mentioned in his father's and mother's wills; he resided at "Bushfield" in Westmoreland County, VA, and was justice, colonel of the militia, and burgess for that county 1746 to 1756. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://www.genealogy.com/forum/surnames/topics/bushrod/59/
2 CONT
2 CONT >My information is that John Bushrod married first Jane Lane Corbin. His children, according to his will, were Hannah (married John Augustine Washington) and Elizabeth (married Philip Smith). He married secondly Mildred Washington
2 CONC . Mildred had been married once before, and some sources site her married name as Seaton. There is a source that lists Catherine Bushrod, who married William Taylor, as a child of this John Bushrod. This source does not mention El
2 CONC izabeth Bushrod Smith.
2 CONT
2 CONT >>During the research I've done along with my cousin Richard Bushrod, we've come across quite a few discrepencies.One of those was in regards to Catherine Bushrod.A few source list her as the daughter of John Bushrod.However, in h
2 CONC is will he never once mentioned a Catherine as a daughter.He only mentions his current wife Mildred and Hannah and Elizabeth.Also, in Mildred's will nothing is mentioned ofa Catherine (neither Hannah nor Elizabeth).I have not cont
2 CONC inue to list Catherine as a daughter of John, for my belief is that if she was, he would've mentioned her in his will somewhere.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------
2 CONT
2 CONT Virginia Genealogies: A Genealogy of the Glassell Family of Scotland and ...By Horace Edwin Hayden
2 CONT
2 CONT >a. John Bushrod, b. Glouc'r Co., Jan. 30, 1663; d. Feb. 6, 17*9, ittpra: m. Hannah, dau. of Wm. and Elizabeth Keene, North'd Co.; had—4. John, m. Anne, dau. of Thomas Bushrod, and pro. had '''Col. John''', Burgess West'd, 1748-17
2 CONC 55; m. Jenny, dau. of Col. Gawin Corbin, and had Hannah, m. John Augustine Washington, and had Hon. Bushrod W. 5. a son 6. Apphia, m, 171a o), Wm. Fauntleroy, &c , &c. (Va His. Mag., I., w)
2 CONT
2 CONT -------
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://www.frostandgilchrist.com/getperson.php?personID=I17761&tree=frostinaz01
2 CONT
2 CONT (1) There is some uncertainty as to who John BUSHROD's wives were. His wives may have included any or all of the following women: Anne BUSHROD, Jenny (or Jane) CORBIN, and Mildred SEATON. The only wife of John BUSHROD mentioned in
2 CONC  his will was named Mildred.
2 CONT
2 CONT (2) Ritchie, Ruth and Wood, Sudie Rucker, Garner-Keene Families of Northern Neck, Virginia, Charlottesville, VA: Jarman Printing, 1952, pp. 208-209:
2 CONT
2 CONT >John Bushrod . . . inherited "Bushfield" and married Mildred Corbin of King and Queen County, a first cousin of Colonel Thomas Lee of Stratford. They had a daughter Hannah Bushrod, who married John Augustine Washington, brother o
2 CONC f General George Washington. Their son, Bushrod Washington, born June 5, 1762, at "Bushfield," was an Associate Justice of the U. S. Supreme Court and it was to him that George Washington left Mt. Vernon and all his books and pape
2 CONC rs. He married Julia Anne Blackburn. As they had no children, upon his death in 1829 he left Mt. Vernon to a nephew, John Augustine Washington, son of his brother, Corbin Washington, who married Hannah Lee, a daughter of Hon. Rich
2 CONC ard Henry Lee. He in turn left it to his son, John Augustine Washington III, who was the last private owner of the estate. Thus all the private owners of Mt. Vernon after George Washington's death were descendants of Thomas and Ma
2 CONC ry Keene of Northumberland County. George Washington visited numerous times at "Bushfield." The first John Augustine Washington and his wife, Hannah, who was the second mistress of Mt. Vernon, are buried at "Bushfield."
2 CONT -------
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://dgmweb.net/FGS/B/BushrodJohn-JaneLaneCorbin.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Stella Pickett Hardy.  1911.  Colonial Families of the Southern States of America.  T.A. Wright, New York, NY (republished 1958, Southern Book Co., Baltimore, MD; republished 1965, Genealogical Publ. Co., Baltimore, MD; online at 
2 CONC books.google.com). 
2 CONT
2 CONT >20—3.  Jane Lane, b. 1710-17; m. Col. John Bushrod, of "Bushfield," Westmoreland Co., Va., who served as a Burgess, 1748-55; son of Hon. John and Anne (Bushrod) Bushrod, gr.-dau. of Hon. John and Hannah (Keene) Bushrod, of Glouce
2 CONC ster Co., Va., and of Hon. Thomas Bushrod 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------
