1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/John-Thornton/6000000210820363850?through=6000000003601163931
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT [https://services.dar.org/members/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A114864 DAR Ancestor # A114864]
2 CONT Service Description:  1) ALSO CAPT & MAJOR; COL WILLIAM GRAYSON, CONT LINE
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT John Thornton was the son of Francis Thornton (d. betw 1747-1802) and Frances Gregory.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/67058133/john_thornton
2 CONT
2 CONT John Thornton (c1743-1822) born at "Fall Hill," Fredericksburg, Spotsylvania County, Va. Married his 2nd cousin in 1784, Jane Augusta Washington (1756-1833) who was born at Popes Creek in Westmoreland County, Va. In place of her f
2 CONC ather, who had died, her uncle Gen. George Washington gave the bride away. He last served (1778-79) as a lt. colonel of Grayson's additional Continental regiment. Later he commanded a regiment of Virginia militia at the decisive b
2 CONC attle of Yorktown in 1781.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Five children were
2 CONT # Mary Thornton (c1785-c1806) who married c1801, Dr. Isaac Winston (1777-1867);
2 CONT # John Augustine Thornton (c1787-1817);
2 CONT # Frances Gregory Thornton (c1789-1828) who married her 1st cousin, Dr. George Alexander Thornton (c1787-1818);
2 CONT # Jane Augusta Washington (1795-1819/20) who married her 1st cousin, once removed, Rev. Francis Thornton IV (1795-1881);
2 CONT # George Washington Spotswood Thornton (c1795-1824) who married c1824, Mildred Hooe Buckner (c1800-36). The latter marriage had an only child, Jane Washington Augusta Thornton (1824-87), who married in 1848, U.S. Senator James Bur
2 CONC nie Beck (1822-90)(Transylvania Law 1846), of Kentucky.
2 CONT # Caroline R. Thornton [is this certain?]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.geni.com/people/Nancy-Rogers/6000000003601163931 (born about 1763) was not his child.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Info adder per Mary S Lockwood's "Lineage Book of the Charter Members" of the DAR:
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a captain in Third Virginia Regiment, Continental Line; major and lieutenant colonel in 1777, in 1781 commanded a regiment under Lafayette in the Cornwallis campaign.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT From "Will the real Francis Thornton please stand up!"  By Staff/Contributed Jul 2, 2015  < [https://www.rappnews.com/features/will-the-real-francis-thornton-please-stand-up/article_7942c821-0128-5a99-b284-cfd3a1a53854.html link] 
2 CONC >
2 CONT
2 CONT Thornton Hill
2 CONT
2 CONT This large beautiful property, now owned by attorney James W. Fletcher III, was located within the 1731 land grant to Francis II. Of the three sons of Francis II — Francis III, Reuben and John (who married the three Gregory sister
2 CONC s) — only Francis II had sons. Reuben had no children, and John had four daughters (one of whom married Samuel Washington, a brother of President George Washington). Probably because Francis III already owned significant tracts of
2 CONC  land (see above), Reuben and John inherited their father’s property along the Thornton River. Reuben died in 1768, and he willed his share of the property to his brother John.
2 CONT
2 CONT John was a resident of Spotsylvania and afterwards of Caroline County where he lived on his manor plantation on the Rappahannock River. He was a justice of the peace in Spotsylvania County in 1742, was sheriff in 1751, was elected
2 CONC  a member of the House of Burgesses in 1753 and was a senior colonel of the Spotsylvania militia. In 1774 he sold to John Thornton “Jr.” 1,588 acres of Francis II’s land grant for 1700 pounds. This John Thornton Jr. was not his so
2 CONC n, but was the son of Francis III. At the time, it was customary to call someone “Jr.” if he were a nephew of the same name.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the land he purchased, John Thornton (Jr.) built the estate called “Thornton Hill.” In the American Revolution, John commanded a company of “Minute Men” from Culpeper County, was a captain in the 3rd Virginia regiment, Continen
2 CONC tal Line; was a lieutenant colonel in Grayson’s Continental regiment; and commanded a regiment of Virginia militia at Yorktown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Because of his extensive military background, he was generally referred to as Col. John Thornton. He married Jane Augusta Washington (a niece of President George Washington) and they had six children — Mary Thornton, Jane Augusta 
2 CONC Washington Thornton, Frances Gregory Thornton, Caroline R. Thornton, George Washington Spotswood Thornton and John Augustine Thornton. John died in 1822.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his lifetime, John gave the eastern portion (which included today’s Fletcher’s Mill and part of Sperryville) of his inherited 1,588 acres to his son John Augustine Thornton. The western portion (which he supplemented with a
2 CONC dditional land purchases and which included “Thornton Hill”) was bequeathed to his wife and to his son George Washington Spotswood Thornton in John’s will of 1822.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
