1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GSln=WA&GSfn=g&GSpartial=1&GSbyrel=all&GSdyrel=all&GSst=48&GScntry=4&GSob=n&GSsr=1121&GRid=19158160&df=all& Findagrave]
2 CONT
2 CONT George Fayette Washington was born In Mt Vernon, Fairfax, VA on Jan. 17, 1790  and died Sep. 6, 1867 in Frederick, VA.  He is buried in Mount Hebron Cemetery, Winchester City, Virginia, USA.
2 CONT
2 CONT He owned the Alexander Ross home, which he called Waverly near the Hopewell meeting house at Clearbrook.  At his death his will proved January 1868, he left Waverly and his other property to Philip Williams in trust for the joint 
2 CONC use and benefit of his son, Matthew Burwell Bassett Washington, and his wife Anna. 
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT
2 CONT * parents: The orphaned children of Charles Washington's oldest son, George Augustine Washington, and of Martha Washington's niece Frances Bassett Washington Lear, were named George Fayette, Charles Augustine, and Anna Maria. The 
2 CONC children lived with their stepfather Tobias Lear at Walnut Tree Farm, a part of the Mount Vernon Clifton's Neck land which Washington left to the two little boys.
2 CONT
2 CONT Married
2 CONT # 18 NOV 1813 in Jefferson Co., Virginia to Maria Frame (Birth: OCT 1793 in Virginia Death: Bef 1830) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children include
2 CONT # Charles Augustine WASHINGTON b: 9 AUG 1814
2 CONT # Francis Massey WASHINGTON b: 21 JAN 1816
2 CONT # George Fayette WASHINGTON II b: 21 FEB 1830
2 CONT # Matthew Burwell WASHINGTON b: 15 AUG 1830
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT * [http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Last_Will_and_Testament_of_George_Washington Will of George Washington, July 7, 1799] ...  I give and bequeath to '''George Fayette Washington''' and Lawrence Augustine Washington [17] and their he
2 CONC irs my estate east of little hunting creek lying on the River Potomac, including the farm of 360 acres, leased to Tobias Lear as noticed before and containing in the whole, by deeds, two thousand and twenty-seven acres be it more 
2 CONC or less which said estate, it is my will and desire should be equitably and advantageously divided between them, according to quantity, quality and other circumstances when the youngest shall have arrived at the age of twenty-one 
2 CONC years, by three judicious and disinterested men, one to be chosen by each of the brothers and the third by these two ....
2 CONT * After Tobias Lear's death, the farm was occupied by two generations of the Washington family: George Fayette Washington, a nephew, and Charles Augustine Washington, a great-nephew.  http://www.ahs.org/river_farm/history.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT # "The Washingtons: A Family History: Volume 1: Seven Generations of the Presidential Branch.  Justin Glenn. Savas Publishing, Jun 11, 2014 - History - 726 page. Google Books. Accessed April 17 2017. [https://books.google.com/book
2 CONC s?id=gpzwAwAAQBAJ&pg=PA1792-IA14&lpg=PA1792-IA14&dq=george+fayette+washington+maria+frame&source=bl&ots=MhKkpcuEI0&sig=YSnRsJz7Zsh-8XAA5DuazOg0PLk&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiJi5TpnarTAhXqgVQKHbKtASIQ6AEIPDAG#v=onepage&q&f=false page 1
2 CONC 792]
2 CONT # Burke's Peerage of the American Presidents, pp 40-42
2 CONT # 1850 census - District 16, Frederick, Virginia.  George F. Washington owns 21 slaves
2 CONT # 1860 census - District 4, Frederick, Virginia - post office Winchester.  George F. Washington owns 34 slaves.
2 CONT # In "The Farmers’ Repository," (a Virginia newspaper), Thursday, November 25, 1813, page 2, column 4: "Married- On Thursday, by Rev. W. Mead, Mr. Fayette Washington, to Miss Maria Frame, daughter of Mr. M. Frame, of Charles-town,
2 CONC  Va. (now in W. Va.)."
2 CONT
