1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://worldconnect.rootsweb.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=barsmi&id=I4891
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Was a neighbor of John Piper, who became the guardian of Martha/Margaret Frank upon the death of her mother, Sarah, in 1725.
2 CONT
2 CONT Believed to be a son of Thomas and Elizabeth (DAWSON) SPILMAN, and grandson of James and Mary SPILMAN and grandson of Clement SPILMAN of Westmoreland Co., VA.  See also: Find A Grave Memorial# 25911029, Clement SPILMAN.
2 CONT
2 CONT James SPILMAN, in 1705, was a commercial envoy to the Russian Government at Moscow. His brothers were Clement, and Robert. He had sons, Thomas SPILMAN married Anne --?--, and Clement SPILMAN married Hannah FOX-KENNER. Thomas and A
2 CONC nne SPILMAN had a son William SPILMAN, died 1760, King George Co., Virginia. Dixon, Ben F., VIRGINIA SPILMAN MISCELLANY, VIRGINIA SPILMANS; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Clement SPILMAN, of Westmoreland Co., Va., married, ca. 1707, to Hannah (FOX) KENNER widow of Rodham KENNER (1671-1706). She had children by both marriages. Frances SPILMAN, a dau. of Clement and Hannah (FOX) SPILMAN, married Parr
2 CONC ish GARNER whose daughter Spelman GARNER married John CRALLE' Clement SPILMAN appears in the records of Westmoreland Co., Virginia in 1712 as suing Francis KENNER for a negro slave given to the plaintiff's wife, Hannah (FOX) KENNE
2 CONC R SPILMAN by her former husband Rodham KENNER. - WILLIAM AND MARY QUARTERLY, v. 9, p.185; v.14, p.178-179; v.17, pp.60-63.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the will of William FOX proved 1718 Westmoreland Co., Va., there is a reference to a Frances SPILMAN who married Parrish GARNER and to whom a daughter, Spelman GARNER, was born. Spelman GARNER married John CRALLE' and bore him 
2 CONC three children. - Melville, Malcolm L. THE SPILMAN PAPERS (Forestville, California 1965), p.12.
2 CONT
2 CONT An inventory was returned in to Court in Westmoreland County, Va., on 15 Aug. 1718, for a Thomas SPILMAN, deceased. The Court had given custody of his children to Capt. Augustine WASHINGTON, but John GREEN, an uncle, sought to hav
2 CONC e this order rescinded and himself appointed as their guardian. His petition was denied on 24 Sept. 1718 in these words: "Upon hearing the Petition of John GREEN relating to the children of Thomas SPELLMAN, deceased, of whom he al
2 CONC leged to be uncle: It appearing by the oath of John COOPER that the said SPELLMAN desired that Augustine WASHINGTON, Gentleman, should take care of his children, the Court are of the opinion that the said WASHINGTON ought to keep 
2 CONC them and do order that they be bound to him accordingly as the law directs." - Westmoreland Co. Deeds and Wills, Bk.6, pp.363-4; Melville, Malcolm L. THE SPILMAN PAPERS (Forestville, California 1965); p.336; Holtzclaw, B. C., ANCE
2 CONC STRY AND DESCENDANTS OF THE NASSAU-SIEGEN IMMIGRANTS TO VIRGINIA (1964), pp.467.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas SPILMAN died intestate in Washington Parish, Westmoreland Co., Virginia. In 1740 the inventory of Thomas SPILMAN of Westmoreland Co., Va. was returned into Court by Anne, his relict. His son, William SPELMAN, died in 1760, 
2 CONC and left in King George County sons Thomas, William and John, daughters Lettice, Delia and Margaret. - WILLIAM AND MARY QUARTERLY, v.14, p.178-179; Virkus's ABRIDGED COMPENDIUM OF AMERICAN GENEALOGY, v. ,p.218.
2 CONT
2 CONT In a deed dated 20 February 1738/9 (recorded 27 Feb. 1738), Robert FRANK and Mary his wife of the Parish of Washington in the County of Westmoreland sold, for six hundred pounds of tobacco, to William SPILLMAN, "a parcel of land w
2 CONC hich his grandfather Robert FRANK left him by will, being an estimation 160 acres, more or less, relation being thereto had will more fully appear, scituate, lying and being in Washington Parish in the County of Westmoreland, the 
2 CONC said land entirely lying on the eastern side of the head of Appomattock Creek, which said land all entirely of the eastern side of the head of said creek, the said Robert FRANK and Mary his wife doth unto the said William SPILLMAN
2 CONC , his heirs, executors, administrators, and assigns doth sell, alien and confirm with all houses, --- etc." Witnesses to the deed were Aaron HARDWICK, George FLETCHER, and Stafford LIGHTBURN. - Westmoreland Deeds and Wills: Bk.9, 
2 CONC pp.7a,8; Melville, Malcolm L. THE SPILMAN PAPERS (Forestville, California 1965), p.353-355.
2 CONT
2 CONT In a deed dated 27 May 1738, William SPILMAN purchased fifty acres "between the church path and the Meadow Branch" from John PLUNKETT and his wife Frances of Hanover Parish which they bought from John GREENING, "it having been wil
2 CONC led him by Robert FRANK." Westmoreland Deeds and wills, Bk.8, p.333.
2 CONT
2 CONT William SPILMAN (died 1760) of Westmoreland County, Virginia in his will named sons Thomas SPILMAN, William SPILLMAN and John SPILLMAN; and daughters Lettice DULIN and Margaret SPILLMAN. He left 200 acres more or less to his three
2 CONC  sons. This land was purchased in 1738 by William SPILMAN, and had previously been in the hands of heirs of Robert FRANK (d.1726 Westmoreland Co., Va.) The will of William SPILMAN was witnessed by Robert FRANK, William PIPER, and 
2 CONC Peter JETT, and was entered for probate on 29 April 1760. This William SPILMAN is believed to have married Margaret FRANK. Note that Robert FRANK signed as a witness to the will of William SPILMAN. - Melville, Malcolm L. THE SPILM
2 CONC AN PAPERS (Forestville, California 1965), p.350; Westmoreland County, Virginia Wills, Book 4, p.289, et seq; Holtzclaw, B. C., ANCESTRY AND DESCENDANTS OF THE NASSAU-SIEGEN IMMIGRANTS TO VIRGINIA (1964), pp.468.
2 CONT
2 CONT The two parcels of land purchased by William SPILMAN in 1738 constitute the 200 acres more or less inherited by William's three sons, Thomas, John, and William, named in his will of 1760.
2 CONT
2 CONT John SPILMAN of the Parrish of Brunswick, King George County, Virginia deeded his portion of the land in Westmoreland County inherited from his father, William SPILMAN (d.1760), to his brothers Thomas SPILMAN and William SPILMAN o
2 CONC f the Parrish of Washington, Westmoreland Co., Va. Seven years later in 1777 a boundary change placed all of this land in King George County. This land consisted of "all that undivided third part of and in all containing two hundr
2 CONC ed acres situate, lying, and being in the Parish of Washington and County of Westmoreland, the aforesaid land being part of a tract belonging to Robert FRANK and on the north side of the main branch of Mattox Creek and bounded as 
2 CONC followeth, beginning at a marked black oak and extending west to the line of William PIPER to marked red oak, thence along the said PIPER line N by W to marked hickorie, thence down the main branch of said creek and then along the
2 CONC  said dam to the uppermost line, thence to a black oak, thence East to a live oak in a small branch, finally to the first station, which said tract of land was given and devised to the said John SPILMAN, Thomas SPILMAN, and Willia
2 CONC m SPILMAN party hereto, in and by the last will and testament of the said William SPILMAN the father, deceased, etc." - Westmoreland County, Va. Deeds and Wills, Bk.14, p.508; Melville, Malcolm L. THE SPILMAN PAPERS (Forestville, 
2 CONC California 1965), pp.356-358; Holtzclaw, B. C., ANCESTRY AND DESCENDANTS OF THE NASSAU-SIEGEN IMMIGRANTS TO VIRGINIA (1964), pp.468.
2 CONT
2 CONT William SPILMAN, son of William and Margaret (FRANK) SPILMAN, married, ca. 1760, to Frances MASSEY, dau. of Lovell MASSEY who refers to her in his will as "my daughter Frances SPILMAN" and leaves her "my negro man, Will, to use hi
2 CONC m as her own during her natural life...." William and Frances (MASSEY) SPILMAN had one known child, John SPILMAN (ca.1765-1825). - Westmoreland Co., Va. Deeds and Wills, Bk.14, p.508.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Believed to be a son of Thomas and Elizabeth (DAWSON) SPILMAN, and grandson of James and Mary SPILMAN and grandson of Clement SPILMAN of Westmoreland Co., VA.  See also: Find A Grave Memorial# 25911029, Clement SPILMAN.
2 CONT
2 CONT James SPILMAN, in 1705, was a commercial envoy to the Russian Government at Moscow. His brothers were Clement, and Robert. He had sons, Thomas SPILMAN married Anne --?--, and Clement SPILMAN married Hannah FOX-KENNER. Thomas and A
2 CONC nne SPILMAN had a son William SPILMAN, died 1760, King George Co., Virginia. Dixon, Ben F., VIRGINIA SPILMAN MISCELLANY, VIRGINIA SPILMANS; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Clement SPILMAN, of Westmoreland Co., Va., married, ca. 1707, to Hannah (FOX) KENNER widow of Rodham KENNER (1671-1706). She had children by both marriages. Frances SPILMAN, a dau. of Clement and Hannah (FOX) SPILMAN, married Parr
2 CONC ish GARNER whose daughter Spelman GARNER married John CRALLE' Clement SPILMAN appears in the records of Westmoreland Co., Virginia in 1712 as suing Francis KENNER for a negro slave given to the plaintiff's wife, Hannah (FOX) KENNE
2 CONC R SPILMAN by her former husband Rodham KENNER. - WILLIAM AND MARY QUARTERLY, v. 9, p.185; v.14, p.178-179; v.17, pp.60-63.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the will of William FOX proved 1718 Westmoreland Co., Va., there is a reference to a Frances SPILMAN who married Parrish GARNER and to whom a daughter, Spelman GARNER, was born. Spelman GARNER married John CRALLE' and bore him 
2 CONC three children. - Melville, Malcolm L. THE SPILMAN PAPERS (Forestville, California 1965), p.12.
2 CONT
2 CONT An inventory was returned in to Court in Westmoreland County, Va., on 15 Aug. 1718, for a Thomas SPILMAN, deceased. The Court had given custody of his children to Capt. Augustine WASHINGTON, but John GREEN, an uncle, sought to hav
2 CONC e this order rescinded and himself appointed as their guardian. His petition was denied on 24 Sept. 1718 in these words: "Upon hearing the Petition of John GREEN relating to the children of Thomas SPELLMAN, deceased, of whom he al
2 CONC leged to be uncle: It appearing by the oath of John COOPER that the said SPELLMAN desired that Augustine WASHINGTON, Gentleman, should take care of his children, the Court are of the opinion that the said WASHINGTON ought to keep 
2 CONC them and do order that they be bound to him accordingly as the law directs." - Westmoreland Co. Deeds and Wills, Bk.6, pp.363-4; Melville, Malcolm L. THE SPILMAN PAPERS (Forestville, California 1965); p.336; Holtzclaw, B. C., ANCE
2 CONC STRY AND DESCENDANTS OF THE NASSAU-SIEGEN IMMIGRANTS TO VIRGINIA (1964), pp.467.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas SPILMAN died intestate in Washington Parish, Westmoreland Co., Virginia. In 1740 the inventory of Thomas SPILMAN of Westmoreland Co., Va. was returned into Court by Anne, his relict. His son, William SPELMAN, died in 1760, 
2 CONC and left in King George County sons Thomas, William and John, daughters Lettice, Delia and Margaret. - WILLIAM AND MARY QUARTERLY, v.14, p.178-179; Virkus's ABRIDGED COMPENDIUM OF AMERICAN GENEALOGY, v. ,p.218.
2 CONT
2 CONT In a deed dated 20 February 1738/9 (recorded 27 Feb. 1738), Robert FRANK and Mary his wife of the Parish of Washington in the County of Westmoreland sold, for six hundred pounds of tobacco, to William SPILLMAN, "a parcel of land w
2 CONC hich his grandfather Robert FRANK left him by will, being an estimation 160 acres, more or less, relation being thereto had will more fully appear, scituate, lying and being in Washington Parish in the County of Westmoreland, the 
2 CONC said land entirely lying on the eastern side of the head of Appomattock Creek, which said land all entirely of the eastern side of the head of said creek, the said Robert FRANK and Mary his wife doth unto the said William SPILLMAN
2 CONC , his heirs, executors, administrators, and assigns doth sell, alien and confirm with all houses, --- etc." Witnesses to the deed were Aaron HARDWICK, George FLETCHER, and Stafford LIGHTBURN. - Westmoreland Deeds and Wills: Bk.9, 
2 CONC pp.7a,8; Melville, Malcolm L. THE SPILMAN PAPERS (Forestville, California 1965), p.353-355.
2 CONT
2 CONT In a deed dated 27 May 1738, William SPILMAN purchased fifty acres "between the church path and the Meadow Branch" from John PLUNKETT and his wife Frances of Hanover Parish which they bought from John GREENING, "it having been wil
2 CONC led him by Robert FRANK." Westmoreland Deeds and wills, Bk.8, p.333.
2 CONT
2 CONT William SPILMAN (died 1760) of Westmoreland County, Virginia in his will named sons Thomas SPILMAN, William SPILLMAN and John SPILLMAN; and daughters Lettice DULIN and Margaret SPILLMAN. He left 200 acres more or less to his three
2 CONC  sons. This land was purchased in 1738 by William SPILMAN, and had previously been in the hands of heirs of Robert FRANK (d.1726 Westmoreland Co., Va.) The will of William SPILMAN was witnessed by Robert FRANK, William PIPER, and 
2 CONC Peter JETT, and was entered for probate on 29 April 1760. This William SPILMAN is believed to have married Margaret FRANK. Note that Robert FRANK signed as a witness to the will of William SPILMAN. - Melville, Malcolm L. THE SPILM
2 CONC AN PAPERS (Forestville, California 1965), p.350; Westmoreland County, Virginia Wills, Book 4, p.289, et seq; Holtzclaw, B. C., ANCESTRY AND DESCENDANTS OF THE NASSAU-SIEGEN IMMIGRANTS TO VIRGINIA (1964), pp.468.
