2 DATE 18 DEC 1755
2 ADDR
3 STAE VA
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Mary Howsen
2 CONT * Birth:	1705 
2 CONT * Death:	1755 - Northumberland, Virginia
2 CONT * Parents:	John Howson, Elizabeth Kenner
2 CONT * Husband:	Lawrence Parrott
2 CONT
2 CONT Children 
2 CONT #  Mary Ann Parrott
2 CONT #  William Parrott b:  in Northumberland Co., Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT ==notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=cw4peter&id=I051670
2 CONT
2 CONT The Lawrence Parrott & his descendants in central Kentucky and beyond source shows a 1710 date of birth, which is questionable.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Parrott made her will 12 August 1749. It was probated 18 December 1755. North’d Co. Record Book # 10, 1753-56 , page 244. She named her son, William Parrott, sole executor of her estate. Mary Haynie, granddaughter, who is und
2 CONC er 10 years of age in 1749, is the only other named legatee. She is to receive a pistol if she lives to the age of 10 years. Witnesses were Wm Cooke, Wm Edwards , Ann Simson or Limon?
2 CONT
2 CONT No record has ever been found to document the last name of Mary, wife of Lawrence. Hence, the relationships shown here to her ancestors are speculative. The use of the name “Rhodam” among her descendants suggests that she was some
2 CONC how related to that family. In addition, the presence of the Howsons in Northumberland county may have been the reason why Larenced moved there from Gloucester county. Finally, Woodie Brown Parrott speculates that her last name wa
2 CONC s Howson, based on the following:
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 21, 1736, three court-appointed commissioners returned a report to the Northumberland County court of their division of a tract of land between Richard Howson and Lawrence Parrott. It is very likely that this represented 
2 CONC a partition of realty among tenants in common; a join interest as such is created by co-heirs to a given parcel of real estate. Under the old English common law, of course, a woman held no property in her own legal entity separate
2 CONC  and apart from that of her husband, during the husband’s lifetime, and any legacy to which she was might have been entitled was always vested in the husband’s name. Thus if Mary was a Howson before marriage and co-heir with Richa
2 CONC rd Howson, it is not unusual that Mary would not have been named in the estate partitioning.
2 CONT
2 CONT  ============
2 CONT
2 CONT Note too that her father's sister would have been Hannah Howson, who married Francis Kenner, brother of her mother , Elizabeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
