1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 1. Robert Vaulx, Sr, a London merchant, from Cumberland co., England. Lived at Queen's Creek. York Co., VA, married Elizabeth Burwell, dau of Edward burwell and Dorothy Bedell
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Robert Vaulx, II, born 1651, died c. 1685, justice of peace. Westmoreland Co., VA, Lived on Pope'ds Creek., married Mary Foxall, born 1665, England. she was married 4 times: Robert Vaulx, Alex Gorges, Edward Dubbleston, Caleb B
2 CONC utler (through this marriage she became the ancestress of George Washington).
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Robert Vaulx III, born ?, died c. 1721, Westmoreland Co., VA, married Elizabeth Kenner, born Northumberland Co., va, married 1st Richard Bushrod, 3rd the Rev. Lawrence DeButts. She was the dau of Rodham Kenner, born March 23, 1
2 CONC 671, died August 21, 1706, Northumberland Co., VA, Justice of Peace, Burgess 1696, married Hannah Fox, c. 1690, Lancester co., VA, She was born July 25, 1671, Northumberland Co., VA, married 2nd Clement Spellman
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Elizabeth Vaulx, no dates for her, married 1715 to Col. George Eskridge who lived at Sandy Point, Westmoreland Co., VA. George Eskridge was married 1st to Rebecca Bonum. He came to VA in 1667. He was the guardian of Mary Ball, 
2 CONC mother of George Washington and Mary named her son, George Washington, for her guardian. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Vaulx married 3 times: 1st Richard Craddock, 2nd Daniel Porten, 3rd George Eskridge
2 CONT
2 CONT This information came from Family Tree Makers, "Virginia Genealogies" #1, CD #162
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Historical Atlas of Westmoreland County, Virginia" by David Eaton, 1942. ""Robert Vaulx, the emigrant, had brothers, Thomas, James and Humphrey. He married Elizabeth Burwell. Robert Vaulx patented land, 6,000 acres, with Major 
2 CONC William Gooch, uncle of Governor Gooch, as co-partners, June 9, 1655. Major Gooch dying without heirs in the year 1655, at the age of 29 years, Robert Vaulx, according to the laws of England, repatented the 6,000 acres in his own 
2 CONC name, March 1, 1656. He again repatented the same land March 24, 1662. Robert Vaulx, Sr. was a merchant in London and he willed the land to his son, Robert Vaulx II. Robert Vaulx, Jr. married Mary Foxhall and died in 1685, leaving
2 CONC  his father surviving him in London.""Westmoreland Court Order, November 19, 1685: Ordered that William Horton, surveyor, survey 6,000 acres patented by Robert Vaulx, June 5, 1655, in accordance with the petition of Mary Vaulx, no
2 CONC  Gorges, and no surveyor to enter upon said land without consent of Mr. Robert Vaulx of London, merchant, now represented by his brother, Mr. Humphrey Vaulx, who appeared in court this day. Mrs. Mary Gorges complained to the court
2 CONC  that some persons had confederated to deprive her of the 6,000 acres (called Vaulxland) and on which 'her husband had long lived.' She had a son Robert Vaulx III, who married Mary Ball. Mary Vaulx, after her first husband's death
2 CONC  in 1865, married Alexander Gorges. Robert Vaulx II was a Justice of Westmoreland County. Alexander Gorges died prior to October 31, 1688, on which date his will was probated. The 6,000 acre tract was sold by the family to small h
2 CONC olders, but a large tract of this land, 2,643 acres, came to the Fitzhugh family and then to George Washington Parke Custis, who, in 1834, had it surveyed into small holdings and sold."." In another chapter - "John Foxhall willed 
2 CONC Underwood Mill to James Vaulx and John Elliott, Jr. in 1695-8.".
