1 NAME Barnabus /Wilcox/
2 GIVN Barnabus
2 SURN Wilcox
2 _MARNM Wilcox
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1645
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bristol
3 CTRY England
1 BURI
2 DATE AFT 14 SEP 1690
2 PLAC Friends Arch Street Meeting House Burial Ground
2 ADDR
3 CITY Philadelphia
3 STAE Pennsylvania
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 14 SEP 1690
2 ADDR
3 STAE Province of Pennsylvania
3 CTRY Colonial America
1 OCCU Merchant and member of colonial assembly 1685, Justice O
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Barnabas was a member of the Society of Friends in Bristol and then  William Penn's Colony of Quakers in Philadelphia. He was the colonies  first rope maker.
2 CONT
2 CONT His political service was cut short by his early death. He appeared in  Quaker records as early as 1667 but does not seem to have played a  significant role in the sect, for his name is not found in either the  Bristol monthly Mee
2 CONC ting or Somerset Quarterly meeting minutes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Realizing the Colony's need for its own rope maker, Penn induced Barnabus  to purchase 1,000 acres by offering him terms equivalent to those given  first purchaser, that is, the right to two prime lots in Philadelphia.  Barnabus w
2 CONC atched the lots being surveyed, then returned to Bristol for  his family, only to discover that the two lots had been granted to others  in his absence. When he approached Penn to rectify the situation, the  proprietor equivocated
2 CONC  and by the time of Barnabus's death in 1690, the  promised lots had not been forthcoming.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite this initial setback, Barnabus accumulated 800 acres in Chester  County, 700 acres on the west side of the Schuykill River in the northern  Liberties of Philadelphia, 800 acres in Bristol Township, and another 250  acres i
2 CONC n an unspecified site in the county of Philadelphia. He may have  rented a property on Front St. between Mulberry and High Sts, although he  appears to have resided on the west side of the Schuylkill River.  In  1689 he purchased 
2 CONC 50 acres from Wm. Clayton near Mill Creek, where he  intended to build a mill, over the objections of Wm. Markham, who feared  that it might flood Wm. Penn's mill downstream.  By the time of his  death, Barnabus had sold 15 acres 
2 CONC of that property, as well as his 800  acres in Bristol Township.
2 CONT
2 CONT Barnabus was involved in overseeing some of the work on Philadelphia's  monthly meetings Center Square Meeting House and he also supplied 136  pounds of nails for the project.
2 CONT
2 CONT Barnabus made a nuncupative Will before witnesses, who committed it to  writing on Sept. 20, 1690, six days after his burial, and who exhibited  it in the Register General's office on 4 Oct., he failed to name an  executor and die
2 CONC d intestate. His widow Sarah, on 4 O t, requested and  received letters of Admin. on his estate after the witnesses testified  that the writing was truly his last Will. He bequeathed all his real and  personal estate to his childr
2 CONC en to be divided equally among them after  his widow's death. No inventory of his estate has been found, but Sarah  Wilcox's estate, after death in 1692 was valued at 841 pounds, including  the house and lot in Philadelphia worth 
2 CONC 70 pounds.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Barnabas Wilcox, [http://drphysick.com/samuelpowe.html Justice of Philadelphia], 1686 to 1690. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Barnabas was born in England and married Sarah (last name unk) in Bedminster Parish, Bristol abt 1665/6. They were both members of The Society of Friends. They emigrated to America in September 1683 aboard The William and Mary wit
2 CONC h their five children. Sarah bore one more child in America. The Wilcoxes attended Society of Friends Meetings where they settled in Philadelphia. <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Barnabas was a rope maker by trade, and the first in the colony. He held public office. In 1685 he was elected to the Assembly, in February 1682/3 he was elected to the Provincial Grand Jury. In August 1687 he was on the committee
2 CONC  to plan the road between Barnabanor and Darby Townships to the ferry at Philadelphia.<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Barnabas held several different jobs; in 1685 he was the Justice of the Peace, as held other positions that helped to advance the progress of Philadelphia. [https://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=63340963 Find a Gr
2 CONC ave]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT *[https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Wilcox-1379 Wikitree]
2 CONT *[https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/MNHT-99B Family Search]
