1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *Do not confuse with https://www.geni.com/people/Helen-Foster/6000000003166551855?through=6000000016670862342. They are two different women.
2 CONT *Ellen was not married to https://www.geni.com/people/Patrick-Foster-of-Wing/6000000225887903929. He was married to Helene.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ellen Foster (unknown maiden name) was an Elizabethan-era woman who lived in the borough of Wendover, Buckinghamshire, England, during the late 16th and early 17th centuries. Like her husband, Peter Foster, her historical footprin
2 CONC t is entirely preserved through English probate court files, rather than personal narrative records.
2 CONT
2 CONT Parentage:
2 CONT Her parents are not documented and are UNKNOWN.
2 CONT
2 CONT Factual Biographical Profile
2 CONT Birth: Estimated circa 1550 to 1555 in England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: She married Peter Foster, a fuller (cloth worker) from Wendover, likely around 1574–1576. Her exact marriage date and venue are missing from surviving parish archives.
2 CONT
2 CONT Documented children
2 CONT Chronological Birth Order
2 CONT 1. Richard Foster (born c. 1575)
2 CONT Status: Co-eldest child.
2 CONT Context: He was already a married adult when his father drafted his will. He remained in Wendover and fathered John Foster, who later immigrated to Salem, Massachusetts.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Katharen (Catherine) Foster (born c. 1577)
2 CONT Status: Likely the eldest daughter.
2 CONT Context: She married Ralph Tompkins before 1609. This couple famously immigrated to New England aboard the ship Truelove in 1635.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Ales Foster (born c. 1579)Status: Deceased before April 1609.
2 CONT Context: Her given name is unknown, but her existence is factually proven by her father’s will, which leaves a legacy to her daughter, Ales (Alice) Skinner.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Thomas Foster (born c. 1580)Status: Younger son [Thomas Foster Sr. (abt.1580-1618) | WikiTree FREE Family Tree].
2 CONT Context: He took over his father’s fulling (textile) business in Wendover and married Elizabeth Seamer around 1606 [Thomas Foster Sr. (abt.1580-1618)
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Margery Foster (born c. 1586)
2 CONT Status: Married daughter.
2 CONT Context: She is listed in the 1609 probate records by her married name, Margery Cooke.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Elizabeth Foster (born c. 1588)
2 CONT Status: Unmarried daughter.
2 CONT Context: She was still single and living at the family home in April 1609.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. Isabel Foster (born c. 1593)
2 CONT Status: Youngest daughter.Context: She is listed last among the daughters in the will and designated as unmarried, indicating she was likely a teenager in 1609.
2 CONT
2 CONT Residency: She resided with her family in Wendover, Buckinghamshire, a rural market town.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: She passed away after April 20, 1609 in Wendover, Buckinghamshire, England
2 CONT -Because she was alive when her husband drafted his will, she survived into the Jacobean era, but her exact burial date is unrecorded due to missing parish registers. It is highly probable that Ellen died in Wendover. We know she 
2 CONC was alive on April 20, 1609, because her husband, Peter, appointed her as an executor of his estate in his will [WikiTree Profile for Peter Foster]. Because early modern working-class widows rarely relocated from their home villag
2 CONC es—where their established family network, children, and property were located—she almost certainly lived out her remaining days in Wendover. Her exact date of death is unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: Ellen is buried at St. Mary the Virgin in Aylesbury, Buckinghamshire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Primary Source Documentation
2 CONT Ellen's historical existence is legally proven by a single surviving manuscript: the 1609 Last Will and Testament of Peter Foster (Archdeaconry Court of Buckingham, Register Wf, volume 18, folio 37A).The legal text outlines her st
2 CONC atus through three vital roles:Identity: She is explicitly designated in writing by her husband as "my wife Ellen". No maiden name is provided anywhere in the probate record.
2 CONT
2 CONT Executorship: Peter appointed her to help manage or oversee his estate legacies, proving she was legally recognized, mentally capable, and trusted to handle the family's assets and business wind-down.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maternal Link: The will names her children (Richard, Thomas, Katharen, Margery, Elizabeth, and Isabel), confirming her as the matriarch of the family branch that eventually produced early New England colonists.Common Genealogical 
2 CONC DiscrepanciesBecause Ellen’s true maiden name is lost to history, her identity is heavily corrupted on online platforms like MyHeritage and Ancestry. Professional researchers separate the facts from these common fabrications:The "
2 CONC Greenway" Error: Countless internet trees claim her name was Helene Greenway. This is an error. Helene Greenway was a completely different woman who lived 13 miles away in Wing, Buckinghamshire, and married Patrick Foster. Ellen o
2 CONC f Wendover has no documented connection to the Greenways.The "Newton" Error: Some merged profiles list her as Helene Newton Foster. This middle name or secondary surname has no basis in 1609 English legal documents.The Death Date 
2 CONC Myth: Many trees state she died on July 13, 1609. This is false; July 13, 1609, is simply the day her husband's will was officially proved in court after his death. Her actual date of death remains historically unproven.
