1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Margery Foster (married name Cooke) was a daughter of Peter and Ellen Foster of Wendover, Buckinghamshire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Factual Documentation (The Primary Source)
2 CONT The definitive primary source proving Margery's identity is the last will and testament of her father, Peter Foster, written April 20, 1609, and proved on July 13, 1609 (Archdeaconry of Buckingham, Wf/18/37A).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Legal Entry: Her father explicitly leaves a bequest to "my daughter, Margery Cooke".
2 CONT
2 CONT Marital Status: This legal phrasing conclusively documents that she was alive and already married to a man with the surname Cooke prior to April 1609.The Missing Parish Record: Her precise dates for baptism, marriage, and burial d
2 CONC o not exist. As noted in the Wendover Marriage Register Archives, the original parish books prior to 1626 are completely missing or destroyed.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Biographical Details & Timeline Analysis
2 CONT Because direct vital records are missing, genealogists reconstruct her life chronology based on her marriage status in 1609:
2 CONT Estimated Birth (c. 1586): Family tree data on platforms like FamilySearch often approximate her birth year around 1586. This tracks cleanly with the average age of marriage for Elizabethan women (typically between 18 and 22), mea
2 CONC ning she likely married Mr. Cooke around 1604–1608.The "Cooke" Husband: Beyond his surname being recorded in the 1609 probate entry, the first name and occupation of Margery’s husband remain completely unverified in surviving Buck
2 CONC inghamshire records.
