2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT RALPH TOMPKINS (TOMKINS) & PHILIP KNIGHT
2 CONT                FAMILIES OF SALEM MASSACHUSETTS  
2 CONT
2 CONT                    RALPH TOMPKINS OF SALEM                       
2 CONT
2 CONT                              First Generation
2 CONT
2 CONT 	1.	RALPH TOMPKINS was born about 1585 in England.1 He died in 1666 in Bridgewater, Plymouth Co., MA.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ralph Tompkins immigrated to this country September 1635 on the ship TRUELOVE.  The passenger list of this vessel lists Ralph and his family as follows: "RALPH TOMKINS, Husb - Ae 50; KAT. TOMKINS vxor, Ae 58; ELIZABETH TOMKINS, Ae
2 CONC  18; SAMVEL TOMKINS Ae 22; MARIE TOMKINS Ae 14" 2
2 CONT
2 CONT Ralph was a member of the First Church in Dorchester, MA in 1636 and a freeman in Dorchester MA on 2 May 1638.3 He was left a household article in the estate of widow Judith Smead of Dorchester in 1639 4 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 29 Jan 1648/9 the Salem selectman granted a small  piece of meadow lying near Major-General Endecotts land in a corner by a small brook and a great swamp5 There are other land evidences recorded in Essex County for Ralph  Tompk
2 CONC ins.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 27 Nov. 1666, administration of Ralph's estate was granted to John Tompkins who presented an inventory on 12 Nov. 1666.  The Court ordered that the five acres of land mentioned in the inventory be given to the said John and aft
2 CONC er Ralph's debts were paid the remainder of the estate was to be given to Mary, daughter of John Foster 6
2 CONT
2 CONT The inventory of Ralph Tompkins estate was taken by Thomas Gardner and John Kitchin. 12  Nov. 1666  Samuel (his mark) Aburne, aged 52 years deposed that at the burial of the wife of Ralph Tomkins, late deceased, as soon as the com
2 CONC pany had departed he went into said Ralph, who was weak and not likely long to survive his wife, to put him in mind of making his will.  He also wished to speak with him about the will which his wife made which was to dispose of w
2 CONC hat was hers before her marriage. Deponent told Tompkins that his sister, Tompkins wife, had bequeathed all her property to Mary Foster because she had been so helpful to her during her long sickness, doing for her what nobody els
2 CONC e would do. Tompkins replied that he fully agreed to this but he would like to have the use of it during his life and he would rather increase than diminish it.  Before making his will he would first talk with his son Foster, and 
2 CONC deponent thought that it was his intention to give what he had to Mary Foster.  Also that Tomkins was of this mind when he was removing to Bridgewater, and when, thinking never to see him again, depondent reminded  him of Mary Fos
2 CONC ter.7
2 CONT
2 CONT An (her M Mark) Small aged about 50 years, deposed that Goodwife Tompkins told her that she had willed all to Mary Foster and that the cow was brought up from a calf for said Mary.8Edward Grove, aged about 40 years also deposed..9
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathaniel Felton, aged about 50 years, deposed that Tompkins desired to have the bed and other household stuff, but if he removed to Bridgewater to his son Samuel's.........10 
2 CONT
2 CONT RALPH married (1) KATHERINE FOSTER, daughter of PETER FOSTER and ELLEN, on 6 Nov 1608 in Wendover, Buckinghamshire, England.11 Katherine was born about 1577 in England  per ship’s passenger list. She was living on 3 Apr 1642 when 
2 CONC she was admitted to the First Church of Salem, MA.12
2 CONT
2 CONT RALPH and KATHERINE had the following children:13 
2 CONT
2 CONT +	2	M	i.   JOHN Sr. TOMPKINS was born about 1610 and died on 23 Jun 1681.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	3	M	ii.  SAMUEL TOMPKINS was born about 1613. He died in 1676 in Bridgewater, MA. Will dated 1673 - age 63, his land went to Francis Cary brought up and lived with Samuel.  will mentions brother John Tompkins, cousins Mary Foster
2 CONC , Elizabeth White, Hannah Doggett.14 SAMUEL married LETTICE FOSTER on 11 Oct 1639.15
2 CONT
2 CONT 	4	F	iii.  ELIZABETH TOMPKINS was born about 1617.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	5	M	iv.    MICAH?? TOMKINS was born about 1615.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	6	F	v.     MARIE TOMPKINS was bapt 17 Apr 1620
2 CONT
2 CONT RALPH also married (2) HANNAH ABORNE. Hannah died about 1665/1666..16 Her birth date is uncertain.  She was perhaps the daughter of THOMAS ABORNE/EBORNE, an early settler of Salem, MA. She may have been  much younger than her husb
2 CONC and and within the child bearing years but  this is conjectural. Samuel Aborne calls Hannah his sister in Ralph Tompkins estate papers. Samuel deposed age 52 in 1666 
2 CONT
2 CONT They had the following child
2 CONT
2 CONT +	7	F	vi.	MARTHA TOMPKINS was born about 1636.
2 CONT
2 CONT                          Second Generation
2 CONT
2 CONT 	2.	JOHN Sr. TOMPKINS (RALPH) was born about 1610. He died on 23 Jun 1681 in Salem, Essex Co., MA.17
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tompkins Sr. came to America prior to 20 March 1636 when he was promised he would be received as an inhabitant of Salem, MA "in case he procure free  dismission" from some other church.18On 12 July 1637  two acres of land was
2 CONC  granted to him fulfilling this promise 19
2 CONT
2 CONT On 22 Aug. 1663 the Salem selectmen granted John Tompkins all the land lying at the end of the five acres he purchased of Jefferie Estee next to Robert Cotte 20 John was a freeman in Salem in 164221 
2 CONT
2 CONT At a Salem Quarterly Court Meeting June 1672 John Tomkins Sr. & John Tomkins Jr. among others, certified that the "being nere neighbores vnto Elizabeth Goodel the daughter of  Edw. Beachom and having had acquaintance with her from
2 CONC  childhood to her marriage Do testify yt according to our best observation and judgment shee hath been of an honest civill conversation & one yt would not wrong the truth in her speeches." 22
2 CONT
2 CONT At a Commissioners Court at Salem 2 Mar 1679 John Tomkins, among others, refused to take the oath as tythingmen and were fined 23
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of is death he owned land, a dwelling house, barn, outhouse, orchard and about 17 acres of improved land valued at 100 pounds.24
2 CONT
2 CONT An inventory of John's estate was taken 30 June 1681 by Edward Batter & Nathaniel Felton Jr. amounting to 141-17-00d.  Administration of the estate was granted to Mary, the relict on 28 June 1681.  An addition to the inventory of 
2 CONC 9f taken by the same persons  mentioned that the estate of the relict before marriage was 125-12-00d.  This record mentions  John's children in the following order: Nathaniel, John, Sarah, Elizabeth, Mary, Deborah and   Priscilla 
2 CONC Tompkins. The estate papers contain the depositions of Josiah White and  Rememberance, his wife - these deponents testified that they heard John Tompkins Sr. say sundry  times that it was his will that his son Nathaniel Tompkins b
2 CONC eing his eldest son should have his then dwelling house with the barne and ground thereunto belonging for he had given his other children something considerable for their portions and this was spoken a short time before the  said 
2 CONC Tompkins his disease - Sworn 30 Nov. 1681. The widow was to have two thirds of the  house and land during her life and son John Jr. 5li; the five childen of Hugh Jones, whose wife  Hannah was now deceased were to have 20s each.  T
2 CONC he remainder of the estate was equally  divided among the seven children of the deceased.25 
2 CONT
2 CONT John may be the John Tomkins who married (1) MARGARET GOODMAN on 27 Aug 1632 in Edlesborough, England.26 Margaret died on 18 Jul 1672 in Salem, Essex Co., MA.27
2 CONT
2 CONT On 30 June 1653 John Tompkin’s wife was fined for wearing a silk hood .28 
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHN and MARGARET had the following children:29
