2 CONT The property of John Tompkins Sr. descended to John Tompkins Jr. and his brother Nathaniel.  The year following John Sr's death Nathaniel Tompkins released the homestead to John Jr.  When John Jr. died the house and 14 acres of la
2 CONC nd which went with it were valued at 85 pounds.  On 27 Feb. 1705/6 John and Samuel Foster who had lived in Salem, in the north field or north neck, fifty odd years, deposed that they knew that John Tompkins of Salem, who the prece
2 CONC ding month, had been possessed in his own right of a dwelling house, barne and about 14  land arable and pasture, in ye north field, in Salem, bounded 'northerly with ye river known by  ye name of Cowhoufe River easterly partly up
2 CONC on ye land of George Jacobs and partly the lands of John Small, southerly upon ye Land of John Hathorne Esq. & partly upon ye Land of John Waters from ye first day of October 1692 until ye first day of October  anno dom 1704." Thi
2 CONC s land eventually descended to Nathaniel Tompkins, son of John Jr. 54 John Tompkins Jr. deposed 11 Nov. 1670, age about 25.55
2 CONT
2 CONT At a Salem Quarterly Court Meeting June 1677 John Waters, aged 35 years and Stephen Small aged 20 years deposed that they saw George Jacobs Sr. strike John Tomkins Jr., one blow and if the latter had not held him by the arms, he w
2 CONC ould have struck him some more, he being in such a passion. Jacobs paid a fine for this offense.56 
2 CONT
