1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Elizabeth Foster was a daughter of Peter and Ellen Foster of Wendover, Buckinghamshire, England. Like her sisters Isabel and Margery, her historical record is limited but definitive due to her placement in her fath
2 CONC er’s estate documents.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Factual Documentation (The Primary Source)The primary source proving Elizabeth's existence is the last will and testament of her father, Peter Foster, a fuller from Wendover. The document was written on April 20, 1609, and prov
2 CONC ed on July 13, 1609 (Archdeaconry of Buckingham, Wf/18/37A).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Legal Entry: In the text of the will, her father explicitly leaves a bequest to "my daughter Elizabeth Foster".
2 CONT
2 CONT Marital Status in 1609: Because she is legally recorded strictly under her maiden name (Elizabeth Foster), it is factually documented that she was unmarried at the time the will was finalized.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Record Gap: There are no surviving direct parish entries for her birth, baptism, marriage, or burial. The local 1609 Bishop’s Transcripts for Wendover—which would have recorded her father's burial and nearby family events—are 
2 CONC permanently missing.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Biographical Details & Timeline Analysis
2 CONT Because direct vital dates do not exist, genealogists establish her life timeline contextually based on the marriage status of her siblings:
2 CONT Estimated Birth (c. 1588): Crowdsourced platforms like FamilySearch typically approximate her birth year around 1588. This positioning makes sense chronological to the family layout, placing her as one of the younger sisters.
2 CONT Age of Majority Context: If born around 1588, Elizabeth would have been roughly 21 years old in 1609. In the early 17th century, women routinely married between ages 20 and 24, indicating Elizabeth was right at the typical age of 
2 CONC leaving home or preparing for marriage when her father passed away.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Avoiding Tree Confusion
2 CONT When organizing Elizabeth's data in family software, ensure she is kept separate from common ancestral errors:
2 CONT The Single-Mother Myth: Do not confuse her with her deceased, unnamed sister who married a Mr. Skinner and mothered Alice Skinner. Elizabeth was explicitly an unmarried "Foster" in 1609.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Later "Humphrey" Marriage: Be careful not to conflate this generation with later 17th-century Massachusetts lines (such as the family of Thomas Foster of WikiTree), where names like Elizabeth Seamer or Elizabeth Humphrey appea
2 CONC r. This Elizabeth Foster belongs exclusively to the early Buckinghamshire branch.
