1 NOTE {geni:about_me} -Married to an Unknown Skinner
2 CONT -Had a daughter named Ales “Alice Skinner
2 CONT
2 CONT What is Documented in the Primary Source
2 CONT In the last will and testament of Peter Foster of Wendover, written April 20, 1609, he leaves a bequest to "Ales Skinner, my daughter’s child". This brief phrase provides the only historical footprint of the mother:
2 CONT Proof of Relationship: She is legally established as a biological daughter of Peter and Ellen Foster.
2 CONT
2 CONT Proof of Death: She is documented as deceased prior to April 20, 1609. In 17th-century English estate planning, if a child was alive, the inheritance went directly to them; leaving an inheritance straight to a grandchild ("my daug
2 CONC hter's child") indicates the intermediary parent had already passed away.
2 CONT
2 CONT Proof of Marriage: Her marriage to a man with the surname Skinner is legally verified by her daughter Alice's last name.
2 CONT
2 CONT Deduced Timeline and Life Details
2 CONT Professional genealogical research funded by the Foster family associations confirms several likely facts about her life span:
2 CONT Estimated Birth (c. 1573–1580): Her parents married around 1572. Given that her siblings (like Thomas Foster and Margery) were born in the late 1570s and 1580s, she was likely one of the elder children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Estimated Marriage (c. 1595–1598): She would have married Mr. Skinner in Buckinghamshire upon reaching adulthood (typically age 18 to 21 for women of the period).
2 CONT
2 CONT Cause of Death: While undocumented, women in this era most frequently passed away due to complications from childbirth or local disease outbreaks (such as typhus or the plague).
