3 STAE North Carolina
3 CTRY British Colonial America
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT *John didn’t have a middle name.
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse:
2 CONT Elizabeth Ann Murray
2 CONT 1714–Deceased
2 CONT -Marriage: 1720
2 CONT Tyrrell, North Carolina, United States
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT James Ward
2 CONT 1724–Deceased
2 CONT
2 CONT John Ward, II
2 CONT 1725–1780
2 CONT
2 CONT David Ward
2 CONT Abt.1727–Deceased
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Ward
2 CONT 1729–Deceased
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael Ward
2 CONT 1730–1764
2 CONT
2 CONT Dorcas Ward
2 CONT 1732–1804
2 CONT
2 CONT William Ward
2 CONT 1733–Deceased
2 CONT
2 CONT Phereba Ward
2 CONT 1734–1810
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Ward
2 CONT 1737–1785
2 CONT
2 CONT On 22 Nov. 1744, at Newburn, JAMES SMITH, JOHN SMITH and JOHN WARD all received patents to tracts of land. JAMES SMITH'S was for 405 acres in Tyrrell, JOHN SMITH'S for 200 acres in the same county and JOHN WARD'S for 640 acres in 
2 CONC adjoining Beaufort County. The one to WARD is copied below:
2 CONT
2 CONT "GEORGE the second, To all to whom - know ye that We have given and granted unto JOHN WARD a tract of land containing 6 hundred and forty acres lying and being in Beaufort County beginning at a pine on ye north side of Little Cont
2 CONC anknee, (Contentna) running east 320 poles and through the swamp to a pine, thence 320 poles to an oak, thence 320 poles to a pine and thence 320 poles to the first station, to hold - yielding and paying four shillings proclamatio
2 CONC n money yearly for every hundred acres. Seating (?) the same according to his rights, clearing and cultivating three acres for every hundred within three years and those-the auditor within six months" --
2 CONT Dated 21 Nov. 1744 and signed by GAB JOHNSTON Esq. spoken of in the 1743 grant as "His Majesties Capt., General and Governor in Chief over said province."
2 CONT
2 CONT Just over three years after receiving the patent to this section of land, presumedly shortly after he had met the condition for clearing, JOHN WARD conveyed a total of 485 acres lying on Little Contaneeke Creek in Beaufort Co., in
2 CONC  three separate parcels to JOHN WARD JR., MICHAEL WARD and JOHN OVERSTREET. These plots of land adjoined one another, were all on Little Contentna and deeds all dated January 28, 1747/48. They were all registered in Beaufort in Se
2 CONC ptember, 1748 and the non-participating parties of one deed were the witnesses of the others.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus JOHN OVERSTREET and JOHN WARD JR. witnessed the deed to MICHAEL WARD, who witnessed the deed to each of the other, etc., and each of the conveyances was signed by JOHN and wife, ELIZABETH WARD. Even though the land lay in Bea
2 CONC ufort and the deeds were properly recorded there, JOHN WARD is spoken of as of Tyril Co., Province of N.C.
2 CONT
2 CONT The deed to MICHAEL WARD was for 160 acres in Beaufort "land already in his tenue and occupancy," so it is probable that this section of 640 acres contained the 500 acres for which JOHN WARD applied in 1743, "on which MICHAEL WARD
2 CONC  was then living."
2 CONT
2 CONT A little more than a year later on 5 March 1748/49, JOHN WARD of Tyrrell Co. made his will which was recorded in 1750. In it he identifies the grantees of the Beaufort Co. deeds as his children and names two more, his son, DAVID, 
2 CONC who was the father of ROBERT WARD of Halifax Co. N.C., and a second daughter, ELIZABETH NOBLE.
2 CONT 1. To his dearly beloved wife, (ELIZABETH) he left his manor plantation in the county of Terryl with all his stock of cattle, sheep and horses, a negro man named TOM, and all my household furniture until the day of her marriage or
2 CONC  death
2 CONT 2. To his son, MICHAEL WARD, after death of his wife, L35 Va. money which is due by bond from CALEB HOWELL of Beaufort Co.
2 CONT 3. To his son, JOHN WARD, a negro man named TOM, with the cows and calves running on Little Cantanknee.
2 CONT 4. To his son, DAVID WARD, (who had not received any land by deed), he left his home plantation containing about 180 acres, after the death or marriage of his wife.
2 CONT 5. To his daughter, ELIZABETH NOBEL, a plantation of 140 acres of land being in Terryl Co., on which she now lives, after the death of my said wife, and ten head of cattle.
2 CONT 6. To DORCAS OVERSTREET 100 acres on Little Contentna adjacent JOHN WARD'S land. This was in addition to the 165 acres to her husband by deed.
2 CONT
2 CONT The residue of the estate to be equally divided between MICHAEL, JOHN and DAVID WARD and ELIZABETH NOBLE. MICHAEL and JOHN WARD JR. were named executors. The will was signed with JOHN WARD'S mark which was a sprawling capital E. a
2 CONC nd it was witnessed by GRIFFITH HOWELL, JOHN and ELIZABETH BUTLER, the latter making his mark with an E turned on its' face.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Terrell Co., two days before the registration of the will of JOHN WARD, on 14 May 1750, CORNELIUS FITZPATRIC of that county on 12 May 1750, conveyed to JOHN WARD of the same county and state a parcel of land on the North side o
2 CONC f Alligator Ridge in Tyrrell Co., commonly called Cow Ridge, 125 acres. (D. Bk. II p. 415 Tyrrell Co. N.C.) Too late to have been in the will.
2 CONT
2 CONT By deed and by will, we have found that JOHN WARD SR. of Tyrrell conveyed a total of 905 acres, 265 acres more than the patent of 640 acres which was his of 22 Nov. 1744.
2 CONT
2 CONT One hundred and 40 acres of this surplus was no doubt in the 140 acres place which WARD gave by will to his daughter, ELIZ. NOBLE and which lay in Tyrrell Co.
2 CONT
2 CONT The other surplus, or part of it, is accounted for in the homeplace of 180 acres which was left to the young son, DAVID WARD, and which lay in both Tyrrell and Beaufort Cos. The 100 acres needed in Beaufort to make up the full 640
2 CONC  acres, after WARD'S deeds to JOHN JR., MICHAEL and JOHN OVERSTREET (husband of DORCAS) for a total of 485 acres, was partly taken care of in his will for an additional 100 acres to DORCAS OVERSTREET in Beaufort, the land lying on
2 CONC  Little Contentna adj. JOHN WARD.
2 CONT
2 CONT The home plantation in Terrell and Beaufort of JOHN WARD I) of Tyrrell was on Flat Swamp. The land he deeded in 1748 to his sons MICHAEL and JOHN WARD JR. and his SIL. JOHN OVERSTREET was on Little Contentna. When Pitt Co. was mad
2 CONC e from Beaufort in 1760 all of that land on Little Contentna and part on the swamp fell in it.
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHN WARD III) was later in complete possession of the 350 acres which he and THOS. received jointly from ROGER BRIAN and there is no intervening deed which explains this transaction. Why if, as we believe, THOMAS continued to liv
2 CONC e in Norfolk Co. for some years? Perhaps he traded his interest in it for land which he later gave to THOMAS HARRIS, part of the 100 acre tract which was granted to JOHN WARD in 1666 and so called "JOHN WARDS' Old Plantation."
2 CONT
2 CONT We have been following the story of THOMAS WARD IV) only, because until JOHN appears as joint grantee of the 1687 deed, ROGER BRIANT to THOMAS and JOHN WARD, we had found no documents of JOHN WARD in Norfolk records, since 1666. T
2 CONC hat was JOHN II) while JOHN of the deed was his son, JOHN WARD III).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1702, on May 20, JOHN WARD III) of the Western Branch of Elizabeth River, in Norfolk Co. planter, for 4000 pounds of tobacco "sold to JAMES SMITH 50 acres of my patent situated lying -- beginning at the lands of ROGER BRYAN, so
2 CONC uth-easterly along the line of JOHN SMITH to ye lands of THOMAS SMITH and then northeasterly along a line of marked trees to the land of JOHN BRUCE and ROGER BRYAN to the lands of Champion Stark being the first station.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the same page but of a later date, Sept. 15, 1702 or 1703, "JAMES SMITH for myself and my executors or administrators for a valuable consideration paid by JOHN WARD of Norfolk Co. - acknowledge, reaffirm sold and make over all 
2 CONC my rights, title, interest, etc., in this bill of sale to him, his heirs, etc. - forever, as full payment - granted unto me by the said WARD and J-,THOMAS and ISAAC SMITH." Signed by JAMES (F) SMITH and MARY (M) SMITH.
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHN WARD gives bond for self and executors in sum of 8000 lbs. of tobacco not to molest JAMES SMITH. Witnessed by ELEAZAR TART SR. and JR. deed acknowledged in the June 1702, court by JOHN WARD and his wife ELIZABETH WARD (Bk. 7,
2 CONC  p. 6).
2 CONT
2 CONT From these deeds, it is not difficult to deduct that ELIZABETH, wife of JOHN WARD, was ELIZABETH SMITH, sister of the "SMITH Brothers" named above and that her husband, JOHN WARD III) may have been an executor or administrator of 
2 CONC her father's estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT On July 18, 1703/4 JOHN WARD of the Western Branch of Elizabeth River in the Co. of Norfolk Virginia with the voluntary consent of ELIZABETH his wife, for 2200 lbs. of tobacco to HENRY BOWES, a small parcel of land on the southwes
2 CONC t side of the branch, aforesaid being part of a parcel of land I bought of ROGER BRYAN containing 350 a. - by patent 1686 - said tract containing 30 acres bounded by head line of SPARROW and side line of JOHN SOLLY and THOMAS SMIT
2 CONC H and alongside line of THOS. BOORMAN - as by sd. patent - HENRY BOWLS." (Bk. 7, p. 40) Signed JOHN (E) WARD, ELIZABETH (Q) WARD. Witnesses: MATHEW SPIVEY and THOS. PRICE.
2 CONT
2 CONT With this deed of thirty acres the 350 acres from ROGER BRYAN became 320 all of which were taken care of in the will of JOHN WARD III) which, along with many others of the same era, were not recorded before they were lost or destr
2 CONC oyed. Note the mark with which ELIZABETH WARD signed this document. Her son JOHN was to marry another ELIZABETH who used a different mark on her signature. The first signed with an open capital Q. The latter an upside down E.
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHN WARD IV) acted as executor, with his brother, THOMAS of his father's will. With consent of his wife, ELIZABETH, sold for five shillings, to WM. PRICE and RICHARD PRICE his tract of land on the Western Branch of Elizabeth Rive
2 CONC r 107 acres adj. land WM. ELLIS bought of his brother, JOSEPH WARD, 107 acres, being part of a tract of land my father, JOHN WARD, bought of ROGER BRYAN and gave unto the said WARD. This deed recorded in Bk. G. p. 105, was dated J
2 CONC une 14, 1728, was signed by JOHN WARD and ELIZABETH WARD and was acknowledged in court by WARD 15 Nov. 1728 and by his wife 26 Dec. 1728. The 1730 Tax List of Norfolk Co. Virginia for the Western Branch of Elizabeth River lists JO
2 CONC HN WORDE and THOMAS WORDE the only WARDS in the county. JOHN SMITH married MARY. He died 1711 leaving a will witnessed by ELEAZOR TART, THOS. SMITH, WILLIAM ELLIS and THOS. WARD.
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHN WARD IV) of Western Branch of Elizabeth River sold his land in Norfolk Co. in 1728. In 1729 a JOHN WARD of Bertie Co., later Tyrrell, received by deed from JAMES MURRAY, land that later fell in Tyrrell. JAMES MURRAY and wife,
2 CONC  ELIZABETH, made deeds on the same day for a like amount of land in the same locality to THOMAS BRIDGEMAN and SAMUEL PRESTON (or PRESCOTT).
2 CONT
2 CONT This land had been recently conveyed to MURRAY by JOHN EDWARDS and his wife, ELIZABETH.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 22 Nov. 1744 JOHN WARD of Tyrrell added extensively to his holdings in adjoining Beaufort with a grant for six hundred and forty acres, at Newburn. On the same date both JOHN SMITH and a JAMES SMITH received substantial grants 
2 CONC in Tyrrell Co.
2 CONT
2 CONT About 300 acres of JOHN WARD'S estate fell in Tyrrell including the original land from JAMES MURRAY and his residence.
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of his wife, like JOHN IV) of Western Branch of Elizabeth River, was ELIZABETH and the mark he used to sign his name was the same W used by his namesake in Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Family - Sutherland - "We cousins" (Virginia to Texas): a genealogy of several of the families comprising the Alabama settlement of Austin's Colony, 1830 and 1831, now Texas, and including the other Virginia lines of the S
2 CONC utherland family - Florence Hudson - Strawberry Point, Iowa: Press-Journal Publishing Company, 1958.
2 CONT vol 2: page 106, 141-2.
2 CONT
2 CONT
