1 NAME Joseph /Morrow/
2 GIVN Joseph
2 SURN Morrow
2 _MARNM Morrow
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1760
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Northern Ireland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 30 NOV 1835
2 ADDR
3 CITY Chester
3 STAE SC
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} This Joseph Morrow, b. 1760, is likely ''not'' the son of David Morrow b. 1731 in Virginia, since this Joseph Morrow was born in Ireland as stated in his own Revolutionary War pension application.  Since he was fro
2 CONC m the Chester County, SC, area, he is likely from the group of Morrows there, who included brothers [https://www.geni.com/people/David-Morrow-c1715-c1795/6000000015467797157 '''David Morrow 1715-c1795'''] and [https://www.geni.com
2 CONC /people/Old-Samuel-Morrow/6000000011048324232 ''' "Old" Samuel Morrow 1720-1796'''].
2 CONT
2 CONT A Revolutionary soldier, Joseph Morrow b. 1760 was severely wounded at the battle of Hanging Rock.  He applied for a pension on 19 Dec 1832 due to the injury he suffered at the battle of Hanging Rock, at which time it was believed
2 CONC  he was 72 yrs of age. In this document, he states, "I was born, as I have been told, in the kingdome of Ireland, County of Antrim, in the year 1760"....."I have no record of my age"...."I was living in the State of South Carolina
2 CONC , then Craven County, ''now Chester District'' when called into service and have lived in the same place ever since, and live there now,"....etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Morrow made a Will, dated 8-2-1831, which was probated 12-1-1835, in which he names his wife, Jane; and his four daughters: Sarah White, Betsey Wylie, Jane Wylie, and Polly Thomas; sons: Joseph Morrow, William W. Morrow, an
2 CONC d David Morrow. (A son, John is not named in the will, but family members list such a son, who may have married prior to the Will and settlement already made with him).
2 CONT
2 CONT In his Will he stated that his son, Joseph Morrow (Jr.) was to receive "the west end or side of my plantation......" "provided he, the said Joseph Morrow provides his mother, Jane, with a comfortable support during her natural lif
2 CONC e, either with him or with any of her other children she chooses to live with or where she chooses to live and to support her horse beast if she chooses to keep one."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Joseph Morrow''' in ''Roster of South Carolina Patriots in the Revolution,'' p. 705 (online at Ancestry.com):
2 CONT
2 CONT B. 1760, Ireland.  He served under Lt. Alexander Campbell and Capt. Philip Walker escorting prisoners from Ninety Six to Charleston.  Next, he was drafted under the same two officers and Col. Simons. Thereafter he was under Lt. Hu
2 CONC gh McClure and Capt. William Jones.  After the fall of Charleston, he volunteered under Lt. John Steele, Capt. John McClure, Cols. Lacey and Winn and Gen. Sumter.  At some time, he was under Cols. Neel and Bratton.  He was in the 
2 CONC battles of Huck's Defeat and Hanging Rock, where his arm was shattered by a rifle ball.  In addition, he was in the battles at Mobley's Meeting House and Rocky Mount.
