1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''(Major) Samuel EARLE, III,''' son of Samuel Earle II and Phyllis Bennett, 
2 CONT * was born in Abt. 1692 in Westmoreland County, Virginia 
2 CONT * educated at the College of William and Mary. 
2 CONT * lived for a while in Old Frederick County, Virginia where he was elected the 1st Member of the House of Burgesses from Frederick County holding the seat that would later be held by future (President) George WASHINGTON whose fath
2 CONC er, Augustine WASHINGTON owned land that was adjoining land owned by Samuel EARLE, I who was the paternal grandfather of the aforementioned (Major) Samuel EARLE, III.  
2 CONT * Earle's Ferry near present Front Royal, Virginia
2 CONT * Member of House of Burgesses 1743-1744.  
2 CONT * High Sheriff of Frederick, VA, 1742.  
2 CONT * Church Warden, 1751.  
2 CONT * Justice of Co Court, Frederick, VA 1749-1752.  
2 CONT * Major-Col George William's Fairfax Colonial Regiment.  
2 CONT * Planter, Attorney at Law  
2 CONT * Lived in or owned land in Prince William, Stafford, Fauquier and Frederick Counties, VA. 
2 CONT * Married Anna Sorrell and Elizabeth Holbrook
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT *''The Earles and The Birnies'', by Joseph Earle Birnie, publ 1974, p 37 
2 CONT * http://archiver.rootsweb.ancestry.com/th/read/OLD-FREDERICK-CO-VA/2005-01/1105200175
2 CONT * http://familytreemaker.genealogy.com/users/g/o/r/Barbara-J-Gordon/GENE2-0004.html The Venturers - the Hampton, Harrison, Earle Families of Va, S.C. & Tex.
2 CONT
2 CONT See http://oatesfamily.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/EARLE-NARRATIVE.pdf
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Major of George William Fairfax Colonial Regiment, Attorney & Representative of the House of Burgess,  Member of House of Burgesses.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/GSJS-LLJ
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandfather Samuel Earle 1st was born in England in 1640 and died in 1696 in Virginia. Grandfather Thomas Hayles (Samuel Earle 1's father in law) was born circa 1613 and was "of Westmoreland, Virginia"
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT From book "History and Genealogy of the Earles of Secaucus" by Isaac Newton Earle, published 1925:
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 93: "FOURTH GENERATION ...Samuel Earle 3rd, only son, so far as we know, of Samuel 2nd and his wife, Phillis, was born in Westmoreland county in 1692, resided subsequently in Prince William, Stafford and Fauquier counties, an
2 CONC d finally settled in Frederick county, Va. As he was the ancestor of all the southern Earles this now became the second home of the race.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was educated at William and Mary College, was a planter, attorney-at-law, member of the House of Burgesses from Frederick county in 1742-44, colleague of Lord Fairfax as justice of the county 1749 to 1752, collector of tobacco 
2 CONC 1748, High Sheriff, church warden of the parish in 1751, and Major of George William Fairfax's colonial regiment.
2 CONT
2 CONT His first wife was Anna Sorrel or Sorel, or Sorrell, daughter of Thomas and Elizabeth Sorrel of Westmoreland county. They were married in 1746....
2 CONT
2 CONT There is much conflict as to the order of births in this family, and which children belonged to the first and second marriage... we have reached the conclusion... in assigning the first five children to the first marriage: Samuel 
2 CONC 4th, Judge Baylis, b. 1734; Col. John, b. 1737; Rachel and Hannah.
2 CONT
2 CONT ...Samuel 3rd married as his second wife Elizabeth Holbrook, daughter of Randolph (Randall) and Jeannett Holbrook of Prince William county. The date of the marriage was after 1748 and after the removal to Frederick county... the p
2 CONC robable family of Samuel 3rd and Elizabeth Holbrook, Ezais, the second Samuel, Col. Elias, Isaiah (Zion or Ezias), Elizabeth, Theodosia, Mary and Lettie. This makes 13 [children] in all.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT This is by Lee Hanson
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Earle III. Samuel Earle3(1692 X 1771), like his father, was a planter, but he was also a pioneer, attorney at law, high sheriff, justice, burgess, church warden, investor, soldier, and a major in the colonial regiment. He w
2 CONC as the only son (but as mentioned before XX not the heir) of his father. Born in Westmoreland in 1692, Samuel Earle3 resided in the counties of Prince William, Stafford, Fauquier, and finally in Frederick County. He was educated a
2 CONC t the College of William and Mary and was the first member of the Virginia House of Burgesses from
2 CONT Frederick (1742 X1747). He owned extensive properties in six counties including land next to that of Augustine Washington (the father of George Washington). Samuel Earles first wife was Anna Sorrell, the orphaned daughter of Thoma
2 CONC s and his wife Elizabeth (Occany) Sorrell of Westmoreland. Samuel and Anna were married in 1726. As mentioned previously, Samus father gave the couple a lavish wedding present XX the deed to the
2 CONT Aquia Creek plantation (with its slaves and stock) plus 200 acres at the Yeocomico site. The marriage contract of Sam and Anna is mentioned in the deed: Whereas there is a treaty of marriage . . . between Samuel Earle, my son . . 
2 CONC . and Anna, daughter of Thomas and Elizabeth Sorrell . . .T *
2 CONT
2 CONT The Earles first child, Samuel, was born in Stafford County in 1727, followed by baby Anna (b. ca. 1728). There is a gap in years between Anna and the next child, Baylis (b. 1734), which probably indicates the birth of another chi
2 CONC ld who died young. Baylis was followed by John, Rachel, Hannah and Lettice.
2 CONT Baylis might seem an unusual name for a boy. Like many families of that time, sons frequently were given the surnames from the female side of the family. Baylis Earle (Samuel and Annaus son) was named for Colonel John Baylis of We
2 CONC stmoreland County who had married Samuels sister, Hannah Earle. So Baylis Earle was named for his aunts husband. Since the Baylis family was closely associated with the Earles, records show that the Oates, Earles, and many other r
2 CONC elated families continued to use some form of the name (Baylis, Bayles, Bailes, Bayless) as a given name from that time on.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 5 March 1747, Samuel Earle3 bought 340 acres at the mouth of Muddy Run (in what is now Warren County, Virginia) and made it his permanent home. He built a manor house and a tall, one-story wooden chapel on a promontory overlook
2 CONC ing the sparking Shenandoah River.
2 CONT Below the cliff, at a crossing which became known as Earle's Ford, he constructed a still and gristmill.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel3 had a total of fourteen children, seven by each wife. All of his sons served as soldiers in the American Revolution except Ezias, the youngest. Three sons (Baylis, John, and Elias) moved to South Carolina.
2 CONT
2 CONT History of Spartanburg County: Embracing an Account of Many Important Events ...
2 CONT By John Belton O'Neall Landrum 1900 pg 265-269
2 CONT Samuel Earle, born in Westmoreland county 1692, educated at William and Mary College, was a planter, attorney-at-law, member of the house of burgesses from Frederick 1746, justice, colleague of Lord Fairfax 1749 to 52, collector o
2 CONC f tobacco 1748, high sheriff, church warden 17 51, and major of George William Fairfaxs colonial regiment. He married, as already stated, Anna Sorrel, Richmond county, 1726, she being his first wife, and had issue : Samuel, Baylis
2 CONC , john, Rachel, and Hannah. He married a second time to Elizabeth (daughter of Randolph and Jeannette) Holbrook, of Prince William, and had issue: Esaias, Samuel, Zioh, and Lettie. He died near the present Front Royal, Va.,at a pl
2 CONC ace called Earles Ferry, on the Shenandoah River.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://oatesfamily.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/EARLE-NARRATIVE.pdf
2 CONT Samuel Earle III. Samuel Earle 3 (1692 – 1771), like his father, was a planter, but he
2 CONT was also a pioneer, attorney at law, high sheriff, justice, burgess, church warden, investor,
2 CONT soldier, and a major in the colonial regiment. He was the only son (but as mentioned before ––
2 CONT not the heir) of his father. Born in Westmoreland in 1692, Samuel Earle3 resided in the counties
2 CONT of Prince William, Stafford, Fauquier, and finally in Frederick County. He was educated at the
2 CONT College of William and Mary and was the first member of the Virginia House of Burgesses from
2 CONT Frederick (1742 –1747). He owned extensive properties in six counties including land next to
2 CONT that of Augustine Washington (the father of George Washington).
2 CONT Samuel Earle 3 ‘s first wife was Anna Sorrell, the orphaned daughter of Thomas and his
2 CONT wife Elizabeth (Occany) Sorrell of Westmoreland. Samuel and Anna were married in 1726. As
2 CONT mentioned previously, Sam’s father gave the couple a lavish wedding present –– the deed to the
2 CONT Aquia Creek plantation (with its slaves and stock) plus 200 acres at the Yeocomico site. The
2 CONT marriage contract of Sam and Anna is mentioned in the deed: “Whereas there is a treaty of
2 CONT marriage . . . between Samuel Earle, my son . . . and Anna, daughter of Thomas and Elizabeth
2 CONT Sorrell . . .” *
2 CONT Anna Sorrell was descended from a family of Huguenots who went to England from
2 CONT France at the end of the 16th century. Her father, Thomas Sorrell, was a captain in the James
2 CONT City militia and was killed during Bacon’s Rebellion in 1676.
2 CONT The Earles’ first child, Samuel, was born in Stafford County in 1727, followed by baby
2 CONT Anna (b. ca. 1728). There is a gap in years between Anna and the next child, Baylis (b. 1734),
2 CONT which probably indicates the birth of another child who died young. Baylis was followed by
2 CONT John, Rachel, Hannah and Lettice.
2 CONT “Baylis” might seem an unusual name for a boy. Like many families of that time, sons
2 CONT frequently were given the surnames from the female side of the family. Baylis Earle (Samuel and
2 CONT Anna’s son) was named for Colonel John Baylis of Westmoreland County who had married
2 CONT Samuel’s sister, Hannah Earle. So Baylis Earle was named for his aunt’s husband. Since the
2 CONT Baylis family was closely associated with the Earles, records show that the Oates, Earles, and
2 CONT many other related families continued to use some form of the name (Baylis, Bayles, Bailes,
2 CONT Bayless) as a given name from that time on.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Westmoreland County, Va. Deed Book 8, 5 May 1726, p. 173.
2 CONT - 6 -
2 CONT On 5 March 1747, Samuel Earle 3 bought 340 acres at the mouth of Muddy Run (in what
2 CONT is now Warren County, Virginia) and made it his permanent home. He built a manor house and a
2 CONT tall, one-story wooden chapel on a promontory overlooking the sparking Shenandoah River.
2 CONT Below the cliff, at a crossing which became known as “Earle’s Ford,” he constructed a still and
2 CONT gristmill. It was probably here in the family chapel that the Earle’s eldest daughter, Anna, 19,
2 CONT married Charles Buck in 1747.
2 CONT Anna Sorrel Earle died the following year, 30 December 1748, and was buried in her
2 CONT garden, about 200 yards from the mouth of Muddy Run. Her monument bears the inscription:
2 CONT
2 CONT Here lies the body of Anna
2 CONT the wife of Samuel Earle of this County
2 CONT who departed this life the
2 CONT 30 day of December A Domini 1748
2 CONT in the 41 year of Her Age.
2 CONT She was a Religious, Devout & Godly Woman,
2 CONT a loving & Virtuous Wife,
2 CONT an Indulgent Mother, a Liberal Mistress,
2 CONT a kind Neighbor, a true Pattern of Goodness
2 CONT an example to all Her Followers
2 CONT and by all much lamented.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna’s death left Samuel to cope with a household of children. Hannah Baylis, his sister
2 CONT (now a childless widow), moved to Shenandoah to keep house for her brother. Meanwhile,
2 CONT Samuel 4 (the eldest son of Anna and Samuel 3) then 21 years old, was in Westmoreland County,
2 CONT managing the Yeocomico plantation left him by his grandfather. Sometime in this period, young
2 CONT Samuel 4 died; his estate was left to an infant son (also named, what else, “Samuel”), and the
2 CONT baby’s grandfather was named as administrator. Distances were great, and it was difficult to
2 CONT manage Yeocomico plantation from Muddy Run (see map on page 8). Although Yeocomico was
2 CONT entailed to remain in the Earle family, the court eventually allowed the restrictions in Samuel 2 ‘s
2 CONT will to be set aside. The old Earle’s Creek plantation was sold and the proceeds invested for the
2 CONT infant.
2 CONT While staying at his Aquia plantation in Stafford County, Samuel 3 (then 62) took as his
2 CONT second wife Elizabeth Holdbrooke (a girl “younger than several of his children”). The couple
2 CONT remained in Stafford for the next five years, probably due to the unsettled conditions on the
2 CONT frontier and what was called “the Indian menace.”
2 CONT It was there at the Aquia plantation that three children were born: Theodocia, Elizabeth,
2 CONT and Mary Ann. The frontier finally calmed down in 1757, and Samuel and his wife Elizabeth
2 CONT sold the Aquia Creek plantation and moved to the Town Run place in Fauquier County. Their
2 CONT fourth child was born there in 1760, and Earle, overjoyed to have another male after the death of
2 CONT his eldest son and namesake, used the name again and christened him Samuel Earle IV.
2 CONT The Hungry Run plantation in nearby Loudoun County was thriving. Neighbors hauled
2 CONT their wheat to Earle’s mill in great Conestoga wagons, a style that had been developed during the
2 CONT French and Indian War. After converting the wheat to flour, the wagons rolled on to meet ships
2 CONT - 8 -
2 CONT at Tidewater ports. Perhaps Old Samuel grew weary of traveling to his various plantations; in
2 CONT 1761 he and wife Elizabeth “of Fauquier” sold the Hungry Run plantation. Sometime in 1762,
2 CONT the Earles returned to their Shenandoah Valley home on Muddy Run.
2 CONT Samuel and Elizabeth (Holdbrook) Earle had three more children: Elias, Esaias, and
2 CONT Ezias. They remained in residence at Muddy run where Earle continued to operate his mill, his
2 CONT ferry, and several plantations until his death in 1771 at age 79. His will divided his property
2 CONT among the sons of his second wife (since he had already settled on Baylis and John).
2 CONT . . . Samuel 3 had a total of fourteen children, seven by each wife. All of his sons served as
2 CONT soldiers in the American Revolution except Ezias, the youngest. Three sons (Baylis, John, and
2 CONT Elias) moved to South Carolina. However, it’s the family with the first wife that holds the most
2 CONT interest for us since it contains Baylis Earle:
2 CONT To Samuel 3 and Anna:
2 CONT Samuel4 b. 1727-28 (died before 1752)
2 CONT (daughter) 1728-29 (died before 1761)
2 CONT Baylis b. 8 August 1734 (Stafford Co., VA)
2 CONT m. Mary Prince 16 April 1757 (she was “nearly” 13 years old)
2 CONT d. 6 Jan 1825 at Earlesville in Carolina
2 CONT John 1737 - ca 1800-1802
2 CONT Rachel
2 CONT Hannah
2 CONT Lettice
