1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Capt. Robert Bruce Polk
2 CONT * Birth:	1625 - Donegal, Donegal, Ireland
2 CONT * Death:	Aug 8 1703 - Annapolis, Somerset, Maryland, USA
2 CONT * Parents:	Robert Bruce Polk, Jean Crawford ('''not proven''')
2 CONT * Wife:	Magdalen Tasker
2 CONT
2 CONT links
2 CONT * http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~mysouthernfamily/myff/d0060/g0000031.html
2 CONT *[https://books.google.com/books?id=JdoDAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA565&dq=magdalen+tasker+and+Col.+Porter&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjsurmP9a7dAhVN0FMKHdk2B40Q6AEIKzAA#v=onepage&q=magdalen%20tasker%20and%20Col.%20Porter&f=false Page 565] of Makers 
2 CONC of America: Biographies of Leading Men of Thought and ..., Volume 1 “Tasker Polk”
2 CONT *https://www.genealogy.com/ftm/n/o/l/Melanie-C-Nolan-2/GENE1-0001.html
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Donegal, Ireland, he served as a captain under Col. Porter in Cromwell's army. He then married Col. Porter's widow, Magdalene (Tasker) Porter. They came to America after 1672 and settled on the eastern shores of Maryland. 
2 CONC He, his sons, and his grandsons received grants of land from the Lord Baltimore. In 1689, he was living in Somerset County, Maryland, where he died in 1703.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the 12th generation of the direct Polk/Pollock line and was the second son of Sir Robert and the 6th in direct line to be named Robert. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas, Robert's elder brother, inherited the family estate in Ireland, and the opportunity to secure land of his own in the colonies must have been factored into the decision to leave Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT =<nowiki>--------------------------</nowiki>=
2 CONT
2 CONT "The Pollag" Newsletter of Clan Pollock, 1 July 2000, "The Arrival of Robert and Magdalen Polke/Pollock in America," John F. Polk, Jr., Clan Pollock International Historian, pp. 3-4: 
2 CONT
2 CONT When did Robert and Magdalen Polke/Pollock, the progenitors of the Polk family in America first come to these shores? There will probablly never be a definitive answer to this question, but a sidelight from the history of Maryland
2 CONC  and Donegal, Ireland can tell us how it came about. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1680 Colonel William Stevens, one of the founders and original Commissioners of Somerset County, Maryland, sent a letter to the Presbytery of Donegal in Ulster, asking that a "godly minister" be sent to look after the needs of 
2 CONC the people of Somerset. The actual text of the letter has not survived, but it is referred to in the minutes of the Presbytery. The motives of Stevens can be seen as both enlightened and self-serving. He had acquired very extensiv
2 CONC e land rights in the form of warrants and patented land, probably more than any one else in the county at that time. He clearly needed settlers to increase the value of these holdings and realize a profit. At the same time one has
2 CONC  to admire the open minded liberality of Colonel Stevens, a member of the established church and leader of the local government, in turning to a non-conformist group with which he had no obvious ties, to provide spiritual leadersh
2 CONC ip for the people of his domain. The followers of the Covenant were not known as strong supporters of establishment power, in fact their reputation was quite the opposite. The record of the Presbytery does not indicate that he act
2 CONC ually asked for settlers as well as ministers, but simply that "Col. Stevens from Maryland beside Virginia his desire of a godly minister is represented to us. The meeting will consider it seriously and do what they can in it", da
2 CONC ted 29 December 1680. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Whatever its motivation, Colonel Stevens' letter arrived at a moment of great travail and no doubt had a profound impact on the Presbyterians of Dongegal as a whole. Following the restoration of Charles II in 1660 a number of repr
2 CONC essive measures were taken against Presbyterians in Ulster which made their situation at least as difficult as that of the Catholics, a fact sometimes lost in view of the present day Protestant ascendancy in Northern Ireland. They
2 CONC  had struggled thru [sic] the Ulster Plantation, the Catholic uprising of 1641, the Cromwellian devastation and plantation of Ireland, the restoration of Charles II, and faced the prospect of a Catholic restoration under James II.
2 CONC  In 1670 there had been an aborted plot called Blood's Rebellion in which a number of Presbyterians were implicated. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Most noteworthy for our story was Reverend William Trail, minister from Ballendrait near Lifford, the home of the Tasker family - possibly the man who united Robert and Magdalen Polke/Pollock in marriage and certainly the minister
2 CONC  for their growing family. Rev. Trail was accused on purely circumstantial evidence of complicity in the plot and sent to Dublin for lengthy interrogations on his religious beliefs. He was released unconvicted but with strengthene
2 CONC d faith and returned to Lifford, only to be held in prison during 1682. The affair was highly resented by the Presbyterians and gave them every reason to see their future prospects in Ulster as very bleak. The embattled, fortress 
2 CONC psyche which these people had developed since first settling in Ulster was tightened another notch. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus it is easy to see the letter found a very attentive audience in Donegal. The response was predictably vigorous and its repercussions have echoed through the course of American history. The Presbytery sent not just one but fou
2 CONC r able and dedicated ministers with a clear vision to establish the Presbyterian faith in the New World. 
2 CONT
2 CONT First and foremost among these was the young Rev. Francis Makemie, now recognized by the Presbyterian Church as their founder and patriarch in America. He was newly ordained in 1682 and specifically selected for the task of going 
2 CONC to America and planting the seeds of his faith, which he did to great effect. Makemie arrived in Somerset around the spring of 1683 and stayed for a time at the home of Colonel Stevens, Rehobeth on the Pocomoke, where the earliest
2 CONC  Presbyterian Church edifice in America was erected. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Either coming with him or following very shortly afterwards was William Traile and within the next two years the Reverends Thomas Wilson of Killybegs and Samuel Davis. 
2 CONT
2 CONT While the other three minstered to the people in Somerset, Makemie began to travel around the Chesapeake region establishing numerous frontier churches and ultimately organizing the first Presbytery of the American Church at Phila
2 CONC delphia in 1706. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When the great wave of Scotch-Irish migration from Ulster commenced a decade or so later the Presbyterian structure was in place to lead and enliven them. The subsequent Presbyterian impact on the American frontier as it pushed do
2 CONC wn the great wagon road through Pennsylvania into [the] valley of Virginia and the Carolinas, and afterwards west beyond the Appalachians can truly trace its roots back to this precursor arrival in Somerset. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Along with these ministers it is certain that some of the Presbyterian families of Donegal also elected to cast their lot with the New World, hopefully leaving behind the turmoil of the old. '''Among these would appear to be the f
2 CONC amilies of Knox, McKnitt, Wallace, Alexander, White, Galbraith, Caldwell, Gray - and Polk'''. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Certainly Robert and Magdalen came within a few years of Makemie and Traile. And there were more. An interesting passage appears in a letter of Edward Randolph, a Virginia official, writing in 27 June 1692: "I hear he has continue
2 CONC d Major King to bee ye Navall Officer in Somerset County on ye eastern shore, a place pestred with Scotch & Irish. About 200 families have within ye 2 years arrived from Ireland & settled in your County besides some hundred of fam
2 CONC ily's there before." 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Robert Polke's first appearance in Maryland colonial records was with the patenting of the tracts Polks Lott and Polks Folly in March 1687/8'''. It is likely that the family arrived some time before then, but exactly when will 
2 CONC probably never be known. The Somerset Judicial records for 1683-87 are unfortunately lost, and the land records are complete but offer no additional citations, so there appears to be little chance of additional evidence being foun
2 CONC d that period. Probably the best estimate for the date of arrival of Robert and Magdalen Polke/Pollock and their family in America would be about a year before they patented land - my guess would be 1686. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (Note - In his book "Polk Family and Kinsmen" William H. Polk mentions a cattle earmark registered to a John Pelke in 1680 which can be found in Somerset court records, Liber IKL. However this John Pelke is not one of Robert and M
2 CONC agdalen's children, but another individual, possibly a close relative, who was an earlier arrival. A discussion about him appeared in January 1999 issue of The Pollag under the title "What's in a Name - Variations in Spelling"). 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thecityobserver.com/f0059.html 
2 CONT
2 CONT =<nowiki>----------------------------------------</nowiki>=
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Will===
2 CONT
2 CONT The following is '''the will of Robert Polk''', with spelling and capitalization exactly as shown in the original:
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Name of God, Amen:
2 CONT
2 CONT This '''sixth day of May in the year of our lord, 1699''', I''' Robert Polke of Somerset County''' in the Province of Maryland being of good health and Perfect memory at this present thank be to Almighty God for the same yet knowi
2 CONC ng the uncertainty of this present life and being desirous to settle my affairs doe make this my last will and Testament in manner and form following:
2 CONT
2 CONT First and principally I commend my soule to God who gave it to me, assuredly believing that I shall receive full and free pardon of all my sins and be saved by the pretious Death and Merritts of my Blessed Savior and Redeemer Jesu
2 CONC s Christ and my Body to the Earth from whence it was taken to be buried after a decent and Christian manner at the discretion of my Executor hereafter named and as touching such worldly estate as God in his mercy hath bestowed upo
2 CONC n me. It is my will that it be disposed of as hereafter is expressed.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2ndly I leave to '''my son Robert Polke''' a parcel of land called Lone Ridge being part of a tract of land called Forlorne Hope, formerly belonging to Augustine Standforth but now conveyed to me the said land called Lone Ridge be
2 CONC ginning at a marked pine standing in a slash next to my said Son's House and from thence running north east the number of poles specified in the pattent soe leaving to my son Robert what land belongs to the said pattent on the nor
2 CONC th east side of the said Slash to him the Said Robert Polke and his heirs forever.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3rdly I leave to my said '''son David Polke''' the remainder of the above tract of land called Forlorne Hope as also one hundred acres of land called Polks Folly bounded as per Pattent will appear both said tracts of land to him t
2 CONC he said David and his heirs forever.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4thly I leave to my '''Beloved Wife Magdalen Polke''' my now dwelling house and plantation during her natural life as also a third of what goods and moveables I am possessed with or shall hereafter to the day of my death the said 
2 CONC Goods and moveables to be at her dispossing at her decease. Another third of my Goods and moveables I leave to '''my daughter Martha''' be it little or much here and her heirs forever and as for the other third. It is my will it b
2 CONC e equally divided between '''my sons David and Joseph''', and if it should please God to Remove me before I purchase a seat of Land after my son Joseph, this my will: that my son David give unto my son Joseph four thousand pounds 
2 CONC of Tobacco in the leu of the above said tracts of Land left to my son David and as for what Cattle I have given to my son Joseph they being in his proper Mark it's my will that he enjoy and possess the same he and his heirs foreve
2 CONC r for this boy Christopher must live with Magdalen Polke during her life time then.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5thly I leave to '''my son James''' an Orphan Boy called Christopher Little to him the said James and his heirs during the time of his Indre.
2 CONT
2 CONT I leave unto '''my son Ephraim''' the choice of what stear I have or may have at the day of my death. I leave to '''my sons John and William Polke''' to each of them twelve pence. I constitute and appoint my son David Polke and my
2 CONC  wife Magdalen Polke to be Executors of this my last will.
2 CONT
2 CONT Codicil--I constitute my sons Ephraim and James Polke to be Executors of this my last will and testament disannuling and making voide all former Will or Wills by me made either by word or written.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Witness whereof I have hereunto set my hand and seal the day and year above written this being '''altered the eighth day of August, 1703'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Polke
2 CONT
2 CONT X mark
2 CONT
2 CONT Seal Richard Knight, Mary 0. English, Richard Whittley,
2 CONT
2 CONT And I desire that Martha Pollock may have liberty let her cattle run on the plantation until she gets plantation and as for Sarah Powers she must have a heifer at her freedom day.
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed, Sealed and delivered in the presence of us
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Polk
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Whittley
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Knight
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary 0. English.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the back of said will was thus written: "Vizt:--Memorandum this''' 5th day June the within will was proved''' to the Act and Deed of the within named Robert Polke by the oaths of Richard Whittley, Richard Knight and Mary Englis
2 CONC h before.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Dent. Depty Com'y.
2 CONT
2 CONT Test. 
2 CONT
2 CONT R. McKendree Davis, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dept. Register Orphans Court Anne Arundel County State of Maryland 
2 CONT
2 CONT Annapolis Wills T. B. 1701-3, folios 416-418.  
