1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT ''Last updated 17 March 2025''
2 CONT
2 CONT Elder '''John Crandall''', a Baptist minister, of Newport and Westerly, came from Gloucestershire in England to New England by 1643.  He was arrested at Lynn, July 21,1651 and suffered imprisonment at Boston, was fined and publicl
2 CONC y whipped for his attachment to the Baptist cause. He fled to Rhode Island in 1637. He was early associated with the Baptists at Newport and was made a freeman in 1655. He became the first elder and preacher in Westerly, where his
2 CONC  name is the first in the list of "free inhabitants," 1699.  He died in 1676 at Newport.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elder Crandall was twice married. The name of his first wife is not known (but she was not Mary Opp).  His second wife Hannah Gaylord, daughter of William Gaylord and Anna Porter, died in 1678, as the wife of John Cottrell of West
2 CONC erly.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Extracted from https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Crandall-3 (Last modified 26 Dec 2024)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origin
2 CONT
2 CONT John Crandall  was probably born in 1618 at the parish of Westerleigh, Gloucestershire, England and was baptized there on 15 February 1617/8.[1] There is good circumstantial evidence that John was the son of James Crandall and his
2 CONC  first wife, Eleanor (Unknown) Crandall.[1] John had a half-sister named Anne, daughter of James and his second wife, Joan, who was baptized 30 December 1621.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1603, the Crundall family, which included generations of priests, had been displaced from their home, the Winterbourne church, by a powerful parishioner, Robert Bradston.[1] As a result, not all the children's births were recor
2 CONC ded in the usual manner and some of the family's relationships had to be deduced indirectly.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT There is some Welsh connection in the family's past, although it seems John was born in England. Family tradition, as evidenced by the book by A.P. Crandall, contends that the family had Welsh origins.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marriages and Children
2 CONT
2 CONT John married, as his first wife, to (Unknown) Crandall, who died 2 August 1670 (NOTE: his first wife is NOT Mary Opp, as stated by John Cortland Crandall).[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of John and his first wife, born before 1670:[1]
2 CONT # John, d. betw. 25 January and 14 August 1704, mar. 18 June 1672 to Elizabeth Gorton;
2 CONT # Jane, mar. bef. 1693 Job Babcock;
2 CONT # James;
2 CONT # Peter, d. bef. 29 July 1734; mar. Mary Unknown;
2 CONT # Sarah, mar. 1) bef. 1677, as his second wife, Josiah Witter, mar. 2) Peter Button;
2 CONT # Joseph, b. c 1661, d. 12 September 1737, mar. 1) Deborah Burdick, mar. 2) by 1717 Elizabeth (Witter) Langworthy;
2 CONT # Samuel, b. 1663, d. 19 May 1736, mar. 1685 Sarah (Colby) Crandall (1665-1758).
2 CONT # John married, as his second wife, to Hannah Gaylord, daughter of William and Anna (Porter) Gaylord of Windsor, Connecticut.[1] She married, as her second husband, to John Cottrell of Westerly.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of John and Hannah Gaylord:[1]
2 CONT # Jeremiah, b. aft. 1670, d. August 1718, mar. Priscilla Warner; she mar. 2) Abraham Lockwood of Providence;
2 CONT # Eber, b. 1675, d. betw. 22 August and 15 September 1727, mar. 1) Unknown, mar. 2) Patience Lampear, mar. 3) Mary Cottrell.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Death and Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Possibly injured in Great Swamp Fight of 19 Dec 1675 at Westerly.
2 CONT
2 CONT John died in 1676 at Newport, Rhode Island,[1] as evidenced in a letter from Samuel Hubbard dated Nov 1676.[10] John is buried in a family cemetery located west of the Crandall homestead. He is buried with both of his wives, his s
2 CONC on John Jr. and his wife,[4] in Rhode Island Historical Cemetery #15, where a monument to the family was erected in 1994.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT No will has been found for Elder John Crandall but, in a document dated 3 Oct 1670, he signed over all his goods, chattels, debts., etc. to his son, John Crandall Jr.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT In a document dated 13 May 1678, John Crandall's house and 200 acres of land in Westerly were turned over by John Crandall Jr. to his brothers Jeremiah and Hebe of Newport who were "in the tuition of their mother Hannah Crandall".
2 CONC [7]
2 CONT
2 CONT In The Early History of Narragansett ..., among the listing of Indian and Other Names of Places: "Quequatnck or Qneqiiathanic or Queqvatage - About twenty miles up Pawcatuc river, near where Crandal's mill stood on said river in 1
2 CONC 681." This property was named in a 1699 lawsuit.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Research Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Disputed origins and other disputed claims: There is a false lineage for John Crandall, claiming that he was the son of another John Crandall and Elizabeth Drake, an imaginary woman with an imaginary medieval pedigree.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two early works on John Crandal and his descendants contain a number of errors and include undocumented English lineages.[7] The 1888 work by A.P. Crandall, Genealogy of Elder John Crandall and his Descendants, contains an introdu
2 CONC ctory section (among others) which is now proved to be unfounded, and the second is the 1949 work by John Cortland Crandall, Elder John Crandall of Rhode Island and His Descendants, which seems largely to have been based on the mi
2 CONC stakes of the first.[7] These works make many statements without citing sources, and when later researchers sought documentation, they found either no evidence or suggestion that our John Crandall was confused with a John Cranwell
2 CONC /Craniwell of Boston[7] (see Cranwell's profile in R.C. Anderson's Great Migration Begins.1995 Vol 1 (A-F) pages 492-493. AmericanAncestors (subscription) or Ancestry.com (subscription): Image
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the fictitious claims is that John arrived in Boston in 1634/5. Actually, there is no evidence of his presence in British America prior to 8 September 1643, when he was a member of the Newport, Rhode Island Grand Jury. Many
2 CONC  researchers have searched for any evidence of his presence in Boston or Salem and have found none.[7] John Cortland Crandall contends that John was in Providence by 1637 and explains the lack of records with, "Many of the early P
2 CONC rovidence records were early destroyed." According to researcher, Jerry Huggins, "This is just simply not true - the early Providence records are very complete."[7] Another unsupported claim is that John went to England in 1663 wi
2 CONC th a group of men to to obtain the Rhode Island Charter.
2 CONT
2 CONT See also: "Elder John Crandall in Colonial America... trying to locate the immigrant Crandall ancestor in the records of colonial New England" by Earl P. Crandall, Crandall Family Association Genealogist (1994-2002), March 1998. C
2 CONC randall Family Association website, archived page from 12 Feb 2009
2 CONT
2 CONT .
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Reverend '''John Crandall''', one of the founding settlers of Westerly, Rhode Island was born about 1612 in Monmouthshire, England to James & Eleanor Crandall. Some biographers put his date of birth closer to February 16, 1617, th
2 CONC e date of his christening recorded at Westerleigh, Gloucestershire, England. As such, some historians believe Westerly, Rhode Island was named for the town in England where he was christened, or possibly even born.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Crandall genealogy by John Cortland Crandall likely errs in providing an undocumented lineage for Elder John. That work stated that he was the son of John and Elizabeth (Drake) Crandall. Elizabeth Drake was a first cousin, onc
2 CONC e removed, of Sir Francis Drake. However, no collaborating documentation has been located in England of a couple by the name of John and Elizabeth Drake Crandall. Several members of the Crandall Family Association have done resear
2 CONC ch using parish records from Westerleigh which indicates that Elder John was the son of James and Eleanor (--) Crandall. This work cannot be underestimated in its importance in establishing Elder John's parentage. It is hoped that
2 CONC  further research on the Crandall heritage will help to further elucidate the story of the Crandalls in England and America.
2 CONT
2 CONT While the exact date of Crandall's arrival is not known, it is believed to be 1637 when he arrived in Providence, Rhode Island, then a new settlement and a refuge for dissident Puritans from the Massachusetts Bay Colony.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Providence he came to Newport, Rhode Island, as early as 1651 (The first actual documentation for Elder John Crandall in American is in 1643 when he appears as a grand jury member in Newport) and became a prominent member of 
2 CONC the Baptist church there, subsequently the first elder of the denomination at Westerly, Rhode Island. With John Clarke and Obadiah Holmes, he went to Lynn, Massachusetts, to hold services for the Baptists, was arrested there July 
2 CONC 21, 1651, and sent to prison in Boston. Ten days later he was convicted of breaking the law by holding services and fined five pounds, in default of which he was to be publicly whipped. Upon his promise to appear at the next term 
2 CONC of court he was released.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1655, he was a freeman of Rhode Island; in 1658-59, 1662-63, he was a commissioner.
2 CONT
2 CONT With eight others he signed a letter to the court of commissioners of Rhode Island, dated August 27, 1661, in relation to a tract of land at Westerly, where they and others desired to settle.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a deputy to the general assembly in 1687, and in the fall of that year was living at Westerly. He and Joseph Torrey were appointed commissioners to treat with Connecticut as to jurisdiction over disputed territory, May 14, 
2 CONC 1669, and he was supplied with thirty-five shillings by the colony of Rhode Island to pay his expenses to Connecticut.
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 18, 1669, he received a letter from the governor and assistants of Connecticut, complaining that he and others had appropriated a large tract of land belonging to Stonington, Connecticut. He and Tobias Saunders answere
2 CONC d the complaint for the Westerly people. He was conservator of the peace at Westerly in 1670, and deputy to the general assembly again in 1670-71.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was arrested by the Connecticut authorities, May 2, 1671, and was advised by the Rhode Island government to decline to give bond. The Rhode Island colony promised to pay his expenses and defend him.
2 CONT
2 CONT His first wife, (thought by some to be Mary Opp (born 1633) died August 1, 1670), and he married (second) Hannah Gaylord (born 1647), probably daughter of William Gaylord and Ann (Porter), of Windsor, Connecticut. She died in 1678
2 CONC . He died at Newport, where he had moved because of King Philip's War, in 1676. Crandall is the ancestor of a number of prominent Americans, including Lucille Ball, Katharine Hepburn, Julia Child, Ruth Benedict, Garrison Keillor a
2 CONC nd Frances Folsom Cleveland, wife of the President.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note that there is no record of the name of Elder John's wife in any Rhode Island records nor has a record of the marriage ever been found. Based on the approximate dates of birth of their children (with the eldest, John, born ca.
2 CONC  1649 based on the date he appears as a freeman in Westerly) it would appear likely that Elder John married his first wife in the latter part of the 1640s. It also would seem to indicate that he probably married her in America. Si
2 CONC nce she is referred to as a "Sabbath keeper" in communications from Samuel Hubbard, it is likely that she was of the Seventh Day Baptist faith and perhaps she was a daughter of one of the SDB families in Rhode Island at the time.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT [Broderbund WFT Vol. 4, Ed. 1, Tree #0813, Date of Import: Nov 14, 1999]
2 CONT
2 CONT John Crandal "The Elder"
2 CONT
2 CONT Added by Suzzii on 24 Dec 2007
2 CONT
2 CONT John Crandall left the Church of England and founded the Baptist Church. [Family (8-21-03).FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT There is no documented information about Elder John before his appearance in Newport, Rhode Island in the early 1640's. There are secondary sources that suggest that he had spent some time in Salem, MA. Since he went back there on
2 CONC  occasions, it seems that he may have had some ties there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Several years ago, the son of some LDS (Mormon) Missionaries serving in Gloucestershire, England, contacted me. His parents were in a parish called Westerleigh doing some microfilming of the parish records. This microfilm is now a
2 CONC vailable at the Family History Library in Salt Lake City and can be ordered for loan at any local Family History Center.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the parish records of Westerleigh (also spelled "Westerley" in some places), can be found a baptism for John, sonne of James Crandell baptized 15 February 1617!
2 CONT
2 CONT There is also another child of James Crandell baptized in that parish, a daughter, Anne, baptized in 1621, obviously a daughter of this James and sister of John.
2 CONT
2 CONT This is of particular note because:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1)no other Crandalls of that time frame in England appear frequently.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) the parish name, Westerleigh is most tantalizing, as Elder John was one of the original settlers of Westerly, Rhode Island in 1661.
2 CONT
2 CONT and 3) the parish of Westerleigh is in Gloucestershire, not too far from Monmouthshire, thence not too far from the Welsh border, which fits some of the "traditions" about Elder John coming from Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl P. Crandall, Website editor 1995 - 2002
2 CONT
2 CONT The first photograph is that of The Church of St. James The Great in Westerleigh, Gloucestershire where John1 Crandall was baptized. The church dates from the late 900's, but it was greatly damaged by fire in 1868 and there have b
2 CONC een several structural restorations since that time.
2 CONT
2 CONT The second photograph is the Norman baptismal font.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elder John CRANDALL was employed 1634, in Newport Co, RI. John immigrated, 1634. Destination: Boston, Middlesex Co, MA. The biography of Elder John Crandall that follows was extracted from the book by John Cortland Crandall, Elder
2 CONC  John Crandall of Rhode Island and His Descendants, New Woodstock, NY: Published by the author, 1949.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Crandall, the first Baptist Elder in America, was also a Deputy Commissioner and statesman of Newport and Westerly, Rhode Island, and the head of the Crandall family in America. He was born in Monmouthshire, England, on the l
2 CONC ine between England and Wales in 1612. His mother may have been Scottish. He came to Boston in 1634 and later became a close associate of Roger Williams, the founder of Rhode Island. He was the first Baptist Elder at Westerly, and
2 CONC  "Elder Crandall was well calculated both by talent and sufferings to lead his people in their devotions. He took an active part in the border difficulties between Massachusetts and Connecticut and subsequently between Connecticut
2 CONC  and Rhode Island, concerning the lines between the states." He was first married about 1649, and her name is not known for certain but was probably Mary Opp. He married second, Hannah Gaylord. Elder John Crandall died before Nove
2 CONC mber 29, 1676, at Newport, Rhode Island where he was sojourning on account of the King Philip's War. As early as 1635 Rev. John Crandall was living in Salem where, as elsewhere in the Massachusetts Bay Colony, there was at this ti
2 CONC me much opposition to all dissenters from the authorized tenets of the Puritans. He was the minister of the Salem church, but he adopted the opinions of the Baptists, which were very obnoxious to Congregationalists, and in the Aut
2 CONC umn of 1635, he was dismissed as pastor. As did so many others of the early Baptists of New England he determined to settle in the Narragansett country. The Indians proved friendly, and he obtained from them a grant of land. He ha
2 CONC s been called one of the founders of Providence. He was living there as early as 1637. He was one of the original purchasers from Chief Sosoa of Narragansett of the Misquamicutt tribe, of the land of comprising Westerly, from whic
2 CONC h Hopkinton was later formed. On July 21, 1651, John Crandall, with John Clark and Obadiah Holmes [note: Holmes was also a direct ancestor of William A. Barnett], "being the representatives of the Church of Newport, upon the reque
2 CONC st of William Witter, of Lynn [Massachusetts] arrived there ... (to visit him). The next day they spent in religious services at his house and were then apprehended by two constables at the insistence of the Massachusetts authorit
2 CONC ies while Mr. Clark was preaching., and the next morning they were sent to prison in Boston. [Baptists were forbidden in the Massachusetts Bay Colony, by law]. For the dire offense of holding this little meeting, and on other friv
2 CONC olous pretexts Obadiah Holmes was fined, imprisoned and whipped. [He was tied to the back of a high wagon and whipped with 30 lashes as the wagon moved through Boston. He later said that he could not rest except on his knees and e
2 CONC lbows. The other two were fined and released.] Elder John Crandall died in Newport on November 29, 1676, having moved there on account of the Indian War [King Philip's War]. On this same date, Samuel Hubbard wrote to Mr. Edward St
2 CONC ennett in London, and after speaking of the devastation caused by King Philip's War [Providence was totally destroyed by the Indians] he recounts the recent deaths in the First Baptist Church. He says: "of the old church, first Mr
2 CONC . Joseph Torrey, then my dear brother John Crandall, then Mr. John Clark, then William Weeden, a deacon, then John Salmon; a sad stroke in very deed, young men and maids to this day I never knew or heard the like in New England."
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT "Howard Crandall affirmed that more than five thousand descendants of Elder John Crandall had become ministers. Records of precinct, state and nation disclose the names of Crandalls who have gained places of note in education, sci
2 CONC ence, invention, law, medicine, industry, business and various other fields.  However, the rank and file have been humble, middle-class folks. For the most part they have been known for their sturdiness of character, noble princip
2 CONC als, good citizenship, and consistent religious devotion.  Bruce Verne Crandall wrote a treatise in which he featured the claim that the one outstanding trait of the Crandall family was- âIndependent thinking, oft times developi
2 CONC ng into obstinacy, thoâ accompanied by open-mindedness, conscientiousness, liberality and purposefulness of.â If all that has been written about Elder John Crandall is true than most of the hundreds of thousands, emanating fro
2 CONC m this progenitor, must naturally possess those characteristics, which exemplify the best American life. To wit: Independence of thought and action, democracy of the first water and unadulterated religion."
2 CONT
2 CONT Accessed 8/17/09
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT The dueling sources on John Crandall's genealogy (John Cortland Crandall, author versus several unnamed members of the Crandall Family Association) should both be noted with the research discoveries behind each. The fact that the 
2 CONC former names his mother as Elizabeth Drake, while the later lists it as Eleanor of an unknown maiden name, is not a definitive proof that the former is wrong and the latter is correct just because a record cannot be located in the
2 CONC  United Kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT The parish record listing a James and Eleanor Crandall as his parents indicates the John Cortland Crandall genealogy is probably in error and the Crandall Family Association research is probably correct. However, since the Crandal
2 CONC l Family research source is documented (although it goes no further), it is probably more correct than the John Cortland Crandall source, which relies on similar, but not identical names, in the same geographical area and goes bac
2 CONC k further.
2 CONT
2 CONT Accordingly, it should not be right to state one source is definitely correct and the other is definitely wrong, but simply to state the facts as available and let the reader draw their own conclusion until such time, if any, that
2 CONC  better research is discovered.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT A Genealogy has John born about 1609 in Shropshire, England.  He was said to have come from Wales to Massachusetts Bay Colony in 1634/35.  He was a Baptist Minister, at Providence, RI in 1637, then later at Westerly.  source:  Rep
2 CONC resentative Men & Old Families of Rhode Island page 519.
2 CONT
2 CONT "John Crandall, Colonial pioneer, First Baptist Elder, Deputy Commissioner, and statesman of Newport & Westerly, Rhode Island, was born in Monmouthshire, England on the line between England & Wales.  His mother is supposed to have
2 CONC  been a Scotch lady.  He came to Boston within a very few years after the landing of the Pilgrims, in 1634.  He was associated with the Congregational Church at Salem, as early as 1635, and probably was a close associate of Roger 
2 CONC Williams, one of the founders of Providence." (The descendants of Elder John Crandall) 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Journal of American History begins with the following in the sketch of Elder John Crandall:  "As early as 1635 Rev. John Crandall, who is believed to have been of Welsh ancestry, was living in Salem where, as elsewhere in the 
2 CONC Massachusetts Bay Colony, there was at this time much opposition to all dissenters to the authorized tenets of the Puritans.  John Crandall was the minister of the Salem church, but he adopted the opinions of the Baptists, which w
2 CONC ere very obnoxious to the Congregationalists, and in the autumn of 1635, he was dismissed as pastor.  As did so many others of the Baptist of New England, he determined to settle in the Narragansett Country.  The Indians proved fr
2 CONC iendly, and he obtained from them a grant of land.  He has been called one of the founders of Providence, Rhode Island.  Certainly, he was living in Providence as early as 1637."  
2 CONT
2 CONT An early mention of Elder John Crandall seems to be in Newport, Rhode Island as a grand juror on 8 Sep 1643. He was also mentioned in a case against Thomas Gennings in Newport 3 Dec 1643.   "The Great Migration Begins" notes: "Joh
2 CONC n Crandall, of Providence in 1637, married Elizabeth, daughter of Samuel Gorton, and I suppose, adopted his opinions, as had his Father Elder John Crandall who in Aug 1651 was imprisoned at Boston for a Baptist freeman at Newport 
2 CONC in 1655, removed to Westerly, preaching as a 7th day Baptist and died 1676.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Elder John, along with many others, is bequeathed part of the estate of Henry Sandyes of Boston, MA in a will dated 7 Feb 1651. He is referred to as "of providence".
2 CONT
2 CONT The next mention of Elder John is of the same account, telling of his journey to Lynn, MA from Newport on Baptist church business to visit William Witter, referred to as "an aged member" in July 1651. He traveled with Rev. John Cl
2 CONC arke (pastor of the first Baptist Church in Newport) and Obadiah Holmes.   They spent the day in religious services and were apprehended by two constables at the insistence of the Massachusetts authorities, while Mr. Clarke was pr
2 CONC eaching, and the next morning they were sent to prison in Boston.  All were fined: Clarke, Â£20; Holmes, Â£30; and Elder John Â£5. In default of the fines, each was "to be well whipped." Elder John was finally allowed to go home o
2 CONC n bail, July 21, 1651.  Obediah Holmes (6th G Grandfather of Abraham Lincoln), was fined, imprisoned, & "well whipped", by 30 lashes at a whipping post placed near the old State House at Boston, on Saturday, Sept 5, 1651, because 
2 CONC he refused to pay the fine.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Elder John Crandall & John Clarke were finally allowed to go home on bail, July 21, 1651. Their release from prison was on the promise of appearing at next court."  This incident prompted Rev. John Clarke to write the pamphlet "Il
2 CONC l News from New England" published  in England in 1652
2 CONT
2 CONT Elder John Crandall was again actively identified with the Baptist Church in records, in Newport, July 21, 1651.  His name next is found, with that of Matthew West, in a Freeman's list of Newport, 1655.   (From "Representative Men
2 CONC  & Old Families of Newport, RI). 
2 CONT
2 CONT He is listed as a Commissioner for the town of Newport 2 Nov 1658.
2 CONT
2 CONT In that same year, Elder John, along with Thomas Olney, Samuel Gorton, and John Tripp, was "chosen and authorized to draw up a letter to be sent to Mr. John Clarke in England, to be presented to his Highness and Councell ..."
2 CONT
2 CONT He was appointed as a Commissioner from Newport again on 17 May 16597.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elder John was chosen in 1659, along with Arthur Fenner, Thomas Cooke, and Robert Westcott, "... to marke out the westward bounds of our colony ... "
2 CONT
2 CONT Listed 27 Aug 1661 as one of the original petitioners for the settlement of Askomicutt [Westerly, RI]. The other eight listed are: Josph Torrey, John Cranston, William Vaughan, John Coggeshall, Hugh Mosier, James Barker, Caleb Car
2 CONC r, James Rogers [spellings as in original document].
2 CONT
2 CONT Elder John was listed again as Commissioner for the town of Newport 22 May 1662.
2 CONT
2 CONT Listed as a committeeman from Newport regarding legislation on the same date.
2 CONT
2 CONT Listed again as a Commissioner from Newport 17 June 1662.
2 CONT
2 CONT Listed as a quasi-tax collector in Newport in 1662.
2 CONT
2 CONT Listed as a Commissioner from Newport 12 May 1663.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is mentioned again in Rhode Island history in relationship to his "intrusions" into Stonington, CT. The complaint is dated 30 Oct 1667, mentioning that he had "laid out a mile square of land for his son [John, Jr.] within the l
2 CONC imits of their town."
2 CONT
2 CONT On 14 Apr 1668 he participated in a religious debate in Boston, MA. He is called " John Grendall of Narragansett."
2 CONT
2 CONT A claim was made by John Crandall to about a square mile of land on the west side of the Pawcatuck River.  Connecticut & Massachusetts had much dispute as to their jurisdiction, and in 1671 the dispute grew so serious that Mr. Cra
2 CONC ndall, with others, were actually carried off by the Connecticut authorities and imprisoned at Hartford. Elder John, as a Rhode Island "officer", was seized and taken to New London, CT for trial on 2 May 167118.  RI assembly advis
2 CONC ed him not to yield to Connecticut's claims and assured him of the Colony's support and that his financial losses would be borne by the Colony.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Elder John signs over "all ... my goods, Chattels, Debts, household stuff ... and ... have putt my ... sonn in ... possession of all ... promisees by the delivery unto him of one shilling in silver currant mony ... of England ..."
2 CONC  This is a document dated 3 Oct 1670 and involved his son, John, Jr.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1675, January 23, In a letter from Ruth Burdick to her father Samuel Hubbard of Newport, she says "Brother Crandall hath the ague and fever still and have been but little amongst us this winter.  Sister Crandall is brought to bed 
2 CONC with a son and is in a hopeful way."  (Ruth Burdick's daughter Deborah, Samuel Hubbard's' granddaughter) later married Elder John Crandall's son by his first wife, Joseph.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Elder John Crandall died in Newport, having moved there on account of the Indian War.   Samuel Hubbard writes from Newport to Mr. Edward Stennitt in London, November 29, 1676, regarding the deaths in the First Baptist Church cause
2 CONC d by King Phillips war, "My dear brother John Crandall, then Mr. John Clarke, then William Weeden, a deacon, then John Salmon, a sad stroke in very deed, young men and maids, to this day I never knew or heard the like in New Engla
2 CONC nd".  He also recorded a few years later: "My dear brother John Crandall of Squamicut, is dead and his first wife, a Sabbath keeper, the first that died in that blessed faith in New England.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Elder John Crandall died before November 29, 1676, at Newport, where he was sojourning on account of the Indian War (King Philips' War)  The "Journal of American History" continues the following sketch of the life of Elder John Cr
2 CONC andall.  
2 CONT
2 CONT "His name heads the list of a list of Free Inhabitants of the Town of Westerly, and he was authorized by the colony, with Tobias Saunders, to summon juries and hold courts.  He was one of the original purchasers from Chief Sosoa o
2 CONC f Narragansett of the Misuamicutt tribe, of the land of Westerly, from which Hopkinton was later formed.  The townships of Westerly, Hopkinton, Charleston, & Richmond, as they now are, were a tract called by the Indians Misquamicu
2 CONC tt and on August 27, 1661, John Crandall was one of the nine signers of a petition of the Court of Commissioners for the Colony of Providence Plantations, in session at Portsmouth, for the purchase of that part of the tract which 
2 CONC became Westerly.  
2 CONT
2 CONT His house was near Burdens Pond, and part of it now stands, as one room in the homestead occupied by lineal descendants.  Across from the house, in the old orchard field, is the original cemetery, in which twenty-seven bodies incl
2 CONC uding Elder John, buried in the row nearest the swamp, in the end grave to the left as one stands facing the swamp.  Next to his grave is that of his son John.  Each of the graves here is represented by a field stone some 14 inche
2 CONC s in width, appearing four to eight inches above the ground set at the head while a smaller stone marks the foot of the grave.  There is no inscription on any of the stones.   He was the first Baptist Elder at Westerly and held a 
2 CONC number of public offices at various times.  In 1658, 59, 62, 63, he was a Commissioner and was a Deputy to the General Court in 1667, 70 & 1671 representing Westerly during the latter terms. "
2 CONT
2 CONT CONNECTICUT STATE ARCHIVES: COLONIAL BOUNDARIES 1662-1827,SERIES 1,VOL.1, DOC. 45
2 CONT
2 CONT "Onered Gentellmen
2 CONT
2 CONT Yours bearing date November th 18 last past wee received whear in you wear pleased to signifi unto us that ye have bin often solisited to hear seaverall complaints mad against us by the inhabitance of Stoningtown & allso of other 
2 CONC gentellmen. & that ye saw reason to grant them a time of hearing & did give us timly notis of & wee did not apear. Unto the which wee cannot omit but make you sum return. As to that of complaint mad by the inhabitance of Stoningto
2 CONC wn we do profess that wee ar not sensabell that we ar in any measure guilti which if we wear wee should have prevented thair complaint by rendering satisfaction. But wee ar very sensable of great wrongs that wee have susstained by
2 CONC  them for seaverall years.
2 CONT
2 CONT And as to a time of hearing and giving timely notis - wee being by Chartar & otherwise subjected to another colony could not assent but with the hazard of breaking our obligations - it not withstanding we ar much in clind to a pea
2 CONC ceable and a final issue of those differences by a legal prograce in Law in the jurisdiction whereunto we do belong & by whos athoriti we ar hear setteled whear we dout not but equall justis will take plac. As for your advic to ag
2 CONC ree with those our neighbours of Stoningtown & the other gentellmen we hope that your coloni & ours will in the first plac loveingly agree and then we question not but thar will be an agreement between us and our neighbors of Ston
2 CONC ingtown and the rest of the gentlemen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Seemacut [?] 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Crandall (signature)
2 CONT
2 CONT This 11 of March 1669/70
2 CONT
2 CONT Tobias Saunders (signature)
2 CONT
2 CONT in the Behalf of the Town
2 CONT
2 CONT Onnered Governor wee do desier & intreat ye to signify by this ingion that ye have received the letters then wee shall know wheather he have don faithfully.
2 CONT
2 CONT For the worshipfull John Wintrop Esqr Goverr of Connecticut to be communita to the Asistant thear.
2 CONT
2 CONT Paul A. Saunders (Matthews, NC) 3/25/98
2 CONT
2 CONT Newman's History of the Baptists page 111, states that Dr. John Clark was born in Suffolk Co, & he & his two brothers Thomas & Joseph were closely associated with Elder John Crandall in the church of the R.I. Baptists.  It is clai
2 CONC med that Elder John Crandall came over from Shropshire County with Joseph Clark.  
2 CONT
