0 @N6000000000209542105@ NOTE He was Constable 1639, Town Clerk 1640, and Deputy1 1639, 1646, 1647. His home lot was situated near the junction of what is now the Farmington river with the Connecticut, 
1 CONT between the houses of George Phelps and Joseph Loomis, and nearly opposite those of Henry Wolcott and Matthew Allyn.[:ITAL]
1 CONT The records in England give his descent, in the sixteenth generation, from William de la Grande, a Norman knight, who came in the army of the Norman duke at the conquest, A. D. 1066, and that he acquired lands at or near Kenilwort
1 CONC h, in Warwickshire. His son Ralph (or Roger) became "Grand Porteur" to Henry first, A. D. 1120 to 1140, from which he derived the name Porter. 
1 CONT (The Windsor church was formed in Plymouth, Eng., in March, 1630, by people from Devon, Dorset, Somerset and Warwickshires; the reverends John Maverick and John Warham being ordained, respectively, as pastor and teacher. In 1630 t
1 CONC hey came to New England, settling at Dorchester, Mass., on the first settlement of that town. In the summer of 1635, the arrivals having become numerous, and the settlement "somewhat crowded, and hearing ye fame of ye Quinnitukut 
1 CONC river," a company was formed to make a settlement there, and on the fifteenth day of October they took their departure, carrying with them their church organization, under the Rev. John Warham, the Rev. Mr. Maverick remaining at D
1 CONC orchester. Trumbull says: "The journey from Massachusetts was made in about fourteen days' time; the distance being more than one hundred miles, and through a trackless wilderness. They had no guide but their compass, and made the
1 CONC ir way over mountains, through swamps, thickets and rivers, which were not passable but with the greatest difficulty. They had no cover but the heavens, nor any lodgings but those which simple nature afforded them. They drove with
1 CONC  them one hundred and sixty head of cattle, and subsisted on the way, in a great measure, on the milk of their kine." 
0 @N6000000002303495404@ NOTE Please note that a lot of this genealogy is not &quot;proven&quot;.
1 CONT  I have relied heavily on &quot;The Op Dyck Genealogy&quot; by Charles
1 CONT  Wilson Opdyke for most of the information regarding that
1 CONT  family.  I update this file fairly often, so check back
1 CONT  frequently.
1 CONT !References: Vineland Historical Magazine vol-11 pp.108-113; Perleys History of Salem. !Samuel Porter died at age 21 on a voyage to Barbadoes.
1 CONT REFN: 3778 Stiles, Hx of Ancient Windsor, Vol. II, p. 621
1 CONT From Ancestral File (TM), data as of 2 January 1996.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [spelman.FTW]
1 CONT  REFN: 10771
1 CONT John Porter came to Dorchester, Massachusetts, with his wife and eightchildren on the "Susan Ellen" in 1638. He settled in Windsor,Connecticut, in 1639, and immediately became constable and a deputy tothe general court. He served 
1 CONC in the gen eral court again in 1646 and 1647.He was an ancestor of Ulysses S. Grant and Grover Cleveland.
1 CONT
1 CONT  !References: Vineland Historical Magazine vol-11 pp.108-113; Perleys History of Salem. !Samuel Porter died at age 21 on a voyage to Barbadoes.
1 CONT
1 CONT  [spelman.FTW] REFN: 10771
1 CONT  John Porter came to Dorchester, Massachusetts, with his wife and eightchildren on the "Susan Ellen" in 1638. He settled in Windsor,Connecticut, in 1639, and immediately became constable and a deputy tothe general court. He served
1 CONC  in the ge neral court again in 1646 and 1647.He was an ancestor of Ulysses S. Grant and Grover Cleveland.
1 CONT  REFN: 3778 Stiles, Hx of Ancient Windsor, Vol. II, p. 621
