0 @N6000000003615801652@ NOTE This is the famous one. Founded Carter family in VI, etc. Library books.
1 CONT From Mrs. Elliott's article in the COLONIAL GENEALOGIST IV:2, 1971:
1 CONT Captian THOMAS CARTER came to Nansemond County, Virginia, in 1649/50 about the time Charles I was beheaded. JOHN CARTER of "THE NEST", Lancaster County, Virginia, writing in 1858, said THOMAS' "brother JOHN" came to Virginia with 
1 CONC THOMAS and settled south of the river in Essex. THOMAS appears in Lancaster County, Virginia, before the year 1653, aged 22 when he paid tithes for himself and four servents.l (See William Armstrong Crozier, Ed., Virginia County R
1 CONC ecords, Vol, 5, Virginia Heraldica, Being a A Registry of Virginia Gentry Entitled to Coat Armor [Baltimore: 1965, reprint of the 1908 edition] PP. 87-88.) From Colonel JOHN CARTER, who had gone to Lancaster County from Nansemond 
1 CONC County about the time THOMAS landed in Nansemond County, Virginia, Captian THOMAS bought 800 acres in Lancaster County on the "Easternmost" branch of the Corotoman River and, 1 June 1654, acknowledged the debt in court: 12, 852 po
1 CONC unds of tobacco to be paid the following October "at ye dwelling house of the SD Mr. THo: Carter", and 130 pounds sterling on 18 Sept 1655. By 1663, although as yet unmarried, he had a home in Lancaster County named "Barford" and 
1 CONC was paying tithes on twenty Peron. He was commissioner of
1 CONT Lancaster County Court, 1663;
1 CONT
1 CONT Awarded Knight of the Garter, England's most prestigious award. ??????
1 CONT
1 CONT This award is questionable !!!!!
1 CONT
1 CONT From: A Registry of Virginia Gentry Entitled to Coat of Armor, William Armstrong
1 CONT
1 CONT Crozier, Southern Book Co., Baltimore, 1953 and Genealogical Book Co., Baltimore: and from Predigrees of Some of the Emperor Charlemagne's Descendents, Vol. II.Carter, Lancaster county
1 CONT
1 CONT Crest: On a chapeau gules turned up ermine, a heron argent, beaked, legged and ducally gorged or.The above seal is used on the original will of Thomas Carter,gent., recorded in Lancaster county,
1 CONT
1 CONT August, 1700, also on a deed in Sptsylvania county, made by Joseph Carter in 1739, a grandson of Thomas, and it is also used on the will of another grandson, Dale Carter, gent., recorded in
1 CONT
1 CONT Lancaster county in 1776. By referring to the Dale pedigree and arms it will be seen that the above is not a Carter crest, but the crest of Catherine Dale, his wife, who was a daughter of Edward
1 CONT
1 CONT Dale and his wife Diana Skipwith, sister of Sir Grey Skipwith, Bart., and daughter of Sir Henry Skipwith, Baronet, of Prestwould, Leicestershire, England.In 1653 there appeared in Lancaster county,
1 CONT
1 CONT Mr. Thomas Carter, who paid tithes upon himself and four servants. His home plantation was one he had purchased from Col. John Carter of Corotoman. It is not know, however, that there was any
1 CONT
1 CONT relationship between them. Thomas Carter was Justice in 1683 and in 1670 Deputy Clerk of the county, his father-in-law, Edward Dale, being Clerk from 1655 to 1674. Sometime prior to 1674 he
1 CONT
1 CONT married Catherine Dale, by whom he had six sons and two daughters: (1)Edward Carter, the eldest son, died in Lancaster in 1743, leaving sons, Thomas and William, and a wife, Elizabeth; (2)Thomas
1 CONT
1 CONT Carter, second son, inherited the plantation of Corotoman River. He was a Justice form 1704 to 1728 and a Captain in the Militia. He died in 1733, leaving a wife, Arabella, and eight sons and two
1 CONT
1 CONT grandsons; (3) Henry Carter, third son, was a Justice from 1728 to 1740 and a vestryman of Christ Church until his death. He married prior to 1700, Anne Harris, daughter of Gany Harris,gent. He
1 CONT
1 CONT died in 1743, leaving five sons and three daughters. (4) James Carter, fourth son, removed to Stafford county, where he died October 24,1743. He married in 1724, MKary, daughter of Hugh Brent of
1 CONT
1 CONT Lancaster county, and left six sons and one daughter. (5) Peter Carter, fifth son, inherited a plantation in St. Mary's parish, Lancaster county from his grandfather, Edward Dale, where he lived and
1 CONT
1 CONT died in 1721. He left a second wife named Margaret, and two sons and two daughters, one of each by each wife. (6) Joseph Carter, the youngest son, inherited a plantation in St. Mary's Parish from
1 CONT
1 CONT his grandfather Dale, where he lived until his death in 1759. His wife's name was Anne, and they had at least three sons and a daughter. He was a Justice in 1729, and in 1741 High Sheriff. (7)
1 CONT
1 CONT Elizabeth Carter, the elder daughter, married William George, who died in 1709, leaving several children. Thomas Carter, Sr., had also a son John, and a daughter Catherine.
