0 @N6000000003615446598@ NOTE «b»MARTHA DANDRIDGE«/b» (Frances«sup»6«/sup», Martha«sup»5«/sup», Martha«sup»4«/sup», William«sup»3«/sup», Christopher, Jr.«sup»2«/sup», Christopher«sup»1«/sup») was born June 2, 1731, at Chestnut Grove, in St. Peter's Parish, New
1 CONC  Kent Co., Virginia.  She married (1«sup»st«/sup») «b»DANIEL PARKE CUSTIS«/b», May 10, 1750, at the Dandridge home in Chestnut Grove, New Kent Co., Virginia.  The wedding was performed by the Rev. Mr. Chichley Corbin Thacker, Mini
1 CONC ster of Blisland Parish.  Daniel Parke Custis died July 8, 1757, at his plantation- the White House, in New Kent Co., Virginia.  She married (2«sup»nd«/sup») «b»Colonel GEORGE WASHINGTON«/b», January 6, 1759; the wedding took plac
1 CONC e at Martha's home- the White House, in New Kent Co., Virginia, and the ceremony was performed by Rev. David Mossom, Minister of St. Peter's Parish.  He was born February 22, 1732, in Westmoreland Co., Virginia, the eldest son of 
1 CONC Augustine and Mary (Ball) Washington, and died December 14, 1799, in Mount Vernon, Virginia.  In the spring of 1759, the newly married couple moved to Mount Vernon, after spending some time in Williamsburg, where Colonel Washingto
1 CONC n attended, as a Burgess, a meeting of the General Assembly.  George Washington took office as the First President of the United States on April 30, 1789, in New York City.  He was re-elected President in 1792.
0 @N6000000004881527644@ NOTE In 1749, beautiful 17 year old Martha married Custis. As a wedding gift, the Custis family presented Martha with a family cookbook entitled "Booke of Cookery and Booke of Sweetmeats" Hand written by unknown hand, there is evidence
1 CONC  the book had been in the Custis family for generations likely dating to the early 1600s.
0 @N6000000000437704411@ NOTE REFERENCE: 756
1 CONT
1 CONT Washington, Martha b. June 2, 1731, New Kent county, Va. [U.S.] d. May 22, 1802, Mount Vernon, Va., U.S. MARTHA DANDRIDGE first First Lady in American history, who, as the wi fe of U.S. President George Washington,set many of the 
1 CONC standards and cust oms of her unofficial office. Martha Dandridge grew up among the wealthy plantation families of the Tide water regionof Virginia. In 1749, despite a 20-year age gap, she marri ed Daniel Parke Custis, the heir to
1 CONC  a neighbouring plantation. During the ir life together she bore four children, two of whom died in infancy. A le ngthy legal struggle ensued after her husband died intestate in July 175 7, but she was left a wealthy widow. When G
1 CONC eorge Washington began to cou rt her the following spring, the young plantation owner and commander of V irginia forces in the French and IndianWar was aware of her wealth, but t he attachment between them grew increasingly deep. 
1 CONC The couple married at her home on January 6, 1759, and she and her childr en moved to Mount Vernon, Washington's plantation on the Potomac River. H er wealth helped create the reputation for hospitality with which Mount Ve rnon be
1 CONC came associated. After Washington was chosen to command the Americ an forces in the War of Independence, Martha spent time with her husba nd at his military quarters in Cambridge, Massachusetts; Morristown, New J ersey; Valley For
1 CONC ge, Pennsylvania; and Newburgh, New York. At the war's e nd Washington returned to Mount Vernon, where the couple reared two of Mar tha Washington's grandchildren. In 1789, shortly after her husband's inauguration as president of 
1 CONC the Unit ed States, she joined him in New York City, then the national capital,whe re she managed their home on Broadway. She established their home as a cen tre of social activity, entertaining several times a week. Philadelphia 
1 CONC be came the seat of government in 1790, and the Washingtons moved to a hou se on High Street, where the hospitality became even more elaborate. The F irst Ladywas criticized by some for entertaining on a scale too opulent f or a r
1 CONC epublican government, and she was only too glad to retire to a mo re domestic life atMount Vernon after her husband's second term of offi ce was completed. Later,however, she criticized the Jeffersonian sty le of entertaining as b
1 CONC eing toocasual.
