0 @N6000000003682951793@ NOTE moved to va 1650w/2 sons and 2 1/2 brothers 
1 CONT was a colonel in the County militia a vestryman sheriff a burgess and also a surveyor in the state of Maryland 
1 CONT He was one of the commissioners who settled the bountry line between Va and Maryland 
1 CONT killed by indians
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~milingenealogy/d289.html[catlett.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT 2  PLAC 610
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT John Catlett emigrated from Sittingbourne, England to Virginia around May 23, 1650.John was the Great Great Grandfather of President James Madison. (John Catlett/Elizabeth Gains m.1690 - Rebecca Catlett/Francis Conway m. October 2
1 CONC 1, 1717 - James Madison/Eleanor Conway  married Sept 15, 1749 - President Madison)
1 CONT He immigrated to America around 1650 with his two sons and his half-brothers Ralph and Edward Rowzee.  He was the first (as a Colonel and leader of the expedition) to explore the Blue Ridge Mountains, probably because John was an 
1 CONC expert surveyor (he was literate because there is a surviving letter in Williamsburg).  There are many records of his work in Maryland. Starting in late 1650 there are many land transactions in Rappahannock and nearby counties in 
1 CONC Virginia, involving John and his kin, so he must have had a fortune..  John was one of three commissioners to settle the boundary line between Virginia and Maryland. The others were Edmund Scarborough and Richard Lawrence (a disti
1 CONC nguished Oxford graduate.)
1 CONT John was a Colonel in the Militia, a Presiding Justice of Rappahannock County and was a planter for many years in Rappahannock County before it became Essex.  He was killed by Indians while defending a fort at Port Royal, Virginia
1 CONC .
1 CONT The Expedition:  ".. Colonel Catlett with some other gentlemen took a journey to make further discoveries of the country to the Westwards and arriving at the foot of the mountains early in the morning they left their horses and en
1 CONC deavored to gain the tops of the mountains which they accomplished about four o'clock in the afternoon, and then looking further forward they discovered other mountains, whereof they took the altitude and judged them inaccessible;
1 CONC   which discouraged them from any further attempts, their design being chiefly to discover whether there were any rivers that ran into the South-Ocean...:
0 @N6000000003319654969@ NOTE
1 CONT http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~mysouthernfamily/myff/d008 3/g0000015.html
1 CONT * TITLE: Col. *	RESIDENCE: Kent, ENG & 1650 To Rappahannock Co. VA *	BIRTH: ABT 1622, Prob. Sittingbourne, Kent Or Canterbury, England *	BAPTISM: 12 Mar 1625, St Peters Church, Canterbury *	DEATH: BET 1669 AND 0000, Rappahannock C
1 CONC o. VA
1 CONT Ancestors of American Presidents, compiled by Gary Boyd Roberts, Pub. 1989 by New Eng Hist. Gen. Soc., Boston, MA.
1 CONT Karen Bosze, 2/98 http://ourworld.compuserve.com/homepages/karon_bosze/knorris.htm One ref. used A History of Two VA Fam Transplanted from County Kent, ENG" by Dr. and Mrs. William Carter Stubbs.
1 CONT Robert L. Latimer; EMail: bigunit@wport.com 4/98
1 CONT Elizabeth UNDERWOOD (Taylor Slaughter Catlett Butler ) of Isle of Wight & Old Rappa. Cos, VA http://www.combs-families.org/combs/assoc/u-butler.htm Primary Sources: Underwood Family of Virginia, Augusta B. Fothergill, The Virginia
1 CONC  Magazine of History and Biography, Vol. XXXVIII, 1930, pp. 267-270, 386-393; Vol. XXXIX, 1931, pp. 70-75, 167-173, 272-276; XL, 1932, pp. 91-95; Reprinted in Genealogy of Virginia Families, Vol. V, GPC, Baltimore, 1981, pp. 601-6
1 CONC 35; hereinafter Underwood, Fothergill; and Old Rappahannock Co VA Records (Series), Ruth & Sam Sparacio, Antient Press, McLean, VA. Primary Researcher: Sherrill Underwood Williams NOTICE: This Combs &c. Research Report has been pr
1 CONC ovided for the free use of those engaged in non-commercial genealogical research by the Combs Research Group. Any and all commercial use is strictly prohibited. Researchers are encouraged to copy and distribute this work freely, b
1 CONC ut with the proviso that it may only be copied and circulated in its entirety -- including this notice, and all sources, bibliographies and credits; and excepting electronically in which case permission is freely granted to Hot Li
1 CONC nk to this site instead. Sincerely, The Combs &c Research Group Email: webmaster@combs-families.org.
1 CONT 8. John4 Catlett (Sarah3 Hawkins, Ralph2 , [Unknown]1 ) was born Bef. 12 Mar 1625/26 in Canterbury, Kent, England, and died 1670 in Port Royal, VA. He married (1) [Daughter] Lucas Bef. 1643, daughter of Thomas Lucas and [Unknown].
1 CONC  She was born Abt. 1628. He married (2) Elizabeth Underwood 1657 in Old Rappahannock Co., VA, daughter of William Underwood and Margaret Roberts. She was born Abt. 1632 in Sittingbourne, England, and died Bef. 07 May 1673 in VA.
1 CONT Notes for John Catlett: John Catlett was a justice of the Rappahannock County court, and was an explorer. He and German immigrant John Lederer were among the first settlers to lead an expedition to the top of the Blue Ridge Mounta
1 CONC ins in 1670.
1 CONT "Colonel Catlett was killed by the Indians while defending the fort at Port Royal in 1670, Port Royal is on the Rappahannock river, in Caroline county opposite Port Conway." (Stubbs, pg. 15) Stubbs: "...by his will he left two dau
1 CONC ghters, Elizabeth and Sarah Catlett, 600 acres of land in Rappahannock County, who married brothers, Francis and Robert Taliaferro....Order Book 1686-1692 p. 25."
1 CONT John Catlett emigrated from Sitingbourne , England to Virginia. The Virginia Land Books have an entry whereby on May 23, 1650, John Catlett and Ralph Rowzie were granted 400 acres on south side of the Rappahannock river for headri
1 CONC ghts. John was also granted 450 acres called "Green Hill" on the south side of the Rappahannock river, between the Golden Vale and Cedar Creek. This was his home place and long continued in his family.
1 CONT http://ourworld.compuserve.com/homepages/karon_bosze/earljohn.htm
0 @N6000000005232499335@ NOTE
1 CONT It should be noted that if the Johnson source is correct, the mother would have been 59 years old when John was born.  More likely, his mother was Elizabeth Taliaferro-- or someone else. Diana Johnson's notes list the place of bir
1 CONC th as "Canterburg, Eng." but I believe it should be Canterbury. "John Catlett emigrated from Sittingbourne, England to Virginia around May  23, 1650." -- Per Diana's notes.
0 @N6000000005232499493@ NOTE
1 CONT Major Thomas Catlett born in Kent County, England. Occupation Sheriff of  Essex County, Viriginia. In 1661 Thomas witnessed a deed made in Essex  County, Va., by his father and step-mother Elizabeth Catlett . From Land  Book at Ri
1 CONC chmond Va. Thomas owned 1,000 acres in Spotsylvania County,  Va.-- Book XII pg. 308. (If this is the right person he would have been 14 or 15 years of age when he witnessed the deed-- presuming the birth date of 1646 is correct.)
