0 @N6000000003275282385@ NOTE []
1 CONT
1 CONT !Richard came to Virginia in 1641, and married
1 CONT Ann about this time.
1 CONT   Colonel Lee was an adherent of Charles I, and
1 CONT helped to keep Virginia loyal to the Stuarts,
1 CONT acting as secretary to Sir William Berkely,
1 CONT the governor.
1 CONT   He was clerk of the Council in 1642; attorney
1 CONT general the next year; burgess for the county of York
1 CONT in 1647, for Northumberland in 1651; member of the
1 CONT Council in 1663; secretary of the colony in
1 CONT 1651, 1659-1663; and a colonel of militia in
1 CONT 1651.
1 CONT   A burgess was required to be a freeholder
1 CONT and resident of the County he represented,
1 CONT so he must have lived as early as that in
1 CONT Northumberland and probably of "Cobbs Hall"
1 CONT where John Gibbon, officaial of the Heralds
1 CONT College visited him in 1659."
1 CONT   He was also a magistrate, and a wealthy planter
1 CONT and merchant.  His first plantation was
1 CONT on the York River, near Poropotank Creek, and in
1 CONT later years at Dividing Creeks, in Northumberland.
1 CONT   He also had an estate in Stratford-Langton,
1 CONT County Essex, England, where he spent much time
1 CONT toward the end of his life.
1 CONT   Will Franke also wrote: "He held many important
1 CONT places in the colony.  As noted he was many times
1 CONT a Burgess, served on many important committees,
1 CONT was a member of the King's Privy Council and was
1 CONT Secretary of State for the Colony under Governor
1 CONT Sir William Berkeley.
1 CONT   Col. Lee and his family made many visits to
1 CONT England.  The last visit was in 1660 when he
1 CONT remained there three years, probably for the
1 CONT education of his children.  He died in 1664,
1 CONT leaving a wife and eight children, all mentioned
1 CONT in his will.  He left all of his plate to be
1 CONT equally divided between his three eldest sons.
1 CONT His will was executed in England just before he
1 CONT returned with his family to Virginia.  He lived
1 CONT but a short time after reaching Virginia, dying
1 CONT the early part of 1664."
1 CONT   Mrs. Stanard, in her "Colonial Virginia",
1 CONT mentions the following incident connected with a
1 CONT visit which Lee made to England:
1 CONT   "In 1655, Colonel Richard Lee took some of his
1 CONT plate to London to have its fashion changed.
1 CONT There was a law against exporting silver from
1 CONT England, and when he was about to embark on his
1 CONT homeward voyage the customs officers at Gravesend
1 CONT seized his 'trunk of plate,' but on his affidavit
1 CONT that it was all intended for his own use and that
1 CONT most of it had been brought from Virginia a year
1 CONT and a half before, and that every piece had his
1 CONT coat of arms on it, it was given back to him."
1 CONT   Will Franke compiled a chart on the Lee
1 CONT family in 1929.  He wrote, "Col. Richard Lee
1 CONT was descended from the ancient and honorable
1 CONT family of Lee of Merton Regis in Shropshire,
1 CONT of whom one genealogist said, 'They were
1 CONT ladies and gentlemen before many of the
1 CONT present nobility had emerged from obscurity.'
1 CONT It was in fact one of those ancient county
1 CONT families which numbered among them gentlemen,
1 CONT knights and baronets.
1 CONT   Colonel Lee came to Virginia with his young
1 CONT wife Anna and settled first in York County as
1 CONT is shown by his first land grant dated Aug.
1 CONT 10, 1642.  That he was then married but had
1 CONT no children is shown by name being mentioned in
1 CONT the "head rights".  The grant shows that is was
1 CONT 'for his own personal adventure and his wife
1 CONT Anna' and '17 other persons whom he brought to
1 CONT the colony.'
1 CONT   All of the land patented by Richard Lee
1 CONT between 1642-51 was situated in that part of
1 CONT York which was subsequently included in
1 CONT Gloucester..."
1 CONT   A book called "Lee of VA 1642-1892", edited and
1 CONT published by Edmund Jennings Lee in 1983 in
1 CONT Maryland says NOT to pay attention to a book
1 CONT called "General History of the Lee Family of VA
1 CONT & MD" by E. C. Mead, as there are numerous mistakes.
