0 @N6000000003525921040@ NOTE Hancock Lee was Col. Richard Henry Lee's seventh son.
1 CONT
1 CONT He built and lived at Ditchley.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source:  Virginia Old Churches, Vol. 2
0 @N6000000000437703052@ NOTE REFERENCE: 650
0 @N6000000003275282357@ NOTE []
1 CONT
1 CONT !"Capt. Hancock, of "Ditchley," in Lee
1 CONT Parish, later Wicomico.  He gave a
1 CONT communion cup to the parish, and he
1 CONT was a reader of "The Pilgrim's Progress."
1 CONT   Hancock inherited "Ditchley" from his
1 CONT father.  The plantation contained 900
1 CONT acres and was part of a grant to Colonel
1 CONT Richard Lee.
1 CONT   In "The Storied Kendalls", Anne K. Walker
1 CONT writes:   "In the graveyard at "Ditchley"
1 CONT in Wicomico, Northumberland County, the
1 CONT inscriptions on the tombstones of Hancock
1 CONT Lee Esq. and his two wives are as follows:
1 CONT   "Here Lyeth the Body of Hancock Lee,
1 CONT seventh son of Honorable Richard who
1 CONT Departed this life May 25th Anno Dom 1729
1 CONT AEta 56 years, Also Mary his first wife,
1 CONT only Daughter of William Kendall, Gent
1 CONT who departed this life December the 24th
1 CONT Anno Dom 1694. AEta 33 years.  And Sarah
1 CONT his last wife Daughter of Isacc Allerton
1 CONT Esq. Who departed this life May 17th
1 CONT Anno Dom 1731- AE 60 years.
1 CONT   "Ditchley" was the name of one of the Lee
1 CONT estates in Virginia and took the name from
1 CONT the original estate in England, which was
1 CONT presented to Lionel Lee, who lived during
1 CONT the reign of Richard Coeur de Lion.  The
1 CONT latter rewarded him for his special
1 CONT gallantry at the siege of Acre, during the
1 CONT third crusade, when Lionel in 1192 raised a
1 CONT company of gentlemen and marched with the
1 CONT king to the Holy Land.  On his return, he
1 CONT was made first Earl of Litchfield; the king
1 CONT presented him at that time with the estate
1 CONT of "Ditchley" as solid proof of his
1 CONT approbation."   "Ditchley" still stands [at
1 CONT the writing of Mrs. Walker's book in 1947]
1 CONT in the Northern Neck of Virginia...It was
1 CONT patented in 1651 by Colonel Richard Lee.
1 CONT The first house dated from 1687; the present
1 CONT house was built by Kendall Lee in 1752."
1 CONT   In 1929, Will Franke wrote, "Hancock Lee
1 CONT was a man of importance, filling many
1 CONT honorable places in the Colony.  He was a
1 CONT Burgess, a justice and a naval officer, being
1 CONT Collector of Customs in his district.
1 CONT   He was also a patron of the church.  A
1 CONT chalice which he gave to his church is still
1 CONT in use."
0 @N6000000000437703033@ NOTE REFERENCE: 649
0 @N6000000003615527596@ NOTE (2)William Lee (b. ca. 1651 - bef. 1697/8) "Lee of Virginia" contains conjectures, but no further concrete information.
0 @N6000000008640625256@ NOTE 3. William Lee b. 1678 or 1682, Northampton, Northumberland Co, VA
1 CONT      m. Dorothy Taylor, ca 1703, Westmoreland Co, VA d/o Thomas Taylor & Elizabeth Harwood b. 1681, Surry,
1 CONT      King and Queen Co, VA
1 CONT      Information on Dorothy Taylor comes from "Log Cabins to White House" by Brewer. 13 Apr 1745
1 CONT
1 CONT Dorothy was witness to will of Henry Williams in Richmond Co.,VA. signed as Dorothy Croucher. Dorothy and her brother Thomas were administrators of her husband William Lee's estate. She was left wearing apparel in her mother's wil
1 CONC l. "Dorothy to have my Coyas" d. 1717, Richmond Co, VA (Source: Amelia Co. Order Book 7, p. 226, Letters of Administration of Estate of William Lee to Dorothy Lee, relict of William Lee on 12/4/1717.).
1 CONT
1 CONT Documentation from "Log Cabins to White House" by Brewer. On page 275 "On 19 Jan 1668 William and wife Alice acknowledged debt of 250 acres of land to William Heath.
1 CONT On 5 Sep 1668 William and wife Alice sold their plantation to Thomas Adams (Surry Co. records p.54).
1 CONT
1 CONT From "Log Cabin" book on p.276 it gives William Lee( who married Dorothy Taylor) as one of his children. On p.273, Richard Henry Lee and Anna Constable are given as the parents of William (husband of Alice) but other sources state
1 CONC  that Richard Lee's son William did not marry. Other sources say he married and had a daughter Mary who married a Heath and that he left his estate to her -- which was contested by his brother and returned to the Lee family.
1 CONT
1 CONT There is a William Lee and Mary Lee, witnesses in Richard Jesper's will, dated 1698 in North Farnham Parish, VA. Need to identify them. Dorothy and brother Thomas Taylor were administrators of his estate. They left to William's gr
1 CONC andchildren (children of Wm.3rd) the legacy left to him by his grandmother Elizabeth Taylor. Thomas Hanks received 9 lbs 11 shillings 8 pence; Joseph Hanks 2 lbs2 shillings 6 pence; Richard Lee 4 shillings; none was listed for Joh
1 CONC n Lee who had probably died young.
1 CONT
1 CONT **************************
