1 CONT
1 CONT -----------------------------------************************
1 CONT Edmund Jennings Lee had not found any record of what happened to one son William Lee and blew it off finally saying he appears to have died young.
1 CONT Weelllll sort of.
1 CONT He did die earlier, 1694-5, than other siblings, leaving a 18 year old daughter and three younger minor sons. The daughter Mary had married William Heath at age 15 had two sons, Thomas and Samuel the last being born after his fath
1 CONC er died. She re married to Bartholomew Schriever who is the villain of this story.  He was  of age so grabbed the estate and paid out yearly  a gentlemans living to the three boys; Wm, John, and Richard.  What he paid was far less
1 CONC  than the estate could manage; and they deserved more; but it was legally large enough that they could not sue their guardian until ALL of them were of age. The instant the youngest turned 21 they did sue.
1 CONT BUT
1 CONT Not in probate court    but common pleas court   to recover their inheritance.
1 CONT And not in the original county (Westmoreland) but the county had been split and then re-split in the mean time (at this time it was Surry Co) and worse the judge (a VIP ) did not travel to them he stayed put and they had to come t
1 CONC o him in King and Queen County. (This was a MAJOR estate -and why they had a superior judge in a higher court- in todays terms it would be  multi millionaire stuff).
1 CONT AND
1 CONT It was 7-8 years out of timeŒ7 -8 years later than expected.
1 CONT Wrong place, wrong time, wrong court, and why E J Lee missed it.
1 CONT Bart and Mary got her furniture and what money he had sequestered (the court knew he had milked estate, said so, but did not bother to try to find where he had squirreled money away--{Bermuda}), just gave Mary HER  furniture, and 
1 CONC kicked them off land and divided it 3 ways each boy getting roughly 1/3.   Bart packed up in high dudgeon and went back to England (assume they stopped at Bermuda where $$ were) - and lived life of rich ex colonial gentleman.
1 CONT
1 CONT OK first problem solved. This I got from two separate books /people who had done work independently, and I cross-checked just to be sure. (A Weavin' and Log Cabin to White House
1 CONT Next problem
1 CONT William (ii) son Wm (i) and grandson of Co Richard died 1717 (about 13 years after court bruhoo); leaving 4 sons. And I will stick to our line his son Charles b 1706 moved to new land at North Farnham Parish, Richmond Co., Va.
1 CONT Charles moves again dies 1799 at-really near just out of town- Leesburg, Cumberland Co., Va. leaving cash to a son William (others got land) and just down the road, land adjoining Charles is a William Lee that the previous genealo
1 CONC gists had assumed was his son.
1 CONT BooBoo!
1 CONT It was a reasonable assumption.  Just wrong. Charles died in 1799 Cumberland Co Va and had a son William. There is a William Lee (md Ave Noel) whose land adjoins Charles land so they assume he is the son. Unfortunately they happen
1 CONC ed to buy adjoining lands at about the same time and they were cousins, but unwarranted assumption as to being his son.
1 CONT Then I found the full name of James Garnett Early Lee b. 23 feb 1777 known as Garnett Lee usually, AND known to be son of William and Ave Noel and that triggered my memory that James Lee had md Mary Clark Garnett and I cross conne
1 CONC cted Wm and Ave to James Lee and Mary Garnett who also had known son William but unknown what happened to him if we connect Wm and Ave to Charles. To add to the surety I was right,  Wm and Ave moved about 1788 to Evington Road Bed
1 CONC ford Co Va. just a bit down the road from James and Mary Garnett  -- (Ah Ha!)----- so this time making the assumption that the William who was close to James was his son works. James died by the way 1791  Probate  Bedford Co Va.
1 CONT With that I was able to disconnect he wrong William (md Ave) and get him to correct papa- and so was left connecting MY William (1738-1808) to Charles. Reason for cash was my William had gone on to Hardy Co Va. (now West Va) at th
1 CONC e same time, abt 1760, his father Charles had moved from Richmond Co where he was born (The residual of the original estate was now in Surry Co.-but had been split up so much the land left to Charles was too small to make him well
1 CONC  off - or as well off as he wished) then on to Leesburgh in Cumberland Co and so our William  had no use for land- he had his own- but hundreds of miles away. SO cash.
1 CONT Oh me oh my long story. I also jumped 4 years work in getting all of the above. I followed Loads of dead ends.
