1 CONT The latest on the subject casts doubt that any of these were children of Jane Arledge.
1 CONT
1 CONT Subject: Re: Hardin-Arledge
1 CONT Date: Wed, 26 Aug 1998 22:59:45 EDT
1 CONT From: LEPound@aol.com
1 CONT
1 CONT Hi Pam,
1 CONT
1 CONT I won't try to get into a lot of detail, however, I now believe that the Henry Harding lines that have been thrown around for so many years are incorrect, including much of what you have in your chart.
1 CONT
1 CONT In late June I decided that there were so many contradictions in the Harding lines that I needed to see just what could be proven and what could not be proven and was I surprised.
1 CONT
1 CONT It's taken about two months to copy out all the Harding references in Northumberland County, VA and in other Northern Neck counties up to Loudoun,
1 CONT resulting in hundreds of entries and about 75 pages of material.
1 CONT
1 CONT That said, here are my new comments on your chart:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1.  Henry Harding and Jane Arledge have no provable children.  All sources
1 CONT list four children, Henry, John, Thomas, and William.  This is from a
1 CONT speculative comment in an article written by Mrs. Keach in the 1920's in
1 CONT Tylers Quarter, which is the basis for the structure of the family as
1 CONT currently accepted.
1 CONT
1 CONT 2.  Henry Harding who married Jane Arledge was still under 21 in August 1691
1 CONT per court records.  He most likely married within the next year or two. He
1 CONT died in August 1697 when his wife filed for probate, not May 1698.  Jane is
1 CONT the only name Jane Arledge is known under in the records.
1 CONT
1 CONT 3.  Henry Harding II, supposed son of Henry Harding  and Jane Arledge, was
1 CONT married by early 1708 in Northumberland Co., VA, proven by a court record
1 CONT where Henry and Anne, widow of William Beltcher Jr, probate his estate, not
1 CONT 1714-15.  If Henry II was the son of Henry and Jane, he would have been 14-15
1 CONT years old at marriage.  Not likely.
1 CONT
1 CONT 4.  Henry and Jane's other listed sons are figments of the imagination.
1 CONT Thomas does not exist and was never assigned a family by any researcher. John is most likly the son of William Harding, Henry's brother, proven by a
1 CONT Westmoreland Co. court order.  William was invented and the dates of his
1 CONT children were changed by 10 years from parish registers to make him fit the
1 CONT family.
1 CONT
1 CONT 5.  The only proven child of Henry Harding II is Henry III.  All others are
1 CONT speculation.  Charles belongs in neither position that you have him in. If
1 CONT you look carefully you will note you have him twice, under Henry and under
1 CONT William, both times married to Rachel Lunsford.  William may belong in this
1 CONT family but there is nothing to connect him.  George may belong here too but
1 CONT there is no proof.
1 CONT
1 CONT I need to e-mail Sue Harris and Brent Baughn with the results of myresearch
1 CONT since it is such a radical change.  Ironically, I keep coming back to my
1 CONT original idea of Brent's ancestry, which also mine, the more I research, and
1 CONT not the line he sent you, which is now clearly incorrect.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following are my basic charts.  I have details for each individual amounting
1 CONT to dozens of records in some cases.  I don't know how much interest you have
1 CONT in this line since it is now my belief that Henry and Jane Harding had no
1 CONT children.  The numbering system is generational, that is 1132 is the second
1 CONT child of the third child of the first child.  Hopefully the e-mail does not
1 CONT screw it up too much.  Let me know if you are interested in a full copy of the
1 CONT work I've done.  There are five different families, not one as in most
1 CONT sources.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lee Pound
1 CONT
