1 CONT Notes for Charles M Rector:
1 CONT bluth@xroads.com (William C Luth)
1 CONT researching this family
1 CONT History of Clark Co, CD 450
1 CONT Prior to 1850 Charles Rector built a small distillery at the mouth of Storm's Creek in the northern part of
1 CONT german Township on Mad River. Later he put up a grist and saw-mill at the same place. This mill was
1 CONT afterwards operated for a number of years by Gresham Gard. The mill has long since dissappeared. A
1 CONT race running north into Champaign County is the only evidence of the use of mill power at a former
1 CONT period at this place.
1 CONT PUBLIC RECORDS OF CHAMPAIGN COUNTY, OHIO
1 CONT CHANCERY RECORDS OF THE COURT OF COMMON PLEAS
1 CONT Record Book 7, Page 144 - Heard April Term 1818 - Writ of Injunction Charles Rector
1 CONT Vs
1 CONT Samuel McMullin
1 CONT Charles Rector gave Samuel McMullin a note for $100 in 1814 for which McMullin was to perform six
1 CONT months tour of duty in the Drafted Militia of the State of Ohio under command of Captain Gabriel Cox in
1 CONT the place of Conaway Rector, sonof Charles. After McMullin served the tour he was to obtain an
1 CONT honorable discharge inthe name of Conaway Rector and Charles Rector would then pay the
1 CONT $100 note. About three weeks after the contract was made and McMullin was
1 CONT attached in the service in the place of Conaway, the company of Captain Cox was not needed and the
1 CONT men were sent home. McMullin had to take Charles Rector to court to obtain payment on the note. On
1 CONT 14 April 1817 McMullin said that he did receive a full ample and complete discharge for the tour of duty
1 CONT which the said Conaway was bound to perform and he repeatedly offered the same to Charles. Rector
1 CONT is asking for a writ of injunction against a court ruling of 22 October 1817 because he believes that his
1 CONT son is liable for recall as the tour of six months was not fulfilled. A jury was impanelled: George Petty,
1 CONT Benjamin Doolittle, Jas. Paxton, John McCord, Jno. Thomas, Robert G. Swan, Allen M. Poff, Tolson
1 CONT Ford, William Scott, John Meredith, Pheneas Thornton and Andrew Kirkpatrick. Testimony which was
1 CONT heard by the jury included that of William Bacome who said he was present in the home of Joseph
1 CONT Reynolds when the contract was made between Charles Rector and Samuel McMullin. Jacob
1 CONT Margart/Maggart testified that he served in the tour of duty with Samuel McMullin. Joseph Layton,
1 CONT sworn on 16 December 1819, testified that he received orders from Major General Whiteman to raise
1 CONT from his Brigade a company of men to perform a tour of duty and he issued his orders to Colonel
1 CONT Daugherty and the company was raised under Captain Gabriel Cox. On 10 March 1814 Cox was
1 CONT directed to march to Franklington, remained there for some time and was discharged. He does not
1 CONT know how long the men were in service, but they are entitled to the pay for active duty, and if the orders
1 CONT received by Cox were strictly complied with then the company was legally discharged and the men were
1 CONT entitled to a discharge for a full tour of duty. The deposition of Gabriel Cox was sworn to on 17
1 CONT December 1819. Cox related that when he was at Springfield he accepted McMullin as a replacement
1 CONT for Conaway Rector. McMullin marched with him to Franklington and remained with the company until
1 CONT discharged. The court proceedings end at this point and no judgment was recorded.
