1 CONT
1 CONT Letter from Van Stilley, 18 July 1995:
1 CONT "Job Carter and John Turley were witnesses to Maurice Veale's Prince William Co. lease from John Waugh in 1733. This places Job in Prince William Co. five years after witnessing the Thomas Carter, Jr. Will in Lancaster (Co.), 1728
1 CONC , when he had to be of age. So, we can date Job's departure from Lancaster as between 1728 and 1733.
1 CONT This is as close as we get to placing Peter Carter in Job Carter's family; close enough, I think, to place Job far ahead of any other contender for Peter's father."
1 CONT
1 CONT 24 Apr 1728: Lancaster Co., VA, Will Bk 12, p. 279: Job Carter witnessed will of Thomas Carter, Jr. (probably his uncle)
1 CONT
1 CONT 19 Mar 1733: Job Carter with John Turley, George Foster, and Benj. Tyler, witnessed lease and release of 90 acres from John Waugh to Morris Veale, Pr. William Co., Va., Deed Bk. B, part 1 pgs. 221-222.
1 CONT
1 CONT 19 Nov 1734: Job Carter with John Ferguson, bondsman for Jane Simpson, executrix for estate of Thomas Simpson, dec'd. Pr. William Co., VA., Will Bk. 3, p. 18.
1 CONT
1 CONT 27 Jul 1741: Job Carter with John Turley and Joseph Jacob, appraised estate of Thomas Simpson. Pr. William Co., VA., Bk. C, pg. 320.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1744: Job Carter on Fairfax Co. Poll List for election of Burgesses from Fairfax Co. (two each), next to Samuel Tillet among those who voted for Capt. Lewis Elzey; also voted for Jno. Sturman. Fairfax County, Virginia: A History.
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince William County, Va Minute Book 1752-1753, pp. 184-186, 24 July 1753
1 CONT "Carniby Veale Executor of Morris Veale deceased who was one of the Executors of John Tillett deceased and John Turley the other Executor of the said John Tillet Deced Complainants...
1 CONT The said Complainants by William Ellzey their attorney Exhibited into Court a Bill of Complaint against Job Carter the Defendant thereby setting forth that they were Executors of a Certain John Tillet deceased that some time in th
1 CONC e year of our Lord one thousand seven hundred and thirty three the said John Tillet being possessed of an Estate in goods and Chattles amounting to forty six pounds seven shillings and three pence current money and sixteen hundred
1 CONC  fourteen pounds of Tobacco made his Last Will and Testament in writing and appointed the said Morris Veale and the said John Turley Executors and died leaving his Widow then pregnant and two sons John and George Tillett and by hi
1 CONC s said last Will bequeathed unto his wife Milliant one young horse, to his son George Tillett one young horse and his sadle or another as good unto his son John Tillett one young horse and the child with which the said Milliant wa
1 CONC s pregnant one young mare all the rest of his moveable Estate to be equaly divided between his Wife and children;" now I (Van Stilley) will paraphrase the rest - the baby died and the estate was divided into three parts - then Mil
1 CONC liant married Job Carter until she died in 1749 and the boys - John and George wanted their estate and had not lived with Job since their mother died. Carniby Veale and John Turley asked Job for "their" estate "in a friendly manne
1 CONC r" which he refused to do - and the court told him to do it. The last paragraph - "Peter Carter, Plaintiff, vs Job Carter, Defendant, on Attachment: This day the said Peter and the said Job although solemnly called came not, it is
1 CONC  therefore considered that the said Peter recover against the said Job the sum of thirty pounds one shilling and Eight pence half-penny and also his costs and by him in this behalf Expenses and the said Job Carter in mercy. The Sh
1 CONC eriff having returned that he levied the said attachment in the hands of Daniel Kincheloe ......
1 CONT NOTE by VAS: "The other executor [in John Tillett's will] was Peter Carter, Morris Veale's son-in-law, husband of Amelia (Veale), believed to be a son of the defendant. He may have declined to join this action as a plaintiff if he
1 CONC  was closely related to the defendant"
1 CONT
1 CONT STAFFORD CO. VIRGINIA TITHABLES, Quit Rents, personal Prop. Tax & Related Lists & Petitions, 1723 - 1790, Vol. I, comp. by John Vogt & T. Wm Kethley Jr., pub. 1990 -
1 CONT p. 61 - 1759 - Rents on Wilkinson pat. due in 1758 - Job Carter, Charles Carter
1 CONT
1 CONT By 1768 (p. 66) and 1773 (p. 92) there are only two Carters listed - Joseph and John on Quit Rent List & Rental List.
