1 CONT
1 CONT The will of Captain John Washington directs that he be buried on the plantation where he lived, by his father, mother and brothers.  He gives his wife Ann the planation he lived on; to his eldest son Lawrence Washington land in St
1 CONC afford on upper Machodick; to son John the land he lived on after his wife's death; to sone Henry, land in Stafford; to son Nathaniel land on Appomattox (Mattox) Creek; brother Lawrence and ife Ann, executors.
0 @N6000000002932533686@ NOTE This Henry is not the father of Peggy Margaret Willis, who married Edmund Terrill.  Much of the following information re. Henry's life and span of activities was derived from either 'Fielding Lewis and the Washington Family', or '
1 CONC Forgotton Companions' both by Paula S. Felder.  Henry is referenced in the Genealogies of Virginia Families, Vol. V, p. 494 as the founder of the city of Fredericksburg, Virginia.
1 CONT Prior to moving to Spotsylvania Co,. VA. in 1728, Henry was a vestryman at the Petsworth Parish in Gloucester Co. and served in the capacity along with John Washington.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1718 and again in 1723, Henry is referenced as a Burgess from Gloucester County, Virginia.  In 1726, he received a patent for land in Spotsylvania County and in 1727 when the town of Fredericksburg was laid out, he was one of t
1 CONC he Trustees.  During 1726 and 1727, Henry made several appearances at the Spotsylvania Court, possibly in pursuit of his campaign for election to the House of Burgesses from Spotsylvania County.  He is believed to have made his ho
1 CONC me at Willis Hill just west of the town on land that later became known as Marye's Heights.  In 1727, Henry excheated a tract of 400 acres that had originally been patented by William Hansford in 1723, that adjoined the lands of A
1 CONC ugustine Smith. (Ref: Forgotton Companions, Paula S. Felder, p. 204).
1 CONT
1 CONT 'Although tradition has it hat Henry Willis built his family a home on Willis Hill (now Marye' Heights), circumstantial evidence suggests that he regarded it only as an investment property.  It was more likely his widow Mildred wh
1 CONC o built a home for herself and her young son Lewis some time between 1740 and 1747'.  (Ref: Forgotton Companions, Paula S. Felder, p. 182)  On 2 February 1728, Henry was appointee to the Committee for Propositions and Grievances o
1 CONC f the Virginia Assembly.
1 CONT
1 CONT By 1730, Henry had purchased numerous buildings at the upper end of the then city of Fredericksburg which then housed local tradesman.  These included lots 30 and 32.  By Henry's efforts as a member of the House of Burgesses, Fred
1 CONC ericksburg was named in legislation that created official tobacco inspection station throughout the colony.  Through his own efforts he was subsequently named proprietor of the inspection station in Fredericksburg.  In March 1730,
1 CONC  he was placed in charge of altering the main road going by Fredericksburg to place it at the back of the town. In May 1730, he and John Waller officiated as town trustees on court day at the Germanna Colony in the sale of three l
1 CONC ots. (Ref: Forgotton Companions, Paula S. Felder, p. 77)  Also, in May of 1730, Henry is referenced as a  Lieutenant Colonel in the county militia and as county Corner.  (Ref: Forgotton Companions, Paula S. Felder, p. 79).
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1731, Henry was appointed to the Parish vestry of St. George's Parish.  "Henry Willis, Gent., one of the Majy's Justices of the Peace for Spotsylvania County, administered the oath injoynd by Law to Francis Taliaferro as a Vest
1 CONC ryman in this Parish, who subscribed to the test., and also to be confirmable to the doctrine and disciplines of the Church of England as by law established."  (Ref: Forgotton Companions, Paula S. Felder, p. 67-68).    In November
1 CONC  of 1731, Henry purchased Lot 15 and Lot 62 (which would ultimately be the location of his home) from Moseley Battaley.
1 CONT
1 CONT By 1732, the Accokeek Furance operation of Augustine Washington had captured the attention of Colonel Henry Willis of Fredericksburg. (Ref: Fielding Lewis and the Washington Family, Paula S. Felder, p. 20).
1 CONT When Colonel William Byrd of Westover Plantation visited Fredericksburg in 1732, he noted in his diary that his landlord, Colonel Henry Willis, was 'the top man in the place'.  In April 1732, Henry acquired a triangular acre of la
1 CONC nd adjoing the south end of town from James Williams. (Ref: Forgotton Companions, Paula S. Felder, p. 169) In May of 1732, Henry left the area for the convening of the House of Burgesses.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1733, Henry began work on the construction of a local church located on George Street between Princess Anne and Caroline.  In January 1733 or 1734, Henry married his third wife Mildred Washington, the widow of Roger Gregory.  I
1 CONC n August 1734, he obtained the court's permission to locate the town ferry next to the wharf he owned.  In 1735. Henry purchased half of the Buckner Royston patent from a Buckner heir.  On this land, Henry created the first direct
1 CONC  road into the town of Fredericksburg taking advantage of the Chrisell Mine Road that led from Hazel Run all the way to the North Anna River.  At one time, Henry came to own at least 1,270 acres at the south end of Fredericksburg.
1 CONC   Much of this land boardered Hazel Run.  Also in 1735, Henry obtained an appointment to the prestigious position of clerk of the court of the new Orange county, and the designation of a house he owned on Black Rock Run as a tempo
1 CONC rary court site--even before the first court convened in 1735.   It took two years, but the Orange County justices, many of whom had served with Willis in Spotsylvania, finally obtained a site of their own choosing--but they were 
1 CONC not able to dislodge him as clerk. (Ref: http://www.fredericksburg.com)
1 CONT
1 CONT In July 1736,  Henry had to obtain a judgement against Benjamin Cave, then Sheriff of Spotsylvania Co.,  to force Cave to release more than 9000 lbs. of tobacco due Willis in Clerk of Court Fees.  From 1936 on, Henry's sloppy mana
1 CONC gement of the warehouse situation, as well as constuction of the new Courthouse, kept the court 'in a defensive state of confusion'. (Ref: Forgotton Companions, Paula S. Felder, p. 86, 90). At about this time, it is reported that 
1 CONC there was no love lost between Henry Willis and Augustine Smith, as Henry had complanied to the Governors Council about Smith's failure to execute a proper survey on one of Willis' patents.  In July 1736, Henry was awarded the con
1 CONC tract for a permanent court house to be built on lots 42 and 44 in Fredericksburg. The instructions of the court were quite specific and Henry was bive a firm deadline for the completion of the building. (Ref: Forgotton Companions
1 CONC , Paula S. Felder, p. 88).  In 1736, Henry purchased a parcel from Philemon Cavenaugh that originally had been part of the Bowsey / Smith patent.  (Ref: Forgotton Companions, Paula S. Felder, p. 195).
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1737, he added an ordinary near the wharf that he owned on the Rappahannock River.  Dec. 15, 1737. Henry Willis of St. Geo. Par., Spts. Co., Gent., to Francis Thornton of same par. and county, Gent. 25 ster. 150 a. in St. Geo. 
1 CONC Par., Spts. Co., part of a tract called Biranis. Witnesses, Jams. Belsches, John Gordon, Elisha Gordon. Mar. 7, 1737.  It is reported that Henry played a saving role in the the towns' (Fredericksburg) early years because of his in
1 CONC itiative in building the need facilities at a time when such expertise was in short supply.  But, this expertise was not without it's drawback as in one case, Henry's careless ways were a trial on more than one occasion to his fel
1 CONC low justices.  Once, it is reported, that he so badly  mismanaged the warehouses which held the vital crop of tobacco that the crisis had to be resoved by the Assembly. (Ref: 'Forgotten Companions', Paula S. Felder, pp. 90-91).  I
1 CONC n August of 1740, Henry engaged in an acquisitions marathon and acquired at least seventeen lots in and illegal maneuver accomplished in complicity with several of the local trustees.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 5 August 1740, in one 'last glorious deal', Henry purchased 1/4 of the then town of Fredericksburg.   Although the legality of this move is more than open to question (given the limitation of only two lots per purchaser in the 
1 CONC original legislation), the entire scheme cost Willis no more than 62 lbs.  The eventual return on this investment cannot be absolutly calculated, but at the followin court day the following June when the lots had been improved and
1 CONC  then offered for sale by Henrys' executors, eight of the improved lots brought a total of 402 lbs., the tobacco warehouses netted another 141 lbs and the tavern and ferry complex brought another 250 lbs.  (Ref: Forgotton Companio
1 CONC ns, Paula S. Felder, p. 95-97).  It is apparent that Henry died in September 1740 as a result of numerous references to him and lands that were adjacent to his, from various citations in Northern Neck Grants, Vol. II at pgs. 23, 2
1 CONC 5, 27, 43 and 53.  These citations are derived from Book G and H of the Grants.  By the time of his death, there were half a dozen warehouses', in his complex to hold tobacco at the south end of Fredericksburg.  The executors of H
1 CONC enrys' estate were his brother Francis of Gloucester County and John Grymes of Middlesex County.
1 CONT
1 CONT This database is a work in progress.  The sources, citations, and resources are those which I have utilized to date.  If you can add, correct, or suggest please feel free to let me know at your convenience.  Thanks, Fred
